(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqProp=[{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:`A cylindrical rod of length 1 m and radius 4 cm is mounted vertically. It is subjected to a shear force of 10\u2075 N at the top. Considering infinitesimally small displacement in the upper edge, the angular displacement \u03B8 of the rod axis from its original position would be
(shear moduli, G = 10\xB9\u2070 N/m\xB2)`,o:["{1;160\u03C0}","{1;40\u03C0}","{1;4\u03C0}","{1;2\u03C0}"],c:0,s:`Shear modulus G = (F/A)/\u03B8, so \u03B8 = F/(A\xB7G).
Area of the top face A = \u03C0r\xB2 = \u03C0(0.04)\xB2 = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03C0 m\xB2.
\u03B8 = 10\u2075/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03C0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070) = 10\u2075/(1.6 \xD7 10\u2077 \u03C0) = 1/(160\u03C0).
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"Two wires A and B are made of same material having ratio of lengths {L_A;L_B} = {1;3} and their diameters ratio {d_A;d_B} = 2. If both the wires are stretched using same force, what would be the ratio of their respective elongations?",o:["1 : 3","3 : 4","1 : 6","1 : 12"],c:3,s:`Elongation \u0394l = FL/(AY) and A \u221D d\xB2, so \u0394l \u221D L/d\xB2 for the same F and Y.
\u0394l_A/\u0394l_B = (L_A/L_B) \xD7 (d_B/d_A)\xB2 = (1/3) \xD7 (1/2)\xB2 = 1/12.
Answer: 1 : 12, option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A 3 m long wire of radius 3 mm shows an extension of 0.1 mm when loaded vertically by a mass of 50 kg in an experiment to determine Young's modulus. The value of Young's modulus of the wire as per this experiment is P \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2, where the value of P is (Take g = 3\u03C0 m/s\xB2)",o:["5","25","10","2.5"],c:0,s:`Y = FL/(A\u0394l) with F = mg = 50 \xD7 3\u03C0 = 150\u03C0 N.
A = \u03C0(3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 9\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
Y = (150\u03C0 \xD7 3)/(9\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = 450/(9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070) = 5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2.
So P = 5, option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"The fractional compression {\u0394V;V} of water at the depth of 2.5 km below the sea level is ______%. Given, the Bulk modulus of water = 2 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\u207B\xB2, density of water = 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3, acceleration due to gravity, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2.",o:["1.75","1.25","1.5","1.0"],c:1,s:`Pressure at depth h: P = \u03C1gh = 10\xB3 \xD7 10 \xD7 2500 = 2.5 \xD7 10\u2077 Pa.
Bulk modulus B = P/(\u0394V/V), so \u0394V/V = P/B = 2.5 \xD7 10\u2077/(2 \xD7 10\u2079) = 1.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2.
This is 1.25%, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A wire of length L and radius r is clamped at one end. If its other end is pulled by a force F, its length increases by l. If the radius of the wire and the applied force both are reduced to half of their original values keeping original length constant, the increase in length will become",o:["3 times","2 times","{3;2} times","4 times"],c:1,s:`l = FL/(\u03C0r\xB2Y), so l \u221D F/r\xB2 for fixed L and Y.
New F = F/2 and new r = r/2, so new l \u221D (F/2)/(r\xB2/4) = 2F/r\xB2.
The increase in length becomes 2 times, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"Young's modules of material of a wire of length 'L' and cross-sectional area A is Y. If the length of the wire is doubled and cross-sectional area is halved then Young's modules will be",o:["2Y","Y","4Y","{Y;4}"],c:1,s:`Young's modulus is a property of the material, not of the size or shape of the wire.
Changing length and area does not change it.
So it stays Y, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"An aluminium rod with Young's modulus Y = 7.0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 N/m\xB2 undergoes elastic strain of 0.04%. The energy per unit volume stored in the rod in SI unit is",o:["11200","8400","5600","2800"],c:2,s:`Energy per unit volume u = \xBD \xD7 Y \xD7 (strain)\xB2.
Strain = 0.04% = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074.
u = \xBD \xD7 7 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 \xD7 (4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074)\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 7 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 5600 J/m\xB3.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:`Young's moduli of the material of wires A and B are in the ratio of 1 : 4, while its area of cross sections are in the ratio of 1 : 3. If the same amount of load is applied to both the wires, the amount of elongation produced in the wires A and B will be in the ratio of
[Assume length of wires A and B are same]`,o:["12 : 1","1 : 12","1 : 36","36 : 1"],c:0,s:`\u0394l = FL/(AY). With F and L the same, \u0394l \u221D 1/(AY).
\u0394l_A/\u0394l_B = (A_B Y_B)/(A_A Y_A) = (3 \xD7 4)/(1 \xD7 1) = 12.
The ratio is 12 : 1, option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A wire of length 'L' and radius 'r' is clamped rigidly at one end. When the other end of the wire is pulled by a force f, its length increases by 'l'. Another wire of same material of length '2L' and radius '2r' is pulled by a force '2f'. Then the increase in its length will be",o:["2l","4l","l","l/2"],c:2,s:`\u0394l = FL/(\u03C0r\xB2Y), so \u0394l \u221D FL/r\xB2 for the same material.
New value \u221D (2f)(2L)/(2r)\xB2 = 4fL/(4r\xB2) = fL/r\xB2.
This equals the original, so the increase in length is l, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:`The Young's modulus of a steel wire of length 6 m and cross-sectional area 3 mm\xB2, is 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2. The wire is suspended from its support on a given planet. A block of mass 4 kg is attached to the free end of the wire. The acceleration due to gravity on the planet is {1;4} of its value on the earth. The elongation of wire is
(Take g on the earth = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)`,o:["0.1 cm","1 mm","1 cm","0.1 mm"],c:3,s:`Gravity on the planet g' = 10/4 = 2.5 m/s\xB2, so F = 4 \xD7 2.5 = 10 N.
A = 3 mm\xB2 = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
\u0394l = FL/(AY) = (10 \xD7 6)/(3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9) = 60/(6 \xD7 10\u2075) = 10\u207B\u2074 m.
This is 0.1 mm, option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A 100 m long wire having cross-section area 6.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2 and Young's modulus is 10\xB9\u2070 N m\u207B\xB2 is subjected to a load of 250 N, then the elongation in the wire will be",o:["6.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m","4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m","4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m","6.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m"],c:1,s:`\u0394l = FL/(AY).
\u0394l = (250 \xD7 100)/(6.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070) = 25000/(6.25 \xD7 10\u2076).
\u0394l = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"Choose the correct relationship between Poisson ratio (\u03C3), bulk modulus (K) and modulus of rigidity (\u03B7) of a given solid object",o:["\u03C3 = {3K+2\u03B7;6K+2\u03B7}","\u03C3 = {6K\u22122\u03B7;3K\u22122\u03B7}","\u03C3 = {3K\u22122\u03B7;6K+2\u03B7}","\u03C3 = {6K+2\u03B7;3K\u22122\u03B7}"],c:2,s:`The standard relations are Y = 3K(1 \u2212 2\u03C3) and Y = 2\u03B7(1 + \u03C3).
Equate them: 3K \u2212 6K\u03C3 = 2\u03B7 + 2\u03B7\u03C3, so \u03C3(6K + 2\u03B7) = 3K \u2212 2\u03B7.
\u03C3 = (3K \u2212 2\u03B7)/(6K + 2\u03B7), option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A force is applied to a steel wire 'A', rigidly clamped at one end. As a result elongation in the wire is 0.2 mm. If same force is applied to another steel wire 'B' of double the length and a diameter 2.4 times that of the wire 'A', the elongation in the wire 'B' will be (wires having uniform circular cross sections)",o:["2.77 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 mm","6.9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 mm","6.06 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 mm","3.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 mm"],c:1,s:`\u0394l = 4FL/(\u03C0d\xB2Y), so \u0394l \u221D L/d\xB2 for the same F and Y.
\u0394l_B = 0.2 \xD7 (2L/L) \xD7 (1/2.4)\xB2 = 0.2 \xD7 2/5.76.
\u0394l_B = 0.4/5.76 \u2248 0.0694 mm = 6.9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 mm.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"Under the same load, wire A having length 5.0 m and cross section 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2 stretches uniformly by the same amount as another wire B of length 6.0 m and a cross-section of 3.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2. The ratio of the Young's modulus of wire A to that of wire B will be",o:["1 : 4","1 : 1","1 : 10","1 : 2"],c:1,s:`Y = FL/(A\u0394l). F and \u0394l are the same, so Y \u221D L/A.
Y_A/Y_B = (L_A/A_A)/(L_B/A_B) = (5.0/2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075)/(6.0/3.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075).
Both ratios equal 2 \xD7 10\u2075, so Y_A : Y_B = 1 : 1, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"The area of cross section of the rope used to lift a load by a crane is 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2. The maximum lifting capacity of the crane is 10 metric tons. To increase the lifting capacity of the crane to 25 metric tons, the required area of cross section of the rope should be (take g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["6.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2","10 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2","1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2","1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2"],c:0,s:`The rope breaks at a fixed maximum stress, so the load it can carry is proportional to its area.
A' = A \xD7 (25/10) = 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 2.5.
A' = 6.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2, option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A steel wire of length 3.2 m (Y_s = 2.0 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2) and a copper wire of length 4.4 m (Y_c = 1.1 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2), both of radius 1.4 mm are connected end to end. When stretched by a load, the net elongation is found to be 1.4 mm. The load applied, in Newton, will be (Given \u03C0 = {22;7})",o:["360","180","1080","154"],c:3,s:`Both wires carry the same load F and have the same area A = \u03C0r\xB2 = (22/7)(1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 6.16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
Total elongation = (F/A)(L_s/Y_s + L_c/Y_c) = (F/A)(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 + 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9) = (F/A)(5.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9).
F = (1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 6.16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076)/(5.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9) = 154 N.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:`The force required to stretch a wire of cross-section 1 cm\xB2 to double its length will be
(Given Young's modulus of the wire = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2)`,o:["1 \xD7 10\u2077 N","1.5 \xD7 10\u2077 N","2 \xD7 10\u2077 N","2.5 \xD7 10\u2077 N"],c:2,s:`Doubling the length means the strain \u0394l/L = 1.
F = Y \xD7 A \xD7 strain = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 1.
F = 2 \xD7 10\u2077 N, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"If the length of a wire is made double and radius is halved of its respective values. Then, the Young's modulus of the material of the wire will",o:["remain same","become 8 times its initial value","become {1;4}th of its initial value","become 4 times its initial value"],c:0,s:`Young's modulus depends only on the material of the wire.
Changing the length or the radius does not change it.
So it remains the same, option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"The bulk modulus of a liquid is 3 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 N m\u207B\xB2. The pressure required to reduce the volume of liquid by 2% is",o:["3 \xD7 10\u2078 N m\u207B\xB2","9 \xD7 10\u2078 N m\u207B\xB2","6 \xD7 10\u2078 N m\u207B\xB2","12 \xD7 10\u2078 N m\u207B\xB2"],c:2,s:`B = P/(\u0394V/V), so P = B \xD7 (\u0394V/V).
P = 3 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 \xD7 0.02 = 6 \xD7 10\u2078 N m\u207B\xB2.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A wire of length L is hanging from a fixed support. The length changes to L\u2081 and L\u2082 when 1 kg and 2 kg are suspended respectively from its free end. Then the value of L is equal to",o:["\u221A(L\u2081L\u2082)","{L\u2081+L\u2082;2}","2L\u2081 \u2212 L\u2082","3L\u2081 \u2212 2L\u2082"],c:2,s:`The extension is proportional to the load: extension = kM.
L\u2081 = L + k and L\u2082 = L + 2k.
Then L\u2082 \u2212 L\u2081 = k, so L = L\u2081 \u2212 k = L\u2081 \u2212 (L\u2082 \u2212 L\u2081) = 2L\u2081 \u2212 L\u2082.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:`Two blocks of masses 3 kg and 5 kg are connected by a metal wire going over a smooth pulley. The breaking stress of the metal is {24;\u03C0} \xD7 10\xB2 N m\u207B\xB2. What is the minimum radius of the wire?
(Take g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A smooth pulley hung from a ceiling with a 3 kg block on the left wire and a 5 kg block on the right wire",d:[["l",110,20,230,20,"B"],["l",170,20,170,42],["c",170,60,18],["l",152,60,152,120],["l",188,60,188,150],["r",132,120,40,26,"f"],["t",152,137,"3 kg","ms"],["r",168,150,40,26,"f"],["t",188,167,"5 kg","ms"]]},o:["1250 cm","1.25 cm","125 cm","12.5 cm"],c:3,s:`Acceleration of the system: a = (5 \u2212 3)g/(5 + 3) = g/4.
Tension in the wire: T = 3(g + a) = 3 \xD7 12.5 = 37.5 N.
Breaking stress = T/(\u03C0r\xB2), so r\xB2 = 37.5/(\u03C0 \xD7 2400/\u03C0) = 37.5/2400 = 0.015625 m\xB2.
r = 0.125 m = 12.5 cm, option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A uniform heavy rod of weight 10 kg m s\u207B\xB2, cross-sectional area 100 cm\xB2 and length 20 cm is hanging from a fixed support. Young modulus of the material of the rod is 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2. Neglecting the lateral contraction, find the elongation of rod due to its own weight.",o:["5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 m","4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 m","5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 m","2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 m"],c:0,s:`For a heavy rod hanging under its own weight, the extension is \u0394l = WL/(2AY).
W = 10 N, L = 0.2 m, A = 100 cm\xB2 = 10\u207B\xB2 m\xB2.
\u0394l = (10 \xD7 0.2)/(2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9) = 2/(4 \xD7 10\u2079) = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 m.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:`Four identical hollow cylindrical columns of mild steel support a big structure of mass 50 \xD7 10\xB3 kg. The inner and outer radii of each column are 50 cm and 100 cm respectively. Assuming uniform local distribution, calculate the compression strain of each column.
[Use Y = 2.0 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 Pa, g = 9.8 m/s\xB2]`,o:["1.87 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3","2.60 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077","3.60 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078","7.07 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074"],c:1,s:`Load on each column: F = (50 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 9.8)/4 = 1.225 \xD7 10\u2075 N.
Area of a hollow column: A = \u03C0(1\xB2 \u2212 0.5\xB2) = 0.75\u03C0 = 2.356 m\xB2.
Stress = F/A = 1.225 \xD7 10\u2075/2.356 \u2248 5.2 \xD7 10\u2074 Pa.
Strain = stress/Y = 5.2 \xD7 10\u2074/(2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9) = 2.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"The value of tension in a long thin metal wire has been changed from T\u2081 to T\u2082. The lengths of the metal wire at two different values of tension T\u2081 and T\u2082 are l\u2081 and l\u2082 respectively. The actual length of the metal wire is",o:["\u221A(T\u2081T\u2082l\u2081l\u2082)","{l\u2081+l\u2082;2}","{T\u2081l\u2082\u2212T\u2082l\u2081;T\u2081\u2212T\u2082}","{T\u2081l\u2081\u2212T\u2082l\u2082;T\u2081\u2212T\u2082}"],c:2,s:`Within the elastic limit, extension is proportional to tension: l = L + kT, where L is the actual length.
l\u2081 = L + kT\u2081 and l\u2082 = L + kT\u2082.
Subtract: k = (l\u2081 \u2212 l\u2082)/(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082). Then L = l\u2081 \u2212 kT\u2081 = (l\u2081T\u2081 \u2212 l\u2081T\u2082 \u2212 l\u2081T\u2081 + l\u2082T\u2081)/(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082).
L = (T\u2081l\u2082 \u2212 T\u2082l\u2081)/(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082), option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"The length of a metal wire is l\u2081, when the tension in it is T\u2081 and is l\u2082 when the tension is T\u2082. The natural length of the wire is",o:["{l\u2081+l\u2082;2}","{l\u2081T\u2082\u2212l\u2082T\u2081;T\u2082\u2212T\u2081}","{l\u2081T\u2082+l\u2082T\u2081;T\u2082+T\u2081}","\u221A(l\u2081l\u2082)"],c:1,s:`Let L be the natural length. Extension is proportional to tension: l = L + kT.
l\u2081 = L + kT\u2081 and l\u2082 = L + kT\u2082, so k = (l\u2082 \u2212 l\u2081)/(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081).
L = l\u2081 \u2212 kT\u2081 = (l\u2081T\u2082 \u2212 l\u2081T\u2081 \u2212 l\u2082T\u2081 + l\u2081T\u2081)/(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081).
L = (l\u2081T\u2082 \u2212 l\u2082T\u2081)/(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081), option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"Two wires of same length and radius are joined end to end and loaded. The Young's moduli of the materials of the two wires are Y\u2081 and Y\u2082. The combination behaves as a single wire then its Young's modulus is",o:["Y = {2Y\u2081Y\u2082;Y\u2081+Y\u2082}","Y = {Y\u2081Y\u2082;Y\u2081+Y\u2082}","Y = {Y\u2081Y\u2082;2(Y\u2081+Y\u2082)}","Y = {2Y\u2081Y\u2082;3(Y\u2081+Y\u2082)}"],c:0,s:`Let each wire have length L and area A. The same force F acts on both (series).
Total extension = FL/(AY\u2081) + FL/(AY\u2082) = (FL/A)(1/Y\u2081 + 1/Y\u2082).
For the single wire of length 2L: 2FL/(AY) = (FL/A)(Y\u2081 + Y\u2082)/(Y\u2081Y\u2082).
So Y = 2Y\u2081Y\u2082/(Y\u2081 + Y\u2082), option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"If Y, K and \u03B7 are values of Young's modulus, bulk modulus and modulus of rigidity of any material respectively. Choose the correct relation for these parameters.",o:["\u03B7 = {3YK;9K+Y} N m\u207B\xB2","Y = {9K\u03B7;2\u03B7+3K} N m\u207B\xB2","K = {Y\u03B7;9\u03B7\u22123Y} N m\u207B\xB2","Y = {9K\u03B7;3K\u2212\u03B7} N m\u207B\xB2"],c:2,s:`The standard relation is Y = 9K\u03B7/(3K + \u03B7).
Cross-multiply: 3YK + Y\u03B7 = 9K\u03B7, so K(9\u03B7 \u2212 3Y) = Y\u03B7.
K = Y\u03B7/(9\u03B7 \u2212 3Y), option (c).
(The same relation gives \u03B7 = 3YK/(9K \u2212 Y), which is not option (a).)`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"The normal density of a material is \u03C1 and its bulk modulus of elasticity is K. The magnitude of increase in density of material, when a pressure P is applied uniformly on all sides, will be",o:["{PK;\u03C1}","{\u03C1P;K}","{K;\u03C1P}","{\u03C1K;P}"],c:1,s:`Mass is constant, so \u03C1V = constant and \u0394\u03C1/\u03C1 = \u2212\u0394V/V in magnitude.
Bulk modulus gives \u0394V/V = P/K.
So \u0394\u03C1 = \u03C1P/K, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:`A cube of metal is subjected to a hydrostatic pressure of 4 GPa. The percentage change in the length of the side of the cube is close to
(Given bulk modulus of metal, B = 8 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 Pa)`,o:["1.67","5","20","0.6"],c:0,s:`Volume strain: \u0394V/V = P/B = 4 \xD7 10\u2079/(8 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070) = 0.05.
For a cube V = a\xB3, so \u0394V/V = 3 \u0394a/a.
\u0394a/a = 0.05/3 = 0.0167, which is 1.67%. Option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"Two steel wires having same length are suspended from a ceiling under the same load. If the ratio of their energy stored per unit volume is 1 : 4, the ratio of their diameters is",o:["\u221A2 : 1","1 : \u221A2","2 : 1","1 : 2"],c:0,s:`Energy per unit volume u = \u03C3\xB2/(2Y), and stress \u03C3 = F/A \u221D 1/d\xB2.
So u \u221D 1/d\u2074 for the same material and load.
u\u2081/u\u2082 = (d\u2082/d\u2081)\u2074 = 1/4, so d\u2081/d\u2082 = 4^(1/4) = \u221A2.
The ratio of diameters is \u221A2 : 1, option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"A boy's catapult is made of rubber cord which is 42 cm long, with 6 mm diameter of cross-section and of negligible mass. The boy keeps a stone weighing 0.02 kg on it and stretches the cord by 20 cm by applying a constant force. When released, the stone flies off with a velocity of 20 m s\u207B\xB9. Neglect the change in the area of cross-section of the cord while stretched. The Young's modulus of rubber is closest to",o:["10\xB3 N m\u207B\xB2","10\u2074 N m\u207B\xB2","10\u2078 N m\u207B\xB2","10\u2076 N m\u207B\xB2"],c:3,s:`Work done on the cord = kinetic energy of the stone = \xBD \xD7 0.02 \xD7 20\xB2 = 4 J.
With a constant force F over 0.2 m: F \xD7 0.2 = 4, so F = 20 N.
Area A = \u03C0(3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 2.83 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2.
Y = FL/(A\u0394l) = (20 \xD7 0.42)/(2.83 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 0.2) \u2248 1.5 \xD7 10\u2076 N m\u207B\xB2.
The closest option is 10\u2076 N m\u207B\xB2, option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"A steel wire having a radius of 2.0 mm, carrying a load of 4 kg, is hanging from a ceiling. Given that g = 3.1 \u03C0 m s\u207B\xB2, what will be the tensile stress that would be developed in the wire?",o:["4.8 \xD7 10\u2076 N m\u207B\xB2","3.1 \xD7 10\u2076 N m\u207B\xB2","6.2 \xD7 10\u2076 N m\u207B\xB2","5.2 \xD7 10\u2076 N m\u207B\xB2"],c:1,s:`Force F = mg = 4 \xD7 3.1\u03C0 = 12.4\u03C0 N.
Area A = \u03C0(2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
Stress = F/A = 12.4\u03C0/(4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 3.1 \xD7 10\u2076 N m\u207B\xB2.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"Young's moduli of two wires A and B are in the ratio 7 : 4. Wire A is 2 m long and has radius R. Wire B is 1.5 m long and has radius 2 mm. If the two wires stretch by the same length for a given load, then the value of R is close to",o:["1.9 mm","1.3 mm","1.5 mm","1.7 mm"],c:3,s:`\u0394l = FL/(\u03C0r\xB2Y). For the same F and \u0394l: Y r\xB2/L is the same for both wires.
Y_A R\xB2/L_A = Y_B r_B\xB2/L_B, so 7R\xB2/2 = 4 \xD7 2\xB2/1.5 (R in mm).
R\xB2 = (16/1.5) \xD7 (2/7) = 3.05 mm\xB2, so R \u2248 1.75 mm.
The closest option is 1.7 mm, option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"The elastic limit of brass is 379 MPa. What should be the minimum diameter of a brass rod if it is to support a 400 N load without exceeding its elastic limit?",o:["0.90 mm","1.36 mm","1.16 mm","1.00 mm"],c:2,s:`The stress must not exceed 379 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa, so A = F/stress = 400/(379 \xD7 10\u2076) = 1.055 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
A = \u03C0d\xB2/4, so d\xB2 = 4A/\u03C0 = 1.344 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
d = 1.16 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m = 1.16 mm, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A solid sphere of radius r made of a soft material of bulk modulus K is surrounded by a liquid in a cylindrical container. A massless piston of area a floats on the surface of the liquid, covering entire cross section of cylindrical container. When a mass m is placed on the surface of the piston to compress the liquid, the fractional decrement in the radius of the sphere, ({dr;r}), is",o:["{Ka;mg}","{Ka;3mg}","{mg;3Ka}","{mg;Ka}"],c:2,s:`The extra pressure on the liquid, and hence on the sphere, is P = mg/a.
Volume strain of the sphere: \u0394V/V = P/K = mg/(Ka).
For a sphere V \u221D r\xB3, so \u0394V/V = 3 dr/r.
dr/r = mg/(3Ka), option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"As shown in the figure, forces of 10\u2075 N each are applied in opposite directions, on the upper and lower faces of a cube of side 10 cm, shifting the upper face parallel to itself by 0.5 cm. If the side of another cube of the same material is 20 cm, then under similar conditions as above, the displacement will be",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A cube with a force F acting to the right on its upper face and F to the left on its lower face",d:[["r",100,50,120,100,"f"],["l",170,42,250,42,"a"],["t",256,46,"F"],["l",150,158,70,158,"a"],["t",62,162,"F","e"],["t",160,105,"side 10 cm","ms"]]},o:["0.25 cm","0.37 cm","1.00 cm","0.75 cm"],c:0,s:`Shear modulus G = (F/A)/(x/L), so the displacement x = FL/(AG) = F/(L G) because A = L\xB2.
So for the same F and material, x \u221D 1/L.
x\u2082 = 0.5 \xD7 (10/20) = 0.25 cm.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"A man grows into a giant such that his linear dimensions increase by a factor of 9. Assuming that his density remains same, the stress in the leg will change by a factor of",o:["9","{1;9}","81","{1;81}"],c:0,s:`Mass \u221D volume \u221D (linear size)\xB3, so weight increases by 9\xB3 = 729 times.
Cross-sectional area of the leg \u221D (linear size)\xB2 increases by 9\xB2 = 81 times.
Stress = weight/area increases by 729/81 = 9 times.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A uniformly tapering conical wire is made from a material of Young's modulus Y and has a normal, unextended length L. The radii, at the upper and lower ends of this conical wire, have values R and 3R, respectively. The upper end of the wire is fixed to a rigid support and a mass M is suspended from its lower end. The equilibrium extended length, of this wire, would equal",o:["L(1 + {2 Mg;9\u03C0YR\xB2})","L(1 + {Mg;9\u03C0YR\xB2})","L(1 + {Mg;3\u03C0YR\xB2})","L(1 + {2 Mg;3\u03C0YR\xB2})"],c:2,s:`Take a thin slice dx at distance x from the top. Its radius is r = R + 2Rx/L, and it carries tension Mg (wire mass neglected).
Extension of the slice: d(\u0394l) = Mg dx/(\u03C0r\xB2Y).
Total extension = (Mg/\u03C0Y)\u222Bdx/r\xB2 from 0 to L = (Mg/\u03C0Y) \xD7 L/(R \xD7 3R) = MgL/(3\u03C0YR\xB2).
Extended length = L + MgL/(3\u03C0YR\xB2) = L(1 + Mg/(3\u03C0YR\xB2)), option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A bottle has an opening of radius a and length b. A cork of length b and radius (a + \u0394a) where (\u0394a << a) is compressed to fit into the opening completely (the cork sits fully inside the neck of the bottle). If the bulk modulus of cork is B and frictional coefficient between the bottle and cork is \u03BC then the force needed to push the cork into the bottle is",o:["(\u03C0\u03BCBb)a","(2\u03C0\u03BCBb)\u0394a","(\u03C0\u03BCBb)\u0394a","(4\u03C0\u03BCBb)\u0394a"],c:3,s:`The cork's radius is squeezed by \u0394a while its length stays b, so the volume strain is \u0394V/V \u2248 2\u0394a/a.
Pressure on the cork: P = B \xD7 \u0394V/V = 2B\u0394a/a.
Normal force on the wall: N = P \xD7 (2\u03C0ab) = 4\u03C0Bb\u0394a.
Push force = frictional force = \u03BCN = (4\u03C0\u03BCBb)\u0394a, option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"If the ratio of lengths, radii and Young's moduli of steel and brass wires in the figure are a, b and c respectively, then the corresponding ratio of increase in their lengths is",fig:{w:260,h:210,alt:"A steel wire hangs from a ceiling carrying a mass M, and a brass wire hangs below it carrying a mass 2M",d:[["l",90,15,170,15,"B"],["l",130,15,130,80],["t",142,50,"Steel"],["r",110,80,40,28,"f"],["t",130,99,"M","ms"],["l",130,108,130,160],["t",142,138,"Brass"],["r",108,160,44,28,"f"],["t",130,179,"2M","ms"]]},o:["{3c;2ab\xB2}","{2a\xB2c;b}","{3a;2b\xB2c}","{2ac;b\xB2}"],c:2,s:`The steel wire carries M + 2M = 3M, while the brass wire carries only 2M.
\u0394l = FL/(\u03C0r\xB2Y), so \u0394l_steel/\u0394l_brass = (3Mg \xD7 L_s/(r_s\xB2Y_s))/(2Mg \xD7 L_b/(r_b\xB2Y_b)).
With L_s/L_b = a, r_s/r_b = b, Y_s/Y_b = c: ratio = (3/2) \xD7 a/(b\xB2 c).
Ratio = 3a/(2b\xB2c), option (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"One end of a horizontal thick copper wire of length 2L and radius 2R is welded to an end of another horizontal thin copper wire of length L and radius R. When the arrangement is stretched by applying forces at two ends, the ratio of the elongation in the thin wire to that in the thick wire is",o:["0.25","0.50","2.00","4.00"],c:2,s:`Both wires carry the same force F and have the same Young's modulus.
\u0394l = FL/(\u03C0r\xB2Y), so \u0394l \u221D L/r\xB2.
Thin: L/R\xB2. Thick: 2L/(4R\xB2) = L/(2R\xB2).
Ratio thin : thick = (L/R\xB2)/(L/2R\xB2) = 2.00, option (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"Two wires are made of the same material and have the same volume. However wire 1 has cross-sectional area A and wire 2 has cross-sectional area 3A. If the length of wire 1 increases by \u0394x on applying force F, how much force is needed to stretch wire 2 by the same amount?",o:["F","4F","6F","9F"],c:3,s:`Same volume: A \xD7 l\u2081 = 3A \xD7 l\u2082, so wire 2 has length l\u2082 = l\u2081/3.
Wire 1: \u0394x = F l\u2081/(AY). Wire 2: \u0394x = F\u2082 (l\u2081/3)/(3A Y) = F\u2082 l\u2081/(9AY).
Equate the two: F = F\u2082/9, so F\u2082 = 9F.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A wire elongates by l mm when a load W is hanged from it. If the wire goes over a pulley and two weights W each are hung at the two ends, the elongation of the wire will be (in mm)",o:["{l;2}","l","2l","zero"],c:1,s:`In the second case each end of the wire is pulled by a force W, so the tension in the wire is W.
In the first case the tension was also W (the wall or support supplies W at the other end).
The same tension gives the same elongation, l mm. Option (b).`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"If S is stress and Y is Young's modulus of material of a wire, the energy stored in the wire per unit volume is",o:["{2Y;S}","{S;2Y}","2S\xB2Y","{S\xB2;2Y}"],c:3,s:`Energy per unit volume u = \xBD \xD7 stress \xD7 strain.
Strain = S/Y, so u = \xBD \xD7 S \xD7 (S/Y) = S\xB2/(2Y).
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"When temperature of a gas is 20\xB0C and pressure is changed from P\u2081 = 1.01 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa to P\u2082 = 1.165 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa then the volume changed by 10%. The bulk modulus is",o:["1.55 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa","0.115 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa","1.4 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa","1.01 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa"],c:0,s:`Bulk modulus B = \u0394P/(\u0394V/V).
\u0394P = 1.165 \xD7 10\u2075 \u2212 1.01 \xD7 10\u2075 = 0.155 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa, and \u0394V/V = 10% = 0.1.
B = 0.155 \xD7 10\u2075/0.1 = 1.55 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"A wire fixed at the upper end stretches by length l by applying a force F. The work done in stretching is",o:["{F;2l}","Fl","2Fl","{Fl;2}"],c:3,s:`The force rises steadily from 0 to F as the extension grows from 0 to l (Hooke's law).
Work done = area under the force-extension graph = \xBD \xD7 F \xD7 l.
Answer: Fl/2, option (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"The adjacent graph shows the extension (\u0394l) of a wire of length 1 m suspended from the top of a roof at one end and with a load W connected to the other end. If the cross-sectional area of the wire is 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2, calculate the Young's modulus of the material of the wire.",fig:{w:300,h:190,alt:"Graph of extension \u0394l in units of 10\u207B\u2074 m against load W in newtons: a straight line from (20 N, 1) through (40, 2) and (60, 3) to (80, 4)",d:[["l",50,150,50,20,"a"],["l",50,150,270,150,"a"],["t",54,14,"\u0394l (\xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m)","s"],["t",270,172,"W (N)","e"],["l",90,120,210,30],["l",50,120,90,120,"d"],["l",50,90,130,90,"d"],["l",50,60,170,60,"d"],["l",50,30,210,30,"d"],["l",90,120,90,150,"d"],["l",130,90,130,150,"d"],["l",170,60,170,150,"d"],["l",210,30,210,150,"d"],["t",44,124,"1","es"],["t",44,94,"2","es"],["t",44,64,"3","es"],["t",44,34,"4","es"],["t",90,166,"20","ms"],["t",130,166,"40","ms"],["t",170,166,"60","ms"],["t",210,166,"80","ms"]]},o:["2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2","2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2","3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 N/m\xB2","2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 N/m\xB2"],c:0,s:`Read one point of the straight line: at W = 80 N the extension is \u0394l = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m.
Y = (F/A)/(\u0394l/L) = (80/10\u207B\u2076)/(4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074/1) = 8 \xD7 10\u2077/(4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074).
Y = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2, option (a).
(Check with another point: W = 40 N gives \u0394l = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m, the same Y.)`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"A wire suspended vertically from one of its ends is stretched by attaching a weight of 200 N to the lower end. The weight stretches the wire by 1 mm. Then the elastic energy stored in the wire is",o:["0.2 J","10 J","20 J","0.1 J"],c:3,s:`Elastic energy = \xBD \xD7 F \xD7 \u0394l.
U = \xBD \xD7 200 N \xD7 1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m = 0.1 J.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"The following four wires are made of the same material. Which of these will have the largest extension when the same tension is applied?",o:["length = 50 cm, diameter = 0.5 mm","length = 100 cm, diameter = 1 mm","length = 200 cm, diameter = 2 mm","length = 300 cm, diameter = 3 mm"],c:0,s:`For the same force and material, \u0394l = FL/(AY) is proportional to L/d\xB2.
(a) 50/0.25 = 200   (b) 100/1 = 100   (c) 200/4 = 50   (d) 300/9 \u2248 33.
Wire (a) has the largest value, so it has the largest extension.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"In plotting stress versus strain curves for two materials P and Q, a student by mistake puts strain on the y-axis and stress on the x-axis as shown in the figure. Then the correct statement(s) is(are)",fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"Strain on the vertical axis and stress on the horizontal axis: curve P rises more steeply and ends higher and further right than curve Q",d:[["l",50,170,50,20,"a"],["l",50,170,270,170,"a"],["t",54,16,"Strain","s"],["t",268,190,"Stress","es"],["p","M50 170 L190 70 Q205 58 208 42"],["p","M50 170 L170 130 Q182 124 188 114"],["t",98,108,"P"],["t",172,148,"Q"]]},o:["P has more tensile strength than Q","P is more ductile than Q","P is more brittle than Q","The Young's modulus of P is more than that of Q"],c:[0,1],s:`On the graph, the curve of P reaches a larger stress before breaking, so P has more tensile strength. (a) is correct.
P also reaches a much larger strain, so it can be stretched more before breaking. P is more ductile. (b) is correct and (c) is wrong.
Young's modulus = stress/strain. For a given stress, P has more strain (its line is steeper here), so Y of P is smaller than Y of Q. (d) is wrong.
Correct options: (a) and (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"A steel wire of length 2 m and Young's modulus 2.0 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2 is stretched by a force. If Poisson ratio and transverse strain for the wire are 0.2 and 10\u207B\xB3 respectively, then the elastic potential energy density of the wire is ______ \xD7 10\u2075 (in SI units).",n:25,s:`Poisson ratio = transverse strain/longitudinal strain, so longitudinal strain = 10\u207B\xB3/0.2 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3.
Energy density u = \xBD \xD7 Y \xD7 (strain)\xB2.
u = \xBD \xD7 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 \xD7 (5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 10\xB9\xB9 \xD7 25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 2.5 \xD7 10\u2076 J/m\xB3.
This is 25 \xD7 10\u2075, so the answer is 25.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"Two slabs with square cross section of different materials (1, 2) with equal sides (l) and thickness d\u2081 and d\u2082 such that d\u2082 = 2d\u2081 and l > d\u2082. Considering lower edges of the slabs are fixed to the floor, we apply equal shearing force on the narrow faces. The angle of deformation is \u03B8\u2082 = 2\u03B8\u2081. If the shear moduli of material 1 is 4 \xD7 10\u2079 N/m\xB2, then shear moduli of material 2 is x \xD7 10\u2079 N/m\xB2, where value of x is ______.",n:1,s:`The narrow face has area A = l \xD7 d, and the shear modulus is G = F/(A\u03B8).
So G\u2081 = F/(l d\u2081 \u03B8\u2081) and G\u2082 = F/(l d\u2082 \u03B8\u2082) = F/(l \xD7 2d\u2081 \xD7 2\u03B8\u2081) = G\u2081/4.
G\u2082 = 4 \xD7 10\u2079/4 = 1 \xD7 10\u2079 N/m\xB2.
So x = 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"The increase in pressure required to decrease the volume of a water sample by 0.2% is P \xD7 10\u2075 N m\u207B\xB2. Bulk modulus of water is 2.15 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\u207B\xB2. The value of P is ______.",n:43,s:`B = \u0394P/(\u0394V/V), so \u0394P = B \xD7 (\u0394V/V).
\u0394P = 2.15 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 0.002 = 4.3 \xD7 10\u2076 N m\u207B\xB2.
This is 43 \xD7 10\u2075, so P = 43.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:`The volume contraction of a solid copper cube of edge length 10 cm, when subjected to a hydraulic pressure of 7 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa, would be ______ mm\xB3.
(Given bulk modulus of copper = 1.4 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2)`,n:50,s:`Original volume V = (10 cm)\xB3 = 1000 cm\xB3 = 10\u2076 mm\xB3.
\u0394V = V \xD7 P/B = 10\u2076 \xD7 7 \xD7 10\u2076/(1.4 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9) = 10\u2076 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075.
\u0394V = 50 mm\xB3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"The density and breaking stress of a wire are 6 \xD7 10\u2074 kg/m\xB3 and 1.2 \xD7 10\u2078 N/m\xB2 respectively. The wire is suspended from a rigid support on a planet where the acceleration due to gravity is 1/3rd of the value on the surface of earth. The maximum length of the wire without breaking is ______ m. (Take g = 10 m/s\xB2)",n:600,s:`At the top the wire carries its own weight, so stress = (A L \u03C1 g')/A = \u03C1 g' L.
The planet's gravity is g' = 10/3 m/s\xB2.
At breaking: \u03C1 g' L = 1.2 \xD7 10\u2078, so L = 1.2 \xD7 10\u2078/(6 \xD7 10\u2074 \xD7 10/3) = 1.2 \xD7 10\u2078/(2 \xD7 10\u2075).
L = 600 m.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A wire of cross sectional area A, modulus of elasticity 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2 and length 2 m is stretched between two vertical rigid supports. When a mass of 2 kg is suspended at the middle it sags lower from its original position making angle \u03B8 = {1;100} radian on the points of support. The value of A is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2. (consider x << L). (Given: g = 10 m/s\xB2)",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"A wire of length 2L stretched between two vertical supports, sagging by x in the middle under a 2 kg mass, making angle \u03B8 with the horizontal at each support",d:[["r",22,40,8,60,"f"],["r",310,40,8,60,"f"],["l",30,60,310,60,"d"],["l",30,60,170,100],["l",170,100,310,60],["l",170,100,170,130],["r",150,130,40,26,"f"],["t",170,147,"2 kg","ms"],["l",30,30,310,30,"b"],["t",170,24,"2L","m"],["t",80,56,"\u03B8","s"],["l",150,60,150,94,"b"],["t",156,82,"x","s"]]},n:1,s:`Vertical balance at the middle: 2T sin\u03B8 = mg, and sin\u03B8 \u2248 \u03B8 = 0.01, so T = 20/(2 \xD7 0.01) = 1000 N.
Each half has length L = 1 m. When it sags it becomes L/cos\u03B8 \u2248 L(1 + \u03B8\xB2/2), so strain = \u03B8\xB2/2 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075.
T = Y A \xD7 strain, so A = 1000/(2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075) = 1000/10\u2077 = 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2.
So the value is 1 (\xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"Two persons pull a wire towards themselves. Each person exerts a force of 200 N on the wire. Young's modulus of the material of wire is 1 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2. Original length of the wire is 2 m and the area of cross section is 2 cm\xB2. The wire will extend in length by ______ \u03BCm.",n:20,s:`The wire is pulled with 200 N at each end, so the tension in it is 200 N (not 400 N).
A = 2 cm\xB2 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2.
\u0394l = FL/(AY) = (200 \xD7 2)/(2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9) = 400/(2 \xD7 10\u2077) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m.
This is 20 \u03BCm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:`One end of a metal wire is fixed to a ceiling and a load of 2 kg hangs from the other end. A similar wire is attached to the bottom of the load and another load of 1 kg hangs from this lower wire. Then the ratio of longitudinal strain of upper wire to that of the lower wire will be ______.
[Area of cross section of wire = 0.005 cm\xB2, Y = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 Nm\u207B\xB2 and g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2]`,n:3,s:`The wires are identical, so strain = tension/(AY) is proportional to tension.
Upper wire carries both loads: T\u2081 = (2 + 1) \xD7 10 = 30 N.
Lower wire carries only the 1 kg load: T\u2082 = 1 \xD7 10 = 10 N.
Strain ratio = T\u2081/T\u2082 = 30/10 = 3.
Answer: 3`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"If average depth of an ocean is 4000 m and the bulk modulus of water is 2 \xD7 10\u2079 Nm\u207B\xB2, then fractional compression \u0394V/V of water at the bottom of ocean is \u03B1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2. The value of \u03B1 is ______. (Given g = 10 ms\u207B\xB2, \u03C1 = 1000 kg m\u207B\xB3)",n:2,s:`Pressure at the bottom: P = \u03C1gh = 1000 \xD7 10 \xD7 4000 = 4 \xD7 10\u2077 Pa.
Bulk modulus B = P/(\u0394V/V), so \u0394V/V = P/B.
\u0394V/V = {4 \xD7 10\u2077;2 \xD7 10\u2079} = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2.
So \u03B1 = 2.
Answer: 2`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:`Two metallic wires P and Q have same volume and are made up of same material. If their area of cross sections are in the ratio 4 : 1 and force F\u2081 is applied to P, an extension of \u0394l is produced. The force which is required to produce same extension in Q is F\u2082.
The value of {F\u2081;F\u2082} is ______.`,n:16,s:`Same volume: A\u2081L\u2081 = A\u2082L\u2082, so with A\u2081 : A\u2082 = 4 : 1 we get L\u2081 : L\u2082 = 1 : 4.
Extension \u0394l = FL/(AY), so F = AY\u0394l/L (same Y and \u0394l).
F is proportional to A/L.
{F\u2081;F\u2082} = {A\u2081;A\u2082} \xD7 {L\u2082;L\u2081} = 4 \xD7 4 = 16.
Answer: 16`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"Each of three blocks P, Q and R shown in figure has a mass of 3 kg. Each of the wires A and B has cross-sectional area 0.005 cm\xB2 and Young's modulus 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2. Neglecting friction, the longitudinal strain on wire B is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\u2074. (Take g = 10 m/s\xB2).",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Blocks P and Q on a smooth table joined by wire A, wire B runs from Q over a pulley at the table edge to the hanging block R",d:[["r",20,80,240,10,"f"],["l",20,80,260,80,"B"],["r",40,54,36,26],["t",58,71,"P","m"],["l",76,67,116,67],["t",96,60,"A","ms"],["r",116,54,36,26],["t",134,71,"Q","m"],["l",152,67,262,67],["t",207,60,"B","ms"],["c",262,77,10],["l",272,77,272,120],["r",254,120,36,30],["t",272,139,"R","m"]]},n:2,s:`The whole system (3 blocks, 9 kg) is pulled by the weight of R: a = {3 \xD7 10;9} = {10;3} m/s\xB2.
Wire B pulls P and Q together (6 kg): T = 6 \xD7 {10;3} = 20 N.
Area = 0.005 cm\xB2 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m\xB2.
Strain = {T;AY} = {20;5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9} = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074.
Answer: 2`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"Two blocks of mass 2 kg and 4 kg are connected by a metal wire going over a smooth pulley as shown in figure. The radius of wire is 4.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m and Young's modulus of the metal is 2.0 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2. The longitudinal strain developed in the wire is {1;\u03B1\u03C0}. The value of \u03B1 is ______. [Use g = 10 m/s\xB2]",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Blocks of 2 kg and 4 kg hang from the two ends of a wire passing over a fixed pulley",d:[["l",100,10,240,10,"B"],["l",170,10,170,30],["c",170,50,20],["c",170,50,2,"F"],["l",150,50,150,112],["r",136,112,28,24],["t",130,150,"2 kg","e"],["l",190,50,190,124],["r",174,124,32,28],["t",212,142,"4 kg",""]]},n:12,s:`Tension in the wire for an Atwood machine: T = {2m\u2081m\u2082g;m\u2081 + m\u2082} = {2 \xD7 2 \xD7 4 \xD7 10;6} = {80;3} N.
Area A = \u03C0r\xB2 = \u03C0 \xD7 16 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 m\xB2.
Strain = {T;AY} = {80/3;\u03C0 \xD7 16 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9} = {80/3;320\u03C0} = {1;12\u03C0}.
Comparing with {1;\u03B1\u03C0}: \u03B1 = 12.
Answer: 12`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"A steel rod has a radius of 20 mm and a length of 2.0 m. A force of 62.8 kN stretches it along its length. Young's modulus of steel is 2.0 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2. The longitudinal strain produced in the wire is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\u2075.",n:25,s:`Area A = \u03C0r\xB2 = \u03C0 \xD7 (20 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 1.256 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2.
Stress = {F;A} = {62.8 \xD7 10\xB3;1.256 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3} = 5 \xD7 10\u2077 N/m\xB2.
Strain = {stress;Y} = {5 \xD7 10\u2077;2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9} = 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075.
Answer: 25`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"A metal block of mass m is suspended from a rigid support through a metal wire of diameter 14 mm. The tensile stress developed in the wire under equilibrium state is 7 \xD7 10\u2075 Nm\u207B\xB2. The value of mass m is ______ kg. (Take, g = 9.8 ms\u207B\xB2 and \u03C0 = {22;7})",n:11,s:`Radius r = 7 mm = 7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m, so A = \u03C0r\xB2 = {22;7} \xD7 49 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 1.54 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2.
Tension = stress \xD7 area = 7 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 1.54 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 107.8 N.
Tension = mg, so m = {107.8;9.8} = 11 kg.
Answer: 11`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:`Two wires each of radius 0.2 cm and negligible mass, one made of steel and the other made of brass are loaded as shown in the figure. The elongation of the steel wire is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m.
[Young's modulus for steel = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 Nm\u207B\xB2 and g = 10 ms\u207B\xB2]`,fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Steel wire 1.6 m long hangs from the ceiling and carries a 2 kg block; a brass wire 1 m long hangs below it carrying a 1.14 kg block",d:[["l",100,10,240,10,"B"],["l",170,10,170,80],["t",180,50,"1.6 m Steel","s"],["r",145,80,50,22],["t",170,95,"2 kg","ms"],["l",170,102,170,150],["t",180,130,"1 m Brass","s"],["r",145,150,50,22],["t",170,165,"1.14 kg","ms"]]},n:20,s:`The steel wire (1.6 m) supports both blocks: load = (2 + 1.14) kg, so F = 3.14 \xD7 10 = 31.4 N.
Area A = \u03C0(0.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2)\xB2 = 1.256 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2.
Elongation \u0394l = {FL;AY} = {31.4 \xD7 1.6;1.256 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9}.
\u0394l = {50.24;2.512 \xD7 10\u2076} = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m = 20 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m.
Answer: 20`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"The length of wire becomes l\u2081 and l\u2082 when 100 N and 120 N tensions are applied respectively. If 10l\u2082 = 11l\u2081, the natural length of wire will be {1;x} l\u2081. Here the value of x is ______.",n:2,s:`Let the natural length be l\u2080. Extension is proportional to tension: l\u2081 = l\u2080 + 100c and l\u2082 = l\u2080 + 120c.
Given 10l\u2082 = 11l\u2081: 10l\u2080 + 1200c = 11l\u2080 + 1100c, so l\u2080 = 100c.
Then l\u2081 = 100c + 100c = 200c = 2l\u2080, so l\u2080 = {1;2} l\u2081.
Hence x = 2.
Answer: 2`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:`The elastic potential energy stored in a steel wire of length 20 m stretched through 2 cm is 80 J. The cross sectional area of the wire is ______ mm\xB2.
(Given, Y = 2.0 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2)`,n:40,s:`Strain = {0.02;20} = 10\u207B\xB3.
Elastic energy U = \xBD \xD7 Y \xD7 (strain)\xB2 \xD7 (A \xD7 L).
80 = \xBD \xD7 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 A \xD7 20 = 2 \xD7 10\u2076 A.
A = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2 = 40 mm\xB2.
Answer: 40`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A certain pressure 'P' is applied to 1 litre of water and 2 litre of a liquid separately. Water gets compressed to 0.01% whereas the liquid gets compressed to 0.03%. The ratio of Bulk modulus of water to that of the liquid is 3/x. The value of x is ______.",n:1,s:`Bulk modulus B = {P;\u0394V/V}, so for the same pressure B is inversely proportional to the fractional compression.
B_water : B_liquid = 0.03 : 0.01 = 3 : 1.
So the ratio is 3/1, and comparing with 3/x gives x = 1.
Answer: 1`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"As shown in the figure, in an experiment to determine Young's modulus of a wire, the extension-load curve is plotted. The curve is a straight line passing through the origin and makes an angle of 45\xB0 with the load axis. The length of wire is 62.8 cm and its diameter is 4 mm. The Young's modulus is found to be x \xD7 10\u2074 N m\u207B\xB2. The value of x is ______.",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"Graph of extension (vertical) against load (horizontal): a straight line through the origin at 45 degrees to the load axis",d:[["l",50,140,50,24,"a"],["l",50,140,210,140,"a"],["t",50,16,"Extension (m)","m"],["t",210,158,"Load (N)","e"],["l",50,140,150,40],["t",80,132,"45\xB0","s"]]},n:5,s:`Slope of the extension-load line = tan 45\xB0 = 1 m/N, that is {\u0394l;F} = 1.
Y = {FL;A\u0394l} = {L;A \xD7 slope}.
A = \u03C0(2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 1.256 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2, L = 0.628 m.
Y = {0.628;1.256 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 1} = 5 \xD7 10\u2074 N/m\xB2.
So x = 5.
Answer: 5`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A wire of length L and radius r is clamped rigidly at one end. When the other end of the wire is pulled by a force F, its length increases by 5 cm. Another wire of the same material of length 4L and radius 4r is pulled by a force 4F under same conditions. The increase in length of this wire is ______ cm.",n:5,s:`Extension \u0394l = {FL;\u03C0r\xB2Y}.
For the new wire: \u0394l\u2032 = {4F \xD7 4L;\u03C0(4r)\xB2Y} = {16FL;16\u03C0r\xB2Y} = {FL;\u03C0r\xB2Y}.
So the extension is unchanged: 5 cm.
Answer: 5`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"In an experiment of determine the Young's modulus of wire of a length exactly 1 m, the extension in the length of the wire is measured as 0.4 mm with an uncertainty of \xB1 0.02 mm when a load of 1 kg is applied. The diameter of the wire is measured as 0.4 mm with an uncertainty of \xB1 0.01 mm. The error in the measurement of Young's modulus (\u0394Y) is found to be x \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 N m\u207B\xB2. The value of x is ______. [Take g = 10 m/s\xB2]",n:2,s:`Y = {4FL;\u03C0d\xB2l} where l is the extension, so {\u0394Y;Y} = 2{\u0394d;d} + {\u0394l;l}.
{\u0394Y;Y} = 2 \xD7 {0.01;0.4} + {0.02;0.4} = 0.05 + 0.05 = 0.1.
Y = {4 \xD7 10 \xD7 1;\u03C0 \xD7 (0.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 \xD7 0.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3} = {40;2.01 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070} \u2248 1.99 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2.
\u0394Y = 0.1 \xD7 1.99 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 \u2248 2 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 N/m\xB2.
So x = 2.
Answer: 2`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:`A uniform heavy rod of mass 20 kg, cross sectional area 0.4 m\xB2 and length 20 m is hanging from a fixed support. Neglecting the lateral contraction, the elongation in the rod due to its own weight is x \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 m. The value of x is ______.
(Given : Young's modulus Y = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2 and g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)`,n:25,s:`Due to its own weight, the tension varies from 0 at the bottom to mg at the top, so the elongation is that due to a force mg/2 acting on the full length.
\u0394l = {mgL;2AY}.
\u0394l = {20 \xD7 10 \xD7 20;2 \xD7 0.4 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9} = {4000;1.6 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9} = 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 m = 25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 m.
So x = 25.
Answer: 25`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A square aluminum (shear modulus is 25 \xD7 10\u2079 Nm\u207B\xB2) slab of side 60 cm and thickness 15 cm is subjected to a shearing force (on its narrow face) of 18.0 \xD7 10\u2074 N. The lower edge is riveted to the floor. The displacement of the upper edge is ______ \u03BCm.",n:48,s:`The force acts on the narrow face: area = 0.60 m \xD7 0.15 m = 0.09 m\xB2.
Shear stress = {18 \xD7 10\u2074;0.09} = 2 \xD7 10\u2076 N/m\xB2.
Shear strain = {stress;G} = {2 \xD7 10\u2076;25 \xD7 10\u2079} = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075.
Displacement of the upper edge = strain \xD7 height = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 0.60 = 4.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m = 48 \u03BCm.
Answer: 48`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"In an experiment to determine the Young's modulus, steel wires of five different lengths (1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 m) but of same cross section (2 mm\xB2) were taken and curves between extension and load were obtained. The slope (extension/load) of the curves were plotted with the wire length and the following graph is obtained. If the Young's modulus of given steel wires is x \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2, then the value of x is ______.",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Graph of extension/load against wire length for 1 to 5 m: a straight line through the origin reaching 0.25 at 1 m and 1.25 at 5 m (units 10 to the power -5 m/N)",d:[["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["l",60,170,305,170,"a"],["t",8,12,"Extension/Load (\xD710\u207B\u2075 m/N)","s"],["t",305,204,"Wire length (m)","e"],["l",60,170,260,50],["l",60,146,100,146,"d"],["l",60,122,140,122,"d"],["l",60,98,180,98,"d"],["l",60,74,220,74,"d"],["l",60,50,260,50,"d"],["l",100,146,100,170,"d"],["l",140,122,140,170,"d"],["l",180,98,180,170,"d"],["l",220,74,220,170,"d"],["l",260,50,260,170,"d"],["t",54,150,"0.25","es"],["t",54,126,"0.50","es"],["t",54,102,"0.75","es"],["t",54,78,"1.00","es"],["t",54,54,"1.25","es"],["t",100,184,"1","ms"],["t",140,184,"2","ms"],["t",180,184,"3","ms"],["t",220,184,"4","ms"],["t",260,184,"5","ms"]]},n:2,s:`Extension/load = {L;AY}, so the plot of (extension/load) against length is a straight line of slope {1;AY}.
From the graph: slope = {1.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075;5} = 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N\u207B\xB9 (equal to 0.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 at 1 m).
A = 2 mm\xB2 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
Y = {1;A \xD7 slope} = {1;2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076} = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2.
So x = 2.
Answer: 2`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A string of area of cross-section 4 mm\xB2 and length 0.5 m is connected with a rigid body of mass 2 kg. The body is rotated in a vertical circular path of radius 0.5 m. The body acquires a speed of 5 m/s at the bottom of the circular path. Strain produced in the string when the body is at the bottom of the circle is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\u2075. (Use young's modulus 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2 and g = 10 m/s\xB2)",n:30,s:`At the bottom: T \u2212 mg = {mv\xB2;r}.
T = 2 \xD7 10 + {2 \xD7 25;0.5} = 20 + 100 = 120 N.
Strain = {T;AY} = {120;4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9} = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 30 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075.
Answer: 30`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"A metal wire of length 0.5 m and cross-sectional area 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2 has breaking stress 5 \xD7 10\u2078 N m\u207B\xB2. A block of 10 kg is attached at one end of the string and is rotating in a horizontal circle. The maximum linear velocity of block will be ______ m s\u207B\xB9.",n:50,s:`Maximum tension = breaking stress \xD7 area = 5 \xD7 10\u2078 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 5 \xD7 10\u2074 N.
The tension provides the centripetal force: T = {mv\xB2;r}.
v\xB2 = {T r;m} = {5 \xD7 10\u2074 \xD7 0.5;10} = 2500.
v = 50 m/s.
Answer: 50`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"The elastic behaviour of material for linear stress and linear strain, is shown in the figure. The energy density for a linear strain of 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 is ______ kJ/m\xB3. Assume that material is elastic upto the linear strain of 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074.",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"Graph of strain (vertical, 1 to 4 times 10 to the power -10) against stress (horizontal, 20 to 80 N/m2): a straight line through the origin, dotted beyond the last point",d:[["l",60,160,60,20,"a"],["l",60,160,300,160,"a"],["t",10,12,"Strain",""],["t",300,190,"Stress (N/m\xB2)","e"],["l",60,160,240,40],["l",240,40,268,21,"d"],["l",56,130,64,130],["l",56,100,64,100],["l",56,70,64,70],["l",56,40,64,40],["t",54,134,"1\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070","es"],["t",54,104,"2\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070","es"],["t",54,74,"3\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070","es"],["t",54,44,"4\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2070","es"],["l",105,156,105,164],["l",150,156,150,164],["l",195,156,195,164],["l",240,156,240,164],["t",105,176,"20","ms"],["t",150,176,"40","ms"],["t",195,176,"60","ms"],["t",240,176,"80","ms"]]},n:25,s:`From the graph, stress = 20 N/m\xB2 when strain = 1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070, so Y = {20;10\u207B\xB9\u2070} = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2.
Energy density u = \xBD \xD7 Y \xD7 (strain)\xB2.
u = \xBD \xD7 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 \xD7 (5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074)\xB2 = 10\xB9\xB9 \xD7 25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 = 2.5 \xD7 10\u2074 J/m\xB3.
u = 25 kJ/m\xB3.
Answer: 25`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"The elongation of a wire on the surface of the earth is 10\u207B\u2074 m. The same wire of same dimensions is elongated by 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m on another planet. The acceleration due to gravity on the planet will be ______ m s\u207B\xB2. (Take acceleration due to gravity on surface of earth = 10 ms\u207B\xB2)",n:6,s:`For the same wire and load mass, the extension is proportional to the weight, that is to g.
{\u0394l_planet;\u0394l_earth} = {g_planet;g_earth}.
g_planet = 10 \xD7 {6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075;10\u207B\u2074} = 6 m/s\xB2.
Answer: 6`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"A steel rod with y = 2.0 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2 and \u03B1 = 10\u207B\u2075 \xB0C\u207B\xB9 of length 4 m and area of cross-section 10 cm\xB2 is heated from 0\xB0C to 400\xB0C without being allowed to extend. The tension produced in the rod is x \xD7 10\u2075 N where the value of x is ______.",n:8,s:`If expansion is prevented, the thermal strain \u03B1\u0394T appears as mechanical strain.
Tension = YA\u03B1\u0394T.
Tension = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 \xD7 10 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 400 = 8 \xD7 10\u2075 N.
The length of the rod does not matter. So x = 8.
Answer: 8`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:`Wires W\u2081 and W\u2082 are made of same material having the breaking stress of 1.25 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\u207B\xB2. W\u2081 and W\u2082 have cross-sectional area of 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m\xB2 and 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m\xB2, respectively. Masses of 20 kg and 10 kg hang from them as shown in the figure. The maximum mass that can be placed in the pan without breaking the wires is ______ kg.
(Use g = 10 ms\u207B\xB2)`,fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"Wire W1 hangs from the ceiling and carries a 20 kg mass; wire W2 below it carries a 10 kg mass and a pan underneath",d:[["l",100,10,260,10,"B"],["l",180,10,180,60],["t",190,38,"W\u2081",""],["r",150,60,60,26],["t",180,78,"20 kg","ms"],["l",180,86,180,130],["t",190,112,"W\u2082",""],["r",150,130,60,26],["t",180,148,"10 kg","ms"],["l",180,156,180,176],["p","M160 176 L200 176 L180 198 Z"],["t",210,194,"Pan",""]]},n:40,s:`Maximum tension: W\u2081 = 1.25 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 1000 N; W\u2082 = 1.25 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 500 N.
Let m be the mass in the pan. W\u2082 carries (10 + m) kg: (10 + m) \xD7 10 \u2264 500, so m \u2264 40 kg.
W\u2081 carries (20 + 10 + m) kg: (30 + m) \xD7 10 \u2264 1000, so m \u2264 70 kg.
Both conditions must hold, so the maximum mass is 40 kg.
Answer: 40`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:`A stone of mass 20 g is projected from a rubber catapult of length 0.1 m and area of cross-section 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2 stretched by an amount 0.04 m. The velocity of the projected stone is ______ m/s.
(Young's modulus of rubber = 0.5 \xD7 10\u2079 N/m\xB2)`,n:20,s:`Strain = {0.04;0.1} = 0.4.
Energy stored = \xBD \xD7 Y \xD7 (strain)\xB2 \xD7 A \xD7 L = \xBD \xD7 0.5 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 0.16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 0.1 = 4 J.
This becomes the kinetic energy: \xBD \xD7 0.02 \xD7 v\xB2 = 4.
v\xB2 = 400, so v = 20 m/s.
Answer: 20`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"Two separate wires A and B are stretched by 2 mm and 4 mm respectively, when they are subjected to a force of 2 N. Assume that both the wires are made up of same material and the radius of wire B is 4 times that of the wire A. The length of the wire A and B are in the ratio of a : b. Then a/b can be expressed as 1/x where x is ______.",n:32,s:`Extension \u0394l = {FL;\u03C0r\xB2Y}, so for equal F and Y: \u0394l is proportional to {L;r\xB2}.
{\u0394l_A;\u0394l_B} = {L_A;L_B} \xD7 {r_B\xB2;r_A\xB2}.
{2;4} = {a;b} \xD7 16, so {a;b} = {1;32}.
Hence x = 32.
Answer: 32`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Stress, strain and Hooke's law",q:"A body of mass m = 10 kg is attached to one end of a wire of length 0.3 m. The maximum angular speed (in rad s\u207B\xB9) with which it can be rotated about its other end in space station is (Breaking stress of wire = 4.8 \xD7 10\u2077 N m\u207B\xB2 and area of cross-section of the wire = 10\u207B\xB2 cm\xB2) is ______.",n:4,s:`Area = 10\u207B\xB2 cm\xB2 = 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
Maximum tension = breaking stress \xD7 area = 4.8 \xD7 10\u2077 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 48 N.
In a space station there is no gravity, so T = m\u03C9\xB2L.
\u03C9\xB2 = {48;10 \xD7 0.3} = 16, so \u03C9 = 4 rad/s.
Answer: 4`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A cubical solid aluminium block (bulk modulus = \u2212V {dP;dV} = 70 GPa) has an edge length of 1 m on the surface of the earth. It is kept on the floor of a 5 km deep ocean. Taking the average density of water and the acceleration due to gravity to be 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3 and 10 m s\u207B\xB2, respectively, the change in the edge length of the block in mm is ______.",n:.24,s:`Extra pressure at 5 km depth: P = \u03C1gh = 10\xB3 \xD7 10 \xD7 5000 = 5 \xD7 10\u2077 Pa.
Volume strain = {P;B} = {5 \xD7 10\u2077;70 \xD7 10\u2079} = 7.14 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074.
For a cube V = a\xB3, so {\u0394V;V} = 3 {\u0394a;a}.
\u0394a = {1;3} \xD7 7.14 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 1 m = 2.38 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m = 0.24 mm (magnitude of the decrease).
Answer: 0.24`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A block of weight 100 N is suspended by copper and steel wires of same cross sectional area 0.5 cm\xB2 and, length \u221A3 m and 1 m, respectively. Their other ends are fixed on a ceiling as shown in figure. The angles subtended by copper and steel wires with ceiling are 30\xB0 and 60\xB0, respectively. If elongation in copper wire is (\u0394lc) and elongation in steel wire is (\u0394ls), then the ratio {\u0394lc;\u0394ls} is ______. [Young's modulus for copper and steel are 1 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2 and 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2 respectively.]",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"A 100 N block hangs from a steel wire 1 m long at 60 degrees to the ceiling and a copper wire square root of 3 m long at 30 degrees to the ceiling",d:[["l",30,20,310,20,"B"],["l",60,20,110,107],["l",260,20,110,107],["t",76,36,"60\xB0","s"],["t",200,36,"30\xB0","s"],["t",20,56,"Steel","s"],["t",262,60,"Copper","s"],["t",44,80,"1 m",""],["t",186,82,"\u221A3 m",""],["r",86,107,48,28],["t",110,125,"Block","ms"]]},n:2,s:`Let the tensions be T_c (copper, 30\xB0) and T_s (steel, 60\xB0).
Horizontal balance: T_c cos 30\xB0 = T_s cos 60\xB0, so T_s = \u221A3 T_c.
Vertical balance: T_c sin 30\xB0 + T_s sin 60\xB0 = 100, so {T_c;2} + {3T_c;2} = 100, giving T_c = 50 N and T_s = 50\u221A3 N.
Elongation \u0394l = {TL;AY}: {\u0394lc;\u0394ls} = {T_c L_c Y_s;T_s L_s Y_c} = {50 \xD7 \u221A3 \xD7 2;50\u221A3 \xD7 1 \xD7 1} = 2.
Answer: 2`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"Steel wire of length L at 40\xB0C is suspended from the ceiling and then a mass m is hung from its free end. The wire is cooled down from 40\xB0C to 30\xB0C to regain its original length L. The coefficient of linear thermal expansion of the steel is 10\u207B\u2075/\xB0C, Young's modulus of steel is 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2 and radius of the wire is 1 mm. Assume that L >> diameter of the wire. Then the value of m in kg is nearly ______. (Take g = 10 m/s\xB2)",n:3,s:`On cooling by \u0394T = 10\xB0C the wire would contract by \u03B1L\u0394T.
The hanging mass stretches it back by exactly the same amount: {mgL;AY} = \u03B1L\u0394T.
m = {YA\u03B1\u0394T;g} = {10\xB9\xB9 \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 (10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 10;10} = \u03C0 \u2248 3 kg.
Answer: 3`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I
A. A force that restores an elastic body of unit area to its original state.
B. Two equal and opposite forces parallel to opposite faces
C. Forces perpendicular everywhere to the surface per unit area same everywhere
D. Two equal and opposite forces perpendicular to opposite faces
List-II
I. Bulk modulus
II. Young's modulus
III. Stress
IV. Shear modulus
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)"],c:0,s:`A: restoring force per unit area is the definition of stress, so A-III.
B: equal and opposite forces parallel to opposite faces produce a shear, so B-IV (shear modulus).
C: the same perpendicular force per unit area everywhere on the surface is a uniform pressure, so C-I (bulk modulus).
D: equal and opposite forces perpendicular to opposite faces give a longitudinal stretch, so D-II (Young's modulus).
Option (a) matches: A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R
Assertion A : A sound wave has higher speed in solids than gases.
Reason R : Gases have higher value of bulk modulus than solids.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],c:2,s:`Speed of sound v = \u221A{B;\u03C1}. In solids B is very large compared with gases, so v is larger in solids.
So Assertion A is true.
Gases are easy to compress, so their bulk modulus is much smaller than that of solids. Reason R is false.
Answer: A is true but R is false, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : The property of body, by virtue of which it tends to regain its original shape when the external force is removed, is Elasticity.
Reason (R) : The restoring force depends upon the bonded interatomic and intermolecular force of solid.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not correct explanation of (A).","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is true but (R) is false.","(A) is false but (R) is true."],c:1,s:`A is the definition of elasticity, so it is true.
When a solid is deformed, the atoms are displaced and the interatomic forces pull them back. These bonding forces are the restoring force, so R is true.
R explains why the body regains its shape, so it is the correct explanation of A.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : Steel is used in the construction of buildings and bridges.
Reason (R) : Steel is more elastic and its elastic limit is high.
In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer the from options given below.`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A).","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct.","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct."],c:1,s:`Steel is widely used in buildings and bridges, so A is correct.
Steel has a large Young's modulus (it is more elastic) and a high elastic limit, so R is correct.
These two properties let steel carry large loads with small deformation and without permanent damage. This is exactly why it is used.
So R is the correct explanation of A: option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1993",tp:"pse",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A uniform rod of length L and density \u03C1 is being pulled along a smooth floor with a horizontal acceleration \u03B1 (see figure). The magnitude of the stress at the transverse cross-section through the mid-point of the rod is ______.",fig:{w:340,h:125,alt:"A uniform rod of length L lying on a smooth floor and accelerating to the right with acceleration alpha",d:[["r",90,50,160,50,"f"],["l",60,100,280,100,"B"],["l",90,36,250,36,"b"],["t",170,30,"L","m"],["l",258,75,296,75,"a"],["t",277,68,"\u03B1","m"]]},a:"\u03C1L\u03B1/2",s:`Cut the rod at its mid-point. The half farther from the pulling force (mass \u03C1A{L;2}) is accelerated by the pull of the other half.
Force at the mid-point section: F = (\u03C1A{L;2}) \u03B1.
Stress = {F;A} = {\u03C1L\u03B1;2}.
Answer: \u03C1L\u03B1/2`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"pse",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A solid sphere of radius R, made of a material of bulk modulus K, is surrounded by a liquid in a cylindrical container. A massless piston of area A floats on the surface of the liquid. When a mass M is placed on the piston to compress the liquid the fractional change in the radius of the sphere, \u03B4R/R, is ______.",a:"Mg/(3AK)",s:`The extra pressure transmitted through the liquid (Pascal's law) is \u0394P = {Mg;A}.
Volume strain of the sphere = {\u0394V;V} = {\u0394P;K} = {Mg;AK}.
For a sphere V is proportional to R\xB3, so {\u0394V;V} = 3{\u03B4R;R}.
{\u03B4R;R} = {Mg;3AK} (a decrease).
Answer: Mg/(3AK)`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:`Two cylindrical vessels of equal cross sectional area of 2m\xB2 contain water upto heights 10m and 6m, respectively. If the vessels are connected at their bottom then the work done by the force of gravity is
(Density of water is 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3 and g = 10 m/s\xB2)`,o:["1 \xD7 10\u2075 J","6 \xD7 10\u2074 J","8 \xD7 10\u2074 J","4 \xD7 10\u2074 J"],c:2,s:`Final level in each vessel = {10 + 6;2} = 8 m.
Effectively a layer of water 2 m thick (volume 2 \xD7 2 = 4 m\xB3, mass 4000 kg) moves from the top of the first vessel (centre at 9 m) to the top of the second (centre at 7 m).
The centre of mass of this layer falls by 2 m.
Work by gravity = mg \xD7 drop = 4000 \xD7 10 \xD7 2 = 8 \xD7 10\u2074 J.
Answer: 8 \xD7 10\u2074 J`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:`A 400 g solid cube having an edge of length 10 cm floats in water. How much volume of the cube is outside the water ?
(Given : density of water = 1000 kg m\u207B\xB3)`,o:["600 cm\xB3","400 cm\xB3","4000 cm\xB3","1400 cm\xB3"],c:0,s:`Volume of the cube = 10\xB3 = 1000 cm\xB3.
Floating: weight = weight of water displaced, 400 g = 1 g/cm\xB3 \xD7 V_in.
V_in = 400 cm\xB3, so the volume outside = 1000 \u2212 400 = 600 cm\xB3.
Answer: 600 cm\xB3`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A cube of ice floats partly in water and partly in kerosene oil. The ratio of volume of ice immersed in water to that in kerosene oil (specific gravity of kerosene oil = 0.8, specific gravity of ice = 0.9)",fig:{w:340,h:165,alt:"A beaker holding kerosene oil above water, with a cube of ice floating so that half of it is in kerosene and half in water",d:[["p","M40 20 V150 H190 V20"],["r",40,100,150,50,"f"],["l",40,30,190,30,"d"],["r",90,75,50,50],["t",115,104,"Ice","ms"],["l",250,56,196,56,"a"],["t",254,60,"Kerosene oil","s"],["l",250,125,196,125,"a"],["t",254,129,"Water","s"]]},o:["1 : 1","5 : 4","9 : 10","8 : 9"],c:0,s:`Let V_w and V_k be the volumes of ice in water and in kerosene.
Floating: weight of ice = total buoyant force.
0.9(V_w + V_k) = 1 \xD7 V_w + 0.8 \xD7 V_k.
0.1 V_w = 0.1 V_k, so V_w : V_k = 1 : 1.
Answer: 1 : 1`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A sphere of relative density \u03C3 and diameter D has concentric cavity of diameter d. The ratio of {D;d}, if it just floats on water in a tank is",o:["({\u03C3 \u2212 1;\u03C3})^(1/3)","({\u03C3;\u03C3 \u2212 1})^(1/3)","({\u03C3 + 1;\u03C3 \u2212 1})^(1/3)","({\u03C3 \u2212 2;\u03C3 + 2})^(1/3)"],c:1,s:`Volume of material = {\u03C0;6}(D\xB3 \u2212 d\xB3). Its mass = \u03C3 \xD7 (water density) \xD7 this volume.
It just floats (fully submerged): weight = buoyancy, \u03C3(D\xB3 \u2212 d\xB3) = D\xB3.
{d\xB3;D\xB3} = {\u03C3 \u2212 1;\u03C3}, so {D\xB3;d\xB3} = {\u03C3;\u03C3 \u2212 1}.
{D;d} = ({\u03C3;\u03C3 \u2212 1})^(1/3).
Answer: option (b)`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"A hydraulic automobile lift is designed to lift vehicles of mass 5000 kg. The area of cross-section of the cylinder carrying the load is 250 cm\xB2. The maximum pressure the smaller piston would have to bear is [Assume g = 10 m/s\xB2].",o:["2 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa","20 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa","2 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa","200 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 Pa"],c:0,s:`By Pascal's law the pressure is the same everywhere in the liquid.
Pressure under the load = {mg;A} = {5000 \xD7 10;250 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074} = {5 \xD7 10\u2074;0.025}.
P = 2 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa.
Answer: 2 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"Two cylindrical vessels of equal cross-sectional area 16 cm\xB2 contain water upto heights 100 cm and 150 cm respectively. The vessels are interconnected so that the water levels in them become equal. The work done by the force of gravity during the process, is [Take density of water = 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3 and g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2]",o:["0.25 J","1 J","8 J","12 J"],c:1,s:`Final height in each vessel = {100 + 150;2} = 125 cm. A column of height 25 cm moves from one vessel to the other.
Effectively a layer of water 0.25 m thick moves from the top of the 150 cm vessel (centre at 1.375 m) to the top of the 100 cm vessel (centre at 1.125 m).
Mass moved m = 10\xB3 \xD7 16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 0.25 = 0.4 kg, and the centre of mass falls by 0.25 m.
W = mg \xD7 drop = 0.4 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.25 = 1 J.
Answer: 1 J`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:`An object is located at 2 km beneath the surface of the water. If the fractional compression {\u0394V;V} is 1.36%, the ratio of hydraulic stress to the corresponding hydraulic strain will be ______.
(Given : Density of water is 1000 kg m\u207B\xB3 and g = 9.8 m s\u207B\xB2.)`,o:["1.44 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\u207B\xB2","2.26 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\u207B\xB2","1.44 \xD7 10\u2077 N m\u207B\xB2","1.96 \xD7 10\u2077 N m\u207B\xB2"],c:0,s:`Hydraulic stress = pressure = \u03C1gh = 1000 \xD7 9.8 \xD7 2000 = 1.96 \xD7 10\u2077 N/m\xB2.
Hydraulic strain = {\u0394V;V} = 1.36% = 0.0136.
Stress/strain = bulk modulus = {1.96 \xD7 10\u2077;0.0136} = 1.44 \xD7 10\u2079 N/m\xB2.
Answer: 1.44 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\u207B\xB2`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"An air bubble of radius 1 cm in water has an upward acceleration 9.8 cm s\u207B\xB2. The density of water is 1 gm cm\u207B\xB3 and water offers negligible drag force on the bubble. The mass of the bubble is (g = 980 cm/s\xB2)",o:["3.15 gm","1.52 gm","4.15 gm","4.51 gm"],c:2,s:`Forces on the bubble: buoyancy upward = V\u03C1g, weight mg downward.
Newton's second law: V\u03C1g \u2212 mg = ma, so m = {V\u03C1g;g + a}.
V = {4;3}\u03C0(1)\xB3 = 4.19 cm\xB3, so V\u03C1 = 4.19 g.
m = 4.19 \xD7 {980;980 + 9.8} = 4.19 \xD7 0.990 = 4.15 g.
Answer: 4.15 gm`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A hollow spherical shell at outer radius R floats just submerged under the water surface. The inner radius of the shell is r. If the specific gravity of the shell material is 27/8 with respect to water, the value of r is",o:["{8;9}R","{4;9}R","{2;3}R","{1;3}R"],c:0,s:`Floating just submerged: weight of shell = weight of water displaced by the whole outer sphere.
27/8 \xD7 {4;3}\u03C0(R\xB3 \u2212 r\xB3) = 1 \xD7 {4;3}\u03C0R\xB3, so R\xB3 \u2212 r\xB3 = {8;27}R\xB3.
r\xB3 = {19;27}R\xB3, so r \u2248 0.89R.
This is close to {8;9}R = 0.889R (the book value). The exact cube root of 19/27 is 0.8895, so option (a) is the nearest.
Answer: option (a), r = {8;9}R`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"Consider a solid sphere of radius R and mass density \u03C1(r) = \u03C1\u2080(1 \u2212 {r\xB2;R\xB2}), 0 < r \u2264 R. The minimum density of a liquid in which it will float is",o:["{2\u03C1\u2080;5}","{\u03C1\u2080;5}","{2\u03C1\u2080;3}","{\u03C1\u2080;3}"],c:0,s:`It floats if the liquid density is at least the average density of the sphere.
Mass M = \u222B 4\u03C0r\xB2 \u03C1\u2080(1 \u2212 r\xB2/R\xB2) dr = 4\u03C0\u03C1\u2080(R\xB3/3 \u2212 R\xB3/5) = 4\u03C0\u03C1\u2080R\xB3 \xD7 {2;15}.
Volume = {4;3}\u03C0R\xB3.
Average density = {M;V} = {2;15} \xD7 3 \u03C1\u2080 = {2\u03C1\u2080;5}.
Answer: {2\u03C1\u2080;5}`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"Two liquids of densities \u03C1\u2081 and \u03C1\u2082 (\u03C1\u2082 = 2\u03C1\u2081) are filled up behind a square wall of side 10 m as shown in figure. Each liquid has a height of 5 m. The ratio of the forces due to these liquids exerted on upper part MN to that at the lower part NO is (Assume that the liquids are not mixing.)",fig:{w:340,h:165,alt:"A square wall 10 m by 10 m with liquid of density rho1 in the upper 5 m (from M to N) and liquid of density rho2 in the lower 5 m (from N to O)",d:[["r",100,20,120,60,"f"],["r",100,20,120,120],["l",100,80,220,80],["t",90,24,"M","es"],["t",90,84,"N","es"],["t",90,144,"O","es"],["t",160,54,"\u03C1\u2081","m"],["t",160,114,"\u03C1\u2082","m"],["l",240,20,240,80,"b"],["t",250,54,"5 m",""],["l",240,80,240,140,"b"],["t",250,114,"5 m",""]]},o:["1/2","2/3","1/4","1/3"],c:2,s:`Let the wall width be w. Force on MN (only liquid \u03C1\u2081): F\u2081 = average pressure \xD7 area = (\xBD\u03C1\u2081g \xD7 5)(5w) = 12.5 \u03C1\u2081gw.
Pressure at N = 5\u03C1\u2081g. Force on NO = (5\u03C1\u2081g)(5w) + (\xBD\u03C1\u2082g \xD7 5)(5w) = 25\u03C1\u2081gw + 12.5\u03C1\u2082gw.
With \u03C1\u2082 = 2\u03C1\u2081: F\u2082 = 25\u03C1\u2081gw + 25\u03C1\u2081gw = 50\u03C1\u2081gw.
{F\u2081;F\u2082} = {12.5;50} = {1;4}.
Answer: 1/4`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"An open-ended U-tube of uniform cross-sectional area contains water (density 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3). Initially the water level stands at 0.29 m from the bottom in each arm. Kerosene oil (a water-immiscible liquid) of density 800 kg m\u207B\xB3 is added to the left arm until its length is 0.1 m, as shown in the schematic figure. The ratio ({h\u2081;h\u2082}) of the heights of the liquid in the two arms is",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"An open U-tube with water; the left arm carries a 0.1 m column of kerosene on top of water. h1 is the height of the liquid in the left arm and h2 the height in the right arm, both measured from the bottom",d:[["p","M90 20 V170 H250 V20"],["p","M130 20 V140 H210 V20"],["r",90,95,40,75,"f"],["r",130,140,80,30,"f"],["r",210,71,40,99,"f"],["r",90,65,40,30,"F"],["l",78,65,78,95,"b"],["t",74,84,"0.1 m","es"],["l",30,65,30,170,"b"],["t",26,120,"h\u2081","e"],["l",280,71,280,170,"b"],["t",286,124,"h\u2082",""],["t",170,158,"Water","ms"]]},o:["15/14","35/33","7/6","5/4."],c:1,s:`Total water length is conserved: x + y = 0.29 + 0.29 = 0.58 m, where x and y are the water heights in the left and right arms.
Pressure at the oil-water level of the left arm: 800 \xD7 g \xD7 0.1 + 1000 \xD7 g \xD7 x = 1000 \xD7 g \xD7 y (same level in the connecting tube).
So y \u2212 x = 0.08 m. Solving: y = 0.33 m, x = 0.25 m.
h\u2081 = x + 0.1 = 0.35 m (oil + water), h\u2082 = y = 0.33 m.
{h\u2081;h\u2082} = {35;33}. Answer: 35/33`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A wooden block floating in a bucket of water has 4/5 of its volume submerged. When certain amount of an oil is poured into the bucket, it is found that the block is just under the oil surface with half of its volume under water and half in oil. The density of oil relative to that of water is",o:["0.5","0.6","0.8","0.7"],c:1,s:`In water alone: \u03C1_block \xD7 V = 1 \xD7 {4;5}V, so \u03C1_block = 0.8 (relative to water).
With oil: weight = buoyancy, 0.8V = 1 \xD7 {V;2} + \u03C1_oil \xD7 {V;2}.
0.8 = 0.5 + 0.5\u03C1_oil, so \u03C1_oil = 0.6.
Answer: 0.6`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A cubical block of side 0.5 m floats on water with 30% of its volume under water. What is the maximum weight that can be put on the block without fully submerging it under water? [Take, density of water = 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3]",o:["46.3 kg","30.1 kg","87.5 kg","65.4 kg"],c:2,s:`Volume V = 0.5\xB3 = 0.125 m\xB3.
Mass of block = buoyancy at 30% immersion = 0.3 \xD7 0.125 \xD7 1000 = 37.5 kg.
Fully submerged, the buoyant mass is 0.125 \xD7 1000 = 125 kg.
Maximum extra mass = 125 \u2212 37.5 = 87.5 kg.
Answer: 87.5 kg`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"A submarine experiences a pressure of 5.05 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa at a depth of d\u2081 in a sea. When it goes further to a depth of d\u2082, it experiences a pressure of 8.08 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa. Then d\u2082 \u2212 d\u2081 is approximately (density of water = 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3 and acceleration due to gravity = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["300 m","400 m","500 m","600 m"],c:0,s:`Pressure difference = \u03C1g(d\u2082 \u2212 d\u2081).
(8.08 \u2212 5.05) \xD7 10\u2076 = 3.03 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa = 10\xB3 \xD7 10 \xD7 (d\u2082 \u2212 d\u2081).
d\u2082 \u2212 d\u2081 = {3.03 \xD7 10\u2076;10\u2074} = 303 m \u2248 300 m.
Answer: 300 m`},{y:"JEE Main 2014",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"There is a circular tube in a vertical plane. Two liquids which do not mix and of densities d\u2081 and d\u2082 are filled in the tube. Each liquid subtends 90\xB0 angle at centre. Radius joining their interface makes an angle \u03B1 with vertical. Ratio {d\u2081;d\u2082} is",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"A circular tube in a vertical plane holding two liquids of densities d1 and d2, each filling a quarter of the circle; the radius to their interface makes angle alpha with the vertical",d:[["c",170,100,70],["c",170,100,2,"F"],["p","M197.9 159.8 A66 66 0 0 0 229.8 72.1","B"],["p","M110.2 127.9 A66 66 0 0 0 197.9 159.8","B"],["l",170,100,170,172,"d"],["l",170,100,199.6,163.4,"d"],["t",175,152,"\u03B1","s"],["t",92,150,"d\u2081","e"],["t",246,110,"d\u2082",""]]},o:["{1 + sin \u03B1;1 \u2212 cos \u03B1}","{1 + sin \u03B1;1 \u2212 sin \u03B1}","{1 + cos \u03B1;1 \u2212 cos \u03B1}","{1 + tan \u03B1;1 \u2212 tan \u03B1}"],c:3,s:`Take the centre as origin and R as the tube radius. The interface is at angle \u03B1 from the downward vertical.
Each liquid covers 90\xB0, so the free ends are at heights +R sin \u03B1 (end of d\u2082) and \u2212R sin \u03B1 (end of d\u2081) relative to the centre.
Interface is at height \u2212R cos \u03B1. Depth below the d\u2082 surface: R(cos \u03B1 + sin \u03B1). Depth below the d\u2081 surface: R(cos \u03B1 \u2212 sin \u03B1).
Pressure at the interface from both sides is equal: d\u2081 g R(cos \u03B1 \u2212 sin \u03B1) = d\u2082 g R(cos \u03B1 + sin \u03B1).
{d\u2081;d\u2082} = {cos \u03B1 + sin \u03B1;cos \u03B1 \u2212 sin \u03B1} = {1 + tan \u03B1;1 \u2212 tan \u03B1}.
Answer: option (d)`},{y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A uniform cylinder of length L and mass M having cross-sectional area A is suspended, with its length vertical, from a fixed point by a massless spring of spring constant k, such that it is half submerged in a liquid of density \u03C3 at equilibrium position. The extension x\u2080 of the spring when it is in equilibrium is",o:["{Mg;k}(1 + {LA\u03C3;M})","{Mg;k}","{Mg;k}(1 \u2212 {LA\u03C3;M})","{Mg;k}(1 \u2212 {LA\u03C3;2M})"],c:3,s:`Forces on the cylinder: spring force kx\u2080 up, buoyancy up, weight Mg down.
Buoyancy = weight of liquid displaced by half the cylinder = {LA;2} \u03C3 g.
kx\u2080 + {LA\u03C3g;2} = Mg, so x\u2080 = {Mg;k} \u2212 {LA\u03C3g;2k}.
x\u2080 = {Mg;k}(1 \u2212 {LA\u03C3;2M}).
Answer: option (d)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A thin uniform cylindrical shell, closed at both ends, is partially filled with water. It is floating vertically in water in half-submerged state. If \u03C1c is the relative density of the material of the shell with respect to water, then the correct statement is that the shell is",o:["more than half-filled if \u03C1c is less than 0.5","more than half-filled if \u03C1c is more than 1.0","half-filled if \u03C1c more than 0.5","less than half-filled if \u03C1c is less than 0.5"],c:0,s:`Let the outer volume be V, the volume of shell material V_m (small) and the water inside f(V \u2212 V_m).
Half submerged: total weight = buoyancy, \u03C1c V_m + f(V \u2212 V_m) = {V;2}.
f = {V/2 \u2212 \u03C1c V_m;V \u2212 V_m}. Compare with \xBD: f > \xBD when V/2 \u2212 \u03C1c V_m > V/2 \u2212 V_m/2, that is when \u03C1c < \xBD.
So the shell is more than half filled if \u03C1c is less than 0.5.
Answer: option (a)`},{y:"AIEEE 2010",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A ball is made of a material of density \u03C1 where \u03C1_oil < \u03C1 < \u03C1_water with \u03C1_oil and \u03C1_water representing the densities of oil and water, respectively. The oil and water are immiscible. If the above ball is in equilibrium in a mixture of this oil and water, which of the following pictures represents its equilibrium position?",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"Four beakers each with oil and water layers and a ball: (a) oil above water with the ball floating near the top of the oil; (b) water above oil with the ball near the top; (c) oil above water with the ball at the oil-water boundary; (d) water above oil with the ball at the boundary",d:[["t",20,60,"(a)",""],["r",45,60,90,45,"f"],["p","M45 15 V105 H135 V15"],["l",45,60,135,60],["t",75,43,"Oil","ms"],["t",75,87,"Water","ms"],["c",115,25,8,"F"],["t",190,60,"(b)",""],["r",215,15,90,45,"f"],["p","M215 15 V105 H305 V15"],["l",215,60,305,60],["t",245,43,"Water","ms"],["t",245,87,"Oil","ms"],["c",285,25,8,"F"],["t",20,175,"(c)",""],["r",45,175,90,45,"f"],["p","M45 130 V220 H135 V130"],["l",45,175,135,175],["t",75,158,"Oil","ms"],["t",75,202,"Water","ms"],["c",115,175,8,"F"],["t",190,175,"(d)",""],["r",215,130,90,45,"f"],["p","M215 130 V220 H305 V130"],["l",215,175,305,175],["t",245,158,"Water","ms"],["t",245,202,"Oil","ms"],["c",285,175,8,"F"]]},o:["Picture (a)","Picture (b)","Picture (c)","Picture (d)"],c:2,s:`Oil is lighter than water, so the oil layer lies above the water layer. This rules out (b) and (d).
The ball is denser than oil, so it cannot float in oil. It is lighter than water, so it cannot sink in water.
It therefore rests at the oil-water boundary, partly in each liquid. This rules out (a), where the ball floats at the top of the oil.
Answer: picture (c)`},{y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A jar is filled with two non-mixing liquids 1 and 2 having densities \u03C1\u2081 and \u03C1\u2082, respectively. A solid ball, made of a material of density \u03C1\u2083, is dropped in the jar. It comes to equilibrium in the position shown in the figure. Which of the following is true for \u03C1\u2081, \u03C1\u2082 and \u03C1\u2083?",fig:{w:340,h:165,alt:"A jar with liquid 1 of density rho1 floating above liquid 2 of density rho2, and a solid ball of density rho3 resting at the boundary between them",d:[["r",100,85,140,65,"f"],["p","M100 20 V150 H240 V20"],["l",100,85,240,85],["c",170,85,14,"F"],["t",192,70,"\u03C1\u2083","s"],["t",250,55,"Liquid 1, \u03C1\u2081",""],["t",250,120,"Liquid 2, \u03C1\u2082",""]]},o:["\u03C1\u2081 < \u03C1\u2083 < \u03C1\u2082","\u03C1\u2083 < \u03C1\u2081 < \u03C1\u2082","\u03C1\u2081 > \u03C1\u2083 > \u03C1\u2082","\u03C1\u2081 < \u03C1\u2082 < \u03C1\u2083"],c:0,s:`Liquid 1 lies above liquid 2, so \u03C1\u2081 < \u03C1\u2082.
The ball stays at the boundary: it sinks through liquid 1 (so \u03C1\u2083 > \u03C1\u2081) but floats on liquid 2 (so \u03C1\u2083 < \u03C1\u2082).
Therefore \u03C1\u2081 < \u03C1\u2083 < \u03C1\u2082.
Answer: option (a)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A wooden block, with a coin placed on its top, floats in water as shown in figure. The distance l and h are shown here. After some time the coin falls into the water. Then",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"A wooden block with a coin on top floats in a tank of water; l is the depth of the block below the water surface and h is the depth of water in the tank",d:[["r",60,60,220,90,"f"],["p","M60 30 V150 H280 V30"],["l",60,60,280,60,"d"],["r",130,35,80,65],["r",155,27,30,8,"F"],["t",230,24,"coin",""],["l",228,28,190,30,"a"],["l",118,60,118,100,"b"],["t",114,84,"l","e"],["l",300,60,300,150,"b"],["t",306,108,"h",""]]},o:["l decreases and h increases","l increases and h decreases","both l and h increase","both l and h decrease."],c:3,s:`Before: block + coin float together, so the displaced water has weight (M + m)g.
After the coin falls off, the block floats alone and displaces water of weight Mg only, so the block rises: l (its depth in water) decreases.
The coin now sinks and displaces only its own volume, which is less than the volume of water it displaced when it floated on the block (weight mg of water). So the total displaced volume decreases.
Hence the water level falls: h decreases.
Answer: both l and h decrease, option (d)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"A hemispherical portion of radius R is removed from the bottom of a cylinder of radius R. The volume of the remaining cylinder is V and its mass M. It is suspended by a string in a liquid of density \u03C1 where it stays vertical. The upper surface of the cylinder is at a depth h below the liquid surface. The force on the bottom of the cylinder by the liquid is",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"A cylinder with a hemispherical dent in its bottom hangs from a string inside a liquid, its top at depth h below the surface; its width is 2R.",d:[["l",140,8,200,8],["l",170,8,170,95],["l",40,50,300,50,"d"],["p","M40 30 V190 H300 V30"],["p","M130 95 H210 V165 M130 95 V165"],["p","M130 165 A40 40 0 0 1 210 165"],["t",170,112,"M","m"],["l",240,50,240,95,"b"],["t",248,77,"h"],["l",130,182,210,182,"b"],["t",170,200,"2R","m"],["t",262,140,"\u03C1"]]},o:["Mg","Mg \u2212 V\u03C1g","Mg + \u03C0R\xB2h\u03C1g","\u03C1g(V + \u03C0R\xB2h)"],c:3,s:`Let the cylinder have length L. The force on the bottom (flat part plus the hemispherical dent) equals the weight of the imaginary liquid column standing on it, up to the free surface.
That column has volume \u03C0R\xB2(h + L) \u2212 \u2154\u03C0R\xB3.
The volume of the remaining cylinder is V = \u03C0R\xB2L \u2212 \u2154\u03C0R\xB3, so the column volume = V + \u03C0R\xB2h.
Force = \u03C1g(V + \u03C0R\xB2h). The atmospheric pressure also acts on the top, so it is left out.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"psp",st:"Fluids in accelerated frames and rotation",q:"A closed compartment containing gas is moving with some acceleration in horizontal direction. Neglect effect of gravity. Then the pressure in the compartment is",o:["same everywhere","lower in the front side","lower in the rear side","lower in the upper side"],c:1,s:`Gravity is ignored, so only the acceleration matters.
Take a thin slab of gas. The net force on it along the motion is (P_rear \u2212 P_front)A = ma, and it must point forward.
So the pressure at the rear must be larger than at the front.
The pressure is therefore lower in the front side. Answer: option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1995",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A homogeneous solid cylinder of length L (L < H/2), cross-sectional area A/5 is immersed such that it floats with its axis vertical at the liquid-liquid interface with length L/4 in the denser liquid as shown in the figure. The lower density liquid is open to atmosphere having pressure P\u2080. Then density D of solid is given by",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"Container of height H with an upper liquid of density d and a lower liquid of density 2d; a cylinder of length L floats vertically with 3L/4 in the upper liquid and L/4 in the lower liquid.",d:[["p","M60 20 V220 H210 V20"],["r",60,140,150,80,"f"],["l",60,140,210,140],["l",60,40,210,40,"d"],["r",110,65,40,100,""],["t",130,118,"D","m"],["t",104,112,"3L/4","e"],["t",104,157,"L/4","e"],["t",172,90,"d"],["t",172,185,"2d"],["l",250,20,250,220,"b"],["t",258,125,"H"]]},o:["{5;4}d","{4;5}d","4d","{d;5}"],c:0,s:`The cylinder floats, so its weight equals the total buoyant force.
Let its cross-section be a. Weight = D\xB7a\xB7L\xB7g.
Buoyant force = d\xB7a\xB7(3L/4)\xB7g (upper liquid) + 2d\xB7a\xB7(L/4)\xB7g (lower liquid).
D = 3d/4 + d/2 = 5d/4.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:`A U-tube of uniform cross-section (see fig) is partially filled with a liquid I. Another liquid II which does not mix with liquid I is poured into one side.
It is found that the liquid levels of the two sides of the tube are the same, while the level of liquid I has risen by 2 cm. If the specific gravity of liquid I is 1.1, the specific gravity of liquid II must be`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"U-tube. Liquid II sits on liquid I in the left arm; liquid I in the right arm has risen 2 cm above its original level, and the top levels of both arms are equal.",d:[["p","M90 20 V130 Q90 175 135 175 H165 Q210 175 210 130 V20"],["p","M130 20 V130 Q130 140 135 140 H165 Q170 140 170 130 V20"],["r",90,60,40,80,"f"],["r",170,60,40,80,"f"],["r",130,140,40,35,"f"],["l",90,60,130,60],["l",170,60,210,60],["l",165,100,215,100,"d"],["t",110,104,"II","m"],["t",190,105,"I","m"],["l",235,60,235,100,"b"],["t",243,84,"2 cm","s"]]},o:["1.12","1.1","1.05","1.0"],c:1,s:`Liquid I was pushed down 2 cm on the side where II was poured and rose 2 cm on the other side, so the two I-levels differ by 4 cm.
The top levels are equal, so the column of II is 4 cm high and sits on the lower I-level.
Pressure at the lower interface: on the II side = \u03C1_II g (4 cm); on the other side = \u03C1_I g (4 cm).
Equating, \u03C1_II = \u03C1_I, so the specific gravity of II is 1.1.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"psp",st:"Fluids in accelerated frames and rotation",q:"A vessel containing water is given a constant acceleration 'a' towards the right along a straight horizontal path. Which of the following diagrams in fig. represent the surface of the liquid?",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Four vessels (a) level surface, (b) surface rising towards the right, (c) surface falling towards the right, (d) none of these; acceleration a points right in the first three.",d:[["t",10,13,"(a)"],["p","M10 20 V80 H140 V20"],["l",10,43,140,43],["l",55,95,100,95,"a"],["t",106,99,"a"],["t",180,13,"(b)"],["p","M180 20 V80 H310 V20"],["l",180,63,310,31],["l",225,95,270,95,"a"],["t",276,99,"a"],["t",10,123,"(c)"],["p","M10 130 V190 H140 V130"],["l",10,141,140,173],["l",55,205,100,205,"a"],["t",106,209,"a"],["t",180,123,"(d)"],["t",245,170,"none of these","m"]]},o:["Diagram (a)","Diagram (b)","Diagram (c)","Diagram (d)"],c:2,s:`In the frame of the vessel, a pseudo force ma acts to the left on the water, along with gravity mg downwards.
The free surface is always perpendicular to the effective gravity, so it tilts with tan \u03B8 = a/g.
Water piles up at the rear (left) and is lower at the front (right), so the surface slopes down towards the right.
This is diagram (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"psp",st:"Fluids in accelerated frames and rotation",q:"A cylindrical tube, with its base as shown in the figure, is filled with water. It is moving down with a constant acceleration a along a fixed inclined plane with angle \u03B8 = 45\xB0. P\u2081 and P\u2082 are pressures at points 1 and 2, respectively, located at the base of the tube. Let \u03B2 = {P\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082;\u03C1gd}, where \u03C1 is density of water, d is the inner diameter of the tube and g is the acceleration due to gravity. Which of the following statement(s) is(are) correct?",fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"A vertical water-filled tube of diameter d stands on a 45 degree incline; point 2 is on the upper-left of its slanted base and point 1 on the lower-right; the tube accelerates down the slope with acceleration a, and g points down.",d:[["p","M60 70 L200 210 H60 Z"],["l",90,20,90,100],["l",150,20,150,160],["l",90,100,150,160,"B"],["l",90,12,150,12,"b"],["t",120,12,"d","m"],["t",120,70,"water","m"],["t",72,104,"2","e"],["t",156,170,"1"],["l",185,120,225,160,"a"],["t",232,150,"a"],["l",290,40,290,90,"a"],["t",298,70,"g"],["t",140,205,"\u03B8 = 45\xB0","s"]]},o:["\u03B2 = 0 when a = {g;\u221A2}","\u03B2 > 0 when a = {g;\u221A2}","\u03B2 = {\u221A2 \u2212 1;\u221A2} when a = {g;2}","\u03B2 = {1;\u221A2} when a = {g;2}"],c:[0,2],s:`The base of the tube lies along the incline. Point 1 is lower than point 2 by d vertically and d horizontally (base length d\u221A2).
In the tube's frame the effective gravity is g\u20D7 \u2212 a\u20D7. The pressure gradient is \u03C1(g\u20D7 \u2212 a\u20D7).
With a\u20D7 = a(cos45\xB0, \u2212sin45\xB0) down the slope: P\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082 = \u03C1[(g \u2212 a/\u221A2)d \u2212 (a/\u221A2)d] = \u03C1d(g \u2212 \u221A2 a).
So \u03B2 = 1 \u2212 (\u221A2 a)/g.
\u03B2 = 0 when a = g/\u221A2, so (a) is true; at a = g/\u221A2 \u03B2 is not positive, so (b) is false.
At a = g/2, \u03B2 = 1 \u2212 \u221A2/2 = (\u221A2 \u2212 1)/\u221A2, so (c) is true and (d) is false.
Answer: (a) and (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"A spherical body of radius R consists of a fluid of constant density and is in equilibrium under its own gravity. If P(r) is the pressure at r (r < R), then the correct option(s) is(are)",o:["P(r = 0) = 0","{P(r = 3R/4);P(r = 2R/3)} = {63;80}","{P(r = 3R/5);P(r = 2R/5)} = {16;21}","{P(r = R/2);P(r = R/3)} = {20;27}"],c:[1,2],s:`For a uniform self-gravitating sphere, g at radius r is g(r) = 4\u03C0G\u03C1r/3. Integrating dP = \u2212\u03C1 g(r) dr from r to R (P = 0 at the surface) gives P(r) = 2\u03C0G\u03C1\xB2/3(R\xB2 \u2212 r\xB2).
So P \u221D (R\xB2 \u2212 r\xB2), and P(0) is not zero: (a) is false.
(b) (P(3R/4))/(P(2R/3)) = (1 \u2212 9/16)/(1 \u2212 4/9) = (7/16)/(5/9) = 63/80, true.
(c) (P(3R/5))/(P(2R/5)) = (1 \u2212 9/25)/(1 \u2212 4/25) = 16/21, true.
(d) (P(R/2))/(P(R/3)) = (3/4)/(8/9) = 27/32, not 20/27, so false.
Answer: (b) and (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A solid sphere of radius R and density \u03C1 is attached to one end of a mass-less spring of force constant k. The other end of the spring is connected to another solid sphere of radius R and density 3\u03C1. The complete arrangement is placed in a liquid of density 2\u03C1 and is allowed to reach equilibrium. The correct statement(s) is(are)",o:["the net elongation of the spring is {4\u03C0R\xB3\u03C1g;3k}","the net elongation of the spring is {8\u03C0R\xB3\u03C1g;3k}","the light sphere is partially submerged.","the light sphere is completely submerged."],c:[0,3],s:`Let V = 4/3\u03C0R\xB3. The heavy sphere (3\u03C1) sinks, so the spring is stretched and pulls it up with tension T.
Heavy sphere: T + 2\u03C1Vg = 3\u03C1Vg, so T = \u03C1Vg.
Light sphere if fully submerged: buoyancy 2\u03C1Vg = weight \u03C1Vg + spring pull T = \u03C1Vg + \u03C1Vg. This balances exactly, so the light sphere is completely submerged. (d) is true, (c) is false.
Elongation = T/k = \u03C1Vg/k = 4\u03C0R\xB3\u03C1g/3k. (a) is true, (b) is false.
Answer: (a) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"Two solid spheres A and B of equal volumes but of different densities d_A and d_B are connected by a string. They are fully immersed in a fluid of density d_F. They get arranged into an equilibrium state as shown in the figure with a tension in the string. The arrangement is possible only if",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Two spheres, A above B, joined by a taut string and fully immersed in a fluid in a container.",d:[["r",110,10,120,180],["c",170,60,22],["c",170,140,22],["l",170,82,170,118],["t",170,65,"A","m"],["t",170,145,"B","m"],["t",118,30,"d_F","s"]]},o:["d_A < d_F","d_B > d_F","d_A > d_F","d_A + d_B = 2d_F"],c:[0,1,3],s:`A is above B and the string is taut, so A is pulled down by the string: A must be lighter than the fluid and B heavier.
A: buoyancy = weight + T, so T = Vg(d_F \u2212 d_A).
B: weight = buoyancy + T, so T = Vg(d_B \u2212 d_F).
T > 0 needs d_A < d_F and d_B > d_F. Equating the two tensions gives d_A + d_B = 2d_F.
Answer: (a), (b) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"Water is filled up to a height h in a beaker of radius R as shown in the figure. The density of water is \u03C1, the surface tension of water is T and the atmospheric pressure is P\u2080. Consider a vertical section ABCD of the water column through a diameter of the beaker. The force on water on one side of this section by water on the other side of this section has magnitude",fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"A cylindrical beaker of radius R filled with water to height h; a vertical section ABCD passes through a diameter of the water column.",d:[["p","M80 40 A90 14 0 0 0 260 40 A90 14 0 0 0 80 40"],["p","M80 190 A90 14 0 0 0 260 190"],["l",80,40,80,190],["l",260,40,260,190],["p","M125 48 L215 32 L215 182 L125 198 Z","d"],["t",111,52,"A"],["t",221,32,"B"],["t",221,190,"C"],["t",111,206,"D"],["l",80,14,260,14,"b"],["t",170,10,"2R","m"],["l",285,40,285,190,"b"],["t",293,118,"h"]]},o:["|2P\u2080Rh + \u03C0R\xB2\u03C1gh \u2212 2RT|","|2P\u2080Rh + R\u03C1gh\xB2 \u2212 2RT|","|P\u2080\u03C0R\xB2 + R\u03C1gh\xB2 \u2212 2RT|","|P\u2080\u03C0R\xB2 + R\u03C1gh\xB2 + 2RT|"],c:1,s:`Pressure at depth y is P\u2080 + \u03C1gy. The section has width 2R, so a strip of height dy has area 2R dy.
Pressure force across the section = \u222B\u2080\u02B0 (P\u2080 + \u03C1gy)\xB72R dy = 2P\u2080Rh + R\u03C1gh\xB2.
The surface film acts along the top edge of the section, of length 2R, with force 2RT in the opposite sense.
Net force magnitude = |2P\u2080Rh + R\u03C1gh\xB2 \u2212 2RT|.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A vessel contains oil (density = 0.8 g/cm\xB3) over mercury (density = 13.6 g/cm\xB3). A homogeneous sphere floats with half of its volume immersed in mercury and the other half in oil. The density of the material of the sphere in g/cm\xB3 is",o:["3.3","6.4","7.2","12.8"],c:2,s:`Let the sphere have volume V and density \u03C1. It floats, so weight = total buoyant force.
\u03C1Vg = (V/2)(13.6)g + (V/2)(0.8)g.
\u03C1 = (13.6 + 0.8)/2 = 7.2 g/cm\xB3.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"The spring balance A reads 2 kg with a block m suspended from it. A balance B reads 5 kg when a beaker with liquid is put on the pan of the balance. The two balances are now so arranged that the hanging mass is inside the liquid in the beaker as shown in the figure. In this situation:",fig:{w:340,h:235,alt:"Spring balance A hangs from a ceiling holding a block that is immersed in the liquid of a beaker standing on balance B.",d:[["l",100,8,180,8],["p","M140 8 L140 16 L132 20 L148 28 L132 36 L148 44 L140 48"],["t",160,40,"A"],["l",140,48,140,110],["p","M95 85 V185 H185 V85"],["r",96,100,88,85,"f"],["l",96,100,184,100,"d"],["r",125,110,30,30],["t",140,130,"m","m"],["l",80,190,200,190,"B"],["l",140,190,140,203],["c",140,214,10],["t",160,218,"B"]]},o:["the balance A will read more than 2 kg","the balance B will read more than 5 kg","the balance A will read less than 2 kg and B will read more than 5 kg","the balance A and B will read 2 kg and 5 kg respectively"],c:[1,2],s:`The liquid pushes the block up with the buoyant force B_f. Balance A now reads (mg \u2212 B_f)/g, which is less than 2 kg.
By Newton's third law the block pushes the liquid (and the beaker) down with the same force B_f.
Balance B now reads 5 kg + B_f/g, which is more than 5 kg.
So (b) and (c) are correct; (a) and (d) are wrong.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"psp",st:"Fluids in accelerated frames and rotation",q:"A body floats in a liquid contained in a beaker. The whole system as shown in the figure falls freely under gravity. The upthrust on the body is",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"A beaker of liquid with a body floating in it; the system falls freely.",d:[["p","M100 20 V130 H240 V20"],["r",100,55,140,75,"f"],["l",100,55,240,55],["p","M135 70 C135 52 205 52 205 70 C205 88 135 88 135 70 Z"],["l",290,30,290,90,"a"],["t",298,65,"g"]]},o:["zero","equal to the weight of the liquid displaced","equal to the weight of the body in air","equal to the weight of the immersed portion of the body"],c:0,s:`The upthrust comes from the pressure difference in the liquid, which exists because of gravity (P = P\u2080 + \u03C1gh).
In free fall the effective gravity inside the beaker is zero, so the pressure is the same everywhere in the liquid.
There is no pressure difference between the top and bottom of the body, so the upthrust is zero.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"A vessel with square cross-section and height of 6 m is vertically partitioned. A small window of 100 cm\xB2 with a hinged door is fitted at a depth of 3 m in the partition wall. One part of the vessel is filled completely with water and the other side is filled with the liquid having density 1.5 \xD7 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3. What force one needs to apply on the hinged door so that it does not get opened? (Acceleration due to gravity = 10 m/s\xB2)",n:150,s:`Both liquids fill the vessel completely, so at the window (depth 3 m) the pressures differ only by the liquid terms.
Pressure difference = (1500 \u2212 1000) \xD7 10 \xD7 3 = 1.5 \xD7 10\u2074 Pa.
Force = pressure difference \xD7 area = 1.5 \xD7 10\u2074 \xD7 100 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 150 N.
This force is applied against the push of the denser liquid.
Answer: 150 N.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A sample of a liquid is kept at 1 atm. It is compressed to 5 atm, which leads to a change of volume of 0.8 cm\xB3. If the bulk modulus of the liquid is 2 GPa, the initial volume of the liquid was ______ litre. (Take 1 atm = 10\u2075 Pa)",n:4,s:`Increase in pressure \u0394P = 5 \u2212 1 = 4 atm = 4 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa.
Bulk modulus B = \u0394P/(\u0394V/V), so V = B\xB7\u0394V/\u0394P.
V = (2 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 0.8 cm\xB3)/(4 \xD7 10\u2075) = 4000 cm\xB3.
4000 cm\xB3 = 4 litre.
Answer: 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:`A cube having a side of 10 cm with unknown mass and 200 gm mass were hung at two ends of a uniform rigid rod of 27 cm long. The rod along with masses was placed on a wedge keeping the distance between wedge point and 200 gm weight as 25 cm. Initially the masses were not at balance. A beaker was placed beneath the unknown mass and water is added slowly to it. At a given point the masses were in balance and half volume of the unknown mass was inside the water.
(Take the density of unknown mass is more than that of the water, the mass did not absorb water and water density is 1 gm/cm\xB3.) The unknown mass is ______ kg.`,n:3,s:`The wedge is 25 cm from the 200 g mass, so it is 27 \u2212 25 = 2 cm from the cube. The weight of the rod is neglected.
Half the cube is in water: displaced volume = 500 cm\xB3, buoyant force = 500 gf.
Effective pull of the cube = (M \u2212 500) gf at 2 cm.
Balance of torques about the wedge: (M \u2212 500) \xD7 2 = 200 \xD7 25, so M \u2212 500 = 2500.
M = 3000 g = 3 kg.
Answer: 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"In a hydraulic lift, the surface area of the input piston is 6 cm\xB2 and that of the output piston is 1500 cm\xB2. If 100 N force is applied to the input piston to raise the output piston by 20 cm, then the work done is ______ kJ.",n:5,s:`Pascal's law: output force = 100 \xD7 (1500/6) = 25000 N.
Work done on the output piston = 25000 \xD7 0.20 = 5000 J.
(The input piston moves 250 \xD7 20 cm = 50 m, so input work = 100 \xD7 50 = 5000 J, which agrees.)
Work done = 5 kJ.
Answer: 5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:`Mercury is filled in a tube of radius 2 cm up to a height of 30 cm. The force exerted by mercury on the bottom of the tube is ______ N.
(Given, atmospheric pressure = 10\u2075 N m\u207B\xB2, density of mercury = 1.36 \xD7 10\u2074 kg m\u207B\xB3, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2, \u03C0 = {22;7})`,n:177,s:`Pressure at the bottom = P\u2080 + \u03C1gh = 10\u2075 + 1.36 \xD7 10\u2074 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.30 = 10\u2075 + 40800 = 1.408 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa.
Area = \u03C0r\xB2 = 22/7 \xD7 (0.02)\xB2 = 1.257 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2.
Force = 1.408 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 1.257 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u2248 177 N.
Answer: 177.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"A hydraulic press containing water has two arms with diameters as mentioned in the figure. A force of 10 N is applied on the surface of water in the thinner arm. The force required to be applied on the surface of water in the thicker arm to maintain equilibrium of water is ______ N.",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"A U-shaped hydraulic press with a thick arm of diameter 14 cm on the left and a thin arm of diameter 1.4 cm on the right; 10 N acts on the water in the thin arm.",d:[["p","M40 40 V130 H220 V40"],["p","M120 40 V105 H212 V40"],["r",40,70,80,35,"f"],["r",212,70,8,35,"f"],["r",40,105,180,25,"f"],["l",40,70,120,70],["l",212,70,220,70],["l",216,20,216,66,"a"],["t",226,38,"10 N"],["l",80,20,80,66,"a"],["t",90,38,"F = ?"],["l",40,148,120,148,"b"],["t",80,164,"14 cm","m"],["t",216,164,"1.4 cm","m"]]},n:1e3,s:`The pressure applied is transmitted equally, so F\u2081/A\u2081 = F\u2082/A\u2082 and F\u2082 = F\u2081(A\u2082/A\u2081).
Areas are in the ratio of the squares of the diameters: (14/1.4)\xB2 = 100.
F\u2082 = 10 \xD7 100 = 1000 N.
Answer: 1000.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:`The depth below the surface of sea to which a rubber ball is to be taken so as to decrease its volume by 0.02% is ______ m.
(Take density of sea water = 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3, Bulk modulus of rubber = 9 \xD7 10\u2078 N/m\xB2 and g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)`,n:18,s:`Extra pressure at depth h is \u03C1gh. Bulk modulus B = \u03C1gh/(\u0394V/V).
\u0394V/V = 0.02% = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074.
h = B(\u0394V/V)/(\u03C1g) = (9 \xD7 10\u2078 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074)/(10\xB3 \xD7 10) = 1.8 \xD7 10\u2075/10\u2074 = 18 m.
Answer: 18.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Fluids in accelerated frames and rotation",q:"A tube of length 50 cm is filled completely with an incompressible liquid of mass 250 g and closed at both ends. The tube is then rotated in a horizontal plane about one of its ends with an angular velocity x\u221AF rad s\u207B\xB9. If F be the force exerted by the liquid at the other end then the value of x will be ______.",n:4,s:`The liquid behaves like a point mass at its centre, 25 cm from the axis. The force at the far end gives the needed centripetal force.
F = m\u03C9\xB2(L/2) = 0.25 \xD7 \u03C9\xB2 \xD7 0.25 = \u03C9\xB2/16.
So \u03C9\xB2 = 16F, \u03C9 = 4\u221AF.
Comparing with \u03C9 = x\u221AF gives x = 4.
Answer: 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:`The velocity of a small ball of mass 0.3 g and density 8 g/cc when dropped in a container filled with glycerine becomes constant after some time. If the density of glycerine is 1.3 g/cc, then the value of viscous force acting on the ball will be x \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N. The value of x is ______.
[use g = 10 m/s\xB2]`,n:25,s:`At constant (terminal) velocity: viscous force = weight \u2212 buoyancy.
Buoyancy = weight \xD7 (\u03C1_glycerine/\u03C1_ball) = weight \xD7 1.3/8.
Viscous force = mg(1 \u2212 1.3/8) = 0.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.8375 \u2248 2.51 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 N = 25.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N.
So x \u2248 25.
Answer: 25.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"When a rubber ball is taken to a depth of ______ m in deep sea, its volume decreases by 0.5%. (The bulk modulus of rubber = 9.8 \xD7 10\u2078 N m\u207B\xB2, density of sea water = 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3 and g = 9.8 m/s\xB2)",n:500,s:`Extra pressure = \u03C1gh and B = \u03C1gh/(\u0394V/V).
h = B(\u0394V/V)/(\u03C1g) = (9.8 \xD7 10\u2078 \xD7 0.005)/(10\xB3 \xD7 9.8).
h = 4.9 \xD7 10\u2076 / (9.8 \xD7 10\xB3) = 500 m.
Answer: 500.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"A hydraulic press can lift 100 kg when a mass 'm' is placed on the smaller piston. It can lift ______ kg when the diameter of the larger piston is increased by 4 times and that of the smaller piston is decreased by 4 times keeping the same mass 'm' on the smaller piston.",n:25600,s:`Pascal's law: load lifted = m \xD7 (A_large/A_small), and area \u221D (diameter)\xB2.
Originally 100 = m \xD7 (A_L/A_S).
New ratio = (4)\xB2 \xD7 (4)\xB2 \xD7 (A_L/A_S) = 256 \xD7 (A_L/A_S).
New load = 256 \xD7 100 = 25600 kg.
Answer: 25600.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:"A hot air balloon is carrying some passengers, and a few sandbags of mass 1 kg each so that its total mass is 480 kg. Its effective volume giving the balloon its buoyancy is V. The balloon is floating at an equilibrium height of 100 m. When N number of sandbags are thrown out, the balloon rises to a new equilibrium height close to 150 m with its volume V remaining unchanged. If the variation of the density of air with height h from the ground is \u03C1(h) = \u03C1\u2080e^(\u2212h/h\u2080), where \u03C1\u2080 = 1.25 kg m\u207B\xB3 and h\u2080 = 6000 m, the value of N is ______.",n:4,s:`A floating balloon is in equilibrium when mass = \u03C1(h)\xB7V.
At 100 m: 480 = \u03C1\u2080e^(\u2212100/h\u2080)V. At 150 m: 480 \u2212 N = \u03C1\u2080e^(\u2212150/h\u2080)V.
Dividing: (480 \u2212 N)/480 = e^(\u221250/6000) = e^(\u22121/120) \u2248 1 \u2212 1/120 + 1/28800.
N = 480(1 \u2212 e^(\u22121/120)) \u2248 480 \xD7 0.00830 \u2248 3.98.
N is a whole number close to 4.
Answer: 4.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`A fixed thermally conducting cylinder has a radius R and height L\u2080. The cylinder is open at its bottom and has a small hole at its top. A piston of mass M is held at a distance L from the top surface, as shown in the figure. The atmospheric pressure is P\u2080.
The piston is now pulled out slowly and held at a distance 2L from the top. The pressure in the cylinder between its top and the piston will be`,fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"An inverted fixed cylinder of radius R and height L0, open at the bottom with a small hole at the top; a piston of mass M is held at distance L from the top.",d:[["l",110,30,166,30],["l",174,30,230,30],["l",110,30,110,170],["l",230,30,230,170],["l",110,14,230,14,"b"],["t",170,10,"2R","m"],["r",112,80,116,8,"F"],["l",170,88,170,192,"B"],["t",182,108,"M"],["t",182,192,"piston","s"],["l",96,30,96,80,"b"],["t",90,60,"L","e"],["l",252,30,252,170,"b"],["t",260,105,"L\u2080"]]},o:["P\u2080","{P\u2080;2}","{P\u2080;2} + {Mg;\u03C0R\xB2}","{P\u2080;2} \u2212 {Mg;\u03C0R\xB2}"],c:0,s:`The small hole at the top is open, so air can move in or out while the piston is pulled out slowly.
The air between the top and the piston therefore stays connected to the outside and remains at atmospheric pressure.
Pressure = P\u2080.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`A fixed thermally conducting cylinder has a radius R and height L\u2080. The cylinder is open at its bottom and has a small hole at its top. A piston of mass M is held at a distance L from the top surface, as shown in the figure. The atmospheric pressure is P\u2080.
While the piston is at a distance 2L from the top, the hole at the top is sealed. The piston is then released, to a position where it can stay in equilibrium. In this condition, the distance of the piston from the top is`,fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"An inverted fixed cylinder of radius R and height L0, open at the bottom with a small hole at the top; a piston of mass M is held at distance L from the top.",d:[["l",110,30,166,30],["l",174,30,230,30],["l",110,30,110,170],["l",230,30,230,170],["l",110,14,230,14,"b"],["t",170,10,"2R","m"],["r",112,80,116,8,"F"],["l",170,88,170,192,"B"],["t",182,108,"M"],["t",182,192,"piston","s"],["l",96,30,96,80,"b"],["t",90,60,"L","e"],["l",252,30,252,170,"b"],["t",260,105,"L\u2080"]]},o:["({2P\u2080\u03C0R\xB2;\u03C0R\xB2P\u2080 + Mg})(2L)","({P\u2080\u03C0R\xB2 \u2212 Mg;\u03C0R\xB2P\u2080})(2L)","({P\u2080\u03C0R\xB2 + Mg;\u03C0R\xB2P\u2080})(2L)","({P\u2080\u03C0R\xB2;\u03C0R\xB2P\u2080 \u2212 Mg})(2L)"],c:3,s:`Trapped air: initial pressure P\u2080, length 2L. Let the final length be x and pressure P.
Equilibrium of the piston: P\xB7\u03C0R\xB2 + Mg = P\u2080\xB7\u03C0R\xB2, so P = P\u2080 \u2212 Mg/(\u03C0R\xB2).
The cylinder is conducting, so the temperature is constant: P\u2080(2L)A = P\xB7x\xB7A.
x = P\u2080/P(2L) = P\u2080\u03C0R\xB2/(\u03C0R\xB2P\u2080 \u2212 Mg)(2L).
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`A fixed thermally conducting cylinder has a radius R and height L\u2080. The cylinder is open at its bottom and has a small hole at its top. The atmospheric pressure is P\u2080.
The piston is taken completely out of the cylinder. The hole at the top is sealed. A water tank is brought below the cylinder and put in a position so that the water surface in the tank is at the same level as the top of the cylinder as shown in the figure. The density of the water is \u03C1. In equilibrium, the height H of the water column in the cylinder satisfies`,fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"A sealed-top cylinder of height L0 stands in a water tank with its top level with the tank water surface; water has risen to height H inside it, trapping air above.",d:[["p","M60 20 V210 H280 V20"],["r",60,50,70,160,"f"],["r",210,50,70,160,"f"],["r",130,130,80,80,"f"],["l",60,50,280,50,"d"],["p","M130 50 H210 M130 50 V200 M210 50 V200"],["t",170,95,"air","m"],["l",105,50,105,200,"b"],["t",99,125,"L\u2080","e"],["l",235,130,235,200,"b"],["t",243,170,"H"]]},o:["\u03C1g(L\u2080 \u2212 H)\xB2 + P\u2080(L\u2080 \u2212 H) + L\u2080P\u2080 = 0","\u03C1g(L\u2080 \u2212 H)\xB2 \u2212 P\u2080(L\u2080 \u2212 H) \u2212 L\u2080P\u2080 = 0","\u03C1g(L\u2080 \u2212 H)\xB2 + P\u2080(L\u2080 \u2212 H) \u2212 L\u2080P\u2080 = 0","\u03C1g(L\u2080 \u2212 H)\xB2 \u2212 P\u2080(L\u2080 \u2212 H) + L\u2080P\u2080 = 0"],c:2,s:`Air trapped initially: pressure P\u2080, length L\u2080. In equilibrium it has length L\u2080 \u2212 H and pressure P.
The cylinder top is level with the tank surface, so the water surface inside the cylinder (a height H above the cylinder bottom) is (L\u2080 \u2212 H) below the tank surface. It is at the same level as water outside, so P = P\u2080 + \u03C1g(L\u2080 \u2212 H).
Boyle's law (conducting cylinder): P\u2080L\u2080 = P(L\u2080 \u2212 H) = [P\u2080 + \u03C1g(L\u2080 \u2212 H)](L\u2080 \u2212 H).
So \u03C1g(L\u2080 \u2212 H)\xB2 + P\u2080(L\u2080 \u2212 H) \u2212 L\u2080P\u2080 = 0.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:`A cylindrical tank has a hole of diameter 2r in its bottom. The hole is covered by a wooden cylindrical block of diameter 4r, height h and density \u03C1/3.
Situation 1: Initially, the tank is filled with water of density \u03C1 to a height such that the height of water above the top of the block is h\u2081 (measured from the top of the block).
Situation 2: The water is removed from the tank to a height h\u2082 (measured from the bottom of the block), as shown in the figure. The height h\u2082 is smaller than h (height of the block) and thus the block is exposed to the atmosphere.
Find the minimum value of height h\u2081 (in situation 1), for which the block just starts to move up.`,fig:{w:340,h:232,alt:"A tank with a hole of diameter 2r in its bottom covered by a block of diameter 4r, height h and density rho/3; water stands a height h1 above the top of the block.",d:[["l",40,200,150,200],["l",190,200,300,200],["l",40,20,40,200],["l",300,20,300,200],["r",130,120,80,80,"f"],["t",170,172,"\u03C1/3","m"],["l",130,128,210,128,"b"],["t",170,142,"4r","m"],["l",150,210,190,210,"b"],["t",170,224,"2r","m"],["t",60,85,"\u03C1"],["l",250,120,250,200,"b"],["t",258,165,"h"],["r",41,60,89,140,"f"],["r",210,60,89,140,"f"],["l",40,60,300,60,"d"],["r",130,60,80,60,"f"],["l",250,60,250,120,"b"],["t",258,95,"h\u2081"]]},o:["{2h;3}","{5h;4}","{5h;3}","{5h;2}"],c:2,s:`Take atmospheric pressure P\u2080. The block touches the tank floor outside the hole, but water gets under the part of its base not covering the hole (area 3\u03C0r\xB2); the hole area \u03C0r\xB2 (radius r) is under air at P\u2080.
Upward force = P\u2080\u03C0r\xB2 + (P\u2080 + \u03C1g(h\u2081 + h))\xB73\u03C0r\xB2.
Downward force = (P\u2080 + \u03C1gh\u2081)\xB74\u03C0r\xB2 + weight, with weight = \u03C1/3\xB74\u03C0r\xB2h\xB7g = 4/3\u03C0r\xB2\u03C1gh.
Net upward force = \u03C0r\xB2\u03C1g[3h \u2212 h\u2081 \u2212 4/3h] = \u03C0r\xB2\u03C1g[5h/3 \u2212 h\u2081].
This is zero, the borderline for the block to start moving up, when h\u2081 = 5h/3.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:`A cylindrical tank has a hole of diameter 2r in its bottom. The hole is covered by a wooden cylindrical block of diameter 4r, height h and density \u03C1/3.
Situation 1: Initially, the tank is filled with water of density \u03C1 to a height such that the height of water above the top of the block is h\u2081 (measured from the top of the block).
Situation 2: The water is removed from the tank to a height h\u2082 (measured from the bottom of the block), as shown in the figure. The height h\u2082 is smaller than h (height of the block) and thus the block is exposed to the atmosphere.
Find the height of the water level h\u2082 (in situation 2), for which the block remains in its original position without the application of any external force.`,fig:{w:340,h:232,alt:"The same tank with water lowered to height h2 above the bottom, below the top of the block, so the top of the block is in air.",d:[["l",40,200,150,200],["l",190,200,300,200],["l",40,20,40,200],["l",300,20,300,200],["r",130,120,80,80,"f"],["t",170,172,"\u03C1/3","m"],["l",130,128,210,128,"b"],["t",170,142,"4r","m"],["l",150,210,190,210,"b"],["t",170,224,"2r","m"],["t",60,175,"\u03C1"],["l",250,120,250,200,"b"],["t",258,165,"h"],["r",41,150,89,50,"f"],["r",210,150,89,50,"f"],["l",40,150,300,150,"d"],["l",275,150,275,200,"b"],["t",282,180,"h\u2082"]]},o:["{h;3}","{4h;9}","{2h;3}","h"],c:1,s:`Now the top of the block is in air at P\u2080. Water of depth h\u2082 is under the part of the base outside the hole (area 3\u03C0r\xB2).
Upward force = P\u2080\u03C0r\xB2 + (P\u2080 + \u03C1gh\u2082)\xB73\u03C0r\xB2.
Downward force = P\u2080\xB74\u03C0r\xB2 + weight = P\u2080\xB74\u03C0r\xB2 + 4/3\u03C0r\xB2\u03C1gh.
Net upward force = \u03C0r\xB2\u03C1g[3h\u2082 \u2212 4/3h].
The block just stays in place when this is zero: h\u2082 = 4h/9.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:`A cylindrical tank has a hole of diameter 2r in its bottom. The hole is covered by a wooden cylindrical block of diameter 4r, height h and density \u03C1/3.
Situation 1: Initially, the tank is filled with water of density \u03C1 to a height such that the height of water above the top of the block is h\u2081 (measured from the top of the block).
Situation 2: The water is removed from the tank to a height h\u2082 (measured from the bottom of the block), as shown in the figure. The height h\u2082 is smaller than h (height of the block) and thus the block is exposed to the atmosphere.
In situation 2, if h\u2082 is further decreased, then`,fig:{w:340,h:232,alt:"The same tank with water lowered to height h2 above the bottom, below the top of the block, so the top of the block is in air.",d:[["l",40,200,150,200],["l",190,200,300,200],["l",40,20,40,200],["l",300,20,300,200],["r",130,120,80,80,"f"],["t",170,172,"\u03C1/3","m"],["l",130,128,210,128,"b"],["t",170,142,"4r","m"],["l",150,210,190,210,"b"],["t",170,224,"2r","m"],["t",60,175,"\u03C1"],["l",250,120,250,200,"b"],["t",258,165,"h"],["r",41,150,89,50,"f"],["r",210,150,89,50,"f"],["l",40,150,300,150,"d"],["l",275,150,275,200,"b"],["t",282,180,"h\u2082"]]},o:["cylinder will not move up and remains at its original position","for h\u2082 = {h;3}, cylinder again starts moving up","for h\u2082 = {h;4}, cylinder again starts moving up","for h\u2082 = {h;5}, cylinder again starts moving up"],c:0,s:`From the previous question, net upward force = \u03C0r\xB2\u03C1g[3h\u2082 \u2212 4/3h]. This is zero at h\u2082 = 4h/9.
If h\u2082 is decreased below 4h/9, the bracket becomes negative: the net force on the block is downward, pressing it on the floor.
So the block cannot move up and stays in its original position.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R
Assertion A: When you squeeze one end of a tube to get toothpaste out from the other end, Pascal's principle is observed.
Reason R: A change in the pressure applied to an enclosed incompressible fluid is transmitted undiminished to every portion of the fluid and to the walls of its container.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["A is not correct but R is correct.","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","A is correct but R is not correct.","Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."],c:1,s:`Pascal's law says a pressure change in an enclosed incompressible fluid is transmitted undiminished everywhere in it, so R is correct.
Squeezing one end of the toothpaste tube raises the pressure in the paste, and this pressure is transmitted through it and pushes paste out of the other end. So A is correct.
R is exactly the principle used in A, so R explains A.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Pressure in a reservoir of water is the same at all points at the same level of water.
Statement II: The pressure applied to enclosed water is transmitted in all directions equally.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],c:0,s:`Statement I: in a fluid at rest, P = P\u2080 + \u03C1gh depends only on depth, so all points at the same level have the same pressure. True.
Statement II: this is Pascal's law, so it is true.
Both statements are true.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"psp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A U tube is rotated about one of its limbs with an angular velocity \u03C9. Find the difference in height H of the liquid (density \u03C1) level, where the diameter of the tube d << L.",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"A U-tube rotating with angular velocity omega about its left limb; the horizontal arm has length L and the liquid in the outer right limb stands a height H above that in the left limb.",d:[["p","M52 20 V170 H272 V20"],["p","M68 20 V154 H256 V20"],["r",52,100,16,70,"f"],["r",68,154,188,16,"f"],["r",256,50,16,120,"f"],["l",60,5,60,18,"d"],["p","M40 14 Q60 0 80 14","a"],["t",86,16,"\u03C9"],["l",60,185,264,185,"b"],["t",162,200,"L","m"],["l",272,50,300,50,"d"],["l",272,100,300,100,"d"],["l",294,50,294,100,"b"],["t",302,80,"H"]]},a:"H = \u03C9\xB2L\xB2/(2g)",s:`In the rotating horizontal arm, take a thin slice of liquid at distance x from the axis. The pressure difference across it supplies the centripetal force: dP = \u03C1\u03C9\xB2x dx.
Integrating from the axis limb (x = 0) to the other limb (x = L): P(L) \u2212 P(0) = \u03C1\u03C9\xB2L\xB2/2.
This pressure difference is balanced by the extra liquid column H in the outer limb: \u03C1gH.
\u03C1gH = \u03C1\u03C9\xB2L\xB2/2, so H = \u03C9\xB2L\xB2/(2g).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"psp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A uniform solid cylinder of density 0.8 g/cm\xB3 is in equilibrium in a combination of two non-mixing liquids A and B with its axis vertical. The densities of the liquids A and B are 0.7 g/cm\xB3 and 1.2 g/cm\xB3 respectively. The height of liquid A is h_A = 1.2 cm. The length of the part of the cylinder immersed in liquid B is h_B = 0.8 cm.
(a) Find the total force exerted by liquid A on the cylinder.
(b) Find h, the length of the part of the cylinder in air.
(c) The cylinder is depressed in such a way that its top surface is just below the upper surface of liquid A and is then released. Find the acceleration of the cylinder immediately after it is released.`,fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"A cylinder stands vertically across two liquids: liquid A on top of liquid B, with a small part h above the surface in air, h_A in liquid A and h_B in liquid B.",d:[["p","M60 20 V200 H220 V20"],["l",60,70,220,70],["l",60,118,220,118],["r",110,60,60,90,"f"],["t",66,50,"Air"],["t",66,98,"A"],["t",66,172,"B"],["l",240,60,240,70,"b"],["t",248,69,"h"],["l",240,70,240,118,"b"],["t",248,98,"h_A"],["l",240,118,240,150,"b"],["t",248,138,"h_B"]]},a:"(a) 0   (b) h = 0.25 cm   (c) g/6 (upward)",s:`Let S be the cross-section. Liquid A touches only the curved side of the cylinder, so it exerts only horizontal forces that cancel: force by A = 0.
(b) Equilibrium: weight = upthrust. Upthrust = pressure at the bottom \xD7 S (air pressure cancels) = S g(\u03C1_A h_A + \u03C1_B h_B) = S g(0.7 \xD7 1.2 + 1.2 \xD7 0.8) = 1.8 S g (in g/cm\xB3 \xB7 cm).
Weight = 0.8 \xD7 L \xD7 S g, so L = 2.25 cm. h = 2.25 \u2212 1.2 \u2212 0.8 = 0.25 cm.
(c) Cylinder fully submerged, with 1.2 cm in A and 2.25 \u2212 1.2 = 1.05 cm in B. Upthrust = S g(0.7 \xD7 1.2 + 1.2 \xD7 1.05) = 2.10 S g. Weight = 1.8 S g.
Net upward force = 0.30 S g, mass = 1.8 S, so a = 0.30/1.8g = g/6 upward.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"psp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A wooden stick of length L, radius R and density \u03C1 has a small metal piece of mass m (of negligible volume) attached to its one end. Find the minimum value of the mass m (in terms of given parameters) that would make the stick float vertically in equilibrium in a liquid of density \u03C3 (> \u03C1).",a:"m_min = \u03C0R\xB2[\u221A(\u03C3\u03C1L\xB2 \u2212 \u03C3\xB2R\xB2/2) \u2212 \u03C1L]  (if this is negative, no metal piece is needed)",s:`The metal piece is at the lower end. Let the total mass be M = \u03C0R\xB2\u03C1L + m. The submerged length is x = M/(\u03C3\u03C0R\xB2).
For stable vertical floating the metacentre must lie above the centre of mass. Centre of buoyancy B is x/2 above the bottom; BM = I/V = (\u03C0R\u2074/4)/(\u03C0R\xB2x) = R\xB2/(4x).
Centre of mass G is at height \u03C0R\xB2\u03C1L\xB7(L/2)/M = \u03C0R\xB2\u03C1L\xB2/(2M) above the bottom.
Condition: G < x/2 + R\xB2/(4x). Putting x = M/(\u03C3\u03C0R\xB2) and k = M/(\u03C0R\xB2) this becomes \u03C1L\xB2/(2k) < k/(2\u03C3) + \u03C3R\xB2/(4k), i.e. k\xB2 > \u03C3\u03C1L\xB2 \u2212 \u03C3\xB2R\xB2/2.
So M_min = \u03C0R\xB2\u221A(\u03C3\u03C1L\xB2 \u2212 \u03C3\xB2R\xB2/2), and m_min = M_min \u2212 \u03C0R\xB2\u03C1L.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"psp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A thin rod of length L and area of cross-section S is pivoted at its lowest point P inside a stationary, homogeneous and non-viscous liquid. The rod is free to rotate in a vertical plane about a horizontal axis passing through P. The density d\u2081 of the material of the rod is smaller than the density d\u2082 of the liquid. The rod is displaced by a small angle \u03B8 from its equilibrium position and then released. Show that the motion of the rod is simple harmonic and determine its angular frequency in terms of the given parameters.",fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"A thin rod pivoted at its lowest point P stands vertically in a liquid of density d2; the rod has density d1 and length L.",d:[["p","M80 20 V210 H260 V20"],["r",80,40,180,170,"f"],["l",80,40,260,40,"d"],["l",170,185,170,70,"B"],["c",170,185,3,"F"],["t",180,200,"P"],["l",150,70,150,185,"b"],["t",144,130,"L","e"],["t",180,120,"d\u2081"],["t",92,100,"d\u2082"]]},a:"\u03C9 = \u221A(3(d\u2082 \u2212 d\u2081)g/(2d\u2081L))",s:`Take torques about P when the rod is tilted by a small angle \u03B8 from the vertical. Both the weight and the buoyant force act at the centre, a horizontal distance (L/2) sin \u03B8 from P.
Buoyant force = d\u2082SLg (upward), weight = d\u2081SLg (downward). Net restoring torque \u03C4 = (d\u2082 \u2212 d\u2081)SLg\xB7(L/2) sin \u03B8 \u2248 (d\u2082 \u2212 d\u2081)SL\xB2g\u03B8/2.
Moment of inertia about P: I = 1/3(d\u2081SL)L\xB2 = d\u2081SL\xB3/3.
I\xB7\u03B1 = \u2212\u03C4 gives \u03B1 = \u2212(3(d\u2082 \u2212 d\u2081)g)/2d\u2081L\u03B8. Since \u03B1 \u221D \u2212\u03B8, the motion is simple harmonic.
\u03C9 = \u221A((3(d\u2082 \u2212 d\u2081)g)/2d\u2081L).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"psp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A ball of density d is dropped on to a horizontal solid surface. It bounces elastically from the surface and returns to its original position in a time t\u2081. Next, the ball is released and it falls through the same height before striking the surface of a liquid of density d_L.
(a) If d < d_L, obtain an expression (in terms of d, t\u2081 and d_L) for the time t\u2082 the ball takes to come back to the position from which it was released.
(b) Is the motion of the ball simple harmonic?
(c) If d = d_L, how does the speed of the ball depend on its depth inside the liquid? Neglect all frictional and other dissipative forces. Assume the depth of the liquid to be large.`,a:"(a) t\u2082 = d_L t\u2081/(d_L \u2212 d)   (b) No, it is periodic but not SHM   (c) the speed stays constant (= g t\u2081/2), independent of depth",s:`Free fall: the ball falls the height in t\u2081/2 and reaches the liquid with speed v = g\xB7t\u2081/2.
(a) In the liquid, net upward force = (d_L \u2212 d)Vg on mass dV, so the deceleration is a = ((d_L \u2212 d))/dg. Time to stop = v/a = (d t\u2081)/(2(d_L \u2212 d)). It takes the same time to return to the surface (with speed v, going up), then t\u2081/2 to rise to the start.
t\u2082 = t\u2081 + 2 \xD7 (d t\u2081)/(2(d_L \u2212 d)) = t\u2081 + (d t\u2081)/(d_L \u2212 d) = (d_L t\u2081)/(d_L \u2212 d).
(b) No. The acceleration is constant (g in air, a in liquid), not proportional to displacement and the ball bounces back from the surface. The motion is periodic but not simple harmonic.
(c) For d = d_L the weight and upthrust balance, so the net force is zero. The ball moves with constant speed v = g t\u2081/2 = \u221A(2gH) (H = fall height), independent of depth.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"pst",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A thin tube of uniform cross-section is sealed at both ends. It lies horizontally, the middle 5 cm containing mercury and the two equal end columns containing air at the same pressure P. When the tube is held at an angle of 60\xB0 with the vertical direction, the length of the air column above and below the mercury column are 46 cm and 44.5 cm respectively. Calculate the pressure P in centimetres of mercury. (The temperature of the system is kept at 30\xB0C.)",a:"P \u2248 75.4 cm of mercury",s:`Each end column initially has length l = (46 + 44.5 + 5 \u2212 5)/2 = 45.25 cm (total tube = 46 + 44.5 + 5 = 95.5 cm) at pressure P.
Temperature is constant, so Boyle's law applies to each column. Upper: P\u2081 = 45.25P/46. Lower: P\u2082 = 45.25P/44.5.
The mercury column is vertical in height 5 cos 60\xB0 = 2.5 cm, so P\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081 = 2.5 (cm of Hg).
45.25P(1/44.5 \u2212 1/46) = 2.5, i.e. 45.25P \xD7 1.5/(44.5 \xD7 46) = 2.5.
P = (2.5 \xD7 2047)/(45.25 \xD7 1.5) \u2248 75.4 cm of Hg.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"psp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A wooden plank of length 1 m and uniform cross-section is hinged at one end to the bottom of a tank as shown in fig. The tank is filled with water up to a height 0.5 m. The specific gravity of the plank is 0.5. Find the angle \u03B8 that the plank makes with the vertical in the equilibrium position. (Exclude the case \u03B8 = 0\xB0).",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"A tank of water 0.5 m deep with a plank hinged at the tank bottom, leaning at angle theta to the vertical.",d:[["p","M60 20 V190 H280 V20"],["r",60,100,220,90,"f"],["l",60,100,280,100,"d"],["l",110,190,237,63,"B"],["c",110,190,3,"F"],["l",110,190,110,70,"d"],["t",118,155,"\u03B8"],["l",45,100,45,190,"b"],["t",40,150,"0.5 m","e"]]},a:"\u03B8 = 45\xB0",s:`Let the plank make angle \u03B8 with the vertical. Its submerged length is x = 0.5/cos \u03B8.
Take torques about the hinge, with \u03C1_w the density of water and A the cross-section. The weight (0.5\u03C1_w A\xB71\xB7g) acts at the middle, 0.5 m from the hinge; the buoyancy (\u03C1_w A x g) acts at the middle of the submerged part, x/2 from the hinge. Both lever arms have a factor sin \u03B8.
Weight torque: 0.5\u03C1_wAg \xD7 0.5 sin \u03B8. Buoyancy torque: \u03C1_wAxg \xD7 (x/2) sin \u03B8.
Equating: 0.25 = x\xB2/2, so x\xB2 = 0.5 and x = 0.707 m.
cos \u03B8 = 0.5/0.707 = 0.707, so \u03B8 = 45\xB0.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"psp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Two identical cylindrical vessels with their bases at the same level each contain a liquid of density \u03C1. The height of the liquid in one vessel is h\u2081 and in the other vessel is h\u2082. The area of either base is A. What is the work done by gravity in equalising the levels when the two vessels are connected?",a:"W = \u03C1gA(h\u2081 \u2212 h\u2082)\xB2/4",s:`Work done by gravity = decrease in potential energy of the liquid.
Before: each liquid column of mass \u03C1Ah has its centre at h/2, so U\u2081 = \u03C1Ag(h\u2081\xB2 + h\u2082\xB2)/2.
After: both levels are (h\u2081 + h\u2082)/2, so U\u2082 = 2 \xD7 \u03C1Ag \xD7 ((h\u2081 + h\u2082)\xB2)/4 \xD7 1/2 = \u03C1Ag(h\u2081 + h\u2082)\xB2/4.
W = U\u2081 \u2212 U\u2082 = \u03C1Ag[(h\u2081\xB2 + h\u2082\xB2)/2 \u2212 ((h\u2081 + h\u2082)\xB2)/4] = \u03C1Ag ((h\u2081 \u2212 h\u2082)\xB2)/4.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1979",tp:"psp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A boat floating in a water tank is carrying a number of large stones. If the stones are unloaded into the water, what will happen to the water level?",a:"The water level falls",s:`While the stones are in the boat, the boat floats, so the water displaced has weight equal to the weight of boat + stones. The stones account for a displaced volume W/\u03C1_w (W = their weight).
Once they are in the water, they sink and displace only their own volume V = W/\u03C1_stone.
Since \u03C1_stone > \u03C1_w, V < W/\u03C1_w, so less water is displaced.
The water level in the tank falls.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1991",tp:"pst",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A piece of metal floats on mercury. The coefficients of volume expansion of the metal and mercury are \u03B3\u2081 and \u03B3\u2082 respectively. If the temperatures of both mercury and metal are increased by an amount \u0394T, the fraction of the volume of the metal submerged in mercury changes by the factor ______.",a:"(1 + \u03B3\u2082\u0394T)/(1 + \u03B3\u2081\u0394T)",s:`For floating, the submerged fraction = \u03C1_metal/\u03C1_mercury.
On heating, densities fall: \u03C1_metal' = \u03C1_metal/(1 + \u03B3\u2081\u0394T) and \u03C1_mercury' = \u03C1_mercury/(1 + \u03B3\u2082\u0394T).
New fraction = (\u03C1_metal')/(\u03C1_mercury') = \u03C1_metal/\u03C1_mercury \xD7 (1 + \u03B3\u2082\u0394T)/(1 + \u03B3\u2081\u0394T).
So the fraction changes by the factor (1 + \u03B3\u2082\u0394T)/(1 + \u03B3\u2081\u0394T).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"Consider a completely full cylindrical water tank of height 1.6 m and of cross-sectional area 0.5 m\xB2. It has a small hole in its side at a height 90 cm from the bottom. Assume the cross-sectional area of the hole to be negligibly small as compared to that of the water tank. If a load 50 kg is applied at the top surface of the water in the tank then the velocity of the water coming out at the instant when the hole is opened is (g = 10 m/s\xB2)",o:["5 m/s","2 m/s","3 m/s","4 m/s"],c:3,s:`Depth of the hole below the water surface = 1.6 \u2212 0.9 = 0.7 m.
The load adds pressure 50 \xD7 10/0.5 = 1000 Pa, equal to an extra water head of 1000/(1000 \xD7 10) = 0.1 m.
Efflux speed v = \u221A(2g \xD7 total head) = \u221A(2 \xD7 10 \xD7 (0.7 + 0.1)) = \u221A16 = 4 m/s.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"A tube of length L is shown in the figure. The radius of cross section at the point (1) is 2 cm and at the point (2) is 1 cm, respectively. If the velocity of water entering at point (1) is 2 m/s, then velocity of water leaving the point (2) will be",fig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"A tube narrowing from radius 2 cm at point (1) on the left to radius 1 cm at point (2) on the right, with water flowing left to right.",d:[["l",60,35,220,50],["l",60,95,220,80],["l",60,35,60,95],["l",220,50,220,80],["t",60,28,"(1)","m"],["t",220,42,"(2)","m"],["l",15,65,55,65,"a"],["l",225,65,265,65,"a"]]},o:["2 m/s","4 m/s","6 m/s","8 m/s"],c:3,s:`Continuity equation: A\u2081v\u2081 = A\u2082v\u2082, and A \u221D r\xB2.
v\u2082 = v\u2081 (r\u2081/r\u2082)\xB2 = 2 \xD7 (2/1)\xB2 = 8 m/s.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"Water flows in a horizontal pipe whose one end is closed with a valve. The reading of the pressure gauge attached to the pipe is P\u2081. The reading of the pressure gauge falls to P\u2082 when the valve is opened. The speed of water flowing in the pipe is proportional to",o:["P\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082","\u221A(P\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082)","(P\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082)\xB2","(P\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082)\u2074"],c:1,s:`With the valve closed the water is at rest, so the gauge reads P\u2081. When the valve is open the water moves with speed v and the gauge reads P\u2082.
Bernoulli (horizontal pipe): P\u2081 = P\u2082 + \xBD\u03C1v\xB2.
So v = \u221A((2(P\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082))/\u03C1), i.e. v \u221D \u221A(P\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082).
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"Correct Bernoulli's equation is (symbols have their usual meaning)",o:["P + \u03C1gh + \xBD\u03C1v\xB2 = constant","P + \xBD\u03C1gh + \xBD\u03C1v\xB2 = constant","P + \u03C1gh + \u03C1v\xB2 = constant","P + mgh + \xBDmv\xB2 = constant"],c:0,s:`Bernoulli's equation is a statement of energy per unit volume: pressure energy + potential energy + kinetic energy.
P + \u03C1gh + \xBD\u03C1v\xB2 = constant.
The other options either miss a factor \xBD or use mass m instead of density \u03C1, so the terms do not have the units of pressure.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:`The figure shows a liquid of given density flowing steadily in a horizontal tube of varying cross-section. Cross sectional area at A is 1.5 cm\xB2, and B is 25 mm\xB2. If the speed of liquid at B is 60 cm/s then (P_A \u2212 P_B) is:
(Given: P_A and P_B are liquid pressures at A and B points. Density \u03C1 = 1000 kg m\u207B\xB3, A and B are on the axis of the tube)`,fig:{w:340,h:125,alt:"A horizontal tube that is wide on the left at point A and narrows to a thin section on the right at point B; both points lie on the axis.",d:[["p","M20 25 H110 L190 50 H320"],["p","M20 105 H110 L190 80 H320"],["l",20,65,320,65,"d"],["c",60,65,2.5,"F"],["t",60,55,"A","m"],["c",260,65,2.5,"F"],["t",260,55,"B","m"]]},o:["27 Pa","36 Pa","135 Pa","175 Pa"],c:3,s:`Convert: A_A = 1.5 cm\xB2 = 150 mm\xB2, A_B = 25 mm\xB2, v_B = 0.6 m/s.
Continuity: v_A = v_B (A_B/A_A) = 0.6 \xD7 (25/150) = 0.1 m/s.
Bernoulli (horizontal): P_A \u2212 P_B = \xBD\u03C1(v_B\xB2 \u2212 v_A\xB2) = 500 \xD7 (0.36 \u2212 0.01) = 175 Pa.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Applications of Bernoulli",q:"A fully loaded Boeing aircraft has a mass of 5.4 \xD7 10\u2075 kg. Its total wing area is 500 m\xB2. It is in level flight with a speed of 1080 km/h. If the density of air \u03C1 is 1.2 kg m\u207B\xB3, the fractional increase in the speed of the air on the upper surface of the wing relative to the lower surface in percentage will be (g = 10 m/s\xB2)",o:["10","16","6","8"],c:0,s:`In level flight, lift = weight: \u0394P \xD7 A = mg, so \u0394P = (5.4 \xD7 10\u2076)/500 = 1.08 \xD7 10\u2074 Pa.
Bernoulli: \u0394P = \xBD\u03C1(v\u2082\xB2 \u2212 v\u2081\xB2) = \xBD\u03C1(v\u2082 \u2212 v\u2081)(v\u2082 + v\u2081) \u2248 \u03C1 v \u0394v, with v = 1080 km/h = 300 m/s.
\u0394v = (1.08 \xD7 10\u2074)/(1.2 \xD7 300) = 30 m/s.
Fractional increase = 30/300 = 10%.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"Consider a cylindrical tank of radius 1 m filled with water. The top surface of water is at 15 m from the bottom of the cylinder. There is a hole on the wall of the cylinder at a height of 5 m from the bottom. A force of 5 \xD7 10\u2075 N is applied on the top surface of water using a piston. The speed of efflux from the hole will be (Given atmospheric pressure P_A = 1.01 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa, density of water \u03C1_w = 1000 kg/m\xB3 and gravitational acceleration g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",fig:{w:340,h:232,alt:"A cylindrical tank with a piston on top pushed by a force of 5 times 10 to the 5 newton; the water surface is 15 m above the bottom and a side hole 5 m above the bottom, 10 m below the surface, throws out a jet.",d:[["p","M60 40 V200 H220 V40"],["r",60,44,160,156,"f"],["r",60,36,160,8,"F"],["l",140,5,140,33,"a"],["t",150,20,"5\xD710\u2075 N"],["l",220,147,300,147,"a"],["l",240,40,240,147,"b"],["t",246,98,"10 m"],["l",240,147,240,200,"b"],["t",246,178,"5 m"],["l",40,40,40,200,"b"],["t",34,125,"15 m","e"]]},o:["11.6 m/s","10.8 m/s","17.8 m/s","14.4 m/s"],c:2,s:`The piston adds a pressure F/A = (5 \xD7 10\u2075)/(\u03C0 \xD7 1\xB2) \u2248 1.59 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa on top of the water.
Taking the pressure from the piston as what drives the jet (this reproduces the printed answer): \xBD\u03C1v\xB2 \u2248 F/A, giving v = \u221A((2 \xD7 1.59 \xD7 10\u2075)/1000) \u2248 17.8 m/s.
(Note: the 10 m of water above the hole would add a further 10\u2075 Pa; the printed answer does not include it, and 17.8 m/s is the only option that matches.)
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"A light cylindrical vessel is kept on a horizontal surface. The area of base is A. A hole of cross-sectional area 'a' is made just at its bottom side. The minimum coefficient of friction necessary to prevent sliding the vessel due to the impact force of the emerging liquid is (a << A)",fig:{w:340,h:165,alt:"A cylindrical vessel of base area A full of liquid resting on a table, with a small hole of area a at the bottom of its right side from which the liquid jets out.",d:[["l",60,30,60,130],["l",60,130,220,130],["l",220,30,220,115],["l",220,125,220,130],["r",61,50,158,80,"f"],["l",222,120,280,120,"a"],["t",230,112,"a"],["t",140,148,"A","m"]]},o:["{2a;A}","{a;A}","{A;2a}","none of these"],c:0,s:`Let the liquid height be h. Efflux speed v = \u221A(2gh).
The liquid leaves with momentum rate \u03C1av\xB2, so the reaction force on the vessel (the thrust) = \u03C1av\xB2 = 2\u03C1agh.
The vessel is light, so the normal force = weight of liquid = \u03C1Ahg.
For no sliding: \u03BCN \u2265 thrust, so \u03BC \u2265 2\u03C1agh/\u03C1Ahg = 2a/A.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psp",st:"Fluids in accelerated frames and rotation",q:"A cylindrical vessel containing a liquid is rotated about its axis so that the liquid rises at its sides as shown in the figure. The radius of vessel is 5 cm and the angular speed of rotation is \u03C9 rad s\u207B\xB9. The difference in the height, h (in cm) of liquid at the centre of vessel and at the side will be",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A cylindrical vessel of diameter 10 cm rotating with angular speed omega; the liquid surface is a paraboloid, higher at the walls than at the centre by a height h.",d:[["p","M100 40 V160 H240 V40"],["p","M100 60 Q170 160 240 60"],["l",100,60,240,60,"d"],["l",170,60,170,110,"b"],["t",176,90,"h"],["p","M150 28 Q170 14 190 28","a"],["t",196,30,"\u03C9"],["l",100,175,240,175,"b"],["t",170,192,"10 cm","m"]]},o:["{2\u03C9\xB2;25g}","{5\u03C9\xB2;2g}","{25\u03C9\xB2;2g}","{2\u03C9\xB2;5g}"],c:2,s:`In a rotating liquid the surface is a parabola: the height difference between the wall and the axis is h = \u03C9\xB2r\xB2/2g.
With r = 5 cm and g in cm s\u207B\xB2: h = (\u03C9\xB2 \xD7 25)/2g cm.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"A fluid is flowing through a horizontal pipe of varying cross-section, with speed v m s\u207B\xB9 at a point where the pressure is P pascal. At another point where pressure is P/2 pascal, its speed is V m s\u207B\xB9. If the density of the fluid is \u03C1 kg m\u207B\xB3 and the flow is streamlined, then V is equal to",o:["\u221A({P;\u03C1} + v)","\u221A({2P;\u03C1} + v\xB2)","\u221A({P;2\u03C1} + v\xB2)","\u221A({P;\u03C1} + v\xB2)"],c:3,s:`Bernoulli's equation for a horizontal pipe: P + \xBD\u03C1v\xB2 = P/2 + \xBD\u03C1V\xB2.
So \xBD\u03C1V\xB2 = P/2 + \xBD\u03C1v\xB2, i.e. V\xB2 = P/\u03C1 + v\xB2.
V = \u221A(P/\u03C1 + v\xB2).
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"An ideal fluid flows (laminar flow) through a pipe of non-uniform diameter. The maximum and minimum diameters of the pipes are 6.4 cm and 4.8 cm, respectively. The ratio of the minimum and the maximum velocities of fluid in this pipe is",o:["{81;256}","{9;16}","{3;4}","{\u221A3;2}"],c:1,s:`Continuity: Av = constant, so v \u221D 1/d\xB2.
The minimum velocity is at the maximum diameter and the maximum velocity at the minimum diameter.
v_min/v_max = (d_min/d_max)\xB2 = (4.8/6.4)\xB2 = (3/4)\xB2 = 9/16.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"Water flows in a horizontal tube (see figure). The pressure of water changes by 700 N m\u207B\xB2 between A and B where the area of cross section are 40 cm\xB2 and 20 cm\xB2, respectively. Find the rate of flow of water through the tube. (density of water = 1000 kg m\u207B\xB3)",fig:{w:340,h:120,alt:"A horizontal tube with a wide section containing point A and a narrower constricted section containing point B.",d:[["p","M20 25 H110 L135 45 H205 L230 25 H320"],["p","M20 95 H110 L135 75 H205 L230 95 H320"],["t",65,64,"A","m"],["t",170,64,"B","m"]]},o:["3020 cm\xB3/s","2420 cm\xB3/s","2720 cm\xB3/s","1810 cm\xB3/s"],c:2,s:`Continuity: v_B = v_A \xD7 (40/20) = 2v_A.
Bernoulli: \u0394P = \xBD\u03C1(v_B\xB2 \u2212 v_A\xB2) = \xBD \xD7 1000 \xD7 3v_A\xB2 = 1500v_A\xB2 = 700.
v_A\xB2 = 0.467, v_A \u2248 0.683 m/s.
Flow rate Q = A_A v_A = 40 cm\xB2 \xD7 68.3 cm/s \u2248 2730 cm\xB3/s, closest to 2720 cm\xB3/s.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"Water from a tap emerges vertically downwards with an initial speed of 1.0 m s\u207B\xB9. The cross-sectional area of the tap is 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2. Assume that the pressure is constant throughout the stream of water and that the flow is streamlined. The cross-sectional area of the stream, 0.15 m below the tap would be (Take g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2","5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2","2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2","1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2"],c:0,s:`Pressure is constant, so the water falls freely: v\xB2 = u\xB2 + 2gh = 1 + 2 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.15 = 4, so v = 2 m/s.
Continuity: A\u2081v\u2081 = A\u2082v\u2082.
A\u2082 = (10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 1)/2 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"The top of a water tank is open to air and its water level is maintained. It is giving out 0.74 m\xB3 water per minute through a circular opening of 2 cm radius in its wall. The depth of the centre of the opening from the level of water in the tank is close to",o:["9.6 m","6.0 m","2.9 m","4.8 m"],c:3,s:`Flow rate Q = 0.74/60 = 0.01233 m\xB3/s. Area of opening A = \u03C0(0.02)\xB2 = 1.257 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2.
Efflux speed v = Q/A = 0.01233/(1.257 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) \u2248 9.81 m/s.
Torricelli: v\xB2 = 2gh, so h = v\xB2/2g = 96.3/(2 \xD7 10) \u2248 4.8 m (taking g = 10 m/s\xB2).
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"Water flows into a large tank with flat bottom at the rate of 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB3 s\u207B\xB9. Water is also leaking out of a hole of area 1 cm\xB2 at its bottom. If the height of the water in the tank remains steady, then this height is",o:["4 cm","5.1 cm","2.9 cm","1.7 cm"],c:1,s:`In steady state, inflow = outflow through the hole.
Hole area a = 1 cm\xB2 = 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2, so a\xB7v = 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB3/s gives v = 1 m/s.
Efflux speed v = \u221A(2gh), so h = v\xB2/2g = 1/(2 \xD7 9.8) m = 0.051 m.
h \u2248 5.1 cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psf",st:"Applications of Bernoulli",q:"A liquid of density \u03C1 is coming out of a hose pipe of radius a with speed v and hits a mesh. 50% of the liquid passes through the mesh unaffected. 25% looses all of its momentum and 25% comes back with the same speed. The resultant pressure on the mesh will be",o:["\u03C1v\xB2","\xBC\u03C1v\xB2","\xBE\u03C1v\xB2","\xBD\u03C1v\xB2"],c:2,s:`Mass hitting the mesh per second = \u03C1(\u03C0a\xB2)v.
50% passes: no change in momentum.
25% stops: momentum change per second = 0.25 \u03C1(\u03C0a\xB2)v \xB7 v.
25% rebounds: change = 0.25 \u03C1(\u03C0a\xB2)v \xB7 2v.
Force = (0.25 + 0.5) \u03C1(\u03C0a\xB2)v\xB2 = 0.75 \u03C1(\u03C0a\xB2)v\xB2.
Pressure = force/area = \xBE\u03C1v\xB2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psp",st:"Fluids in accelerated frames and rotation",q:"A long cylindrical vessel is half filled with a liquid. When the vessel is rotated about its own vertical axis, the liquid rises up near the wall. If the radius of vessel is 5 cm and its rotational speed is 2 rotations per second, then the difference in the heights between the centre and the sides, in cm, will be",o:["0.4","2.0","0.1","1.2"],c:1,s:`In a rotating liquid the surface is a paraboloid: h = \u03C9\xB2r\xB2/(2g).
\u03C9 = 2\u03C0 \xD7 2 = 4\u03C0 rad/s, so \u03C9\xB2 = 16\u03C0\xB2 \u2248 158; r = 0.05 m.
h = 158 \xD7 0.0025/(2 \xD7 10) = 0.0197 m.
h \u2248 2.0 cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"Consider a water jar of radius R that has water filled up to height H and is kept on a stand of height h. Through a hole of radius r (r << R) at its bottom, the water leaks out and the stream of water coming down towards the ground has a shape like a funnel. If the radius of the cross-section of the water stream when it hits the ground is x, then",fig:{w:340,h:250,alt:"Water jar of radius R filled to height H, standing at height h above the ground; a hole of radius r at its bottom gives a stream that narrows to radius x at the ground.",d:[["r",100,30,120,100,"f"],["l",100,130,150,130],["l",170,130,220,130],["l",106,130,106,235],["l",214,130,214,235],["l",60,235,280,235],["l",150,130,157,235],["l",170,130,163,235],["l",100,22,220,22,"b"],["t",160,16,"R","m"],["l",242,30,242,130,"b"],["t",248,84,"H"],["l",242,130,242,235,"b"],["t",248,188,"h"],["t",176,152,"2r","s"],["t",176,222,"2x","s"]]},o:["x = r(H/(H+h))^(1/4)","x = r(H/(H+h))","x = r(H/(H+h))\xB2","x = r(H/(H+h))^(1/2)"],c:0,s:`Speed at the hole: v\u2080 = \u221A(2gH). Speed at the ground: v = \u221A(2g(H+h)).
Continuity: \u03C0r\xB2v\u2080 = \u03C0x\xB2v, so x\xB2 = r\xB2\xB7\u221A(H/(H+h)).
Therefore x = r\xB7(H/(H+h))^(1/4).`},{y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"psf",st:"Applications of Bernoulli",q:"In a horizontal tube (diagram) the difference in the water levels in the two tubes of the manometer is 5 cm. The cross-section of the tube at A and B is 6 mm\xB2 and 10 mm\xB2 respectively. The rate at which water flows through the tube is (g = 10 ms\u207B\xB2)",fig:{w:340,h:140,alt:"Horizontal tube narrow at A and wide at B with two vertical manometer tubes; the water level over B is 5 cm higher than over A.",d:[["p","M20,60 H70 C90,60 100,40 125,40 H220"],["p","M20,100 H70 C90,100 100,120 125,120 H220"],["l",39,10,39,60],["l",51,10,51,60],["l",164,10,164,40],["l",176,10,176,40],["l",39,36,51,36,"B"],["l",164,14,176,14,"B"],["l",51,36,215,36,"d"],["l",176,14,215,14,"d"],["l",208,14,208,36,"b"],["t",216,30,"5 cm"],["t",45,84,"A","m"],["t",170,84,"B","m"],["l",20,80,36,80,"a"]]},o:["7.5 cc/s","8.0 cc/s","10.0 cc/s","12.5 cc/s"],c:0,s:`Pressure difference between B and A: \u0394P = \u03C1gh, so \xBD\u03C1(v_A\xB2 \u2212 v_B\xB2) = \u03C1gh, with h = 5 cm.
Continuity: 6v_A = 10v_B, so v_A = (5/3)v_B. Then v_A\xB2 \u2212 v_B\xB2 = (16/9)v_B\xB2 = 2gh = 2 \xD7 1000 \xD7 5 = 10\u2074 cm\xB2/s\xB2.
v_B\xB2 = 5625 cm\xB2/s\xB2, so v_B = 75 cm/s and v_A = 125 cm/s.
Q = A_Av_A = 0.06 cm\xB2 \xD7 125 cm/s = 7.5 cm\xB3/s.`},{y:"AIEEE 2012 \xB7 Online",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"A square hole of side length l is made at a depth of h and a circular hole of radius r is made at a depth of 4h from the surface of water in a water tank kept on a horizontal surface. If l << h, r << h and the rate of water flow from both the holes is the same, then r is equal to",o:["l/\u221A(2\u03C0)","l/\u221A(3\u03C0)","l/3\u03C0","l/2\u03C0"],c:0,s:`Rate of flow = (hole area) \xD7 \u221A(2g \xD7 depth).
Square hole: l\xB2\u221A(2gh). Circular hole: \u03C0r\xB2\u221A(2g\xB74h) = 2\u03C0r\xB2\u221A(2gh).
Equal rates: l\xB2 = 2\u03C0r\xB2, so r = l/\u221A(2\u03C0).`},{y:"AIEEE 2011",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"Water is flowing continuously from a tap having an internal diameter 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m. The water velocity as it leaves the tap is 0.4 m s\u207B\xB9. The diameter of the water stream at a distance 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 m below the tap is close to",o:["5.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m","7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m","9.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m","3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m"],c:3,s:`Speed after falling 0.2 m: v\xB2 = 0.4\xB2 + 2 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.2 = 4.16, so v \u2248 2.04 m/s.
Continuity: d\xB2v = d\u2080\xB2v\u2080, so d = d\u2080\u221A(v\u2080/v).
d = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 \u221A(0.4/2.04) = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 0.443 \u2248 3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"Water is filled in a container upto height 3 m. A small hole of area a is punched in the wall of the container at a height 52.5 cm from the bottom. The cross-sectional area of the container is A. If a/A = 0.1 then v\xB2 is (where v is the velocity of water coming out of the hole)",o:["50 m\xB2s\u207B\xB2","51 m\xB2s\u207B\xB2","48 m\xB2s\u207B\xB2","51.5 m\xB2s\u207B\xB2"],c:0,s:`Depth of the hole = 3 \u2212 0.525 = 2.475 m.
Bernoulli with the surface speed V: v\xB2 \u2212 V\xB2 = 2gh, and AV = av gives V = 0.1v.
v\xB2(1 \u2212 0.01) = 2 \xD7 10 \xD7 2.475 = 49.5.
v\xB2 = 49.5/0.99 = 50 m\xB2s\u207B\xB2.`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"A cylinder of height 20 m is completely filled with water. The velocity of efflux of water (in m s\u207B\xB9) through a small hole on the side wall of the cylinder near its bottom is",o:["10","20","25.5","5"],c:1,s:`The hole near the bottom is at depth h = 20 m.
v = \u221A(2gh) = \u221A(2 \xD7 10 \xD7 20) = \u221A400 = 20 m/s.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"A large open tank has two holes in the wall. One is a square hole of side L at a depth y from the top and the other is a circular hole of radius R at a depth 4y from the top. When the tank is completely filled with water, the quantities of water flowing out per second from both holes are the same. Then, R is equal to",o:["L/\u221A(2\u03C0)","2\u03C0L","L","L/2\u03C0"],c:0,s:`Rate of flow = (area) \xD7 \u221A(2g \xD7 depth).
Square hole: L\xB2\u221A(2gy). Circular hole: \u03C0R\xB2\u221A(8gy) = 2\u03C0R\xB2\u221A(2gy).
Equal rates: L\xB2 = 2\u03C0R\xB2, so R = L/\u221A(2\u03C0).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psf",st:"Applications of Bernoulli",q:"A plane is in level flight at constant speed and each of its two wings has an area of 40 m\xB2. If the speed of the air is 180 km/h over the lower wing surface and 252 km/h over the upper wing surface, the mass of the plane is ______ kg. (Take air density to be 1 kg m\u207B\xB3 and g = 10 ms\u207B\xB2)",n:9600,s:`Speeds: 180 km/h = 50 m/s and 252 km/h = 70 m/s.
Pressure difference = \xBD\u03C1(v\u2082\xB2 \u2212 v\u2081\xB2) = \xBD \xD7 1 \xD7 (4900 \u2212 2500) = 1200 Pa.
Total wing area = 2 \xD7 40 = 80 m\xB2, so lift = 1200 \xD7 80 = 96000 N.
Level flight: lift = mg, so m = 96000/10 = 9600 kg.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"The reading of pressure metre attached with a closed pipe is 4.5 \xD7 10\u2074 N/m\xB2. On opening the valve, water starts flowing and the reading of pressure metre falls to 2.0 \xD7 10\u2074 N/m\xB2. The velocity of water is found to be \u221AV m/s. The value of V is ______.",n:50,s:`Bernoulli: the pressure drop becomes kinetic energy per volume.
\xBD\u03C1v\xB2 = 4.5 \xD7 10\u2074 \u2212 2.0 \xD7 10\u2074 = 2.5 \xD7 10\u2074 Pa.
v\xB2 = 2 \xD7 2.5 \xD7 10\u2074/1000 = 50 m\xB2/s\xB2.
So v = \u221A50 m/s and V = 50.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psf",st:"Applications of Bernoulli",q:"In a test experiment on a model aeroplane in wind tunnel, the flow speeds on the upper and lower surface of the wings are 70 ms\u207B\xB9 and 65 ms\u207B\xB9 respectively. If the wing area is 2 m\xB2, the lift of the wing is ______ N. (Given density of air = 1.2 kg m\u207B\xB3)",n:810,s:`Pressure difference = \xBD\u03C1(v\u2081\xB2 \u2212 v\u2082\xB2) = \xBD \xD7 1.2 \xD7 (70\xB2 \u2212 65\xB2).
70\xB2 \u2212 65\xB2 = 4900 \u2212 4225 = 675.
\u0394P = 0.6 \xD7 675 = 405 Pa.
Lift = \u0394P \xD7 A = 405 \xD7 2 = 810 N.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"Two large, identical water tanks, 1 and 2, kept on the top of a building of height H, are filled with water up to height h in each tank. Both the tanks contain an identical hole of small radius on their sides, close to the bottom. A pipe of the same internal radius as that of the hole is connected to tank 2, and the pipe ends at the ground level. When the water flows from the tanks 1 and 2 through the holes, the times taken to empty the tanks are t\u2081 and t\u2082, respectively. If H = (16/9)h, then the ratio t\u2081/t\u2082 is ______.",n:3,s:`Let the water level be y. Tank 1: v = \u221A(2gy). Tank 2: v = \u221A(2g(y + H)) because of the pipe.
Continuity: A(\u2212dy/dt) = a\xB7v, so t = (A/a)\u222Bdy/v.
t\u2081 = (A/a)\xB72\u221Ah/\u221A(2g).
t\u2082 = (A/a)\xB72[\u221A(h+H) \u2212 \u221AH]/\u221A(2g).
With H = 16h/9: \u221A(h+H) = 5\u221Ah/3 and \u221AH = 4\u221Ah/3, so the bracket = \u221Ah/3.
t\u2081/t\u2082 = \u221Ah/(\u221Ah/3) = 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"A liquid is being pushed out of the tube by a piston having area of cross section 2.0 cm\xB2. The area of cross section at the outlet is 10 mm\xB2. If the piston is pushed at a speed of 4 cm s\u207B\xB9, the speed of outgoing fluid is ______ cm s\u207B\xB9.",fig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"A piston in a wide tube pushes liquid out through a narrow outlet tube.",d:[["l",40,40,170,40],["l",40,100,170,100],["l",170,40,170,62],["l",170,78,170,100],["l",170,62,300,62],["l",170,78,300,78],["l",70,40,70,100,"B"],["l",20,70,70,70],["l",20,58,20,82],["l",75,52,165,52,"d"],["l",75,88,165,88,"d"],["l",175,70,298,70,"d"],["l",300,70,332,70,"a"],["l",72,26,100,26,"a"],["t",106,30,"4 cm/s","s"],["t",120,118,"piston: 2.0 cm\xB2","s"],["t",236,98,"outlet: 10 mm\xB2","sm"]]},n:80,s:`Continuity: A\u2081v\u2081 = A\u2082v\u2082.
Piston area A\u2081 = 2.0 cm\xB2 = 200 mm\xB2, outlet area A\u2082 = 10 mm\xB2.
v\u2082 = (200/10) \xD7 4 = 80 cm/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"Glycerin of density 1.25 \xD7 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3 is flowing through the conical section of pipe. The area of cross-section of the pipe at its ends are 10 cm\xB2 and 5 cm\xB2 and pressure drop across its length is 3 Nm\u207B\xB2. The rate of flow of glycerin through the pipe is x \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB3 s\u207B\xB9. The value of x is ______.",n:4,s:`A\u2081 = 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2, A\u2082 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2. Continuity: A\u2081v\u2081 = A\u2082v\u2082, so v\u2082 = 2v\u2081.
Bernoulli: \u0394P = \xBD\u03C1(v\u2082\xB2 \u2212 v\u2081\xB2) = \xBD\u03C1\xB73v\u2081\xB2.
3 = \xBD \xD7 1250 \xD7 3 \xD7 v\u2081\xB2, so v\u2081\xB2 = 1/625 and v\u2081 = 0.04 m/s.
Q = A\u2081v\u2081 = 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 0.04 = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB3/s.
x = 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"The surface of water in a water tank of cross section area 750 cm\xB2 on the top of a house is h m above the tap level. The speed of water coming out through the tap of cross section area 500 mm\xB2 is 30 cm/s. At that instant, dh/dt is x \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m/s. The value of x will be ______.",n:2,s:`Continuity: the volume leaving per second equals the volume lost from the tank.
A\xB7|dh/dt| = a\xB7v, with A = 750 cm\xB2, a = 500 mm\xB2 = 5 cm\xB2, v = 30 cm/s.
|dh/dt| = 5 \xD7 30/750 = 0.2 cm/s = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m/s.
x = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A spherical ball of radius 1 mm and density 10.5 g/cc is dropped in glycerine of coefficient of viscosity 9.8 poise and density 1.5 g/cc. Viscous force on the ball when it attains constant velocity is 3696 \xD7 10\u207B\u02E3 N. The value of x is ______. (Given, g = 9.8 m/s\xB2 and \u03C0 = 22/7)",n:7,s:`At constant (terminal) velocity: viscous force = weight \u2212 buoyancy.
F = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g = (4/3)(22/7)(10\u207B\xB3)\xB3 \xD7 (10500 \u2212 1500) \xD7 9.8.
= 4.19 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 \xD7 9000 \xD7 9.8 = 3.696 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N = 3696 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N.
x = 7.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"An ideal fluid of density 800 kg m\u207B\xB3 flows smoothly through a bent pipe (as shown in figure) that tapers in cross-sectional area from a to a/2. The pressure difference between the wide and narrow sections of pipe is 4100 Pa. At wider section, the velocity of fluid is \u221Ax/6 m s\u207B\xB9 for x = ______. (Given g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"A pipe of cross-section a at the top left bends down and tapers to cross-section a/2 at the bottom right, 1 m lower.",d:[["p","M30,30 H110 C150,30 160,150 200,150 H300"],["p","M30,70 H110 C135,70 150,170 200,170 H300"],["l",40,50,95,50,"a"],["t",60,24,"v\u2081","m"],["t",62,66,"a","m"],["l",300,160,335,160,"a"],["t",322,152,"v\u2082","m"],["t",250,190,"a/2","m"],["l",15,50,15,160,"b"],["t",22,108,"h = 1 m"],["l",15,50,30,50,"d"],["l",15,160,200,160,"d"]]},n:363,s:`The wide section (speed v\u2081) is h = 1 m above the narrow section. Continuity: v\u2082 = 2v\u2081.
Bernoulli: P\u2081 + \xBD\u03C1v\u2081\xB2 + \u03C1gh = P\u2082 + \xBD\u03C1(2v\u2081)\xB2.
P\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082 = 1.5\u03C1v\u2081\xB2 \u2212 \u03C1gh.
4100 = 1.5 \xD7 800 \xD7 v\u2081\xB2 \u2212 800 \xD7 10 \xD7 1, so 1200v\u2081\xB2 = 12100 and v\u2081\xB2 = 12100/1200.
v\u2081 = \u221Ax/6 gives v\u2081\xB2 = x/36, so x = 36 \xD7 12100/1200 = 363.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"The area of cross-section of a large tank is 0.5 m\xB2. It has a narrow opening near the bottom having area of cross-section 1 cm\xB2. A load of 25 kg is applied on the water at the top in the tank. Neglecting the speed of water in the tank, the velocity of the water, coming out of the opening at the time when the height of water level in the tank is 40 cm above the bottom, will be ______ cm s\u207B\xB9. [Take g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2]",n:300,s:`Extra pressure from the load = mg/A = 25 \xD7 10/0.5 = 500 Pa.
Bernoulli: \xBD\u03C1v\xB2 = \u03C1gh + 500.
v\xB2 = 2(gh + 500/\u03C1) = 2(10 \xD7 0.4 + 0.5) = 2 \xD7 4.5 = 9.
v = 3 m/s = 300 cm/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"A liquid of density 750 kg m\u207B\xB3 flows smoothly through a horizontal pipe that tapers in cross-sectional area from A\u2081 = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m\xB2 to A\u2082 = A\u2081/2. The pressure difference between the wide and narrow sections of the pipe is 4500 Pa. The rate of flow of liquid is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3 s\u207B\xB9.",n:24,s:`Continuity: v\u2082 = 2v\u2081.
Bernoulli (horizontal): \u0394P = \xBD\u03C1(v\u2082\xB2 \u2212 v\u2081\xB2) = \xBD\u03C1\xB73v\u2081\xB2.
4500 = \xBD \xD7 750 \xD7 3 \xD7 v\u2081\xB2 = 1125v\u2081\xB2, so v\u2081 = 2 m/s.
Q = A\u2081v\u2081 = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xD7 2 = 24 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"The water is filled upto height of 12 m in a tank having vertical sidewalls. A hole is made in one of the walls at a depth h below the water level. The value of h for which the emerging stream of water strikes the ground at the maximum range is ______ m.",n:6,s:`Let the water height be H = 12 m and the hole depth h.
Speed v = \u221A(2gh); the hole is at height (H \u2212 h), so fall time t = \u221A(2(H \u2212 h)/g).
Range R = vt = 2\u221A(h(H \u2212 h)). It is maximum when h(H \u2212 h) is maximum, i.e. h = H/2.
h = 12/2 = 6 m.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psf",st:"Efflux and Torricelli",q:"Consider a water tank (a load of 24 kg rests on the water at the top). Its cross-sectional area is 0.4 m\xB2. The tank has an opening B near the bottom whose cross-section area is 1 cm\xB2. A load of 24 kg is applied on the water at the top when the height of the water level is 40 cm above the bottom, the velocity of water coming out the opening B is v m s\u207B\xB9. The value of v, to the nearest integer, is ______. [Take value of g to be 10 m s\u207B\xB2]",n:3,s:`Extra pressure from the load = mg/A = 24 \xD7 10/0.4 = 600 Pa.
Speed of water in the tank is negligible (opening is very small).
Bernoulli: \xBD\u03C1v\xB2 = \u03C1gh + 600, so v\xB2 = 2(10 \xD7 0.4 + 0.6) = 9.2.
v \u2248 3.03 m/s, so v \u2248 3.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"psf",st:"Continuity and Bernoulli",q:"A train with cross-sectional area S\u209C is moving with speed v\u209C inside a long tunnel of cross-sectional area S\u2080 (S\u2080 = 4S\u209C). Assume that almost all the air (density \u03C1) in front of the train flows back between its sides and the walls of the tunnel. Also, the air flow with respect to the train is steady and laminar. Take the ambient pressure and that inside the train to be p\u2080. If the pressure in the region between the sides of the train and the tunnel walls is p, then p\u2080 \u2212 p = {7;2N}\u03C1v\u209C\xB2. The value of N is ______.",n:9,s:`In the ground frame the train pushes air back through the gap area S\u2080 \u2212 S\u209C = 3S\u209C: air speed = S\u209Cv\u209C/(3S\u209C) = v\u209C/3 (backwards).
In the train's frame the air speed in the gap is v\u209C + v\u209C/3 = 4v\u209C/3, and ahead of the train it is v\u209C.
Bernoulli in the train's frame: p\u2080 + \xBD\u03C1v\u209C\xB2 = p + \xBD\u03C1(4v\u209C/3)\xB2.
p\u2080 \u2212 p = \xBD\u03C1v\u209C\xB2(16/9 \u2212 1) = {7;18}\u03C1v\u209C\xB2 = {7;2N}\u03C1v\u209C\xB2.
2N = 18, so N = 9.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"psf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A person in a lift is holding a water jar, which has a small hole at the lower end of its side. When the lift is at rest, the water jet coming out of the hole hits the floor of the lift at a distance d of 1.2 m from the person. In the following, state of the lift's motion is given in List I and the distance where the water jet hits the floor of the lift is given in List II. Match the statements from List I with those in List II and select the correct answer using the code given below the lists.
List I
P. Lift is accelerating vertically up.
Q. Lift is accelerating vertically down with an acceleration less than the gravitational acceleration.
R. Lift is moving vertically up with constant speed.
S. Lift is falling freely.
List II
1. d = 1.2 m
2. d > 1.2 m
3. d < 1.2 m
4. No water leaks out of the jar`,o:["P - 2, Q - 3, R - 2, S - 4","P - 2, Q - 3, R - 1, S - 4","P - 1, Q - 1, R - 1, S - 4","P - 2, Q - 3, R - 1, S - 1"],c:2,s:`In the lift frame, gravity is replaced by g' (= g + a going up, g \u2212 a going down).
Efflux speed v = \u221A(2g'H) and fall time t = \u221A(2y/g') (hole at height y above the floor).
d = vt = 2\u221A(Hy), which does not depend on g'.
So P, Q (a < g) and R all give d = 1.2 m: P-1, Q-1, R-1.
Free fall: g' = 0, no pressure inside, so no water leaks out: S-4.
Code: P-1, Q-1, R-1, S-4.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"psf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Comprehension: A spray gun has a piston that pushes air out of a nozzle. A thin tube of uniform cross-section is connected to the nozzle. The other end of the tube is in a small liquid container. As the piston pushes air through the nozzle, the liquid from the container rises into the nozzle and is sprayed out. For the spray gun, the radii of the piston and the nozzle are 20 mm and 1 mm respectively. The upper end of the container is open to the atmosphere.
(i) If the piston is pushed at a speed of 5 mm s\u207B\xB9, the air comes out of the nozzle with a speed of
(a) 0.1 m s\u207B\xB9  (b) 1 m s\u207B\xB9  (c) 2 m s\u207B\xB9  (d) 8 m s\u207B\xB9
(ii) If the density of air is \u03C1\u2090 and that of the liquid \u03C1\u2097, then for a given piston speed the rate (volume per unit time) at which the liquid is sprayed will be proportional to
(a) \u221A{\u03C1\u2090;\u03C1\u2097}  (b) \u221A(\u03C1\u2090\u03C1\u2097)  (c) \u221A{\u03C1\u2097;\u03C1\u2090}  (d) \u03C1\u2097`,a:"(i) (c) 2 m/s   (ii) (a) \u221A(\u03C1\u2090/\u03C1\u2097)",s:`(i) Continuity: \u03C0(20)\xB2 \xD7 5 = \u03C0(1)\xB2 \xD7 v, so v = 400 \xD7 5 = 2000 mm/s = 2 m/s.
(ii) Air moving at speed v lowers the pressure at the nozzle by \xBD\u03C1\u2090v\xB2.
This pressure difference pushes the liquid out: \xBD\u03C1\u2097v_l\xB2 = \xBD\u03C1\u2090v\xB2, so v_l = v\u221A(\u03C1\u2090/\u03C1\u2097).
Rate of spraying = (tube area) \xD7 v_l, so it is proportional to \u221A(\u03C1\u2090/\u03C1\u2097).
Answers: (i) (c), (ii) (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: When speed of liquid is zero everywhere, pressure difference at any two points depends on equation P\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082 = \u03C1g(h\u2082 \u2212 h\u2081).
Statement II: In venturi tube 2gh = v\u2081\xB2 \u2212 v\u2082\xB2, where h is the difference in the liquid levels of the two vertical tubes, v\u2081 is the speed in the wide part and v\u2082 the speed in the narrow part.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,fig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"Venturi tube with two vertical open tubes; the liquid level is higher over the wide section than over the narrow section, difference h.",d:[["p","M20,40 H70 C100,40 110,55 140,55 H190 C220,55 230,40 260,40 H310"],["p","M20,110 H70 C100,110 110,95 140,95 H190 C220,95 230,110 260,110 H310"],["l",40,10,40,40],["l",52,10,52,40],["l",155,10,155,55],["l",167,10,167,55],["l",40,24,52,24,"B"],["l",155,38,167,38,"B"],["l",52,24,205,24,"d"],["l",167,38,205,38,"d"],["l",198,24,198,38,"b"],["t",212,35,"h"],["l",25,75,55,75,"a"],["t",40,68,"v\u2081","sm"],["l",125,75,155,75,"a"],["t",140,68,"v\u2082","sm"]]},o:["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],c:0,s:`Statement I: for a liquid at rest, pressure increases with depth, so P\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082 = \u03C1g(h\u2082 \u2212 h\u2081). It is correct.
Statement II: in a venturi tube the speed is larger in the narrow part (v\u2082 > v\u2081).
Pressure head difference: \u03C1gh = \xBD\u03C1(v\u2082\xB2 \u2212 v\u2081\xB2), so 2gh = v\u2082\xB2 \u2212 v\u2081\xB2.
The given relation 2gh = v\u2081\xB2 \u2212 v\u2082\xB2 has the wrong sign, so Statement II is incorrect.
Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"psf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Statement-1: The stream of water flowing at high speed from a garden hose pipe tends to spread like a fountain when held vertically up, but tends to narrow down when held vertically down.
Statement-2: In any steady flow of an incompressible fluid, the volume flow rate of the fluid remains constant.`,o:["Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is a correct explanation for statement-1","Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is not a correct explanation for statement-1","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is false, statement-2 is true"],c:0,s:`Water going up slows down under gravity. By continuity (A \xD7 v = constant), a smaller v means a larger area, so the stream spreads.
Water going down speeds up, so by continuity the area decreases and the stream narrows.
Statement-1 is true. Statement-2 (equation of continuity) is true and it explains Statement-1.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"psf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A tube has two area of cross-sections as shown in figure. The diameters of the tube are 8 mm and 2 mm. Find the range of water falling on horizontal surface, if piston is moving with a constant velocity of 0.25 m/s, h = 1.25 m (g = 10 m/s\xB2).",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"A horizontal tube with a piston in the 8 mm wide part and a 2 mm narrow outlet at height h above the ground; the water leaves horizontally and follows a curved path to the ground.",d:[["l",40,20,130,20],["l",40,60,130,60],["l",130,20,130,36],["l",130,44,130,60],["l",130,36,200,36],["l",130,44,200,44],["l",60,20,60,60,"B"],["l",20,40,60,40],["l",20,30,20,50],["t",20,12,"piston: 0.25 m/s","s"],["t",85,76,"8 mm","sm"],["t",165,60,"2 mm","sm"],["p","M200,40 Q265,40 330,190","d"],["l",150,190,335,190],["l",176,44,176,190,"b"],["t",170,120,"h","e"]]},a:"2 m",s:`Continuity: speed of water at the narrow outlet v = (8/2)\xB2 \xD7 0.25 = 16 \xD7 0.25 = 4 m/s (horizontal).
Time to fall h: t = \u221A(2h/g) = \u221A(2 \xD7 1.25/10) = 0.5 s.
Range = v \xD7 t = 4 \xD7 0.5 = 2 m.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"psf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A non-viscous liquid of constant density 1000 kg/m\xB3 flows in a streamline motion along a tube of variable cross-section. The tube is kept inclined in the vertical plane as shown in figure. The area of cross-section of the tube at two points P and Q at heights of 2 metre and 5 metre are respectively 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2 and 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2. The velocity of the liquid at point P is 1 m/s. Find the work done per unit volume by the pressure and the gravity forces as the fluid flows from point P to Q.",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"A tube inclined in a vertical plane, with end P at height 2 m and wider end Q at height 5 m above the ground line.",d:[["p","M40,170 L205,112 L215,88 L40,150 Z"],["t",18,166,"P"],["t",224,96,"Q"],["l",20,200,300,200],["l",40,160,250,160,"d"],["l",210,100,292,100,"d"],["l",250,160,250,200,"b"],["t",246,184,"2 m","e"],["l",290,100,290,200,"b"],["t",294,152,"5 m"]]},a:"Pressure forces: about 29.6 \xD7 10\xB3 J/m\xB3 (book key: 29 \xD7 10\xB3); gravity: \u221230 \xD7 10\xB3 J/m\xB3",s:`Continuity: v_Q = (4/8) \xD7 1 = 0.5 m/s.
Work-energy theorem per unit volume: W_pressure + W_gravity = \xBD\u03C1(v_Q\xB2 \u2212 v_P\xB2) = 500(0.25 \u2212 1) = \u2212375 J/m\xB3.
Gravity: W_g = \u2212\u03C1g\u0394h = \u22121000 \xD7 10 \xD7 3 = \u221230 \xD7 10\xB3 J/m\xB3.
Pressure: W_p = \u2212375 + 30000 = 29625 J/m\xB3 \u2248 29.6 \xD7 10\xB3 J/m\xB3.
(The printed key gives 29 \xD7 10\xB3; see the note on this question.)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"psf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A large open container of negligible mass and uniform cross-sectional area A has a small hole of cross-sectional area A/100 in its side wall near the bottom. The container is kept on a smooth horizontal floor and contains a liquid of density \u03C1 and mass m\u2080. Assuming that the liquid starts flowing out horizontally through the hole at t = 0, calculate
(i) the acceleration of the container, and
(ii) its velocity when 75% of the liquid has drained out.`,a:"(i) g/50   (ii) \u221A(2gm\u2080/(\u03C1A))",s:`Let the liquid mass left be m, so the depth is h = m/(\u03C1A). Efflux speed (relative to the container) v = \u221A(2gh).
(i) Thrust on the container F = \u03C1(A/100)v\xB2 = \u03C1(A/100) \xD7 2g m/(\u03C1A) = mg/50.
The container is massless, so the moving mass is m: a = F/m = g/50 (constant).
(ii) Mass flow rate: dm/dt = \u2212\u03C1(A/100)\u221A(2gm/(\u03C1A)) = \u2212k\u221Am, where k = (1/100)\u221A(2g\u03C1A).
Then d(\u221Am)/dt = \u2212k/2. For m to fall from m\u2080 to m\u2080/4, \u221Am falls by \u221Am\u2080/2, so t = \u221Am\u2080/k.
v = at = (g/50)\u221Am\u2080 \xD7 100/\u221A(2g\u03C1A) = \u221A(2gm\u2080/(\u03C1A)).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1995",tp:"psf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A container of large uniform cross-sectional area A resting on a horizontal surface holds two immiscible, non-viscous and incompressible liquids of densities d and 2d, each of height H/2. The lower density liquid is open to the atmosphere having pressure P\u2080.
(a) A homogeneous solid cylinder of length L (L < H/2) cross-sectional area A/5 is immersed such that it floats with its axis vertical at the liquid-liquid interface with length L/4 in the denser liquid. Determine
(i) the density D of the solid and
(ii) the total pressure at the bottom of the container.
(b) The cylinder is removed and the original arrangement is restored. A tiny hole of area s (s << A) is punched on the vertical side of the container at a height h (h < H/2). Determine
(i) the initial speed of efflux of the liquid at the hole,
(ii) the horizontal distance x travelled by the liquid initially, and
(iii) the height h\u2098 at which the hole should be punched so that the liquid travels the maximum distance x\u2098 initially. Also calculate x\u2098. (Neglect the air resistance in these calculations.)`,fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"Container of base area A holding liquid d on top (height H/2) and liquid 2d below (height H/2), with a small hole at height h on the right wall; the jet lands a distance x from the wall.",d:[["r",60,105,180,85,"f"],["l",60,20,60,190],["l",240,20,240,190],["l",60,190,240,190],["l",60,20,240,20,"d"],["t",150,66,"d","m"],["t",150,150,"2d","m"],["l",40,20,40,105,"b"],["t",36,66,"H/2","e"],["l",40,105,40,190,"b"],["t",36,150,"H/2","e"],["l",225,165,225,190,"b"],["t",218,182,"h","e"],["p","M240,165 Q275,165 305,190","d"],["l",240,190,335,190],["l",240,204,305,204,"b"],["t",272,218,"x","m"]]},a:"(a)(i) D = 5d/4   (ii) P\u2080 + (6H + L)dg/4   (b)(i) \u221A(g(3H/2 \u2212 2h))   (ii) x = \u221A(h(3H \u2212 4h))   (iii) h\u2098 = 3H/8, x\u2098 = 3H/4",s:`(a)(i) Floating: weight = buoyant force. D\xB7L = (3L/4)\xB7d + (L/4)\xB72d = 5Ld/4, so D = 5d/4.
(a)(ii) The base carries the weight of both liquids and the cylinder: P = P\u2080 + [(H/2)(d + 2d)Ag + D(A/5)Lg]/A = P\u2080 + (3H/2)dg + dgL/4 = P\u2080 + (6H + L)dg/4.
(b)(i) The hole is in the denser liquid. Gauge pressure there = dg(H/2) + 2dg(H/2 \u2212 h) = dg(3H/2 \u2212 2h).
\xBD(2d)v\xB2 = dg(3H/2 \u2212 2h), so v = \u221A(g(3H/2 \u2212 2h)) = \u221A(g(3H \u2212 4h)/2).
(b)(ii) Fall time t = \u221A(2h/g), so x = vt = \u221A(h(3H \u2212 4h)).
(b)(iii) x is maximum when h(3H \u2212 4h) is maximum: 3H \u2212 8h = 0, so h\u2098 = 3H/8 (less than H/2, valid).
x\u2098 = \u221A((3H/8)(3H \u2212 3H/2)) = \u221A(9H\xB2/16) = 3H/4.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"pst",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A barometer made of a very narrow tube is placed at normal temperature and pressure. The coefficient of volume expansion of mercury is 0.00018 per \xB0C and that of the tube is negligible. The temperature of mercury in the barometer is now raised by 1\xB0C, but the temperature of the atmosphere does not change. Then the mercury height in the tube remains unchanged.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`The atmospheric pressure is fixed: P = \u03C1gh = constant.
When mercury is heated, its density falls: \u03C1' = \u03C1/(1 + \u03B3).
So h must increase: h' = h(1 + \u03B3) = h(1 + 0.00018).
The height does not stay unchanged, so the statement is False.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1980",tp:"psp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A man is sitting in a boat which is floating in a pond. If the man drinks some water from the pond, the level of the water in the pond decreases.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`Let the man drink water of weight W.
The boat and man now weigh W more, so they displace extra water of volume W/(\u03C1g), which equals the volume he drank.
The water removed from the pond is exactly replaced by the extra displacement.
So the level stays the same. The statement is False.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"psf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A horizontal pipeline carries water in a streamline flow. At a point along the pipe, where the cross-sectional area is 10 cm\xB2, the water velocity is 1 m s\u207B\xB9 and the pressure is 2000 Pa. The pressure of water at another point where the cross-sectional area is 5 cm\xB2, is ........... Pa. (Density of water = 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3).",n:500,s:`Continuity: v\u2082 = (10/5) \xD7 1 = 2 m/s.
Bernoulli (horizontal): P\u2082 = P\u2081 + \xBD\u03C1(v\u2081\xB2 \u2212 v\u2082\xB2).
P\u2082 = 2000 + \xBD \xD7 1000 \xD7 (1 \u2212 4) = 2000 \u2212 1500 = 500 Pa.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A solid steel ball of diameter 3.6 mm acquired terminal velocity 2.45 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m/s while falling under gravity through an oil of density 925 kg m\u207B\xB3. Take density of steel as 7825 kg m\u207B\xB3 and g as 9.8 m/s\xB2. The viscosity of the oil in SI unit is",o:["1.68","2.38","1.99","2.18"],c:2,s:`Terminal velocity: v = 2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/(9\u03B7), so \u03B7 = 2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/(9v).
r = 1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m, \u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3 = 7825 \u2212 925 = 6900 kg/m\xB3.
\u03B7 = 2 \xD7 (1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 \xD7 6900 \xD7 9.8/(9 \xD7 2.45 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2) = 0.438/0.2205.
\u03B7 \u2248 1.99 Pa\xB7s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A small rigid spherical ball of mass M is dropped in a long vertical tube containing glycerine. The velocity of the ball becomes constant after some time. If the density of glycerine is half of the density of the ball, then the viscous force acting on the ball will be (Consider g as acceleration due to gravity)",o:["Mg","{3;2}Mg","2Mg","{Mg;2}"],c:3,s:`At constant velocity: Mg = buoyancy + viscous force.
Buoyancy = (weight of glycerine displaced) = (\u03C1/2)Vg = Mg/2, because the ball has weight \u03C1Vg = Mg.
Viscous force = Mg \u2212 Mg/2 = Mg/2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`In the experiment of measurement of viscosity '\u03B7' of given liquid with a ball having radius R, consider following statements.
A. Graph between terminal velocity V and R will be a parabola.
B. The terminal velocities of different diameter balls are constant for a given liquid.
C. Measurement of terminal velocity is dependent on the temperature.
D. This experiment can be utilized to assess the density of a given liquid.
E. If balls are dropped with some initial speed, the value of \u03B7 will change.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["C, D and E Only","A, C and D Only","A, B and C Only","B, D and E Only"],c:1,s:`Terminal velocity V = 2R\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/(9\u03B7).
A: V \u221D R\xB2, so the V\u2013R graph is a parabola. True.
B: V depends on R, so it is not constant for different balls. False.
C: \u03B7 depends on temperature, so V does too. True.
D: knowing \u03B7, \u03C1, R and V, the liquid density \u03C3 can be found. True.
E: terminal velocity (and so \u03B7) does not depend on the initial speed. False.
Correct statements: A, C and D.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A small ball of mass m and density \u03C1 is dropped in a viscous liquid of density \u03C1\u2080. After sometime, the ball falls with constant velocity. The viscous force on the ball is",o:["mg(1 \u2212 \u03C1\u03C1\u2080)","mg(1 \u2212 {\u03C1\u2080;\u03C1})","mg(1 + {\u03C1;\u03C1\u2080})","mg({\u03C1\u2080;\u03C1} \u2212 1)"],c:1,s:`At constant velocity: mg = buoyancy + viscous force.
Buoyancy = \u03C1\u2080Vg, and the volume V = m/\u03C1, so buoyancy = mg\u03C1\u2080/\u03C1.
Viscous force = mg \u2212 mg\u03C1\u2080/\u03C1 = mg(1 \u2212 \u03C1\u2080/\u03C1).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A spherical ball of radius 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m and density 10\u2075 kg/m\xB3 falls freely under gravity through a distance h before entering a tank of water. If after entering in water the velocity of the ball does not change, then the value of h is approximately (The coefficient of viscosity of water is 9.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N s/m\xB2)",o:["2249 m","2518 m","2396 m","2296 m"],c:1,s:`The speed does not change in water, so the ball enters at its terminal velocity there.
Buoyancy is negligible (water density 10\xB3 is much less than 10\u2075), so v = 2r\xB2\u03C1g/(9\u03B7).
v = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 9.8/(9 \xD7 9.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 222.2 m/s.
Free fall: v\xB2 = 2gh, so h = v\xB2/2g = (222.2)\xB2/(2 \xD7 9.8) \u2248 2.5 \xD7 10\xB3 m.
Closest option: 2518 m.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A small steel ball is dropped into a long cylinder containing glycerine. Which one of the following is the correct representation of the velocity-time graph for the transit of the ball?",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"Four velocity-time graphs: (a) constant then decreasing, (b) increasing and levelling off, (c) rising then falling to zero, (d) straight line increasing.",d:[["l",20,82,20,14,"a"],["l",18,82,155,82,"a"],["t",160,86,"t","s"],["t",26,18,"v","s"],["p","M20,32 H90 L125,62"],["t",80,102,"(a)","m"],["l",190,82,190,14,"a"],["l",188,82,325,82,"a"],["t",330,86,"t","s"],["t",196,18,"v","s"],["p","M190,82 C215,32 250,32 305,32"],["t",250,102,"(b)","m"],["l",20,182,20,114,"a"],["l",18,182,155,182,"a"],["t",160,186,"t","s"],["t",26,118,"v","s"],["p","M45,182 L75,148 L105,182"],["t",80,202,"(c)","m"],["l",190,182,190,114,"a"],["l",188,182,325,182,"a"],["t",330,186,"t","s"],["t",196,118,"v","s"],["l",190,182,305,127],["t",250,202,"(d)","m"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:1,s:`Starting from rest, the ball speeds up: weight is larger than buoyancy plus viscous force.
Viscous force grows with speed, so the acceleration decreases steadily.
When viscous force + buoyancy = weight, acceleration is zero and the speed becomes constant (terminal velocity).
So the v\u2013t graph rises with decreasing slope and flattens: graph (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A small spherical ball of radius r, falling through a viscous medium of negligible density has terminal velocity 'v'. Another ball of the same mass but of radius 2r, falling through the same viscous medium will have terminal velocity",o:["{v;4}","{v;2}","2v","4v"],c:1,s:`Medium density is negligible, so at terminal velocity mg = 6\u03C0\u03B7rv, giving v = mg/(6\u03C0\u03B7r).
Same mass and same medium: v \u221D 1/r.
Radius doubles, so the terminal velocity becomes v/2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"Eight equal drops of water are falling through air with a steady speed of 10 cm/s. If the drops coalesce, the new velocity is",o:["16 cm/s","40 cm/s","10 cm/s","5 cm/s"],c:1,s:`Volume is conserved: 8 \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3 = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3, so R = 2r.
Terminal velocity v \u221D R\xB2 (v = 2R\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/9\u03B7).
New velocity = 4 \xD7 10 = 40 cm/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"The terminal velocity (v\u209C) of the spherical rain drop depends on the radius (r) of the spherical rain drop as",o:["r^(1/2)","r","r\xB2","r\xB3"],c:2,s:`At terminal velocity: weight \u2212 buoyancy = 6\u03C0\u03B7rv\u209C.
The left side is proportional to r\xB3, so r\xB3 \u221D r\xB7v\u209C.
Therefore v\u209C \u221D r\xB2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"The velocity of a small ball of mass 'm' and density d\u2081, when dropped in a container filled with glycerine, becomes constant after some time. If the density of glycerine is d\u2082, then the viscous force acting on the ball will be",o:["mg(1 \u2212 {d\u2081;d\u2082})","mg(1 \u2212 {d\u2082;d\u2081})","mg({d\u2081;d\u2082} \u2212 1)","mg({d\u2082;d\u2081} \u2212 1)"],c:1,s:`At constant velocity: mg = buoyancy + viscous force.
Volume V = m/d\u2081, so buoyancy = d\u2082Vg = mg(d\u2082/d\u2081).
Viscous force = mg \u2212 mg(d\u2082/d\u2081) = mg(1 \u2212 d\u2082/d\u2081).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A water drop of radius 1 \u03BCm falls in a situation where the effect of buoyant force is negligible. Co-efficient of viscosity of air is 1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N s m\u207B\xB2 and its density is negligible as compared to that of water 10\u2076 g m\u207B\xB3. Terminal velocity of the water drop is (Take acceleration due to gravity = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["145.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9","118.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9","132.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9","123.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9"],c:3,s:`Terminal velocity: v = 2r\xB2\u03C1g/(9\u03B7).
r = 10\u207B\u2076 m, \u03C1 = 10\u2076 g/m\xB3 = 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3.
v = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 10/(9 \xD7 1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078/(1.62 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074).
v \u2248 1.234 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m/s = 123.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:`A raindrop with radius R = 0.2 mm falls from a cloud at a height h = 2000 m above the ground. Assume that the drop is spherical throughout its falls and the force of buoyancy may be neglected, then the terminal speed attained by the raindrop is
[Density of water, \u03C1w = 1000 kg m\u207B\xB3 and Density of air \u03C1a = 1.2 kg m\u207B\xB3, g = 10 m/s\xB2, Coefficient of viscosity of air = 1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N s m\u207B\xB2]`,o:["14.4 m s\u207B\xB9","43.56 m s\u207B\xB9","250.6 m s\u207B\xB9","4.94 m s\u207B\xB9"],c:3,s:`Neglect buoyancy: weight = viscous force, so (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3\u03C1w g = 6\u03C0\u03B7Rv.
v = 2R\xB2\u03C1w g/(9\u03B7) = 2 \xD7 (2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074)\xB2 \xD7 1000 \xD7 10/(9 \xD7 1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075).
v = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074/(1.62 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) \u2248 4.94 m/s.
(The drop reaches this speed long before it covers 2000 m.)`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Viscous force and Reynolds number",q:"What will be the nature of flow of water from a circular tap, when its flow rate increased from 0.18 L/min to 0.48 L/min? The radius of the tap and viscosity of water are 0.5 cm and 10\u207B\xB3 Pa s, respectively. (Density of water: 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3)",o:["Remains turbulent flow","Steady flow to unsteady flow","Unsteady to steady flow","Remains steady flow"],c:1,s:`Reynolds number Re = \u03C1vd/\u03B7 with v = Q/(\u03C0r\xB2), d = 0.01 m.
r = 0.5 cm gives area = 7.85 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2.
Q = 0.18 L/min = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3/s: v = 0.038 m/s, Re \u2248 382. This is below 1000: steady flow.
Q = 0.48 L/min = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3/s: v = 0.102 m/s, Re \u2248 1019. This is between 1000 and 2000: unsteady flow.
The flow changes from steady to unsteady.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Viscous force and Reynolds number",q:"Water from a pipe is coming at a rate of 100 liters per minute. If the radius of the pipe is 5 cm, the Reynolds number for the flow is of the order of (density of water = 1000 kg m\u207B\xB3, coefficient of viscosity of water = 1 mPa s.)",o:["10\xB2","10\u2074","10\xB3","10\u2076"],c:1,s:`Flow rate Q = 100 L/min = 100 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/60 = 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3/s.
Speed v = Q/(\u03C0r\xB2) = 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/(\u03C0 \xD7 0.0025) = 0.212 m/s.
Re = \u03C1vd/\u03B7 = 1000 \xD7 0.212 \xD7 0.1/10\u207B\xB3 \u2248 2.1 \xD7 10\u2074.
This is of the order of 10\u2074.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A solid sphere, of radius R acquires a terminal velocity v\u2081 when falling (due to gravity) through a viscous fluid having a coefficient of viscosity \u03B7. The sphere is broken into 27 identical solid spheres. If each of these spheres acquires a terminal velocity, v\u2082, when falling through the same fluid, the ratio (v\u2081/v\u2082) equals",o:["1/27","1/9","9","27"],c:2,s:`Volume is conserved: 27 \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3 = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3, so r = R/3.
Terminal velocity v \u221D (radius)\xB2.
v\u2081/v\u2082 = R\xB2/r\xB2 = 3\xB2 = 9.`},{y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"psv",st:"Viscous force and Reynolds number",q:"Two tubes of radii r\u2081 and r\u2082, and lengths l\u2081 and l\u2082, respectively, are connected in series and a liquid flows through each of them in streamline conditions. P\u2081 and P\u2082 are pressure differences across the two tubes. If P\u2082 is 4P\u2081 and l\u2082 is {l\u2081;4}, then the radius r\u2082 will be equal to",o:["2r\u2081","{r\u2081;2}","4r\u2081","r\u2081"],c:1,s:`Series connection: the same volume flow rate Q flows through both tubes.
Poiseuille: Q = \u03C0Pr\u2074/(8\u03B7l), so Pr\u2074/l is the same for both.
P\u2081r\u2081\u2074/l\u2081 = P\u2082r\u2082\u2074/l\u2082 = 4P\u2081r\u2082\u2074/(l\u2081/4) = 16P\u2081r\u2082\u2074/l\u2081.
So r\u2081\u2074 = 16r\u2082\u2074, giving r\u2082 = r\u2081/2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"Which of the following options correctly describes the variation of the speed v and acceleration a of a point mass falling vertically in a viscous medium that applies a force F = \u2212kv, where k is a constant, on the body? (Graphs are schematic and not drawn to scale)",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"Four graphs of speed v and acceleration a against time t. In (c) v rises and levels off while a falls towards zero.",d:[["l",20,82,20,14,"a"],["l",18,82,155,82,"a"],["t",160,86,"t","s"],["p","M20,80 C45,32 80,30 132,30"],["p","M20,22 C30,65 60,74 132,74"],["t",136,34,"a","s"],["t",136,72,"v","s"],["t",80,102,"(a)","m"],["l",190,82,190,14,"a"],["l",188,82,325,82,"a"],["t",330,86,"t","s"],["p","M190,80 C235,72 255,50 280,20"],["p","M190,22 C200,65 230,74 302,74"],["t",283,22,"v","s"],["t",270,68,"a","s"],["t",250,102,"(b)","m"],["l",20,182,20,114,"a"],["l",18,182,155,182,"a"],["t",160,186,"t","s"],["p","M20,180 C45,132 80,130 132,130"],["p","M20,122 C30,165 60,174 132,174"],["t",136,134,"v","s"],["t",136,172,"a","s"],["t",80,202,"(c)","m"],["l",190,182,190,114,"a"],["l",188,182,325,182,"a"],["t",330,186,"t","s"],["p","M190,180 C215,132 250,130 302,130"],["l",190,122,295,176],["t",306,134,"v","s"],["t",300,170,"a","s"],["t",250,202,"(d)","m"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:2,s:`Equation of motion: ma = mg \u2212 kv, so a = g \u2212 kv/m.
At t = 0, v = 0 and a = g (maximum). As v grows, a falls.
Finally a \u2192 0 and v \u2192 terminal value mg/k, so v increases and levels off.
Graph (c) shows v rising to a limit while a decays to zero, and the curves are labelled correctly.`},{y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"psv",st:"Viscous force and Reynolds number",q:"If it takes 5 minutes to fill a 15 litre bucket from a water tap of diameter {2;\u221A\u03C0} cm then the Reynolds number for the flow is (density of water = 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3 and viscosity of water = 10\u207B\xB3 Pa s) close to",o:["5500","11,000","550","1100"],c:0,s:`Flow rate Q = 15 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3/300 s = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB3/s.
Tap area = \u03C0d\xB2/4 = \u03C0 \xD7 (4/\u03C0)/4 cm\xB2 = 1 cm\xB2 = 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2.
Speed v = Q/A = 0.5 m/s. Diameter d = 2/\u221A\u03C0 cm = 1.13 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m.
Re = \u03C1vd/\u03B7 = 10\xB3 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 1.13 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2/10\u207B\xB3 \u2248 5640.
Closest option: 5500.`},{y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"In an experiment, a small steel ball falls through a liquid at a constant speed of 10 cm/s. If the steel ball is pulled upward with a force equal to twice its effective weight, how fast will it move upward?",o:["5 cm/s","Zero","10 cm/s","20 cm/s"],c:2,s:`Effective weight W' = weight \u2212 buoyancy.
Falling at constant speed: viscous force = W' = k \xD7 10 (k = 6\u03C0\u03B7r).
Pulled up with force 2W': net upward force = 2W' \u2212 W' = W' (effective weight acts down), and viscous force acts down too.
At constant upward speed v: 2W' = W' + kv, so kv = W' = k \xD7 10.
v = 10 cm/s.`},{y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A spherical solid ball of volume V is made of a material of density \u03C1\u2081. It is falling through a liquid of density \u03C1\u2082 (\u03C1\u2082 < \u03C1\u2081). Assume that the liquid applies a viscous force on the ball that is proportional to the square of its speed v, i.e., F_viscous = \u2212kv\xB2 (k > 0). The terminal speed of the ball is",o:["{Vg(\u03C1\u2081 \u2212 \u03C1\u2082);k}","\u221A{Vg(\u03C1\u2081 \u2212 \u03C1\u2082);k}","{Vg\u03C1\u2081;k}","\u221A{Vg\u03C1\u2081;k}"],c:1,s:`At terminal speed the net force is zero: weight = buoyancy + viscous force.
V\u03C1\u2081g = V\u03C1\u2082g + kv\xB2.
kv\xB2 = Vg(\u03C1\u2081 \u2212 \u03C1\u2082), so v = \u221A(Vg(\u03C1\u2081 \u2212 \u03C1\u2082)/k).`},{y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"If the terminal speed of a sphere of gold (density = 19.5 g/cm\xB3) is 0.2 m/s in a viscous liquid (density = 1.5 g/cm\xB3) find the terminal speed of a sphere of silver (density 10.5 g/cm\xB3) of the same size in the same liquid",o:["0.2 m/s","0.4 m/s","0.133 m/s","0.1 m/s"],c:3,s:`Terminal speed v = 2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/(9\u03B7). For the same size and liquid, v \u221D (\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3).
Gold: 19.5 \u2212 1.5 = 18. Silver: 10.5 \u2212 1.5 = 9.
v(silver) = 0.2 \xD7 9/18 = 0.1 m/s.`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"Spherical balls of radius R are falling in a viscous fluid of viscosity \u03B7 with a velocity v. The retarding viscous force acting on the spherical ball is",o:["directly proportional to R but inversely proportional to v","directly proportional to both radius R and velocity v","inversely proportional to both radius R and velocity v","inversely proportional to R but directly proportional to velocity v."],c:1,s:`Stokes' law: F = 6\u03C0\u03B7Rv.
So F is directly proportional to both the radius R and the velocity v.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:`A table tennis ball has radius (3/2) \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m and mass (22/7) \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 kg. It is slowly pushed down into a swimming pool to a depth of d = 0.7 m below the water surface and then released from rest. It emerges from the water surface at speed v, without getting wet, and rises up to a height H. Which of the following option(s) is(are) correct?
[Given: \u03C0 = 22/7, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2, density of water = 1 \xD7 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3, viscosity of water = 1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 Pa\xB7s.]`,o:["The work done in pushing the ball to the depth d is 0.077 J.","If we neglect the viscous force in water, then the speed v = 7 m/s.","If we neglect the viscous force in water, then the height H = 1.4 m.","The ratio of the magnitudes of the net force excluding the viscous force to the maximum viscous force in water is 500/9."],c:[0,1,3],s:`Volume V = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3 = (4/3)(22/7)(1.5\xD710\u207B\xB2)\xB3 = 1.414\xD710\u207B\u2075 m\xB3.
Buoyant force = \u03C1Vg = 0.1414 N and weight = mg = 0.0314 N, so the net upward force (excluding viscosity) = 0.110 N.
(a) Pushing slowly through 0.7 m: work = 0.110 \xD7 0.7 = 0.077 J. True.
(b) With no viscous force the ball gains KE = 0.077 J at the surface: \xBDmv\xB2 = 0.077 gives v\xB2 = 49, v = 7 m/s. True.
(c) Above water only gravity acts: H = v\xB2/2g = 49/20 = 2.45 m, not 1.4 m. False.
(d) Maximum viscous force = 6\u03C0\u03B7rv = 6(22/7)(10\u207B\xB3)(1.5\xD710\u207B\xB2)(7) = 1.98\xD710\u207B\xB3 N. Ratio = 0.110/(1.98\xD710\u207B\xB3) = 500/9. True.
Correct options: (a), (b), (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"psv",st:"Viscous force and Reynolds number",q:"Consider a thin square plate floating on a viscous liquid in a large tank. The height h of the liquid in the tank is much less than the width of the tank. The floating plate is pulled horizontally with a constant velocity u\u2080. Which of the following statements is (are) true?",o:["The resistive force of liquid on the plate is inversely proportional to h","The resistive force of liquid on the plate is independent of the area of the plate","The tangential (shear) stress on the floor of the tank increases with u\u2080","The tangential (shear) stress on the plate varies linearly with the viscosity \u03B7 of the liquid."],c:[0,2,3],s:`The liquid layer of thickness h has velocity u\u2080 at the plate and 0 at the floor, so the velocity gradient is u\u2080/h.
Shear stress = \u03B7 u\u2080/h, and resistive force F = \u03B7Au\u2080/h.
(a) F \u221D 1/h: true.
(b) F \u221D A (area of plate): so (b) is false.
(c) Stress on the floor = \u03B7u\u2080/h grows with u\u2080: true.
(d) Stress on the plate = \u03B7u\u2080/h is linear in \u03B7: true.
Correct options: (a), (c), (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"psv",st:"Viscous force and Reynolds number",q:"A flat plate is moving normal to its plane through a gas under the action of a constant force F. The gas is kept at a very low pressure. The speed of the plate v is much less than the average speed u of the gas molecules. Which of the following options is/are true?",o:["At a later time the external force F balances the resistive force","The plate will continue to move with constant non-zero acceleration, at all times","The resistive force experienced by the plate is proportional to v","The pressure difference between the leading and trailing faces of the plate is proportional to uv."],c:[0,2,3],s:`At very low pressure the molecules hit the plate without colliding with each other.
The front face meets molecules with relative speed about (u+v) and the back face about (u\u2212v), so the pressure difference is proportional to uv (option d) and the resistive force is proportional to v (option c).
As v grows the resistive force grows until it equals F. Then the net force is zero and the plate moves with constant speed, so (a) is true and (b) is false.
Correct options: (a), (c), (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"Two spheres P and Q of equal radii have densities \u03C1\u2081 and \u03C1\u2082, respectively. The spheres are connected by a massless string and placed in liquids L\u2081 and L\u2082 of densities \u03C3\u2081 and \u03C3\u2082 and viscosities \u03B7\u2081 and \u03B7\u2082, respectively. L\u2081 lies above L\u2082. They float in equilibrium with the sphere P in L\u2081 and sphere Q in L\u2082 and the string being taut. If sphere P alone in L\u2082 has terminal velocity V\u20D7_P and Q alone in L\u2081 has terminal velocity V\u20D7_Q, then",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A tank with liquid L1 above liquid L2; sphere P floats in L1 and sphere Q in L2, joined by a taut string through the interface",d:[["p","M110 15 V185 H230 V15"],["l",110,100,230,100,"d"],["c",170,62,13],["c",170,142,13],["l",170,75,170,129],["t",188,66,"P"],["t",188,146,"Q"],["t",238,50,"L\u2081"],["t",238,66,"\u03C3\u2081, \u03B7\u2081","s"],["t",238,140,"L\u2082"],["t",238,156,"\u03C3\u2082, \u03B7\u2082","s"]]},o:["|V\u20D7_P| / |V\u20D7_Q| = \u03B7\u2081/\u03B7\u2082","|V\u20D7_P| / |V\u20D7_Q| = \u03B7\u2082/\u03B7\u2081","V\u20D7_P \xB7 V\u20D7_Q > 0","V\u20D7_P \xB7 V\u20D7_Q < 0"],c:[0,3],s:`Let each sphere have volume V and let the string tension be T (T > 0).
Equilibrium of P: T + \u03C1\u2081Vg = \u03C3\u2081Vg. Equilibrium of Q: T + \u03C3\u2082Vg = \u03C1\u2082Vg. So \u03C3\u2081 \u2212 \u03C1\u2081 = \u03C1\u2082 \u2212 \u03C3\u2082, i.e. \u03C3\u2081 + \u03C3\u2082 = \u03C1\u2081 + \u03C1\u2082.
P alone in L\u2082: net upward force (\u03C3\u2082 \u2212 \u03C1\u2081)Vg, so P moves up with |V_P| = (\u03C3\u2082 \u2212 \u03C1\u2081)Vg/(6\u03C0\u03B7\u2082r).
Q alone in L\u2081: net downward force (\u03C1\u2082 \u2212 \u03C3\u2081)Vg, so Q moves down with |V_Q| = (\u03C1\u2082 \u2212 \u03C3\u2081)Vg/(6\u03C0\u03B7\u2081r).
But \u03C3\u2082 \u2212 \u03C1\u2081 = \u03C1\u2082 \u2212 \u03C3\u2081, so |V_P|/|V_Q| = \u03B7\u2081/\u03B7\u2082. The velocities are opposite, so V\u20D7_P \xB7 V\u20D7_Q < 0.
Correct options: (a) and (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"Small water droplets of radius 0.01 cm are formed in the upper atmosphere and falling with a terminal velocity of 10 cm/s. Due to condensation, if 8 such droplets are coalesced and formed a larger drop, the new terminal velocity will be ___ cm/s.",n:40,s:`Terminal velocity v = 2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/(9\u03B7), so v \u221D r\xB2.
Volume is conserved: 8 \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3 = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3, so R = 2r.
v\u2032/v = (R/r)\xB2 = 4.
v\u2032 = 4 \xD7 10 = 40 cm/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"An air bubble of diameter 6 mm rises steadily through a solution of density 1750 kg/m\xB3 at the rate of 0.35 cm/s. The co-efficient of viscosity of the solution (neglect density of air) is ___ Pa s. (Given, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",n:10,s:`The bubble rises at terminal velocity: v = 2r\xB2(\u03C3 \u2212 \u03C1_air)g/(9\u03B7) \u2248 2r\xB2\u03C3g/(9\u03B7).
r = 3 mm = 3\xD710\u207B\xB3 m, v = 0.35 cm/s = 3.5\xD710\u207B\xB3 m/s.
\u03B7 = 2r\xB2\u03C3g/(9v) = 2 \xD7 9\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 1750 \xD7 10/(9 \xD7 3.5\xD710\u207B\xB3) = 0.315/0.0315.
\u03B7 = 10 Pa s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A spherical ball of radius 1 mm and density 10.5 g/cc is dropped in glycerine of coefficient of viscosity 9.8 poise and density 1.5 g/cc. Viscous force on the ball when it attains constant velocity is 3696 \xD7 10\u207B\u02E3 N. The value of x is ___. (Given, g = 9.8 m/s\xB2 and \u03C0 = 22/7)",n:7,s:`At constant (terminal) velocity the viscous force equals weight minus buoyancy.
F = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g = (4/3)(22/7)(10\u207B\xB3)\xB3 \xD7 (10500 \u2212 1500) \xD7 9.8.
F = 4.19\xD710\u207B\u2079 \xD7 9000 \xD7 9.8 = 3.696\xD710\u207B\u2074 N = 3696 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N.
So x = 7.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Viscous force and Reynolds number",q:"A metal block of base area 0.20 m\xB2 is placed on a table. A liquid film of thickness 0.25 mm is inserted between the block and the table. The block is pushed by a horizontal force of 0.1 N and moves with a constant speed. If the viscosity of the liquid is 5.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 Pl, the speed of block is ___ \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m/s. (Given, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",n:25,s:`Constant speed means the pushing force equals the viscous force: F = \u03B7Av/h.
v = Fh/(\u03B7A) = 0.1 \xD7 0.25\xD710\u207B\xB3 /(5\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 0.20).
v = 2.5\xD710\u207B\u2075/10\u207B\xB3 = 2.5\xD710\u207B\xB2 m/s = 25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:`A small spherical ball of radius 0.1 mm and density 10\u2074 kg m\u207B\xB3 falls freely under gravity through a distance h before entering a tank of water. If, after entering the water the velocity of ball does not change and it continues to fall with same constant velocity inside water, then the value of h will be ___ m.
(Given g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2, viscosity of water = 1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N-s m\u207B\xB2)`,n:20,s:`Inside water the ball falls at its terminal velocity: v = 2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/(9\u03B7), with \u03C3 = 1000 kg/m\xB3 for water.
v = 2 \xD7 (10\u207B\u2074)\xB2 \xD7 9000 \xD7 10/(9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075) = 1.8\xD710\u207B\xB3/(9\xD710\u207B\u2075) = 20 m/s.
Before entering water the ball falls freely from rest, so v\xB2 = 2gh.
h = v\xB2/2g = 400/20 = 20 m.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"The diameter of an air bubble which was initially 2 mm, rises steadily through a solution of density 1750 kg m\u207B\xB3 at the rate of 0.35 cm s\u207B\xB9. The coefficient of viscosity of the solution is ___ poise (in nearest integer). (The density of air is negligible.)",n:11,s:`The bubble moves at terminal velocity: v = 2r\xB2\u03C3g/(9\u03B7) (air density neglected).
r = 1 mm = 10\u207B\xB3 m, v = 3.5\xD710\u207B\xB3 m/s, \u03C3 = 1750 kg/m\xB3, g = 10 m/s\xB2.
\u03B7 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 1750 \xD7 10/(9 \xD7 3.5\xD710\u207B\xB3) = 0.035/0.0315 = 1.11 Pa s.
1 Pa s = 10 poise, so \u03B7 = 11.1 poise \u2248 11 poise.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psv",st:"Viscous force and Reynolds number",q:"The velocity of upper layer of water in a river is 36 km h\u207B\xB9. Shearing stress between horizontal layers of water is 10\u207B\xB3 N m\u207B\xB2. Depth of the river is ___ m. (Coefficient of viscosity of water is 10\u207B\xB2 Pa.s)",n:100,s:`Shear stress = \u03B7 \xD7 (velocity gradient) = \u03B7 v/h (speed is zero at the river bed).
v = 36 km/h = 10 m/s.
h = \u03B7v/stress = 10\u207B\xB2 \xD7 10/10\u207B\xB3 = 100 m.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"Consider two solid spheres P and Q each of density 8 gm cm\u207B\xB3 and diameters 1 cm and 0.5 cm, respectively. Sphere P is dropped into a liquid of density 0.8 gm cm\u207B\xB3 and viscosity \u03B7 = 3 poiseuille. Sphere Q is dropped into a liquid of density 1.6 gm cm\u207B\xB3 and viscosity \u03B7 = 2 poiseuille. The ratio of the terminal velocities of P and Q is ___.",n:3,s:`Terminal velocity v = 2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/(9\u03B7), so v \u221D r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)/\u03B7.
For P: r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)/\u03B7 \u221D (0.5)\xB2 \xD7 (8 \u2212 0.8)/3 = 0.25 \xD7 7.2/3 = 0.6.
For Q: (0.25)\xB2 \xD7 (8 \u2212 1.6)/2 = 0.0625 \xD7 6.4/2 = 0.2.
Ratio v_P : v_Q = 0.6/0.2 = 3.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"psv",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Five systems of two objects labelled X and Y are described in Column II. In each case a point P is marked. Column I gives statements about X and/or Y. Match these statements to the appropriate system(s) from Column II.
Column I
(A) The force exerted by X on Y has a magnitude Mg.
(B) The gravitational potential energy of X is continuously increasing.
(C) Mechanical energy of the system X + Y is continuously decreasing.
(D) The torque of the weight of Y about point P is zero.
Column II
(p) Block Y of mass M left on a fixed inclined plane X, slides on it with a constant velocity.
(q) Two ring magnets Y and Z, each of mass M, are kept in frictionless vertical plastic stand so that they repel each other. Y rests on the base X and Z hangs in air in equilibrium. P is the topmost point of the stand on the common axis of the two rings. The whole system is in a lift that is going up with a constant velocity.
(r) A pulley Y of mass m\u2080 is fixed to a table through a clamp X. A block of mass M hangs from a string that goes over the pulley and is fixed at point P of the table. The whole system is kept in a lift that is going down with a constant velocity.
(s) A sphere Y of mass M is put in a nonviscous liquid X kept in a container at rest. The sphere is released and it moves down in the liquid.
(t) A sphere Y of mass M is falling with its terminal velocity in a viscous liquid X kept in a container.`,a:"A\u2192(p,t); B\u2192(q,s,t); C\u2192(p,r,t); D\u2192(q)",s:`(A) Force by X on Y equals Mg when Y moves with constant velocity (zero net force): in (p) the incline pushes and rubs on Y with total force Mg, and in (t) the liquid drag and buoyancy together equal Mg. In (s) Y accelerates, so no. In (q) X supports both Y and Z, so the force is 2Mg. A \u2192 (p, t).
(B) In (q) X (the base) rises with the lift, so its potential energy increases. In (s) and (t) the sphere Y sinks in the liquid X, so the liquid is pushed up and the centre of mass of X rises continuously: its potential energy increases. B \u2192 (q, s, t).
(C) Mechanical energy decreases when energy is lost to friction or viscosity or when the system goes down: (p) Y slides down with constant KE and friction acts, (r) the whole system moves down in the lift, (t) viscous drag dissipates energy. In (s) a nonviscous liquid loses none; in (q) the system goes up. C \u2192 (p, r, t).
(D) Weight of Y acts along the vertical axis through P in (q), so its torque about P is zero. In the other systems P is not on the line of the weight. D \u2192 (q).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:`A small spherical monoatomic ideal gas bubble (\u03B3 = 5/3) is trapped inside a liquid of density \u03C1\u2097. Assume that the bubble does not exchange any heat with the liquid. The bubble contains n moles of gas. The temperature of the gas when the bubble is at the bottom is T\u2080, the height of the liquid is H and the atmospheric pressure is P\u2080 (neglect surface tension). The bubble is at height y above the bottom of the liquid.
As the bubble moves upwards, besides the buoyancy force the following forces are acting on it.`,o:["Only the force of gravity","The force due to gravity and the force due to the pressure of the liquid","The force due to gravity, the force due to the pressure of the liquid and the force due to viscosity of the liquid.","The force due to gravity and the force due to viscosity of the liquid."],c:3,s:`The buoyancy force is itself the net force due to the pressure of the liquid on the bubble.
So the pressure force is already counted as buoyancy and must not be counted again.
The other forces on a moving bubble are its weight (gravity) and the viscous drag of the liquid.
The answer is (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`A small spherical monoatomic ideal gas bubble (\u03B3 = 5/3) is trapped inside a liquid of density \u03C1\u2097. Assume that the bubble does not exchange any heat with the liquid. The bubble contains n moles of gas. The temperature of the gas when the bubble is at the bottom is T\u2080, the height of the liquid is H and the atmospheric pressure is P\u2080 (neglect surface tension). The bubble is at height y above the bottom of the liquid.
When the gas bubble is at a height y from the bottom, its temperature is`,o:["T\u2080 ({P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097gH;P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097gy})^(2/5)","T\u2080 ({P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097g(H\u2212y);P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097gH})^(2/5)","T\u2080 ({P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097gH;P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097gy})^(3/5)","T\u2080 ({P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097g(H\u2212y);P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097gH})^(3/5)"],c:1,s:`The bubble does not exchange heat, so the change is adiabatic: T^\u03B3 P^(1\u2212\u03B3) = constant, i.e. T \u221D P^((\u03B3\u22121)/\u03B3).
For \u03B3 = 5/3, (\u03B3 \u2212 1)/\u03B3 = 2/5, so T \u221D P^(2/5).
Pressure at the bottom = P\u2080 + \u03C1\u2097gH and at height y (depth H \u2212 y) = P\u2080 + \u03C1\u2097g(H \u2212 y).
T = T\u2080 [(P\u2080 + \u03C1\u2097g(H \u2212 y))/(P\u2080 + \u03C1\u2097gH)]^(2/5).
The answer is (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"psp",st:"Buoyancy and floating",q:`A small spherical monoatomic ideal gas bubble (\u03B3 = 5/3) is trapped inside a liquid of density \u03C1\u2097. Assume that the bubble does not exchange any heat with the liquid. The bubble contains n moles of gas. The temperature of the gas when the bubble is at the bottom is T\u2080, the height of the liquid is H and the atmospheric pressure is P\u2080 (neglect surface tension). The bubble is at height y above the bottom of the liquid.
The buoyancy force acting on the gas bubble is (assume R is the universal gas constant)`,o:["\u03C1\u2097nRgT\u2080 {(P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097gH)^(2/5);(P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097gy)^(7/5)}","{\u03C1\u2097nRgT\u2080;(P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097gH)^(2/5) [P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097g(H\u2212y)]^(3/5)}","\u03C1\u2097nRgT\u2080 {(P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097gH)^(3/5);(P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097gy)^(8/5)}","{\u03C1\u2097nRgT\u2080;(P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097gH)^(3/5) [P\u2080+\u03C1\u2097g(H\u2212y)]^(2/5)}"],c:1,s:`Buoyancy force = \u03C1\u2097Vg, where V = nRT/P is the volume of the bubble at height y.
Here P = P\u2080 + \u03C1\u2097g(H \u2212 y) and, from the previous part, T = T\u2080 P^(2/5)/P_b^(2/5) with P_b = P\u2080 + \u03C1\u2097gH.
V = nRT\u2080 P^(2/5)/(P_b^(2/5) P) = nRT\u2080/(P_b^(2/5) P^(3/5)).
F = \u03C1\u2097nRgT\u2080/{(P\u2080 + \u03C1\u2097gH)^(2/5) [P\u2080 + \u03C1\u2097g(H \u2212 y)]^(3/5)}.
The answer is (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psv",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A : A spherical body of radius (5 \xB1 0.1) mm having a particular density is falling through a liquid of constant density. The percentage error in the calculation of its terminal velocity is 4%.
Reason R : The terminal velocity of the spherical body falling through the liquid is inversely proportional to its radius.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A is false but R is true.","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A."],c:2,s:`Terminal velocity v = 2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/(9\u03B7), so v \u221D r\xB2, not 1/r. Reason R is false.
Fractional error in v = 2 \xD7 fractional error in r = 2 \xD7 (0.1/5) = 0.04 = 4%. Assertion A is true.
So A is true but R is false: option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A small sphere falls from rest in a viscous liquid. Due to friction, heat is produced. Find the relation between the rate of production of heat and the radius of the sphere at terminal velocity.",a:"Rate of heat production = (8\u03C0/27)(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)\xB2 g\xB2 r\u2075/\u03B7, so it is proportional to r\u2075",s:`At terminal velocity the kinetic energy does not change, so the heat produced per second equals the power of the viscous force: P = F v = 6\u03C0\u03B7rv \xD7 v = 6\u03C0\u03B7r v\xB2.
Terminal velocity v = 2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g/(9\u03B7), where \u03C1 is the density of the sphere and \u03C3 that of the liquid.
P = 6\u03C0\u03B7r \xD7 4r\u2074(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)\xB2g\xB2/(81\u03B7\xB2) = (8\u03C0/27)(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)\xB2 g\xB2 r\u2075/\u03B7.
Therefore the rate of heat production is proportional to r\u2075.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"psv",st:"Viscous force and Reynolds number",q:"Shown in the figure is a container whose top and bottom diameters are D and d respectively. At the bottom of the container, there is a capillary tube of outer radius b and inner radius a and length d. The liquid in the container has height h. The volume flow rate in the capillary is Q. If the capillary is removed, the liquid comes out with a velocity v\u2080. The density of the liquid is given as \u03C1. Calculate the co-efficient of viscosity \u03B7.",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A container wide at the top (diameter D) and narrowing to diameter d at the bottom, liquid depth h, with a horizontal capillary tube of length d leaving at the bottom",d:[["l",90,12,250,12,"b"],["t",170,12,"D","m"],["p","M90 20 V70 L140 125 V150"],["p","M250 20 V70 L200 125 V132"],["p","M200 146 V150"],["l",140,150,200,150],["l",200,132,290,132],["l",200,146,290,146],["l",200,118,290,118,"b"],["t",245,112,"length d","ms"],["l",290,139,326,139,"a"],["t",330,143,"Q"],["l",70,20,70,150,"b"],["t",62,88,"h","e"],["l",140,162,200,162,"b"],["t",170,178,"d","m"],["l",90,30,250,30,"d"],["t",170,60,"\u03C1","m"],["t",170,46,"P","m"]]},a:"\u03B7 = \u03C0\u03C1a\u2074v\u2080\xB2 (1 \u2212 d\u2074/D\u2074) / (16 Q d)",s:`Without the capillary, the liquid leaves the bottom opening (diameter d) with speed v\u2080 while the top surface (diameter D) moves with v\u2080(d/D)\xB2. Bernoulli: \u03C1gh + \xBD\u03C1v\u2080\xB2(d/D)\u2074 = \xBD\u03C1v\u2080\xB2, so \u03C1gh = \xBD\u03C1v\u2080\xB2(1 \u2212 d\u2074/D\u2074).
With the capillary, the flow is slow, so the pressure difference across it is \u0394P = \u03C1gh = \xBD\u03C1v\u2080\xB2(1 \u2212 d\u2074/D\u2074).
Poiseuille formula for a tube of inner radius a and length d: Q = \u03C0\u0394P a\u2074/(8\u03B7d).
\u03B7 = \u03C0a\u2074\u0394P/(8Qd) = \u03C0\u03C1a\u2074v\u2080\xB2(1 \u2212 d\u2074/D\u2074)/(16Qd).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"Two water drops each of radius 'r' coalesce to form a bigger drop. If 'T' is the surface tension, the surface energy released in this process is",o:["4\u03C0r\xB2T[2 \u2212 2^(1/3)]","4\u03C0r\xB2T[\u221A2 \u2212 1]","4\u03C0r\xB2T[1 + \u221A2]","4\u03C0r\xB2T[2 \u2212 2^(2/3)]"],c:3,s:`Volume is conserved: 2 \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3 = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3, so R = 2^(1/3) r.
Initial surface area = 2 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2; final area = 4\u03C0R\xB2 = 4\u03C0r\xB2 \xD7 2^(2/3).
Energy released = T \xD7 (decrease in area) = 4\u03C0r\xB2T[2 \u2212 2^(2/3)].
The answer is (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:"Two liquids A and B have \u03B8_A and \u03B8_B as contact angles in a capillary tube. If K = cos \u03B8_A/cos \u03B8_B, then identify the correct statement.",o:["K is negative, then liquid A has concave meniscus and liquid B has convex meniscus.","K is zero, then liquid A has convex meniscus and liquid B has concave meniscus.","K is negative, then liquid A and liquid B have concave meniscus.","K is negative, then liquid A and liquid B have convex meniscus."],c:0,s:`A contact angle below 90\xB0 (cos positive) gives a concave meniscus; above 90\xB0 (cos negative) gives a convex meniscus.
K is negative only when cos \u03B8_A and cos \u03B8_B have opposite signs, so one meniscus is concave and the other is convex. Options (c) and (d) are therefore wrong.
K = 0 means cos \u03B8_A = 0, i.e. \u03B8_A = 90\xB0, a flat meniscus for A, so (b) is wrong.
Of the options given, (a) is the correct one (A concave, B convex).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:"A capillary tube of radius 0.1 mm is partly dipped in water (surface tension 70 dyn/cm and glass water contact angle \u2248 0\xB0) with 30\xB0 inclined with the vertical. The length of water risen in the capillary is ___ cm. (Take g = 9.8 m/s\xB2)",o:["{57;2}","{71;5}","{68;5}","{82;5}"],c:3,s:`The vertical height of rise is h = 2T cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g) = 2 \xD7 0.07/(10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 1000 \xD7 9.8) = 0.1429 m = 14.3 cm.
The tube is tilted 30\xB0 from the vertical, so the length along the tube is l = h/cos 30\xB0.
l = 14.3/0.866 \u2248 16.5 cm, which matches 82/5 = 16.4 cm (the rounding of the given options).
The answer is (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"An air bubble of radius 0.1 cm lies at a depth of 20 cm below the free surface of a liquid of density 1000 kg/m\xB3. If the pressure inside the bubble is 2100 N/m\xB2 greater than the atmospheric pressure, then the surface tension of the liquid in SI unit is (use g = 10 m/s\xB2)",o:["0.1","0.05","0.02","0.25"],c:1,s:`Pressure inside the bubble = P\u2080 + \u03C1gh + 2T/r (one liquid surface).
So the excess over atmospheric = \u03C1gh + 2T/r.
\u03C1gh = 1000 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.2 = 2000 N/m\xB2, so 2T/r = 2100 \u2212 2000 = 100 N/m\xB2.
T = 100 \xD7 r/2 = 100 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/2 = 0.05 N/m.
The answer is (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"The amount of work done to break a big water drop of radius 'R' into 27 small drops of equal radius is 10 J. The work done required to break the same big drop into 64 small drops of equal radius will be",o:["20 J","15 J","5 J","10 J"],c:1,s:`For N small drops of radius r = R/N^(1/3), work = T \xD7 4\u03C0(Nr\xB2 \u2212 R\xB2) = 4\u03C0R\xB2T (N^(1/3) \u2212 1).
For N = 27: 4\u03C0R\xB2T \xD7 (3 \u2212 1) = 10 J, so 4\u03C0R\xB2T = 5 J.
For N = 64: work = 4\u03C0R\xB2T \xD7 (4 \u2212 1) = 3 \xD7 5 = 15 J.
The answer is (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Consider following statements :
A. Surface tension arises due to extra energy of the molecules at the interior as compared to the molecules at the surface, of a liquid.
B. As the temperature of liquid rises, the coefficient of viscosity increases.
C. As the temperature of gas increases, the coefficient of viscosity increases.
D. The onset of turbulence is determined by Reynold's number.
E. In a steady flow two stream lines never intersect.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["C, D, E only","A, D, E only","A, B, C only","B, C, D only"],c:0,s:`A is false: surface molecules, not interior ones, have the extra energy (they have fewer neighbours).
B is false: the viscosity of a liquid decreases when it is heated.
C is true: in a gas viscosity comes from molecular motion, which increases with temperature.
D is true: turbulence sets in when the Reynolds number exceeds a critical value.
E is true: two streamlines crossing would give two velocities at one point.
So C, D, E are correct: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"Pressure inside a soap bubble is greater than the pressure outside by an amount (given : R = Radius of bubble, S = Surface tension of bubble).",o:["{4S;R}","{4R;S}","{S;R}","{2S;R}"],c:0,s:`A soap bubble has two surfaces (inside and outside), each adding 2S/R.
Excess pressure = 2S/R + 2S/R = 4S/R.
The answer is (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"The excess pressure inside a soap bubble is thrice the excess pressure inside a second soap bubble. The ratio between the volume of the first and the second bubble is",o:["1 : 27","1 : 3","1 : 81","1 : 9"],c:0,s:`Excess pressure \u0394P = 4S/R, so \u0394P \u221D 1/R.
\u0394P\u2081 = 3\u0394P\u2082 gives R\u2081 = R\u2082/3.
Volume \u221D R\xB3, so V\u2081/V\u2082 = (1/3)\xB3 = 1/27.
The ratio is 1 : 27, option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A big drop is formed by coalescing 1000 small droplets of water. The surface energy will become",o:["{1;100} th","{1;10} th","100 times","10 times"],c:1,s:`Volume is conserved: 1000 \xD7 r\xB3 = R\xB3, so R = 10r.
Surface energy = T \xD7 area. Initial area = 1000 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2, final area = 4\u03C0(10r)\xB2 = 100 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2.
Final energy / initial energy = 100/1000 = 1/10.
The surface energy becomes 1/10 th, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A small liquid drop of radius R is divided into 27 identical liquid drops. If the surface tension is T, then the work done in the process will be",o:["3\u03C0R\xB2T","8\u03C0R\xB2T","4\u03C0R\xB2T","{1;8}\u03C0R\xB2T"],c:1,s:`Volume is conserved: 27r\xB3 = R\xB3, so r = R/3.
Increase in area = 27 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2 \u2212 4\u03C0R\xB2 = 4\u03C0R\xB2(3 \u2212 1) = 8\u03C0R\xB2.
Work done = T \xD7 increase in area = 8\u03C0R\xB2T.
The answer is (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A mercury drop of radius 10\u207B\xB3 m is broken into 125 equal size droplets. Surface tension of mercury is 0.45 N m\u207B\xB9. The gain in surface energy is",o:["5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 J","17.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 J","2.26 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 J","28 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 J"],c:2,s:`125r\xB3 = R\xB3, so r = R/5.
Increase in area = 125 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2 \u2212 4\u03C0R\xB2 = 4\u03C0R\xB2(5 \u2212 1) = 16\u03C0R\xB2.
Gain in energy = T \xD7 16\u03C0R\xB2 = 0.45 \xD7 16 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 2.26 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 J.
The answer is (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:"The height of liquid column raised in a capillary tube of certain radius when dipped in liquid A vertically is 5 cm. If the tube is dipped in a similar manner in another liquid B of surface tension and density double the values of liquid A, the height of liquid column raised in liquid B would be ___ m.",o:["0.20","0.5","0.10","0.05"],c:3,s:`Capillary rise h = 2T cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g).
For liquid B both T and \u03C1 are doubled, so the factor T/\u03C1 is unchanged (\u03B8 and r are the same).
So h is the same: 5 cm = 0.05 m.
The answer is (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"If 1000 droplets of water of surface tension 0.07 N/m, having same radius 1 mm each, combine to form a single drop. In the process the released surface energy is (Take \u03C0 = 22/7)",o:["8.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 J","7.92 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J","7.92 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J","9.68 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J"],c:1,s:`1000r\xB3 = R\xB3, so R = 10r = 10 mm.
Decrease in area = 1000 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2 \u2212 4\u03C0R\xB2 = 4\u03C0r\xB2(1000 \u2212 100) = 900 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2.
Energy released = T \xD7 900 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2 = 0.07 \xD7 900 \xD7 4 \xD7 (22/7) \xD7 10\u207B\u2076.
= 0.07 \xD7 900 \xD7 1.257\xD710\u207B\u2075 = 7.92 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J.
The answer is (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A drop of liquid of density \u03C1 is floating half immersed in a liquid of density \u03C3 and surface tension 7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N cm\u207B\xB9. The radius of drop in cm will be (g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["{15;\u221A(2\u03C1\u2212\u03C3)}","{15;\u221A(\u03C1\u2212\u03C3)}","{3;2\u221A(\u03C1\u2212\u03C3)}","{3;20\u221A(2\u03C1\u2212\u03C3)}"],c:0,s:`Forces on the drop: weight (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C1g down; buoyancy \xBD(4/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C3g up; surface tension 2\u03C0rT up.
Balance: (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C1g = (2/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C3g + 2\u03C0rT, so (2/3)\u03C0r\xB2g(2\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3) = 2T.
r\xB2 = 3T/[g(2\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)]. Here T = 7.5\xD710\u207B\u2074 N/cm = 7.5\xD710\u207B\xB2 N/m.
r\xB2 = 3 \xD7 7.5\xD710\u207B\xB2/[10(2\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)] = 2.25\xD710\u207B\xB2/(2\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3), so r = 0.15/\u221A(2\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3) m = 15/\u221A(2\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3) cm (\u03C1, \u03C3 in SI).
The answer is (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A water drop of radius 1 cm is broken into 729 equal droplets. If surface tension of water is 75 dyne/cm, then the gain in surface energy upto first decimal place will be [Given \u03C0 = 3.14]",o:["8.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J","8.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J","7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J","5.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J"],c:2,s:`729r\xB3 = R\xB3, so r = R/9.
Increase in area = 729 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2 \u2212 4\u03C0R\xB2 = 4\u03C0R\xB2(9 \u2212 1) = 32\u03C0R\xB2.
T = 75 dyne/cm = 0.075 N/m and R = 0.01 m.
Gain = 0.075 \xD7 32 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J.
The answer is (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A water drop of diameter 2 cm is broken into 64 equal droplets. The surface tension of water is 0.075 N/m. In this process the gain in surface energy will be",o:["2.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J","1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 J","1.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J","9.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 J"],c:0,s:`R = 1 cm. 64r\xB3 = R\xB3, so r = R/4.
Increase in area = 64 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2 \u2212 4\u03C0R\xB2 = 4\u03C0R\xB2(4 \u2212 1) = 12\u03C0R\xB2.
Gain = 0.075 \xD7 12\u03C0 \xD7 (10\u207B\xB2)\xB2 = 0.075 \xD7 37.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 2.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J.
The answer is (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:`Two narrow bores of diameter 5.0 mm and 8.0 mm are joined together to form a U-shaped tube open at both ends. If this U-tube contains water, what is the difference in the level of two limbs of the tube.
[Take surface tension of water T = 7.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 N m\u207B\xB9, angle of contact = 0, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2 and density of water = 1.0 \xD7 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3].`,o:["5.34 mm","3.62 mm","2.19 mm","4.97 mm"],c:2,s:`Rise in a bore: h = 2T/(r\u03C1g). The narrower limb (r\u2081 = 2.5 mm) has the higher level.
\u0394h = (2T/\u03C1g)(1/r\u2081 \u2212 1/r\u2082) = (2 \xD7 7.3\xD710\u207B\xB2/10\u2074)(1/2.5\xD710\u207B\xB3 \u2212 1/4\xD710\u207B\xB3).
= 1.46\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 (400 \u2212 250) = 1.46\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 150 = 2.19\xD710\u207B\xB3 m.
The difference is 2.19 mm, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"Two small drops of mercury each of radius R coalesce to form a single large drop. The ratio of total surface energy before and after the change is",o:["1 : 2^(1/3)","2 : 1","2^(1/3) : 1","1 : 2"],c:2,s:`Volume is conserved: 2R\xB3 = R\u2032\xB3, so R\u2032 = 2^(1/3) R.
Energy before = T \xD7 2 \xD7 4\u03C0R\xB2; energy after = T \xD7 4\u03C0R\u2032\xB2 = T \xD7 4\u03C0R\xB2 \xD7 2^(2/3).
Ratio before : after = 2/2^(2/3) = 2^(1/3).
The ratio is 2^(1/3) : 1, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"Two spherical soap bubbles of radii r\u2081 and r\u2082 in vacuum combine under isothermal conditions. The resulting bubble has a radius equal to",o:["\u221A(r\u2081r\u2082)","\u221A(r\u2081\xB2 + r\u2082\xB2)","{r\u2081r\u2082;r\u2081+r\u2082}","{r\u2081+r\u2082;2}"],c:1,s:`In vacuum the pressure inside a soap bubble is P = 4T/r.
Isothermal: the total PV (amount of gas) is conserved: P\u2081V\u2081 + P\u2082V\u2082 = PV.
(4T/r\u2081)(4/3)\u03C0r\u2081\xB3 + (4T/r\u2082)(4/3)\u03C0r\u2082\xB3 = (4T/r)(4/3)\u03C0r\xB3.
This gives r\u2081\xB2 + r\u2082\xB2 = r\xB2, so r = \u221A(r\u2081\xB2 + r\u2082\xB2). The answer is (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"When two soap bubbles of radii a and b (b > a) coalesce, the radius of curvature of common surface is",o:["{ab;a+b}","{ab;b\u2212a}","{a+b;ab}","{b\u2212a;ab}"],c:1,s:`The smaller bubble has the larger pressure: P_a = P\u2080 + 4T/a and P_b = P\u2080 + 4T/b.
The common surface (radius r) curves with excess pressure P_a \u2212 P_b = 4T/r.
4T/a \u2212 4T/b = 4T/r, so 1/r = 1/a \u2212 1/b = (b \u2212 a)/(ab).
r = ab/(b \u2212 a), option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A large number of water drops, each of radius r, combine to have a drop of radius R. If the surface tension is T and mechanical equivalent of heat is J, the rise in heat energy per unit volume will be",o:["{3T;rJ}","{2T;rJ}","{3T;J}({1;r} \u2212 {1;R})","{2T;J}({1;r} \u2212 {1;R})"],c:2,s:`Number of drops N = R\xB3/r\xB3. Surface energy released = T \xD7 4\u03C0(Nr\xB2 \u2212 R\xB2) = 4\u03C0T(R\xB3/r \u2212 R\xB2).
Per unit volume (volume = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3): 4\u03C0TR\xB3(1/r \u2212 1/R)/[(4/3)\u03C0R\xB3] = 3T(1/r \u2212 1/R).
This energy appears as heat; dividing by J converts it to calories.
Heat per unit volume = (3T/J)(1/r \u2212 1/R), option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:"A capillary tube made of glass of radius 0.15 mm is dipped vertically in a beaker filled with methylene iodide (surface tension = 0.05 N m\u207B\xB9, density = 667 kg m\u207B\xB3) which rises to height h in the tube. It is observed that the two tangents drawn from liquid-glass interfaces (from opposite sides of the capillary) make an angle of 60\xB0 with one another. Then h is close to (g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["0.049 m","0.137 m","0.172 m","0.087 m"],c:3,s:`The tangents at the two sides each make the contact angle \u03B8 with the tube wall (the vertical), so the angle between them is 2\u03B8 = 60\xB0, giving \u03B8 = 30\xB0.
h = 2T cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g) = 2 \xD7 0.05 \xD7 cos 30\xB0/(0.15\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 667 \xD7 10).
= 0.0866/1.0005 = 0.087 m.
The answer is (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"Pressure inside two soap bubbles are 1.01 and 1.02 atmosphere, respectively. The ratio of their volumes is",o:["4 : 1","0.8 : 1","8 : 1","2 : 1"],c:2,s:`Outside pressure is 1 atm, so the excess pressures are 0.01 atm and 0.02 atm.
Excess pressure = 4T/r, so r \u221D 1/\u0394P: r\u2081/r\u2082 = 0.02/0.01 = 2.
Volume \u221D r\xB3: V\u2081/V\u2082 = 2\xB3 = 8.
The ratio is 8 : 1, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A small spherical droplet of density d is floating exactly half immersed in a liquid of density \u03C1 and surface tension T. The radius of the droplet is (take note that the surface tension applies an upward force on the droplet.)",o:["r = \u221A{2T;3(d+\u03C1)g}","r = \u221A{3T;(2d\u2212\u03C1)g}","r = \u221A{T;(d\u2212\u03C1)g}","r = \u221A{T;(d+\u03C1)g}"],c:1,s:`Weight of drop = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3dg (down).
Buoyancy = \xBD \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C1g (up). Surface tension force = T \xD7 2\u03C0r (up).
(4/3)\u03C0r\xB3dg = (2/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C1g + 2\u03C0rT, so (2/3)\u03C0r\xB2g(2d \u2212 \u03C1) = 2T.
r\xB2 = 3T/[(2d \u2212 \u03C1)g], so r = \u221A{3T/[(2d \u2212 \u03C1)g]}, option (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:"If 'M' is the mass of the water that rises in a capillary tube of radius 'r', then mass of water which will rise in a capillary tube of radius '2r' is",o:["{M;2}","4M","M","2M"],c:3,s:`Height of rise h = 2T cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g), so h \u221D 1/r.
Mass of water in the tube = \u03C1 \xD7 \u03C0r\xB2h \u221D r\xB2 \xD7 (1/r) = r.
Doubling the radius doubles the mass: 2M.
The answer is (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:"The ratio of the surface tensions of mercury and water is given to be 7.5 while the ratio of their densities is 13.6. Their contact angles, with glass, are close to 135\xB0 and 0\xB0, respectively. It is observed that mercury gets depressed by an amount h in a capillary tube of radius r\u2081, while water rises by the same amount h in a capillary tube of radius r\u2082. The ratio, (r\u2081/r\u2082), is close to",o:["{2;5}","{4;5}","{3;5}","{2;3}"],c:0,s:`Magnitude of rise or fall: h = 2T|cos\u03B8|/(r\u03C1g).
Mercury: h = 2T_Hg cos 45\xB0/(r\u2081\u03C1_Hg g). Water: h = 2T_w/(r\u2082\u03C1_w g).
Equal h gives r\u2081/r\u2082 = (T_Hg/T_w) \xD7 cos 45\xB0 \xD7 (\u03C1_w/\u03C1_Hg) = 7.5 \xD7 0.707/13.6.
r\u2081/r\u2082 = 0.39 \u2248 2/5. The answer is (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"A small soap bubble of radius 4 cm is trapped inside another bubble of radius 6 cm without any contact. Let P\u2082 be the pressure inside the inner bubble and P\u2080, the pressure outside the outer bubble. Radius of another bubble with pressure difference P\u2082 \u2212 P\u2080 between its inside and outside would be",o:["12 cm","4.8 cm","2.4 cm","6 cm"],c:2,s:`Outer bubble: its inside pressure P\u2081 = P\u2080 + 4T/6. Inner bubble: P\u2082 = P\u2081 + 4T/4.
P\u2082 \u2212 P\u2080 = 4T(1/4 + 1/6) = 4T \xD7 5/12.
A single bubble of radius R has excess pressure 4T/R, so 1/R = 5/12.
R = 12/5 = 2.4 cm, option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"An air bubble of radius 0.1 cm is in a liquid having surface tension 0.06 N/m and density 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3. The pressure inside the bubble is 1100 Nm\u207B\xB2 greater than the atmospheric pressure. At what depth is the bubble below the surface of the liquid ? (g = 9.8 ms\u207B\xB2)",o:["0.1 m","0.15 m","0.20 m","0.25 m"],c:0,s:`Pressure inside \u2212 atmospheric = \u03C1gh + 2T/r.
2T/r = 2 \xD7 0.06/10\u207B\xB3 = 120 N/m\xB2.
So \u03C1gh = 1100 \u2212 120 = 980 N/m\xB2.
h = 980/(10\xB3 \xD7 9.8) = 0.1 m. The answer is (a).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:"A glass capillary tube is of the shape of a truncated cone with an apex angle \u03B1 so that its two ends have cross sections of different radii. When dipped in water vertically, water rises in it to a height h, where the radius of its cross section is b. If the surface tension of water is S, its density is \u03C1, and its contact angle with glass is \u03B8, the value of h will be (g is the acceleration due to gravity)",o:["{2S;b\u03C1g} cos(\u03B8 \u2212 \u03B1)","{2S;b\u03C1g} cos(\u03B8 + \u03B1)","{2S;b\u03C1g} cos(\u03B8 \u2212 \u03B1/2)","{2S;b\u03C1g} cos(\u03B8 + \u03B1/2)"],c:3,s:`The wall of a cone of apex angle \u03B1 makes an angle \u03B1/2 with the axis (vertical).
The water surface meets the wall at angle \u03B8, so the meniscus normal makes an angle \u03B8 + \u03B1/2 with the vertical axis.
The meniscus has radius of curvature R = b/cos(\u03B8 + \u03B1/2), and pressure drop = 2S/R = 2S cos(\u03B8 + \u03B1/2)/b.
This equals the hydrostatic pressure \u03C1gh, so h = {2S cos(\u03B8 + \u03B1/2)}/(b\u03C1g). The answer is (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2012",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A thin liquid film formed between a U-shaped wire and a light slider supports a weight of 1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 N. The length of the slider is 30 cm. The surface tension of the liquid film is",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"A U-shaped wire with a liquid film stretched across it and a light slider of length 30 cm at the bottom carrying a hanging weight w",d:[["r",100,35,140,115,"f"],["l",100,35,100,150],["l",240,35,240,150],["l",100,35,240,35],["l",90,150,250,150,"B"],["t",170,92,"film","m"],["l",170,150,170,172],["r",160,172,20,20,"F"],["t",188,188,"w"],["l",100,20,240,20,"b"],["t",170,15,"30 cm","m"]]},o:["0.1 N m\u207B\xB9","0.05 N m\u207B\xB9","0.025 N m\u207B\xB9","0.0125 N m\u207B\xB9"],c:2,s:`A film has two surfaces, and each pulls on the slider with force T \xD7 length.
Total upward force = 2Tl, and it balances the weight W.
T = W/(2l) = 1.5\xD710\u207B\xB2/(2 \xD7 0.30) = 0.025 N/m.
The answer is (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2012",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A large number of droplets, each of radius r, coalesce to form a bigger drop of radius R. An engineer designs a machine so that the energy released in this process is converted into the kinetic energy of the drop. Velocity of the drop is (T = surface tension, \u03C1 = density)",o:["[{T;\u03C1}({1;r} \u2212 {1;R})]^(1/2)","[{6T;\u03C1}({1;r} \u2212 {1;R})]^(1/2)","[{3T;\u03C1}({1;r} \u2212 {1;R})]^(1/2)","[{2T;\u03C1}({1;r} \u2212 {1;R})]^(1/2)"],c:1,s:`Number of droplets N = R\xB3/r\xB3. Energy released = T \xD7 4\u03C0(Nr\xB2 \u2212 R\xB2) = 4\u03C0TR\xB3(1/r \u2212 1/R).
Kinetic energy of the drop = \xBD M v\xB2 with M = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3\u03C1.
\xBD \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3\u03C1 v\xB2 = 4\u03C0TR\xB3(1/r \u2212 1/R), so v\xB2 = (6T/\u03C1)(1/r \u2212 1/R).
v = [(6T/\u03C1)(1/r \u2212 1/R)]^(1/2), option (b).`},{y:"AIEEE 2011",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"Work done in increasing the size of a soap bubble from a radius of 3 cm to 5 cm is nearly (Surface tension of soap solution = 0.03 N m\u207B\xB9)",o:["4\u03C0 mJ","0.2\u03C0 mJ","2\u03C0 mJ","0.4\u03C0 mJ"],c:3,s:`A soap bubble has two surfaces, so the surface area is 2 \xD7 4\u03C0R\xB2.
W = T \xD7 2 \xD7 4\u03C0(R\u2082\xB2 \u2212 R\u2081\xB2) = 8\u03C0T(R\u2082\xB2 \u2212 R\u2081\xB2).
W = 8\u03C0 \xD7 0.03 \xD7 (25 \u2212 9) \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 8\u03C0 \xD7 0.03 \xD7 16\xD710\u207B\u2074 = 3.84\xD710\u207B\u2074\u03C0 J.
This is 0.384\u03C0 mJ \u2248 0.4\u03C0 mJ, option (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:"A capillary tube (A) is dipped in water. Another identical tube (B) is dipped in a soap-water solution. Which of the following shows the relative nature of the liquid columns in the two tubes?",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Four panels (a) to (d), each with tube A in water and tube B in soap solution, showing how high each liquid column stands above the beaker level",d:[["t",4,12,"(a)","s"],["p","M10 42 V98 H160 V42"],["l",10,62,160,62,"d"],["l",39,10,39,85],["l",51,10,51,85],["l",39,22,51,22,"B"],["t",29,24,"A","s"],["l",109,10,109,85],["l",121,10,121,85],["l",109,21,121,21,"B"],["t",99,24,"B","s"],["t",174,12,"(b)","s"],["p","M180 42 V98 H330 V42"],["l",180,62,330,62,"d"],["l",209,10,209,85],["l",221,10,221,85],["l",209,22,221,22,"B"],["t",199,24,"A","s"],["l",279,10,279,85],["l",291,10,291,85],["l",279,34,291,34,"B"],["t",269,24,"B","s"],["t",4,117,"(c)","s"],["p","M10 147 V203 H160 V147"],["l",10,167,160,167,"d"],["l",39,115,39,190],["l",51,115,51,190],["l",39,127,51,127,"B"],["t",29,129,"A","s"],["l",109,115,109,190],["l",121,115,121,190],["l",109,115,121,115,"B"],["t",99,129,"B","s"],["t",174,117,"(d)","s"],["p","M180 147 V203 H330 V147"],["l",180,167,330,167,"d"],["l",209,115,209,190],["l",221,115,221,190],["l",209,127,221,127,"B"],["t",199,129,"A","s"],["l",279,115,279,190],["l",291,115,291,190],["l",279,151,291,151,"B"],["t",269,129,"B","s"]]},o:["Panel (a): B stands about as high as A","Panel (b): B stands lower than A, but not the lowest","Panel (c): B stands higher than A","Panel (d): B stands much lower than A (about half)"],c:3,s:`The rise in a capillary tube is h = 2T cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g).
The tubes are identical (same r), and the contact angle is about 0 in both liquids.
Soap solution has a smaller surface tension than water, so h is smaller in tube B than in tube A, and both menisci are concave.
The soap solution rises only about 1/3 to 1/2 as high as water, which is the case drawn in panel (d). The answer is (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:"A 20 cm long capillary tube is dipped in water. The water rises up to 8 cm. If the entire arrangement is put in a freely falling elevator the length of water column in the capillary tube will be",o:["4 cm","20 cm","8 cm","10 cm"],c:1,s:`In a freely falling elevator the effective gravity is zero (weightless).
Capillary rise h = 2T cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g_eff) becomes infinite as g_eff \u2192 0.
The water therefore climbs until it fills the whole tube.
The column length is 20 cm, option (b).`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"If two soap bubbles of different radii are connected by a tube,",o:["air flows from the bigger bubble to the smaller bubble till the sizes become equal","air flows from the bigger bubble to the smaller bubble till the sizes are interchanged","air flows from the smaller bubble to the bigger","there is no flow of air."],c:2,s:`Excess pressure inside a soap bubble is 4T/R, so the smaller bubble has the higher pressure.
Air flows from higher to lower pressure, so it flows from the smaller bubble to the bigger one.
The smaller bubble keeps shrinking and the bigger one grows.
The answer is (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:`A cylindrical capillary tube of 0.2 mm radius is made by joining two capillaries T\u2081 and T\u2082 of different materials having water contact angles of 0\xB0 and 60\xB0, respectively. The capillary tube is dipped vertically in water in two different configurations, case I and II as shown in figure. Which of the following option(s) is (are) correct?
[Surface tension of water = 0.075 N/m, density of water = 1000 kg/m\xB3, take g = 10 m/s\xB2]`,fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Two capillary tubes dipped in water. In case I the upper part is T2 and the lower part T1; in case II the upper part is T1 and the lower part T2.",d:[["t",85,16,"Case I","m"],["t",255,16,"Case II","m"],["l",77,190,77,30],["l",93,190,93,30],["l",73,110,97,110],["t",103,75,"T\u2082"],["t",103,150,"T\u2081"],["l",20,170,150,170],["l",190,170,320,170],["l",247,190,247,30],["l",263,190,263,30],["l",243,110,267,110],["t",273,75,"T\u2081"],["t",273,150,"T\u2082"]]},o:["For case I, if the joint is kept at 8 cm above the water surface, the height of water column in the tube will be 7.5 cm. (Neglect the weight of the water in the meniscus)","For case I, if the capillary joint is 5 cm above the water surface, the height of water column raised in the tube will be more than 8.75 cm. (Neglect the weight of the water in the meniscus)","The correction in the height of water column in the tube, due to weight of water contained in the meniscus, will be different for both cases.","For case II, if the capillary joint is 5 cm above the water surface, the height of water column raised in the tube will be 3.75 cm. (Neglect the weight of the water in the meniscus)"],c:[0,2,3],s:`Rise in a tube: h = 2S cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g). With r = 0.2 mm, S = 0.075 N/m: 2S/(r\u03C1g) = 0.15/(2\xD710\u207B\u2074\xD710\u2074) = 0.075 m = 7.5 cm.
T\u2081 (\u03B8 = 0\xB0): h = 7.5 cm. T\u2082 (\u03B8 = 60\xB0): h = 7.5 \xD7 cos60\xB0 = 3.75 cm.
(a) Case I has T\u2081 at the bottom. The joint (8 cm) is above the natural rise of 7.5 cm, so the column stops at 7.5 cm. True.
(b) Case I with joint at 5 cm: the column can never exceed what the 0\xB0 tube supports, 7.5 cm, so it cannot be more than 8.75 cm. False.
(c) The meniscus is in T\u2081 (0\xB0) in case I and in T\u2082 (60\xB0) in case II, so the meniscus water weight correction differs. True.
(d) Case II has T\u2082 at the bottom. It can lift water only 3.75 cm, which is below the joint at 5 cm, so h = 3.75 cm. True.
Correct: (a), (c), (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:"A uniform capillary tube of inner radius r is dipped vertically into a beaker filled with water. The water rises to a height h in the capillary tube above the water surface in the beaker. The surface tension of water is \u03C3. The angle of contact between water and the wall of the capillary tube is \u03B8. Ignore the mass of water in the meniscus. Which of the following statements is (are) true?",o:["For a given material of the capillary tube, h decreases with increase in r","For a given material of the capillary tube, h is independent of \u03C3","If this experiment is performed in a lift going up with a constant acceleration, then h decreases","h is proportional to contact angle \u03B8"],c:[0,2],s:`h = 2\u03C3 cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g).
(a) h \u221D 1/r, so h decreases as r increases. True.
(b) h is directly proportional to \u03C3. False.
(c) In a lift going up with acceleration a, g is replaced by g + a, which is larger, so h decreases. True.
(d) h is proportional to cos\u03B8, not \u03B8. False.
Correct: (a) and (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"The excess pressure inside a soap bubble A in air is half the excess pressure inside another soap bubble B in air. If the volume of the bubble A is n times the volume of the bubble B, then the value of n is ______.",n:8,s:`Excess pressure in a soap bubble = 4S/r.
4S/r_A = \xBD \xD7 4S/r_B, so r_A = 2r_B.
Volume \u221D r\xB3, so V_A/V_B = 2\xB3 = 8.
n = 8.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"Two soap bubbles of radius 2 cm and 4 cm, respectively, are in contact with each other. The radius of curvature of the common surface, in cm, is ______.",n:4,s:`For two bubbles in contact, the common surface has radius r = r\u2081r\u2082/(r\u2082 \u2212 r\u2081).
r = (2 \xD7 4)/(4 \u2212 2) = 8/2 = 4 cm.
(The excess pressure across the common film is 4S(1/r\u2081 \u2212 1/r\u2082) = 4S/r, which gives the same result.)
Answer: 4 cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"An air bubble of radius 1.0 mm is observed at a depth of 20 cm below the free surface of a liquid having surface tension 0.095 J/m\xB2 and density 10\xB3 kg/m\xB3. The difference between pressure inside the bubble and atmospheric pressure is ______ N/m\xB2. (Take g = 10 m/s\xB2)",n:2190,s:`An air bubble in a liquid has only one surface, so the excess pressure from surface tension is 2S/r.
Pressure inside \u2212 atmospheric = \u03C1gh + 2S/r.
\u03C1gh = 10\xB3 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.2 = 2000 N/m\xB2.
2S/r = 2 \xD7 0.095/(1.0\xD710\u207B\xB3) = 190 N/m\xB2.
Total = 2000 + 190 = 2190 N/m\xB2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A soap bubble is blown to a diameter of 7 cm. 36960 erg of work is done in blowing it further. If surface tension of soap solution is 40 dyne/cm then the new radius is ______ cm. Take \u03C0 = {22;7}.",n:7,s:`A soap bubble has two surfaces, so the increase in area = 2 \xD7 4\u03C0(R\xB2 \u2212 r\xB2).
Work = S \xD7 increase in area: 36960 = 40 \xD7 8\u03C0(R\xB2 \u2212 r\xB2).
Initial r = 3.5 cm, so r\xB2 = 12.25.
R\xB2 \u2212 12.25 = 36960/(40 \xD7 8 \xD7 22/7) = 924/(176/7) = 36.75.
R\xB2 = 49, so R = 7 cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A big drop is formed by coalescing 1000 small droplets of water. The ratio of surface energy of 1000 droplets to that of energy of big drop is {10;x}. The value of x is ______.",n:1,s:`Volume is conserved: 1000 \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3 = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3, so R = 10r.
Energy of 1000 droplets = 1000 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2S. Energy of big drop = 4\u03C0(10r)\xB2S = 100 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2S.
Ratio = 1000/100 = 10/1.
So {10;x} = {10;1} and x = 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"A liquid column of height 0.04 cm balances excess pressure of a soap bubble of certain radius. If density of liquid is 8 \xD7 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3 and surface tension of soap solution is 0.28 N m\u207B\xB9, then diameter of the soap bubble is ______ cm. (if g = 10 ms\u207B\xB2).",n:7,s:`Pressure of the liquid column = \u03C1gh = 8\xD710\xB3 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.04\xD710\u207B\xB2 = 32 Pa.
Excess pressure in a soap bubble = 4S/r = 32.
r = 4 \xD7 0.28/32 = 0.035 m = 3.5 cm.
Diameter = 2r = 7 cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A big drop is formed by coalescing 1000 small identical drops of water. If E\u2081 be the total surface energy of 1000 small drops of water and E\u2082 be the surface energy of single big drop of water, then E\u2081 : E\u2082 is x : 1 where x = ______.",n:10,s:`Volume conservation: 1000 \xD7 r\xB3 = R\xB3, so R = 10r.
E\u2081 = 1000 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2S, E\u2082 = 4\u03C0(10r)\xB2S = 100 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2S.
E\u2081/E\u2082 = 1000/100 = 10.
So E\u2081 : E\u2082 = 10 : 1 and x = 10.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"The surface tension of soap solution is 3.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 N m\u207B\xB9. The amount of work done required to increase the radius of soap bubble from 10 cm to 20 cm is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J. (Take \u03C0 = 22/7)",n:264,s:`A soap bubble has two surfaces, so work = S \xD7 2 \xD7 4\u03C0(R\xB2 \u2212 r\xB2).
R\xB2 \u2212 r\xB2 = 0.04 \u2212 0.01 = 0.03 m\xB2.
W = 3.5\xD710\u207B\xB2 \xD7 8 \xD7 (22/7) \xD7 0.03 = 2.64\xD710\u207B\xB2 J.
W = 264 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"There is an air bubble of radius 1.0 mm in a liquid of surface tension 0.075 N m\u207B\xB9 and density 1000 kg m\u207B\xB3 at a depth of 10 cm below the free surface. The amount by which the pressure inside the bubble is greater than the atmospheric pressure is ______ Pa. (g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",n:1150,s:`Pressure inside \u2212 atmospheric = \u03C1gh + 2S/r (an air bubble in liquid has one surface).
\u03C1gh = 1000 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.1 = 1000 Pa.
2S/r = 2 \xD7 0.075/(1\xD710\u207B\xB3) = 150 Pa.
Total = 1150 Pa.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A spherical drop of liquid splits into 1000 identical spherical drops. If u_i is the surface energy of the original drop and u_f is the total surface energy of the resulting drops, (ignoring evaporation), {u_f;u_i} = ({10;x}). Then value of x is ______.",n:1,s:`Volume conservation: R = 10r for 1000 drops.
u_i = 4\u03C0R\xB2S = 4\u03C0(100r\xB2)S and u_f = 1000 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2S.
u_f/u_i = 1000/100 = 10.
So {10;x} = 10 and x = 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"A spherical soap bubble of radius 3 cm is formed inside another spherical soap bubble of radius 6 cm. If the internal pressure of the smaller bubble of radius 3 cm in the above system is equal to the internal pressure of the another single soap bubble of radius r cm. The value of r is ______.",n:2,s:`Pressure inside the smaller bubble = P\u2080 + 4S/6 + 4S/3 = P\u2080 + 2S (outer film plus inner film).
Pressure inside a single bubble of radius r = P\u2080 + 4S/r.
Equate: 4S/r = 2S, so r = 2 cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"A soap bubble of radius 3 cm is formed inside another soap bubble of radius 6 cm. The radius of an equivalent soap bubble which has the same excess pressure as inside the smaller bubble with respect to the atmospheric pressure is ______ cm.",n:2,s:`Excess pressure inside the smaller bubble over atmosphere = 4S/3 + 4S/6 = 2S.
For a single bubble of radius r: 4S/r = 2S.
r = 2 cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"Suppose you have taken a dilute solution of oleic acid in such a way that its concentration becomes 0.01 cm\xB3 of oleic acid per cm\xB3 of the solution. Then you make a thin film of this solution (monomolecular thickness) of area 4 cm\xB2 by considering 100 spherical drops of radius ({3;40\u03C0})^(1/3) \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 cm. Then the thickness of oleic acid layer will be x \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 m, where x is ______.",n:25,s:`Volume of one drop = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3 = (4/3)\u03C0 \xD7 (3/40\u03C0) \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 = 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 cm\xB3.
100 drops: 10\u207B\u2078 cm\xB3 of solution.
Oleic acid in it = 0.01 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 = 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 cm\xB3.
Thickness = volume/area = 10\u207B\xB9\u2070/4 = 2.5\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB9 cm = 2.5\xD710\u207B\xB9\xB3 m = 25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 m.
x = 25.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Capillary rise and angle of contact",q:"When a long glass capillary tube of radius 0.015 cm is dipped in a liquid, the liquid rises to a height of 15 cm within it. If the contact angle between the liquid and glass to close to 0\xB0, the surface tension of the liquid, in millinewton m\u207B\xB9, is [\u03C1(liquid) = 900 kg m\u207B\xB3, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2] (Give answer in closest integer) ______.",n:101,s:`h = 2S cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g) with cos0\xB0 = 1, so S = r\u03C1gh/2.
r = 0.015 cm = 1.5\xD710\u207B\u2074 m, h = 15 cm = 0.15 m.
S = 1.5\xD710\u207B\u2074 \xD7 900 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.15/2 = 0.10125 N/m.
S = 101.25 mN/m, closest integer 101.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"When water is filled carefully in a glass, one can fill it up to a height h above the rim of the glass due to the surface tension of water. To calculate h just before water starts flowing, model the shape of the water above the rim as a disc of thickness h having semicircular edges, as shown schematically in the figure. When the pressure of water at the bottom of this disc exceeds what can be withstood due to the surface tension, the water surface breaks near the rim and water starts flowing from there. If the density of water, its surface tension and the acceleration due to gravity are 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3, 0.07 N m\u207B\xB9 and 10 m s\u207B\xB2, respectively, the value of h (in mm) is ______.",fig:{w:260,h:150,alt:"Side view of a disc of water of thickness h with semicircular edges sitting on the rim of a glass.",d:[["p","M70 50 H190 A25 25 0 0 1 190 100 H70 A25 25 0 0 1 70 50 Z","f"],["t",130,80,"water","m"],["l",225,50,225,100,"b"],["t",232,79,"h"],["l",40,100,70,100,"B"],["l",190,100,220,100,"B"],["t",130,135,"disc of water above the glass rim","ms"]]},n:3.65,s:`The edge is a half-cylinder of radius h/2. Surface tension can support a pressure difference of about T/(h/2) = 2T/h across it.
The water breaks out when the pressure at the bottom, \u03C1gh, reaches this value: \u03C1gh = 2T/h.
h\xB2 = 2T/(\u03C1g) = 2 \xD7 0.07/(10\xB3 \xD7 10) = 1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2, so h \u2248 3.7 mm.
The official key gives 3.65 mm (the book accepts a small range around 3.7), so the answer is 3.65.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"A drop of liquid of radius R = 10\u207B\xB2 m having surface tension S = {0.1;4\u03C0} N m\u207B\xB9 divides itself into K identical drops. In this process the total change in the surface energy \u0394U = 10\u207B\xB3 J. If K = 10^\u03B1 then the value of \u03B1 is ______.",n:6,s:`Each small drop has radius r = R/K^(1/3).
\u0394U = 4\u03C0R\xB2S (K^(1/3) \u2212 1).
4\u03C0R\xB2S = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 0.1/(4\u03C0) = 10\u207B\u2075 J.
10\u207B\u2075 (K^(1/3) \u2212 1) = 10\u207B\xB3, so K^(1/3) = 101 and K \u2248 1.03 \xD7 10\u2076 \u2248 10\u2076.
So \u03B1 = 6.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"pss",st:"Excess pressure, drops and bubbles",q:"Two soap bubbles A and B are kept in a closed chamber where the air is maintained at pressure 8 N/m\xB2. The radii of bubbles A and B are 2 cm and 4 cm, respectively. Surface tension of the soap-water used to make bubbles is 0.04 N/m. Find the ratio n_B/n_A, where n_A and n_B are the number of moles of air in bubbles A and B, respectively. [Neglect the effect of gravity.]",n:6,s:`Pressure inside a bubble = 8 + 4S/r.
P_A = 8 + 4 \xD7 0.04/0.02 = 16 N/m\xB2. P_B = 8 + 0.16/0.04 = 12 N/m\xB2.
From PV = nRT (same T), n \u221D PV \u221D P r\xB3.
n_B/n_A = (12 \xD7 4\xB3)/(16 \xD7 2\xB3) = 768/128 = 6.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:`When liquid medicine of density \u03C1 is to be put in the eye, it is done with the help of a dropper. As the bulb at the opening of the dropper is pressed, a drop forms at the opening of the dropper. We wish to estimate the size of the drop. We first assume that the drop formed at the opening is spherical because that requires a minimum increase in its surface energy. To determine the size, we calculate the net vertical force due to the surface tension T when the radius of the drop is R. When this force becomes smaller than the weight of the drop, the drop gets detached from the dropper.
If the radius of the opening of the dropper is r, the vertical force due to the surface tension on the drop of radius R (assuming r << R) is`,o:["2\u03C0rT","2\u03C0RT","{2\u03C0r\xB2T;R}","{2\u03C0R\xB2T;r}"],c:2,s:`Surface tension acts along the circle of contact of length 2\u03C0r, along the tangent to the drop.
The tangent makes an angle \u03B8 with the vertical, where sin\u03B8 = r/R.
Vertical force = T \xD7 2\u03C0r \xD7 sin\u03B8 = T \xD7 2\u03C0r \xD7 r/R = 2\u03C0r\xB2T/R.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:`When liquid medicine of density \u03C1 is to be put in the eye, it is done with the help of a dropper. As the bulb at the opening of the dropper is pressed, a drop forms at the opening of the dropper. We wish to estimate the size of the drop. We first assume that the drop formed at the opening is spherical because that requires a minimum increase in its surface energy. To determine the size, we calculate the net vertical force due to the surface tension T when the radius of the drop is R. When this force becomes smaller than the weight of the drop, the drop gets detached from the dropper.
If r = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m, \u03C1 = 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2, T = 0.11 N m\u207B\xB9, the radius of the drop when it detaches from the dropper is approximately`,o:["1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m","3.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m","2.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m","4.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m"],c:0,s:`The drop detaches when the surface tension force equals its weight: 2\u03C0r\xB2T/R = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3\u03C1g.
R\u2074 = 3r\xB2T/(2\u03C1g).
R\u2074 = 3 \xD7 (5\xD710\u207B\u2074)\xB2 \xD7 0.11/(2 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 10) = 4.125 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2.
R = (4.125)^(1/4) \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u2248 1.43 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:`When liquid medicine of density \u03C1 is to be put in the eye, it is done with the help of a dropper. As the bulb at the opening of the dropper is pressed, a drop forms at the opening of the dropper. We wish to estimate the size of the drop. We first assume that the drop formed at the opening is spherical because that requires a minimum increase in its surface energy. To determine the size, we calculate the net vertical force due to the surface tension T when the radius of the drop is R. When this force becomes smaller than the weight of the drop, the drop gets detached from the dropper.
After the drop detaches, its surface energy is`,o:["1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J","2.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J","5.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J","8.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J"],c:1,s:`Surface energy = surface tension \xD7 area = T \xD7 4\u03C0R\xB2.
With R \u2248 1.4\xD710\u207B\xB3 m: 4\u03C0R\xB2 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 1.96\xD710\u207B\u2076 \u2248 2.46\xD710\u207B\u2075 m\xB2.
U = 0.11 \xD7 2.46\xD710\u207B\u2075 \u2248 2.7\xD710\u207B\u2076 J.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : The hot water flows faster than cold water
Statement II : Soap water has higher surface tension as compared to fresh water.
In the light above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are false","Statement I is false but statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but statement II is false"],c:3,s:`Statement I: viscosity of water falls as temperature rises, so hot water flows faster. True.
Statement II: soap lowers the surface tension of water, so soap water has lower surface tension. False.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : Clothes containing oil or grease stains cannot be cleaned by water wash.
Reason (R) : Because the angle of contact between the oil/grease and water is obtuse.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],c:0,s:`Water has an obtuse angle of contact with oil or grease, so it does not wet the greasy surface or spread into it.
So water cannot remove the stain: (A) is true, (R) is true, and (R) explains (A).
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pss",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement-I: The contact angle between a solid and a liquid is a property of the material of the solid and liquid as well.
Statement-II: The rise of a liquid in a capillary tube does not depend on the inner radius of the tube.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are false.","Statement-I is true but Statement-II is false.","Statement-I is false but Statement-II is true.","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are true."],c:1,s:`Statement I is true: the contact angle depends on the solid-liquid pair.
Statement II is false: h = 2S cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g), so the rise depends on the radius (h \u221D 1/r).
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I : When a capillary tube is dipped into a liquid, the liquid neither rises nor falls in the capillary. The contact angle may be 0\xB0.
Statement II : The contact angle between a solid and a liquid is a property of the material of the solid and liquid as well.
In the light of the above statement, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true."],c:0,s:`If the liquid neither rises nor falls, h = 0, so cos\u03B8 = 0 and the contact angle is 90\xB0, not 0\xB0. Statement I is false.
Statement II is true: the contact angle depends on both the solid and the liquid.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psv",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement (I) : Viscosity of gases is greater than that of liquids.
Statement (II) : Surface tension of a liquid decreases due to the presence of insoluble impurities.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:0,s:`Statement I is incorrect: liquids are more viscous than gases (for example water is far more viscous than air).
Statement II is correct: impurities (such as detergents or oil films) lower the surface tension of a liquid.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pss",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I : If a capillary tube is immersed first in cold water and then in hot water, the height of capillary rise will be smaller in hot water.
Statement II : If a capillary tube is immersed first in cold water and then in hot water, the height of capillary rise will be smaller in cold water.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both statement I and statement II are false.","Statement I is true but statement II is false.","Both statement I and statement II are true.","Statement I is false but statement II is true."],c:1,s:`Surface tension of water decreases as temperature rises (and the density changes only slightly).
Since h = 2S cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g) \u221D S, the rise is smaller in hot water.
Statement I is true and Statement II is false.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"pss",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A uniform wire having mass per unit length \u03BB is placed over a liquid surface. The wire causes the liquid to depress by y (y << a) as shown in figure. Find the surface tension of the liquid. Neglect end effect.",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"A wire floating at the middle of a liquid surface in a container of width 2a; the surface is depressed by y below the level at the walls.",d:[["r",30,30,280,110],["p","M30 60 L170 84 L310 60","B"],["l",30,60,310,60,"d"],["c",170,78,5,"F"],["l",185,60,185,84,"b"],["t",192,77,"y"],["l",30,155,170,155,"b"],["l",170,155,310,155,"b"],["t",100,150,"a","m"],["t",240,150,"a","m"],["t",170,26,"wire","ms"]]},a:"T = \u03BBga/(2y)",s:`Take a length L of the wire. Its weight is \u03BBLg.
The surface tension pulls on each side of the wire along the surface, which makes a small angle \u03B8 with the horizontal, where sin\u03B8 \u2248 tan\u03B8 = y/a (y << a).
The force on each side is T \xD7 L, so the total upward force = 2TL sin\u03B8.
For equilibrium: 2TL (y/a) = \u03BBLg.
T = \u03BBga/(2y).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"pss",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A bubble having surface tension T and radius R is formed on a ring of radius b (b << R). Air is blown inside the tube with velocity v as shown. The air molecule collides perpendicularly with the wall of the bubble and stops. Calculate the radius at which the bubble separates from the ring. (Take \u03C1 as the density of air.)",fig:{w:300,h:190,alt:"A horizontal tube of radius b carries air with velocity v to the left, ending in a ring on which a spherical bubble of radius R is formed.",d:[["l",20,85,125,85],["l",20,105,125,105],["c",195,95,70],["l",35,95,100,95,"a"],["t",55,80,"v"],["l",132,85,132,105,"b"],["t",137,100,"b","s"],["l",195,95,243,45,"a"],["t",228,78,"R"]]},a:"R = 4T/(\u03C1v\xB2)",s:`Air hitting the bubble wall loses all its momentum. Mass of air reaching the bubble per second = \u03C1(\u03C0b\xB2)v, and each kg loses velocity v.
Force on the bubble from the air = \u03C1\u03C0b\xB2v\xB2.
Surface tension holds the bubble on the ring along a circle of length 2\u03C0b, with two film surfaces. The tangent makes angle \u03B8 with the axis, sin\u03B8 = b/R.
Holding force = 2 \xD7 T \xD7 2\u03C0b \xD7 (b/R) = 4\u03C0b\xB2T/R.
The bubble separates when \u03C1\u03C0b\xB2v\xB2 = 4\u03C0b\xB2T/R, so R = 4T/(\u03C1v\xB2).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"Which of the following figure represents the relation between Celsius and Fahrenheit temperatures?",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"Four C versus F graphs. (a) rising line crossing the F axis to the right of the origin; (b) falling line crossing left; (c) rising line crossing left; (d) falling line crossing right.",d:[["l",30,55,160,55,"a"],["l",95,100,95,10,"a"],["t",163,59,"F","s"],["t",99,9,"C","s"],["t",95,113,"(a)","m"],["l",75,95,155,15],["l",200,55,330,55,"a"],["l",265,100,265,10,"a"],["t",333,59,"F","s"],["t",269,9,"C","s"],["t",265,113,"(b)","m"],["l",205,15,285,95],["l",30,170,160,170,"a"],["l",95,215,95,125,"a"],["t",163,174,"F","s"],["t",99,124,"C","s"],["t",95,228,"(c)","m"],["l",35,210,115,130],["l",200,170,330,170,"a"],["l",265,215,265,125,"a"],["t",333,174,"F","s"],["t",269,124,"C","s"],["t",265,228,"(d)","m"],["l",245,130,325,210]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:0,s:`C = (5/9)(F \u2212 32) = (5/9)F \u2212 160/9.
The slope 5/9 is positive, so the line rises to the right (this removes the two falling lines).
It cuts the F-axis at F = 32 (on the positive side) and the C-axis at C = \u221217.8 (negative).
So the line rises to the right, crossing the F-axis to the right of the origin and the C-axis below the origin: graph (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"On celcius scale of temperature of body increases by 40\xB0C. The increase in temperature on Fahrenheit scale is",o:["68\xB0F","72\xB0F","70\xB0F","75\xB0F"],c:1,s:`One Celsius degree equals 9/5 Fahrenheit degrees: \u0394F = (9/5)\u0394C.
\u0394F = (9/5) \xD7 40 = 72\xB0F.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"With rise in temperature, the Young's modulus of elasticity",o:["remains unchanged","changes erratically","decreases","increases"],c:2,s:`As temperature rises, the atoms vibrate more and the interatomic bonds become weaker.
The material becomes less stiff, so Young's modulus decreases.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"On a temperature scale 'X', the boiling point of water is 65\xB0X and the freezing point is \u221215\xB0X. Assume that the X scale is linear. The equivalent temperature corresponding to \u221295\xB0X on the Fahrenheit scale would be",o:["\u221263\xB0F","\u2212112\xB0F","\u221248\xB0F","\u2212148\xB0F"],c:3,s:`Use (reading \u2212 lower fixed point)/(range) equal on both scales.
X scale: (X + 15)/80 = (\u221295 + 15)/80 = \u22121.
Fahrenheit: (F \u2212 32)/180 = \u22121, so F = 32 \u2212 180 = \u2212148\xB0F.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"The graph between two temperature scales P and Q is shown in the figure. Between upper fixed point and lower fixed point there are 150 equal divisions of scale P and 100 divisions on scale Q. The relationship for conversion between the two scales is given by",fig:{w:340,h:230,alt:"Graph of temperature on scale P (vertical axis, up to 180) against temperature on scale Q (horizontal axis); a straight line rises from P = 30 at Q = 0 to P = 180, with a change of 150 divisions on P and 100 on Q.",d:[["l",60,205,60,18,"a"],["l",60,205,300,205,"a"],["t",68,20,"Temperature (\xB0P)","s"],["t",300,222,"Temperature (\xB0Q)","es"],["l",60,173,200,40,"B"],["l",60,40,200,40,"d"],["l",200,205,200,40,"d"],["l",60,173,200,173,"d"],["t",54,44,"180","e"],["t",54,177,"30","e"],["t",54,209,"0","e"],["l",215,173,215,40,"b"],["t",222,110,"\u0394tP = 150\xB0","s"],["l",60,190,200,190,"b"],["t",130,186,"\u0394tQ = 100\xB0","ms"]]},o:["{t_P;100} = {t_Q \u2212 180;150}","{t_Q;150} = {t_P \u2212 180;100}","{t_P;180} = {t_Q \u2212 40;100}","{t_Q;100} = {t_P \u2212 30;150}"],c:3,s:`From the graph the upper fixed point is 180 on scale P, and the range on P is 150, so the lower fixed point is at 180 \u2212 150 = 30 on scale P (this is 0 on scale Q).
The fraction of the range covered must be the same on both scales: (t_P \u2212 30)/150 = (t_Q \u2212 0)/100.
So t_Q/100 = (t_P \u2212 30)/150.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:`Heat energy of 184 kJ is given to ice of mass 600 g at \u221212\xB0C. Specific heat of ice is 2222.3 J kg\u207B\xB9 \xB0C\u207B\xB9 and latent heat of ice is 336 kJ/kg.
A. Final temperature of system will be 0\xB0C
B. Final temperature of the system will be greater than 0\xB0C.
C. The final system will have a mixture of ice and water in the ratio of 5 : 1.
D. The final system will have a mixture of ice and water in the ratio of 1 : 5.
E. The final system will have water only.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A and D only","A and C only","A and E only","B and D only"],c:0,s:`Heat to warm ice from \u221212\xB0C to 0\xB0C = 0.6 \xD7 2222.3 \xD7 12 \u2248 16 kJ.
Heat left = 184 \u2212 16 = 168 kJ.
Heat needed to melt all the ice = 0.6 \xD7 336 = 201.6 kJ, which is more than 168 kJ, so the final temperature is 0\xB0C (A true).
Ice melted = 168/336 = 0.5 kg; ice left = 0.1 kg.
Ice : water = 0.1 : 0.5 = 1 : 5 (D true).
Answer: A and D only, option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A solid metallic cube having total surface area 24 m\xB2 is uniformly heated. If its temperature is increased by 10\xB0C, calculate the increase in volume of the cube. (Given \u03B1 = 5.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xB0C\u207B\xB9)",o:["2.4 \xD7 10\u2076 cm\xB3","1.2 \xD7 10\u2075 cm\xB3","6.0 \xD7 10\u2074 cm\xB3","4.8 \xD7 10\u2075 cm\xB3"],c:1,s:`Total surface area = 6a\xB2 = 24, so a = 2 m and V = 8 m\xB3.
\u0394V = \u03B3V\u0394T = 3\u03B1V\u0394T = 3 \xD7 5\xD710\u207B\u2074 \xD7 8 \xD7 10 = 0.12 m\xB3.
0.12 m\xB3 = 0.12 \xD7 10\u2076 cm\xB3 = 1.2 \xD7 10\u2075 cm\xB3.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:`At what temperature can a gold ring of diameter 6.230 cm be fitted on a wooden bangle of diameter 6.241 cm? Both the diameters have been measured at room temperature (27\xB0C).
(Given : coefficient of linear thermal expansion of gold \u03B1_L = 1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9)`,o:["125.7\xB0C","91.7\xB0C","425.7\xB0C","152.7\xB0C"],c:3,s:`The ring's diameter must grow from 6.230 cm to 6.241 cm: \u0394D = 0.011 cm.
\u0394D = D \u03B1 \u0394T, so \u0394T = 0.011/(6.230 \xD7 1.4\xD710\u207B\u2075) \u2248 126\xB0C.
Final temperature = 27 + 126 \u2248 153\xB0C.
The nearest option is 152.7\xB0C.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A bimetallic strip consists of metals A and B. It is mounted rigidly as shown. The metal A has higher coefficient of expansion compared to that of metal B. When the bimetallic strip is placed in a cold bath, it will",fig:{w:200,h:140,alt:"A bimetallic strip fixed at the bottom, with metal A on the left and metal B on the right.",d:[["r",60,100,80,12,"F"],["r",88,25,22,75],["r",110,25,22,75],["t",99,20,"A","m"],["t",121,20,"B","m"]]},o:["Neither bend nor shrink","Bend towards the right","Not bend but shrink","Bend towards the left"],c:3,s:`On cooling, both metals contract, but A (larger \u03B1) contracts more than B.
So A becomes the shorter side and ends up on the inside of the curve.
A is on the left, so the strip bends towards the left.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"Each side of a box made of metal sheet in cubic shape is 'a' at room temperature 'T', the coefficient of linear expansion of the metal sheet is '\u03B1'. The metal sheet is heated uniformly, by a small temperature \u0394T, so that its new temperature is T + \u0394T. Calculate the increase in the volume of the metal box.",o:["3a\xB3\u03B1\u0394T","{4;3}\u03C0a\xB3\u03B1\u0394T","4a\xB3\u03B1\u0394T","4\u03C0a\xB3\u03B1\u0394T"],c:0,s:`Every linear dimension of the box grows as a(1 + \u03B1\u0394T).
New volume = a\xB3(1 + \u03B1\u0394T)\xB3 \u2248 a\xB3(1 + 3\u03B1\u0394T) for small \u03B1\u0394T.
Increase in volume = 3a\xB3\u03B1\u0394T.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"Two different wires having lengths L\u2081 and L\u2082 and respective temperature coefficient of linear expansion \u03B1\u2081 and \u03B1\u2082 are joined end-to-end. Then the effective temperature coefficient of linear expansion is",o:["{\u03B1\u2081L\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082L\u2082;L\u2081 + L\u2082}","2\u221A(\u03B1\u2081\u03B1\u2082)","{\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082;2}","4 {\u03B1\u2081\u03B1\u2082;\u03B1\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082} \xB7 {L\u2081L\u2082;(L\u2081 + L\u2082)\xB2}"],c:0,s:`Total expansion = L\u2081\u03B1\u2081\u0394T + L\u2082\u03B1\u2082\u0394T.
For the joined wire of length L\u2081 + L\u2082: (L\u2081 + L\u2082)\u03B1 \u0394T = L\u2081\u03B1\u2081\u0394T + L\u2082\u03B1\u2082\u0394T.
\u03B1 = (\u03B1\u2081L\u2081 + \u03B1\u2082L\u2082)/(L\u2081 + L\u2082).
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A leakproof cylinder of length 1 m, made of a metal which has very low coefficient of expansion is floating vertically in water at 0\xB0C such that its height above the water surface is 20 cm. When the temperature of water is increased to 4\xB0C, the height of the cylinder above the water surface becomes 21 cm. The density of water at T = 4\xB0C, relative to the density at T = 0\xB0C is close to",o:["1.03","1.01","1.26","1.04"],c:1,s:`The cylinder's weight does not change, so the weight of water displaced is the same: \u03C1\u2080 \xD7 (80 cm) \xD7 A = \u03C1\u2084 \xD7 (79 cm) \xD7 A.
Submerged length is 80 cm at 0\xB0C and 79 cm at 4\xB0C.
\u03C1\u2084/\u03C1\u2080 = 80/79 = 1.0127 \u2248 1.01.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"At 40\xB0C, a brass wire of 1 mm radius is hung from the ceiling. A small mass, M is hung from the free end of the wire. When the wire is cooled down from 40\xB0C to 20\xB0C it regains its original length of 0.2 m. The value of M is close to (coefficient of linear expansion and Young's modulus of brass are 10\u207B\u2075/\xB0C and 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2, respectively; g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["1.5 kg","9 kg","0.5 kg","0.9 kg"],c:1,s:`On cooling the wire would shrink by \u03B1L\u0394T. The load M stretches it back by MgL/(AY). Equate the strains: Mg/(AY) = \u03B1\u0394T.
M = AY\u03B1\u0394T/g with A = \u03C0(10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 3.14\xD710\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
M = 3.14\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 20/10 \u2248 6.3 kg.
The nearest option is 9 kg, so the answer is (b) (the official key).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"A uniform cylindrical rod of length L and radius r, is made from a material whose Young's modulus of elasticity equals Y. When this rod is heated by temperature T and simultaneously subjected to a net longitudinal compressive force F, its length remains unchanged. The coefficient of volume expansion, of the material of the rod, is (nearly) equal to",o:["9F/(\u03C0r\xB2YT)","F/(3\u03C0r\xB2YT)","6F/(\u03C0r\xB2YT)","3F/(\u03C0r\xB2YT)"],c:3,s:`Length unchanged: thermal strain = compressive strain, so \u03B1T = F/(\u03C0r\xB2Y).
\u03B1 = F/(\u03C0r\xB2YT).
Coefficient of volume expansion \u03B3 = 3\u03B1 = 3F/(\u03C0r\xB2YT).
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"A rod, of length L at room temperature and uniform area of cross-section A, is made of a metal having coefficient of linear expansion \u03B1/\xB0C. It is observed that an external compressive force F, is applied on each of its ends, prevents any change in the length of the rod, when its temperature rises by \u0394T K. Young's modulus Y, for this metal is",o:["{2F;A\u03B1\u0394T}","{F;A\u03B1\u0394T}","{F;2A\u03B1\u0394T}","{F;A\u03B1(\u0394T \u2212 273)}"],c:1,s:`The thermal strain \u03B1\u0394T is cancelled by the compressive strain F/(AY).
F/(AY) = \u03B1\u0394T.
Y = F/(A\u03B1\u0394T).
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"Two rods A and B of identical dimensions are at temperature 30\xB0C. If A is heated upto 180\xB0C and B upto T \xB0C, then the new lengths are the same. If the ratio of the coefficients of linear expansion of A and B is 4 : 3, then the value of T is",o:["200\xB0C","270\xB0C","230\xB0C","250\xB0C"],c:2,s:`Same original length and same final length, so the expansions are equal: \u03B1_A(180 \u2212 30) = \u03B1_B(T \u2212 30).
(4)(150) = (3)(T \u2212 30).
T \u2212 30 = 200, so T = 230\xB0C.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A thermometer graduated according to a linear scale reads a value x\u2080 when in contact with boiling water, and x\u2080/3 when in contact with ice. What is the temperature of an object in \xB0C, if this thermometer in the contact with the object reads x\u2080/2 ?",o:["35","25","60","40"],c:1,s:`The reading above the ice point as a fraction of the full range is the same as on the Celsius scale.
Fraction = (x\u2080/2 \u2212 x\u2080/3)/(x\u2080 \u2212 x\u2080/3) = (x\u2080/6)/(2x\u2080/3) = 1/4.
Temperature = (1/4) \xD7 100 = 25\xB0C.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"An external pressure P is applied on a cube at 0\xB0C so that it is equally compressed from all sides. K is the bulk modulus of the material of the cube and \u03B1 is its coefficient of linear expansion. Suppose we want to bring the cube to its original size by heating. The temperature should be raised by",o:["{P;3\u03B1K}","{P;\u03B1K}","{3\u03B1;PK}","3PK\u03B1"],c:0,s:`Pressure P compresses the volume by \u0394V/V = P/K.
Heating by \u0394T expands the volume by 3\u03B1\u0394T.
To restore the size: 3\u03B1\u0394T = P/K.
\u0394T = P/(3\u03B1K).
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"A compressive force, F is applied at the two ends of a long thin steel rod. It is heated, simultaneously, such that its temperature increases by \u0394T. The net change in its length is zero. Let l be the length of the rod, A its area of cross-section, Y its Young's modulus, and \u03B1 its coefficient of linear expansion. Then, F is equal to",o:["l\xB2Y\u03B1\u0394T","{AY;\u03B1\u0394T}","AY\u03B1\u0394T","lAY\u03B1\u0394T"],c:2,s:`Thermal strain \u03B1\u0394T is cancelled by the compressive strain F/(AY).
F/(AY) = \u03B1\u0394T.
F = AY\u03B1\u0394T.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"A steel rail of length 5 m and area of cross section 40 cm\xB2 is prevented from expanding along its length while the temperature rises by 10\xB0C. If coefficient of linear expansion and Young's modulus of steel are 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9 and 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2 respectively, the force developed in the rail is approximately",o:["2 \xD7 10\u2079 N","3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N","2 \xD7 10\u2077 N","1 \xD7 10\u2075 N"],c:3,s:`Force = YA\u03B1\u0394T (the length 5 m is not needed).
F = 2\xD710\xB9\xB9 \xD7 40\xD710\u207B\u2074 \xD7 1.2\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 10.
F = 2\xD710\xB9\xB9 \xD7 4\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 1.2\xD710\u207B\u2074 = 9.6\xD710\u2074 N \u2248 1 \xD7 10\u2075 N.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A pendulum clock loses 12 s a day if the temperature is 40\xB0C and gains 4 s a day if the temperature is 20\xB0C. The temperature at which the clock will show correct time, and the co-efficient of linear expansion (\u03B1) of the metal of the pendulum shaft are respectively",o:["25\xB0C; \u03B1 = 1.85 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075/\xB0C","60\xB0C; \u03B1 = 1.85 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074/\xB0C","30\xB0C; \u03B1 = 1.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/\xB0C","55\xB0C; \u03B1 = 1.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2/\xB0C"],c:0,s:`Period T \u221D \u221AL, so \u0394T/T = \xBD\u03B1\u0394\u03B8. The fractional time error per day = \xBD\u03B1(\u03B8 \u2212 \u03B8\u2080), where \u03B8\u2080 is the temperature of correct time.
At 40\xB0C: \xBD\u03B1(40 \u2212 \u03B8\u2080) = 12/86400. At 20\xB0C: \xBD\u03B1(\u03B8\u2080 \u2212 20) = 4/86400.
Dividing: (40 \u2212 \u03B8\u2080)/(\u03B8\u2080 \u2212 20) = 3, so 40 \u2212 \u03B8\u2080 = 3\u03B8\u2080 \u2212 60 and \u03B8\u2080 = 25\xB0C.
Then \xBD\u03B1 \xD7 15 = 12/86400, so \u03B1 = 24/(15 \xD7 86400) = 1.85 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075/\xB0C.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A simple pendulum made of a bob of mass m and a metallic wire of negligible mass has time period 2 s at T = 0\xB0C. If the temperature of the wire is increased and the corresponding change in its time period is plotted against its temperature, the resulting graph is a line of slope S. If the coefficient of linear expansion of metal is \u03B1 then the value of S is",o:["{\u03B1;2}","2\u03B1","\u03B1","{1;\u03B1}"],c:2,s:`T = 2\u03C0\u221A(L/g) \u221D \u221AL, so a rise in temperature \u03B8 changes the period by \u0394T = T\u2080 \xD7 \xBD\u03B1\u03B8.
With T\u2080 = 2 s: \u0394T = 2 \xD7 \xBD\u03B1\u03B8 = \u03B1\u03B8.
The graph of \u0394T against \u03B8 is a line of slope S = \u03B1.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2014",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"The pressure that has to be applied to the ends of a steel wire of length 10 cm to keep its length constant when its temperature is raised by 100\xB0C is (For steel Young's modulus is 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2 and coefficient of thermal expansion is 1.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9)",o:["2.2 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa","2.2 \xD7 10\u2078 Pa","2.2 \xD7 10\u2079 Pa","2.2 \xD7 10\u2077 Pa"],c:1,s:`Pressure = Y \xD7 strain = Y\u03B1\u0394T (the length is not needed).
P = 2\xD710\xB9\xB9 \xD7 1.1\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 100 = 2.2 \xD7 10\u2078 Pa.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"The ratio of the coefficient of volume expansion of a glass container to that of a viscous liquid kept inside the container is 1 : 4. What fraction of the inner volume of the container should the liquid occupy so that the volume of the remaining vacant space will be same at all temperatures?",o:["2 : 5","1 : 4","1 : 64","1 : 8"],c:1,s:`The vacant volume stays constant if the liquid and the container expand by the same volume: V_l \u03B3_l \u0394T = V_c \u03B3_c \u0394T.
V_l/V_c = \u03B3_c/\u03B3_l = 1/4.
The liquid should fill 1 : 4 of the container.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"A wooden wheel of radius R is made of two semicircular parts (see figure). The two parts are held together by a ring made of a metal strip of cross sectional area S and length L. L is slightly less than 2\u03C0R. To fit the ring on the wheel, it is heated so that its temperature rises by \u0394T and it just steps over the wheel. As it cools down to surrounding temperature, it presses the semicircular parts together. If the coefficient of linear expansion of the metal is \u03B1, and its Young's modulus is Y, the force that one part of the wheel applies on the other part is",fig:{w:240,h:200,alt:"A wheel of radius R made of two semicircular halves, surrounded by a metal ring.",d:[["c",120,100,60],["c",120,100,67,"d"],["l",120,40,120,160,"d"],["l",120,100,162,58,"a"],["t",146,92,"R","s"]]},o:["SY\u03B1\u0394T","\u03C0SY\u03B1\u0394T","2SY\u03B1\u0394T","2\u03C0SY\u03B1\u0394T"],c:2,s:`On cooling the ring tries to go back to length L, but the wheel keeps it at about 2\u03C0R, so its strain is \u03B1\u0394T.
Tension in the ring: T = Y S \u03B1\u0394T.
Cut the wheel along the line where the two halves meet. The ring pulls each half with tension T at both ends of that line, so the force between the halves = 2T.
Force = 2SY\u03B1\u0394T.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:"An ideal gas is expanding such that PT\xB2 = constant. The coefficient of volume expansion of the gas is",o:["1/T","2/T","3/T","4/T"],c:2,s:`From PV = nRT, P = nRT/V. Then PT\xB2 = nRT\xB3/V = constant, so V \u221D T\xB3.
Coefficient of volume expansion \u03B3 = (1/V)(dV/dT) = (1/V)(3V/T) = 3/T.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1980",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A metal ball immersed in alcohol weighs W\u2081 at 0\xB0C and W\u2082 at 50\xB0C. The co-efficient of cubical expansion of the metal is less than that of the alcohol. Assuming that the density of the metal is large compared to that of alcohol, it can be shown that",o:["W\u2081 > W\u2082","W\u2081 = W\u2082","W\u2081 < W\u2082","All of these."],c:2,s:`Apparent weight = Mg \u2212 (weight of alcohol displaced) = Mg \u2212 V\u03C1_a g.
On heating, V grows by the factor (1 + \u03B3_m \u0394T) and \u03C1_a falls by (1 + \u03B3_a \u0394T), so V\u03C1_a changes by the factor (1 + (\u03B3_m \u2212 \u03B3_a)\u0394T), which is less than 1 because \u03B3_a > \u03B3_m.
The displaced alcohol weighs less at 50\xB0C, so the apparent weight is greater.
W\u2082 > W\u2081, i.e. W\u2081 < W\u2082.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:"As shown schematically in the figure, two vessels contain water solutions (at temperature T) of potassium permanganate (KMnO\u2084) of different concentrations n\u2081 and n\u2082 (n\u2081 > n\u2082) molecules per unit volume with \u0394n = (n\u2081 \u2212 n\u2082) << n\u2081. When they are connected by a tube of small length l and cross-sectional area S, KMnO\u2084 starts to diffuse from the left to the right vessel through the tube. Consider the collection of molecules to behave as dilute ideal gases and the difference in their partial pressure in the two vessels causing the diffusion. The speed v of the molecules is limited by the viscous force \u2212\u03B2v on each molecule, where \u03B2 is a constant. Neglecting all terms of the order (\u0394n)\xB2, which of the following is/are correct? (k_B is the Boltzmann constant)",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Two vessels holding KMnO4 solutions of concentration n1 on the left and n2 on the right, joined by a short tube of length l and cross-section S.",d:[["r",20,30,100,140],["r",220,30,100,140],["l",120,108,220,108],["l",120,138,220,138],["l",20,60,120,60,"d"],["l",220,60,320,60,"d"],["t",70,100,"n\u2081","m"],["t",270,100,"n\u2082","m"],["t",170,20,"n\u2081 > n\u2082","ms"],["t",170,100,"S","m"],["l",135,123,205,123,"a"],["l",120,155,220,155,"b"],["t",170,175,"l","m"]]},o:["The force causing the molecules to move across the tube is \u0394n k_B T S.","Force balance implies n\u2081\u03B2vl = \u0394n k_B T.","Total number of molecules going across the tube per sec is {\u0394n;l}\xB7{k_B T;\u03B2}\xB7S.","Rate of molecules getting transferred through the tube does not change with time."],c:[0,1,2],s:`Treat the solute as a dilute gas, so pressure p = n k_B T.
Pressure difference = \u0394n k_B T, so force on the molecules in the tube = \u0394n k_B T S. (a) is correct.
The tube holds about n\u2081 S l molecules, each feeling drag \u03B2v. Balance: n\u2081 S l \u03B2v = \u0394n k_B T S, so n\u2081\u03B2vl = \u0394n k_B T. (b) is correct.
Molecules crossing per second = n\u2081 v S = {\u0394n k_B T;\u03B2 l}\xB7S = {\u0394n;l}\xB7{k_B T;\u03B2}\xB7S. (c) is correct.
As diffusion goes on, \u0394n decreases with time, so the rate decreases. (d) is wrong.
Answer: (a), (b), (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A bimetallic strip is formed out of two identical strips one of copper and the other of brass. The co-efficients of linear expansion of the two metals are \u03B1_C and \u03B1_B. On heating, the temperature of the strip goes up by \u0394T and the strip bends to form an arc of radius of curvature R. Then R is",o:["proportional to \u0394T","inversely proportional to \u0394T","proportional to |\u03B1_B \u2212 \u03B1_C|","inversely proportional to |\u03B1_B \u2212 \u03B1_C|"],c:[1,3],s:`Let each strip have thickness t. After heating, the two strips have lengths l(1 + \u03B1_B\u0394T) and l(1 + \u03B1_C\u0394T).
The strips lie one over the other, so their arcs differ in radius by t. For the same angle \u03B8, the difference in arc length is t\u03B8.
So t\u03B8 = l|\u03B1_B \u2212 \u03B1_C|\u0394T. Also l \u2248 R\u03B8, so \u03B8 = l/R.
This gives R = {t;|\u03B1_B \u2212 \u03B1_C|\u0394T}.
So R is inversely proportional to \u0394T and inversely proportional to |\u03B1_B \u2212 \u03B1_C|. Answer: (b) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"Two rods of different materials having co-efficients of thermal expansion \u03B1\u2081, \u03B1\u2082 and Young's modulii Y\u2081, Y\u2082 respectively are fixed between two rigid massive walls. The rods are heated such that they undergo the same increase in temperature. There is no bending of the rods. If \u03B1\u2081 : \u03B1\u2082 = 2 : 3, the thermal stresses developed in the two rods are equal provided Y\u2081 : Y\u2082 is equal to",o:["2 : 3","1 : 1","3 : 2","4 : 9"],c:2,s:`A rod that cannot expand develops thermal stress = Y\u03B1 \u0394T.
Both rods have the same \u0394T, so the stresses are equal when Y\u2081\u03B1\u2081 = Y\u2082\u03B1\u2082.
Y\u2081 : Y\u2082 = \u03B1\u2082 : \u03B1\u2081 = 3 : 2.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:`A steel rod of length 1 m and cross sectional areas 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2 is heated from 0\xB0C to 200\xB0C without being allowed to extend or bend. The compressive tension produced in the rod is ____ \xD7 10\u2074 N.
(Given Young's modulus of steel = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2, coefficient of linear expansion = 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9)`,n:4,s:`The rod cannot expand, so the thermal strain is converted to compressive stress.
Stress = Y \u03B1 \u0394T, so force F = Y A \u03B1 \u0394T.
F = 2\xD710\xB9\xB9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 200 = 4\xD710\u2074 N.
Answer: 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A hole is drilled in a metal sheet. At 27\xB0C, the diameter of hole is 5 cm. When the sheet is heated to 177\xB0C, the change in diameter of hole is d \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 cm. The value of d will be ____ if coefficient of linear expansion of the metal is 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xB0C\u207B\xB9.",n:12,s:`A hole expands as if it were made of the metal itself, so its diameter grows like any linear dimension.
\u0394D = D \u03B1 \u0394T = 5 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 (177 \u2212 27).
\u0394D = 5 \xD7 1.6\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 150 = 1.2\xD710\u207B\xB2 cm = 12 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 cm.
Answer: d = 12.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A faulty thermometer reads 5\xB0C in melting ice and 95\xB0C in steam. The correct temperature on absolute scale will be ____ K when the faulty thermometer reads 41\xB0C.",n:313,s:`The faulty scale has 95 \u2212 5 = 90 divisions between the fixed points, while the true scale has 100.
True temperature = {41 \u2212 5;95 \u2212 5} \xD7 100 = {36;90} \xD7 100 = 40\xB0C.
In kelvin: 40 + 273 = 313 K.
Answer: 313.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:`A unit scale is to be prepared whose length does not change with temperature and remains 20 cm, using a bimetallic strip made of brass and iron each of different length. The length of both components would change in such a way that difference between their lengths remains constant. If length of brass is 40 cm and length of iron will be ____ cm.
(\u03B1_iron = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9 and \u03B1_brass = 1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9)`,n:60,s:`The difference of lengths stays constant, so both lengths must expand by the same amount: L_b \u03B1_b \u0394T = L_i \u03B1_i \u0394T.
L_i = L_b \xD7 {\u03B1_b;\u03B1_i} = 40 \xD7 {1.8;1.2} = 60 cm.
Check: 60 \u2212 40 = 20 cm, as required.
Answer: 60.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"A rod CD of thermal resistance 10.0 K W\u207B\xB9 is joined at the middle of an identical rod AB as shown in figure. The ends A, B and D are maintained at 200\xB0C, 100\xB0C and 125\xB0C respectively. The heat current in CD is P watt. The value of P is ____.",fig:{w:300,h:180,alt:"Rod AB horizontal with A at 200 degrees C on the left and B at 100 degrees C on the right; rod CD joined at the middle point C going down to D at 125 degrees C.",d:[["l",40,60,260,60,"B"],["l",150,60,150,135,"B"],["t",40,45,"A","m"],["t",40,80,"200\xB0C","m"],["t",260,45,"B","m"],["t",260,80,"100\xB0C","m"],["t",160,52,"C"],["t",160,138,"D"],["t",150,160,"125\xB0C","m"]]},n:2,s:`Rod AB has resistance 10 K/W, so each half (AC and CB) has 5 K/W. Rod CD has 10 K/W.
Let the temperature of C be T. Heat entering C from A equals heat leaving to B and D.
{200 \u2212 T;5} = {T \u2212 100;5} + {T \u2212 125;10}
2(200 \u2212 T) = 2(T \u2212 100) + (T \u2212 125), so 725 = 5T and T = 145\xB0C.
P = {145 \u2212 125;10} = 2 W.
Answer: 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:`The area of cross-section of a railway track is 0.01 m\xB2. The temperature variation is 10\xB0C. Coefficient of linear expansion of material of track is 10\u207B\u2075/\xB0C. The energy stored per metre in the track is ____ J/m.
(Young's modulus of material of track is 10\xB9\xB9 Nm\u207B\xB2)`,n:5,s:`The track cannot expand, so thermal strain = \u03B1 \u0394T = 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 10 = 10\u207B\u2074.
Energy per unit volume = \xBD Y (strain)\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 = 5\xD710\xB2 J/m\xB3.
Volume per metre = A \xD7 1 = 0.01 m\xB3.
Energy per metre = 5\xD710\xB2 \xD7 0.01 = 5 J/m.
Answer: 5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic moduli and wires",q:"A uniform metallic wire is elongated by 0.04 m when subjected to a linear force F. The elongation, if its length and diameter is doubled and subjected to the same force will be ____ cm.",n:2,s:`Elongation \u0394l = {F l;A Y}.
Doubling the diameter makes the area 4 times. Doubling the length doubles l.
New \u0394l = {F(2l);(4A)Y} = \xBD \xD7 \u0394l.
New elongation = \xBD \xD7 0.04 m = 0.02 m = 2 cm.
Answer: 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A bakelite beaker has volume capacity of 500 cc at 30\xB0C. When it is partially filled with V_m volume (at 30\xB0C) of mercury, it is found that the unfilled volume of the beaker remains constant as the temperature is varied. If \u03B3_(beaker) = 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xB0C\u207B\xB9 and \u03B3_(mercury) = 1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xB0C\u207B\xB9, where \u03B3 is the coefficient of volume expansion, then V_m (in cc) is close to ____.",n:20,s:`The unfilled volume stays constant, so the beaker and the mercury must expand by equal volumes.
V_beaker \u03B3_beaker \u0394T = V_m \u03B3_mercury \u0394T.
V_m = {500 \xD7 6\xD710\u207B\u2076;1.5\xD710\u207B\u2074} = 20 cc.
Answer: 20.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A non-isotropic solid metal cube has coefficients of linear expansion as 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xB0C\u207B\xB9 along the x-axis and 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xB0C\u207B\xB9 along the y and the z-axis. If the coefficient of volume expansion of the solid is C \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xB0C\u207B\xB9 then the value of C is ____.",n:60,s:`For small expansions the volume coefficient is the sum of the three linear coefficients: \u03B3 = \u03B1_x + \u03B1_y + \u03B1_z.
\u03B3 = 5\xD710\u207B\u2075 + 5\xD710\u207B\u2076 + 5\xD710\u207B\u2076 = 60\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xB0C\u207B\xB9.
Answer: C = 60.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:`A cylindrical vessel of height 500 mm has an orifice (small hole) at its bottom. The orifice is initially closed and water is filled in it up to height H. Now the top is completely sealed with a cap and the orifice at the bottom is opened. Some water comes out from the orifice and the water level in the vessel becomes steady with height of water column being 200 mm. Find the fall in height (in mm) of water level due to opening of the orifice.
[Take atmospheric pressure = 1.0 \xD7 10\u2075 N/m\xB2, density of water = 1000 kg/m\xB3 and g = 10 m/s\xB2. Neglect any effect of surface tension.]`,n:6,s:`At the start the trapped air is at atmospheric pressure P\u2080 = 10\u2075 Pa and has height (500 \u2212 H) mm.
In the steady state the water column is 200 mm and the air column is 500 \u2212 200 = 300 mm. At the orifice the water pressure equals P\u2080, so air pressure P = P\u2080 \u2212 \u03C1gh = 10\u2075 \u2212 1000 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.2 = 0.98\xD710\u2075 Pa.
Boyle's law (same cross-section): 10\u2075 \xD7 (500 \u2212 H) = 0.98\xD710\u2075 \xD7 300, so 500 \u2212 H = 294 and H = 206 mm.
Fall in level = 206 \u2212 200 = 6 mm.
Answer: 6.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A : When a rod lying freely is heated, no thermal stress is developed in it.
Reason R : On heating, the length of the rod increases.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A is true but R is false.","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is false but R is true."],c:2,s:`A free rod is able to expand, so there is no thermal strain and no thermal stress. A is true.
On heating, the length of a rod does increase. R is true.
But the stress is absent because the expansion is not prevented, not simply because the length increases. (If the ends were fixed, the length would still tend to increase, yet stress would appear.)
So R does not correctly explain A.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"pst",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A cubical block of co-efficient of linear expansion \u03B1_s is submerged partially inside a liquid of co-efficient of volume expansion \u03B3_l. On increasing the temperature of the system \u0394T, the height of the cube inside the liquid remains unchanged. Find the relation between \u03B1_s and \u03B3_l.",a:"\u03B3_l = 2\u03B1_s",s:`Let the side of the cube be a, its mass m, and the submerged depth h. The block floats, so weight = buoyancy: m g = \u03C1_l a\xB2 h g.
So \u03C1_l a\xB2 h = m. On heating, m and h do not change, so \u03C1_l a\xB2 must stay the same.
After heating: a\xB2 becomes a\xB2(1 + 2\u03B1_s\u0394T) and \u03C1_l becomes {\u03C1_l;1 + \u03B3_l\u0394T}.
So {1 + 2\u03B1_s\u0394T;1 + \u03B3_l\u0394T} = 1.
Hence \u03B3_l = 2\u03B1_s.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"psv",st:"Stokes' law and terminal velocity",q:"A small spherical body of radius r is falling under gravity in a viscous medium. Due to friction the medium gets heated. How does the rate of heating depend on radius of body when it attains terminal velocity?",a:"Rate of heating \u221D r\u2075",s:`At terminal velocity the viscous drag equals the effective weight (weight \u2212 buoyancy), so the work done by gravity is all turned into heat.
Rate of heating = drag \xD7 speed = 6\u03C0\u03B7r v_t \xD7 v_t = 6\u03C0\u03B7 r v_t\xB2.
Terminal velocity v_t = {2r\xB2(\u03C1 \u2212 \u03C3)g;9\u03B7}, so v_t \u221D r\xB2.
Rate \u221D r \xD7 (r\xB2)\xB2 = r\u2075.
Answer: the rate of heating is proportional to r\u2075.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"The apparatus shown in the figure consists of four glass columns connected by horizontal sections. The height of two central columns B and C are 49 cm each. The two outer columns A and D are open to the atmosphere. A and C are maintained at a temperature of 95\xB0C while the columns B and D are maintained at 5\xB0C. The height of the liquid in A and D measured from the base are 52.8 cm and 51 cm respectively. Determine the co-efficient of thermal expansion of the liquid.",fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"Four connected glass columns A, B, C, D: A and B joined at the bottom, B and C joined at the top, C and D joined at the bottom. A and C at 95 degrees C, B and D at 5 degrees C; A and D are open at the top.",d:[["p","M40 20 V190 H130 V35 H210 V190 H300 V20"],["p","M60 20 V175 H110 V15 H230 V175 H280 V20"],["l",40,32,60,32,"d"],["l",110,43,130,43,"d"],["l",210,43,230,43,"d"],["l",280,37,300,37,"d"],["t",46,36,"52.8 cm","es"],["t",304,41,"51 cm","s"],["t",170,60,"B, C: 49 cm","ms"],["t",50,208,"A 95\xB0C","m"],["t",120,208,"B 5\xB0C","m"],["t",220,208,"C 95\xB0C","m"],["t",290,208,"D 5\xB0C","m"]]},a:"\u03B3 \u2248 1.96 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xB0C\u207B\xB9 (\u2248 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xB0C\u207B\xB9)",s:`B and C are joined at the top, so the gas pressure p above the liquid in B and C is the same. Let \u03C1\u2089\u2085 and \u03C1\u2085 be the liquid densities at 95\xB0C and 5\xB0C.
Left U (A and B), same level at the base: P\u2080 + \u03C1\u2089\u2085 g (52.8) = p + \u03C1\u2085 g (49).
Right U (C and D): p + \u03C1\u2089\u2085 g (49) = P\u2080 + \u03C1\u2085 g (51).
Add the two: \u03C1\u2089\u2085 (101.8) = \u03C1\u2085 (100), so {\u03C1\u2085;\u03C1\u2089\u2085} = 1.018.
For small \u03B3, \u03C1_t \u2248 \u03C1\u2080(1 \u2212 \u03B3t): 100(1 \u2212 5\u03B3) = 101.8(1 \u2212 95\u03B3), so 9171\u03B3 = 1.8.
\u03B3 \u2248 1.96 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xB0C\u207B\xB9 (the exact form {1 + 95\u03B3;1 + 5\u03B3} = 1.018 gives 89.91\u03B3 = 0.018, \u03B3 \u2248 2.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xB0C\u207B\xB9).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"A thin rod of negligible mass and area of cross-section 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2, suspended vertically from one end, has a length of 0.5 m at 100\xB0C. The rod is cooled to 0\xB0C, but prevented from contracting by attaching a mass at the lower end. Find (i) this mass, and (ii) the energy stored in the rod. Given for the rod, Young's modulus = 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2, coefficient of linear expansion = 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9 and g = 10 m/s\xB2.",a:"(i) 40 kg   (ii) 0.1 J",s:`The mass must stretch the rod by exactly the amount it tends to contract: Mg = Y A \u03B1 \u0394T.
Mg = 10\xB9\xB9 \xD7 4\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 100 = 400 N, so M = 40 kg.
Stretch \u0394l = l \u03B1 \u0394T = 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 100 = 5\xD710\u207B\u2074 m.
Energy stored = \xBD F \u0394l = \xBD \xD7 400 \xD7 5\xD710\u207B\u2074 = 0.1 J.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1979",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:`A composite rod is made by joining a copper rod, end to end, with a second rod of different material but of the same cross-section. At 25\xB0C, the composite rod is 1 metre in length, of which the length of the copper rod is 30 cm. At 125\xB0C the length of the composite rod increases by 1.91 mm. When the composite rod is not allowed to expand by holding it between two rigid walls, it is found that the length of the two constituents do not change with the rise in temperature. Find the Young's modulus and the coefficient of linear expansion of the second rod.
[Given : Coefficient of linear expansion of copper = 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 per\xB0C, Young's modulus of copper = 1.3 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2]`,a:"\u03B1\u2082 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 per \xB0C,  Y\u2082 = 1.105 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2",s:`Free expansion of copper (30 cm): 300 mm \xD7 1.7\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 100 = 0.51 mm.
So the second rod (70 cm) expands 1.91 \u2212 0.51 = 1.40 mm: 700 \xD7 \u03B1\u2082 \xD7 100 = 1.40, giving \u03B1\u2082 = 2\xD710\u207B\u2075 per \xB0C.
Between rigid walls neither length changes, so each rod is compressed by its free expansion. The force on both is the same and the area is the same, so the stresses are equal.
Y\u2081 \u03B1\u2081 \u0394T = Y\u2082 \u03B1\u2082 \u0394T, so Y\u2082 = {1.3\xD710\xB9\xB9 \xD7 1.7\xD710\u207B\u2075;2\xD710\u207B\u2075} = 1.105\xD710\xB9\xB9 N/m\xB2.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1978",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"A sinker of weight W\u2080 has an apparent weight W\u2081 when placed in a liquid at a temperature T\u2081 and W\u2082 when weighed in the same liquid at a temperature T\u2082. The coefficient of cubical expansion of the material of the sinker is \u03B2. What is the coefficient of volume expansion of the liquid?",a:"\u03B3 = {(W\u2082 \u2212 W\u2081) + \u03B2(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081)(W\u2080 \u2212 W\u2081);(W\u2080 \u2212 W\u2082)(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081)}",s:`Apparent weight = W\u2080 \u2212 buoyant force, so the buoyant force is B\u2081 = W\u2080 \u2212 W\u2081 at T\u2081 and B\u2082 = W\u2080 \u2212 W\u2082 at T\u2082.
Buoyant force = V \u03C1_l g. From T\u2081 to T\u2082 the sinker's volume becomes V(1 + \u03B2\u0394T) and the liquid density becomes {\u03C1;1 + \u03B3\u0394T}, where \u0394T = T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081.
So {B\u2082;B\u2081} = {1 + \u03B2\u0394T;1 + \u03B3\u0394T}, that is {W\u2080 \u2212 W\u2082;W\u2080 \u2212 W\u2081} = {1 + \u03B2\u0394T;1 + \u03B3\u0394T}.
Solve: 1 + \u03B3\u0394T = (1 + \u03B2\u0394T){W\u2080 \u2212 W\u2081;W\u2080 \u2212 W\u2082}.
\u03B3 = {(W\u2082 \u2212 W\u2081) + \u03B2(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081)(W\u2080 \u2212 W\u2081);(W\u2080 \u2212 W\u2082)(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081)}.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1978",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:"A column of mercury of length 10 cm is contained in the middle of a horizontal tube of length 1 m which is closed at both the ends. The two equal lengths contain air at standard atmospheric pressure of 0.76 m of mercury. The tube is now turned to vertical position. By what distance will the column of mercury be displaced?",a:"2.95 cm",s:`Each air column is (100 \u2212 10)/2 = 45 cm long at 76 cm of Hg.
In the vertical tube the mercury moves down by x. Upper air: length 45 + x, pressure P. Lower air: length 45 \u2212 x, pressure P + 10 (in cm of Hg).
Boyle's law for each: P(45 + x) = 76 \xD7 45 and (P + 10)(45 \u2212 x) = 76 \xD7 45.
Eliminate P: 10(45\xB2 \u2212 x\xB2) = 2 \xD7 3420 x, so x\xB2 + 684x \u2212 2025 = 0.
x = {\u2212684 + \u221A(684\xB2 + 4 \xD7 2025);2} \u2248 2.95 cm.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"psh",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Water in a closed tube (see figure) is heated with a lamp placed vertically below the arm A. Water will begin to circulate along the tube in counter clockwise direction.",fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"A closed rectangular water tube with arm A on the left and arm B on the right, and a lamp flame placed below arm A.",d:[["r",90,30,120,120],["r",110,50,80,80],["t",80,95,"A","e"],["t",220,95,"B"],["p","M100 172 C90 162 96 154 100 146 C104 154 110 162 100 172 Z","f"],["t",100,192,"lamp","m"]]},o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`The lamp heats the water in arm A. Hot water is less dense and rises up arm A.
It then moves along the top of the tube towards B, cools, and sinks down arm B. It returns along the bottom to A.
So the circulation is up on the left, across the top to the right, and down on the right. That is clockwise, not counter clockwise.
The statement is false.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"pst",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A gas thermometer is used as a standard thermometer for measurement of temperature. When the gas container of the thermometer is immersed in water at its triple point 273.16 K, the pressure in the gas thermometer reads 3.0 \xD7 10\u2074 N/m\xB2. When the gas container of the same thermometer is immersed in another system, the gas pressure reads 3.5 \xD7 10\u2074 N/m\xB2. The temperature of this system is therefore ____ \xB0C.",a:"45.6 \xB0C",s:`For a constant-volume gas thermometer, T \u221D P.
T = 273.16 \xD7 {3.5\xD710\u2074;3.0\xD710\u2074} \u2248 318.7 K.
In \xB0C this is about 318.7 \u2212 273 \u2248 45.6 \xB0C (the key's value; using 273.15 gives 45.5).
Answer: about 45.6 \xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"Consider a rectangular sheet of solid material of length l = 9 cm and width d = 4 cm. The coefficient of linear expansion is \u03B1 = 3.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9 at room temperature and one atmospheric pressure. The mass of sheet m = 0.1 kg and the specific heat capacity C_V = 900 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9. If the amount of heat supplied to the material is 8.1 \xD7 10\xB2 J then change in area of the rectangular sheet is",o:["2.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2","6.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m\xB2","4.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m\xB2","3.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m\xB2"],c:0,s:`Temperature rise: \u0394T = {Q;m C} = {810;0.1 \xD7 900} = 9 K.
Area expansion: \u0394A = 2\u03B1 A \u0394T.
A = 9 cm \xD7 4 cm = 36 cm\xB2 = 36\xD710\u207B\u2074 m\xB2.
\u0394A = 2 \xD7 3.1\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 36\xD710\u207B\u2074 \xD7 9 \u2248 2.0\xD710\u207B\u2076 m\xB2.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`Water falls from a height of 200 m into a pool. Calculate the rise in temperature of the water assuming no heat dissipation from the water in the pool.
(Take g = 10 m/s\xB2, specific heat of water = 4200 J/(kg K))`,o:["0.23 K","0.36 K","0.48 K","0.14 K"],c:2,s:`The loss of potential energy of the water becomes heat: m g h = m s \u0394T.
\u0394T = {g h;s} = {10 \xD7 200;4200} \u2248 0.476 K.
This is about 0.48 K.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:`An amount of ice of mass 10\u207B\xB3 kg and temperature \u221210\xB0C is transformed to vapour of temperature 110\xB0C by applying heat. The total amount of work required for this conversion is,
(Take, specific heat of ice = 2100 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9, specific heat of water = 4180 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9, specific heat of steam = 1920 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9, Latent heat of ice = 3.35 \xD7 10\u2075 J kg\u207B\xB9 and Latent heat of steam = 2.25 \xD7 10\u2076 J kg\u207B\xB9)`,o:["3043 J","3024 J","3003 J","3022 J"],c:0,s:`Add the heat of each stage for m = 10\u207B\xB3 kg.
Ice \u221210\xB0C to 0\xB0C: 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 2100 \xD7 10 = 21 J.
Melting: 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 3.35\xD710\u2075 = 335 J.
Water 0\xB0C to 100\xB0C: 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 4180 \xD7 100 = 418 J.
Vaporisation: 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 2.25\xD710\u2076 = 2250 J.
Steam 100\xB0C to 110\xB0C: 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 1920 \xD7 10 = 19.2 J.
Total = 21 + 335 + 418 + 2250 + 19.2 = 3043.2 J \u2248 3043 J. Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:`A gun fires a lead bullet of temperature 300 K into a wooden block. The bullet having melting temperature of 600 K penetrates into the block and melts down. If the total heat required for the process is 625 J, then the mass of the bullet is ____ grams.
(Latent heat of fusion of lead = 2.5 \xD7 10\u2074 JKg\u207B\xB9 and specific heat capacity of lead = 125 J Kg\u207B\xB9)`,o:["15","5","10","20"],c:2,s:`Heat = heat to warm the bullet from 300 K to 600 K + heat to melt it.
Q = m(125 \xD7 300 + 2.5\xD710\u2074) = m(37500 + 25000) = 62500 m.
625 = 62500 m, so m = 0.01 kg = 10 g.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"A block of ice at \u221210\xB0C is slowly heated and converted to steam at 100\xB0C. Which of the following curves represent the phenomenon qualitatively?",fig:{w:340,h:265,alt:"Four temperature versus heat-supplied graphs (a) to (d), each showing a different pattern of sloping and flat segments.",d:[["l",30,122,30,22,"a"],["l",30,122,165,122,"a"],["t",52,34,"(a)","m"],["t",26,30,"T","e"],["t",165,136,"Heat","e"],["p","M34 120 L66 120 L82 90 L110 90 L110 56 L140 56 L156 36"],["l",200,122,200,22,"a"],["l",200,122,335,122,"a"],["t",222,34,"(b)","m"],["t",196,30,"T","e"],["t",335,136,"Heat","e"],["p","M204 120 L222 104 L224 66 L296 66 L326 36"],["l",30,242,30,142,"a"],["l",30,242,165,242,"a"],["t",52,154,"(c)","m"],["t",26,150,"T","e"],["t",165,256,"Heat","e"],["p","M38 240 L56 212 L86 212 L108 178 L140 178"],["l",200,242,200,142,"a"],["l",200,242,335,242,"a"],["t",222,154,"(d)","m"],["t",196,150,"T","e"],["t",335,256,"Heat","e"],["p","M205 240 L230 240 L252 212 L284 212 L326 176"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:2,s:`Follow the heating in stages: ice warms from \u221210\xB0C to 0\xB0C (a sloping line from the start), melts at 0\xB0C (flat), water warms from 0\xB0C to 100\xB0C (steeper in heat but gentler in temperature, since c_water is larger), then boils at 100\xB0C (flat, the longest part, because L_vaporisation is much larger than L_fusion). The process ends at steam at 100\xB0C, so the graph stops on the flat part.
Only graph (c) has this shape: slope, short flat, slope, long flat. Graph (a) starts with a flat part and (b), (d) lack the second flat part. Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:`An ice cube of dimensions 60 cm \xD7 50 cm \xD7 20 cm is placed in an insulation box of wall thickness 1 cm. The box keeping the ice cube at 0\xB0C of temperature is brought to a room of temperature 40\xB0C. The rate of melting of ice is approximately
(Latent heat of fusion of ice is 3.4 \xD7 10\u2075 J kg\u207B\xB9 and thermal conductivity of insulated wall is 0.05 W m\u207B\xB9\xB0C\u207B\xB9)`,o:["61 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 kg s\u207B\xB9","61 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 kg s\u207B\xB9","208 kg s\u207B\xB9","30 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 kg s\u207B\xB9"],c:1,s:`Surface area of the box = 2(0.6\xD70.5 + 0.5\xD70.2 + 0.6\xD70.2) = 1.04 m\xB2.
Heat flow rate = {k A \u0394T;d} = {0.05 \xD7 1.04 \xD7 40;0.01} = 208 W.
This heat melts ice: {dm;dt} = {208;3.4\xD710\u2075} \u2248 6.1\xD710\u207B\u2074 kg/s.
That is 61 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 kg/s. Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`A 100 g of iron nail is hit by a 1.5 kg hammer striking at a velocity of 60 m s\u207B\xB9. What will be the rise in temperature of the nail if one fourth of energy of the hammer goes into heating the nail?
[Specific heat capacity of iron = 0.42 J g\u207B\xB9 \xB0C\u207B\xB9]`,o:["675\xB0C","1600\xB0C","16.07\xB0C","6.75\xB0C"],c:2,s:`Kinetic energy of the hammer = \xBD \xD7 1.5 \xD7 60\xB2 = 2700 J.
Heat given to the nail = \xBC \xD7 2700 = 675 J.
m c \u0394T = 675: 100 \xD7 0.42 \xD7 \u0394T = 675.
\u0394T = {675;42} \u2248 16.07\xB0C.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:`A copper block of mass 5.0 kg is heated to a temperature of 500\xB0C and is placed on a large ice block. What is the maximum amount of ice that can melt?
[Specific heat of copper : 0.39 J g\u207B\xB9 \xB0C\u207B\xB9 and latent heat of fusion of water : 335 J g\u207B\xB9]`,o:["1.5 kg","5.8 kg","2.9 kg","3.8 kg"],c:2,s:`The most ice melts when the copper cools all the way to 0\xB0C.
Heat given out by copper = 5000 g \xD7 0.39 \xD7 500 = 9.75\xD710\u2075 J.
Mass of ice melted = {9.75\xD710\u2075;335} g \u2248 2910 g \u2248 2.9 kg.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:`A lead bullet penetrates into a solid object and melts. Assuming that 40% of its kinetic energy is used to heat it, the initial speed of bullet is
(Given, initial temperature of the bullet = 127\xB0C,
Melting point of the bullet = 327\xB0C,
Latent heat of fusion of lead = 2.5 \xD7 10\u2074 J kg\u207B\xB9,
Specific heat capacity of lead = 125 J/kg K)`,o:["125 m s\u207B\xB9","500 m s\u207B\xB9","250 m s\u207B\xB9","600 m s\u207B\xB9"],c:1,s:`Heat needed per kg = c \u0394T + L = 125 \xD7 200 + 2.5\xD710\u2074 = 5\xD710\u2074 J/kg.
40% of the kinetic energy supplies it: 0.4 \xD7 \xBD v\xB2 = 5\xD710\u2074.
0.2 v\xB2 = 5\xD710\u2074, so v\xB2 = 2.5\xD710\u2075 and v = 500 m/s.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:"The temperature of equal masses of three different liquids x, y and z are 10\xB0C, 20\xB0C and 30\xB0C respectively. The temperature of mixture when x is mixed with y is 16\xB0C and that when y is mixed with z is 26\xB0C. The temperature of mixture when x and z are mixed will be",o:["25.62\xB0C","20.28\xB0C","28.32\xB0C","23.84\xB0C"],c:3,s:`For equal masses, heat lost = heat gained gives s\u2081\u0394T\u2081 = s\u2082\u0394T\u2082.
x and y: s_x(16 \u2212 10) = s_y(20 \u2212 16), so s_x : s_y = 4 : 6 = 2 : 3.
y and z: s_y(26 \u2212 20) = s_z(30 \u2212 26), so s_y : s_z = 4 : 6 = 2 : 3.
So s_x : s_y : s_z = 4 : 6 : 9.
x and z: 4(T \u2212 10) = 9(30 \u2212 T), so 13T = 310 and T \u2248 23.84\xB0C.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`The height of victoria falls is 63 m. What is the difference in temperature of water at the top and at the bottom of fall?
[Given 1 cal = 4.2 J and specific heat of water = 1 cal g\u207B\xB9 \xB0C\u207B\xB9] (take g = 9.8 m s\u207B\xB2)`,o:["14.76\xB0C","1.476\xB0C","0.147\xB0C","0.014\xB0C"],c:2,s:`The potential energy lost becomes heat: m g h = m s \u0394T.
s = 1 cal/(g\xB0C) = 4200 J/(kg\xB0C).
\u0394T = {g h;s} = {9.8 \xD7 63;4200} = {617.4;4200} \u2248 0.147\xB0C.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psp",st:"Pressure and Pascal's law",q:"The pressure acting on a submarine is 3 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa at a certain depth. If the depth is doubled, the percentage increase in the pressure acting on the submarine would be (Assume that atmospheric pressure is 1 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa, density of water is 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["{3;200} %","{200;3} %","{200;5} %","{5;200} %"],c:1,s:`Total pressure P = P\u2080 + \u03C1gh, so \u03C1gh = 3\xD710\u2075 \u2212 1\xD710\u2075 = 2\xD710\u2075 Pa at the first depth.
At double the depth \u03C1gh = 4\xD710\u2075 Pa, so the pressure becomes 1\xD710\u2075 + 4\xD710\u2075 = 5\xD710\u2075 Pa.
Increase = 5\xD710\u2075 \u2212 3\xD710\u2075 = 2\xD710\u2075 Pa.
Percentage increase = {2\xD710\u2075;3\xD710\u2075} \xD7 100 = {200;3} %.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:`A calorimeter of water equivalent 20 g contains 180 g of water at 25\xB0C. 'm' grams of steam at 100\xB0C is mixed till the temperature of the mixture is 31\xB0C. The value of 'm' is close to
(Latent heat of water = 540 cal g\u207B\xB9, specific heat of water = 1 cal g\u207B\xB9\xB0C\u207B\xB9)`,o:["2 g","4 g","3.2 g","2.6 g"],c:0,s:`Heat gained by calorimeter and water = (20 + 180) \xD7 1 \xD7 (31 \u2212 25) = 1200 cal.
Heat lost by steam = m \xD7 540 (condensing) + m \xD7 1 \xD7 (100 \u2212 31) = 609 m cal.
609 m = 1200, so m \u2248 1.97 g \u2248 2 g.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"The specific heat of water = 4200 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 and the latent heat of ice = 3.4 \xD7 10\u2075 J kg\u207B\xB9. 100 grams of ice at 0\xB0C is placed in 200 g of water at 25\xB0C. The amount of ice that will melt as the temperature of water reaches 0\xB0C is close to (in grams)",o:["64.6","69.3","61.7","63.8"],c:2,s:`Heat given out when 200 g of water cools from 25\xB0C to 0\xB0C = 0.2 \xD7 4200 \xD7 25 = 21000 J.
This melts a mass m of ice: m \xD7 3.4\xD710\u2075 = 21000.
m = {21000;3.4\xD710\u2075} kg \u2248 0.0618 kg \u2248 61.7 g (the 100 g of ice is enough, so not all of it melts).
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:"A bullet of mass 5 g, travelling with a speed of 210 m/s, strikes a fixed wooden target. One half of its kinetic energy is converted into heat in the bullet while the other half is converted into heat in the wood. The rise of temperature of the bullet if the specific heat of its material is 0.030 cal/(g\xB0C) (1 cal = 4.2 \xD7 10\u2077 ergs) close to",o:["87.5\xB0C","83.3\xB0C","119.2\xB0C","38.4\xB0C"],c:0,s:`Kinetic energy = \xBD \xD7 0.005 \xD7 210\xB2 = 110.25 J. Half of this heats the bullet: 55.125 J.
In calories: 55.125 / 4.2 = 13.125 cal.
Mass of bullet = 5 g, so heat capacity = 5 \xD7 0.030 = 0.15 cal/\xB0C.
\u0394T = {13.125;0.15} = 87.5\xB0C.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:"A massless spring (k = 800 N/m), attached with a mass (500 g) is completely immersed in 1 kg of water. The spring is stretched by 2 cm and released so that it starts vibrating. What would be the order of magnitude of the change in temperature of water when the vibrations stop completely? (Assume that the water container and spring receive negligible heat and specific heat of mass = 400 J/kg K, specific heat of water = 4184 J/kg K)",o:["10\u207B\u2074 K","10\u207B\xB3 K","10\u207B\xB9 K","10\u207B\u2075 K"],c:3,s:`The energy stored in the stretched spring is finally turned into heat: \xBD k x\xB2 = \xBD \xD7 800 \xD7 (0.02)\xB2 = 0.16 J.
This heat warms the water (1 kg) and the mass (0.5 kg).
Heat capacity = 1 \xD7 4184 + 0.5 \xD7 400 = 4384 J/K.
\u0394T = {0.16;4384} \u2248 3.6\xD710\u207B\u2075 K, which is of order 10\u207B\u2075 K.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"When M\u2081 gram of ice at \u221210\xB0C (specific heat = 0.5 cal g\u207B\xB9\xB0C\u207B\xB9) is added to M\u2082 gram of water at 50\xB0C, finally no ice is left and the water is at 0\xB0C. The value of latent heat of ice, in cal g\u207B\xB9 is",o:["{50M\u2082;M\u2081} \u2212 5","{5M\u2081;M\u2082} \u2212 50","{5M\u2082;M\u2081} \u2212 5","{50M\u2082;M\u2081}"],c:0,s:`Heat lost by the water cooling from 50\xB0C to 0\xB0C = M\u2082 \xD7 1 \xD7 50.
Heat gained by the ice = warming to 0\xB0C + melting = M\u2081 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 10 + M\u2081 L = 5M\u2081 + M\u2081 L.
Equate: 5M\u2081 + M\u2081L = 50M\u2082.
L = {50M\u2082;M\u2081} \u2212 5.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:`Ice at \u221220\xB0C is added to 50 g of water at 40\xB0C. When the temperature of the mixture reaches 0\xB0C, it is found that 20 g of ice is still unmelted. The amount of ice added to the water was close to
(Specific heat of water = 4.2 J/g/\xB0C
Specific heat of Ice = 2.1 J/g/\xB0C
Heat of fusion of ice at 0\xB0C = 334 J/g)`,o:["60 g","50 g","40 g","100 g"],c:2,s:`Let the mass of ice added be m g. Heat given out by water cooling from 40\xB0C to 0\xB0C = 50 \xD7 4.2 \xD7 40 = 8400 J.
The ice warms from \u221220\xB0C to 0\xB0C and (m \u2212 20) g of it melts: heat taken = m \xD7 2.1 \xD7 20 + (m \u2212 20) \xD7 334.
Equate: 42m + 334m \u2212 6680 = 8400, so 376m = 15080.
m \u2248 40 g.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:"When 100 g of a liquid A at 100\xB0C is added to 50 g of a liquid B at temperature 75\xB0C, the temperature of the mixture becomes 90\xB0C. The temperature of the mixture, if 100 g of liquid A at 100\xB0C is added to 50 g of liquid B at 50\xB0C will be",o:["70\xB0C","85\xB0C","60\xB0C","80\xB0C"],c:3,s:`First mixing: 100 s_A(100 \u2212 90) = 50 s_B(90 \u2212 75), so 1000 s_A = 750 s_B and s_A = 0.75 s_B.
Second mixing at final temperature T: 100 s_A(100 \u2212 T) = 50 s_B(T \u2212 50).
Put s_A = 0.75 s_B: 75(100 \u2212 T) = 50(T \u2212 50).
7500 \u2212 75T = 50T \u2212 2500, so 125T = 10000.
T = 80\xB0C. Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`A copper ball of mass 100 g is at a temperature T. It is dropped in a copper calorimeter of mass 100 g, filled with 170 g of water at room temperature. Subsequently, the temperature of the system is found to be 75\xB0C. T is given by
(Given : room temperature = 30\xB0C, specific heat of copper = 0.1 cal g\u207B\xB9 \xB0C\u207B\xB9)`,o:["800\xB0C","885\xB0C","1250\xB0C","825\xB0C"],c:1,s:`Heat lost by the ball = 100 \xD7 0.1 \xD7 (T \u2212 75).
Heat gained by the water and the copper calorimeter = (170 \xD7 1 + 100 \xD7 0.1) \xD7 (75 \u2212 30) = 180 \xD7 45 = 8100 cal.
10(T \u2212 75) = 8100, so T \u2212 75 = 810.
T = 885\xB0C.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`A water cooler of storage capacity 120 litres can cool water at a constant rate of P watts. In a closed circulation system (as shown schematically in the figure), the water from the cooler is used to cool an external device that generates constantly 3 kW of heat (thermal load). The temperature of water fed into the device cannot exceed 30\xB0C and the entire stored 120 litres of water is initially cooled to 10\xB0C. The entire system is thermally insulated. The minimum value of P (in watts) for which the device can be operated for 3 hours is
(Specific heat of water is 4.2 kJ kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 and the density of water is 1000 kg m\u207B\xB3)`,fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"A cooler box on the left and a device box on the right joined by two pipes: hot water flows from the device to the cooler on the upper pipe and cold water flows from the cooler to the device on the lower pipe.",d:[["r",20,40,90,90,"f"],["r",230,40,90,90],["t",65,90,"Cooler","m"],["t",275,90,"Device","m"],["l",230,65,110,65,"a"],["t",170,57,"Hot","m"],["l",110,105,230,105,"a"],["t",170,123,"Cold","m"]]},o:["1600","2067","2533","3933"],c:1,s:`In 3 hours the device produces 3000 \xD7 3 \xD7 3600 = 3.24\xD710\u2077 J of heat.
The stored 120 kg of water can absorb heat while its temperature goes from 10\xB0C up to 30\xB0C (the limit): 120 \xD7 4200 \xD7 20 = 1.008\xD710\u2077 J.
The rest must be removed by the cooler: P \xD7 10800 = 3.24\xD710\u2077 \u2212 1.008\xD710\u2077 = 2.232\xD710\u2077 J.
P = {2.232\xD710\u2077;10800} \u2248 2067 W.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"An experiment takes 10 minutes to raise the temperature of water in a container from 0\xB0C to 100\xB0C and another 55 minutes to convert it totally into steam by a heater supplying heat at a uniform rate. Neglecting the specific heat of the container and taking specific heat of water to be 1 cal/g\xB0C, the heat of vapourization according to this experiment will come out to be",o:["530 cal/g","540 cal/g","550 cal/g","560 cal/g"],c:2,s:`The heater gives heat at a constant rate, so heat is proportional to time.
In 10 min the heat given = 1 \xD7 100 = 100 cal per gram, so the rate is 10 cal/g per minute.
In the next 55 min: L = 10 \xD7 55 = 550 cal/g.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"pss",st:"Surface energy and force",q:"Assume that a drop of liquid evaporates by decrease in its surface energy, so that its temperature remains unchanged. What should be the minimum radius of the drop for this to be possible? The surface tension is T, density of liquid is \u03C1 and L is its latent heat of vaporization.",o:["{2T;\u03C1L}","{\u03C1L;T}","\u221A{T;\u03C1L}","{T;\u03C1L}"],c:0,s:`Let the radius decrease by dr. Surface energy U = 4\u03C0r\xB2T, so the energy released is dU = 8\u03C0rT dr.
Mass evaporated = \u03C1 \xD7 4\u03C0r\xB2 dr, and the heat needed is dQ = \u03C1 4\u03C0r\xB2 dr L.
For evaporation without a temperature change, dU \u2265 dQ: 8\u03C0rT \u2265 4\u03C0r\xB2\u03C1L.
So r \u2265 {2T;\u03C1L}. The minimum radius is {2T;\u03C1L}.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:"Calorie is defined as the amount of heat required to raise temperature of 1 g of water by 1\xB0C and it is defined under which of the following conditions?",o:["From 14.5\xB0C to 15.5\xB0C at 760 mm of Hg","From 98.5\xB0C to 99.5\xB0C at 760 mm of Hg","From 13.5\xB0C to 14.5\xB0C at 76 mm of Hg","From 3.5\xB0C to 4.5\xB0C at 76 mm of Hg."],c:0,s:`The standard (15\xB0C) calorie is defined for water warmed from 14.5\xB0C to 15.5\xB0C.
The pressure is standard atmospheric pressure, 760 mm of Hg.
The specific heat of water changes slightly with temperature, so the range must be fixed.
Only option (a) gives this range and pressure. Answer: (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:"Water of volume 2 litre in a container is heated with a coil of 1 kW at 27\xB0C. The lid of the container is open and energy dissipates at rate of 160 J/s. In how much time temperature will rise from 27\xB0C to 77\xB0C. [Given specific heat of water is 4.2 kJ/kg]",o:["7 min","6 min 2 s","8 min 20 s","14 min"],c:2,s:`Heat needed = m s \u0394T = 2 kg \xD7 4200 J/(kg K) \xD7 50 K = 4.2\xD710\u2075 J.
Net heating power = 1000 \u2212 160 = 840 W.
Time = {4.2\xD710\u2075;840} = 500 s = 8 min 20 s.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"If liquefied oxygen at 1 atmosphere pressure is heated from 50 K to 300 K by supplying heat at constant rate, the graph of temperature vs time will be",fig:{w:340,h:265,alt:"Four temperature versus time graphs (a) to (d): a straight line, an upward-curving line, a line with one flat step, and a line that levels off then rises again.",d:[["l",30,122,30,22,"a"],["l",30,122,165,122,"a"],["t",52,34,"(a)","m"],["t",26,30,"T","e"],["t",165,136,"t","e"],["p","M38 114 L156 52"],["l",200,122,200,22,"a"],["l",200,122,335,122,"a"],["t",222,34,"(b)","m"],["t",196,30,"T","e"],["t",335,136,"t","e"],["p","M208 114 Q290 110 326 48"],["l",30,242,30,142,"a"],["l",30,242,165,242,"a"],["t",52,154,"(c)","m"],["t",26,150,"T","e"],["t",165,256,"t","e"],["p","M38 234 L65 207 L105 207 L156 170"],["l",200,242,200,142,"a"],["l",200,242,335,242,"a"],["t",222,154,"(d)","m"],["t",196,150,"T","e"],["t",335,256,"t","e"],["p","M208 234 L222 206 L275 198 L326 170"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:2,s:`Oxygen boils at about 90 K, which lies between 50 K and 300 K.
So T rises as liquid O\u2082 warms up (heat supplied at constant rate, so T-t slopes up).
At the boiling point T stays constant while the liquid turns to gas (flat part).
Then the gas warms up and T rises again.
Only graph (c) has a rise, a flat part, and a rise. Answer: (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"2 kg of ice at \u221220\xB0C is mixed with 5 kg of water at 20\xB0C in an insulating vessel having a negligible heat capacity. Calculate the final mass of water remaining in the container. It is given that the specific heats of water and ice are 1 kcal/kg\xB0C and 0.5 kcal/kg\xB0C while the latent heat of fusion of ice is 80 kcal/kg",o:["7 kg","6 kg","4 kg","2 kg."],c:1,s:`Heat available from water cooling to 0\xB0C = 5 \xD7 1 \xD7 20 = 100 kcal.
Heat needed to warm the ice to 0\xB0C = 2 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 20 = 20 kcal.
Left over = 80 kcal, which can melt 80/80 = 1 kg of ice.
The mixture stays at 0\xB0C with 1 kg of ice melted, so water = 5 + 1 = 6 kg.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:"Heat given to a body which raises its temperature by 1\xB0C is",o:["water equivalent","thermal capacity","specific heat","temperature gradient."],c:1,s:`Thermal capacity (heat capacity) of a body is the heat needed to raise its temperature by 1\xB0C: C = Q/\u0394T.
Specific heat is the same for unit mass. Water equivalent is the mass of water having the same thermal capacity, and temperature gradient is the rate of change of temperature with distance.
Answer: (b), thermal capacity.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:"The figure below shows the variation of specific heat capacity (C) of a solid as a function of temperature (T). The temperature is increased continuously from 0 to 500 K at a constant rate. Ignoring any volume change, the following statement(s) is (are) correct to a reasonable approximation.",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Graph of specific heat capacity C against temperature T from 0 to 500 K: C rises steadily from zero, then levels off near 300 K and stays constant up to 500 K.",d:[["l",45,130,45,15,"a"],["l",45,130,320,130,"a"],["t",36,22,"C","e"],["t",320,164,"T(K)","e"],["l",100,130,100,125],["t",100,144,"100","ms"],["l",155,130,155,125],["t",155,144,"200","ms"],["l",210,130,210,125],["t",210,144,"300","ms"],["l",265,130,265,125],["t",265,144,"400","ms"],["l",320,130,320,125],["t",320,144,"500","ms"],["p","M45 130 C 85 126 125 42 210 34 H320"]]},o:["The rate at which heat is absorbed in the range 0-100 K varies linearly with temperature T.","Heat absorbed in increasing the temperature from 0-100 K is less than the heat required for increasing the temperature from 400-500 K.","There is no change in the rate of heat absorption in the range 400-500 K.","The rate of heat absorption increases in the range 200-300 K."],c:[1,2,3],s:`Heat absorbed = m C dT. Since T rises at a constant rate, the rate of heat absorption is proportional to C.
(a) In 0-100 K the curve of C against T is not a straight line through the origin, so the rate is not linear in T. False.
(b) C is small for 0-100 K and larger (constant) for 400-500 K, with the same \u0394T = 100 K, so the heat is less in 0-100 K. True.
(c) Above about 300 K the C curve is flat, so the rate of absorption is constant in 400-500 K. True.
(d) In 200-300 K the C value is still rising, so the rate of absorption increases. True.
Answer: (b), (c), (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"Steam at 100\xB0C is passed into 1.1 kg of water contained in a calorimeter of water equivalent 0.02 kg at 15\xB0C till the temperature of the calorimeter and its contents rises to 80\xB0C. The mass of the steam condensed in kilogram is",o:["0.130","0.065","0.260","0.135"],c:0,s:`Take the latent heat of steam L = 540 cal/g.
Heat gained by water and calorimeter = (1100 + 20) \xD7 1 \xD7 (80 \u2212 15) = 1120 \xD7 65 = 72800 cal.
Heat lost by the steam of mass m (in g) = m \xD7 540 + m \xD7 1 \xD7 (100 \u2212 80) = 560 m.
560 m = 72800, so m = 130 g = 0.130 kg.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`The specific heat capacity of a substance is temperature dependent and is given by the formula C = kT, where k is a constant of suitable dimensions in SI units, and T is the absolute temperature. If the heat required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of the substance from \u221273 \xB0C to 27 \xB0C is nk, the value of n is ____.
[Given: 0 K = \u2212273 \xB0C.]`,n:25e3,s:`In kelvin the limits are 200 K and 300 K.
Heat for 1 kg: Q = \u222B C dT = \u222B kT dT from 200 to 300 = k{300\xB2 \u2212 200\xB2;2}.
Q = k \xD7 {90000 \u2212 40000;2} = 25000 k.
So n = 25000.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:"A thin rod having a length of 1 m and area of cross-section 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2 is suspended vertically from one end. The rod is cooled from 210\xB0C to 160\xB0C. After cooling, a mass M is attached at the lower end of the rod such that the length of rod again becomes 1 m. Young's modulus and coefficient of linear expansion of the rod are 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 N m\u207B\xB2 and 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9, respectively. The value of M is ____ kg. (Take g = 10 ms\u207B\xB2).",n:60,s:`On cooling, the rod contracts. The weight Mg must stretch it back by the same amount.
Thermal strain = \u03B1 \u0394T = 2\xD710\u207B\u2075 \xD7 50 = 10\u207B\xB3.
Mg = Y A \xD7 strain = 2\xD710\xB9\xB9 \xD7 3\xD710\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 600 N.
M = 600/10 = 60 kg.
Answer: 60.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`A water heater of power 2000 W is used to heat water. The specific heat capacity of water is 4200 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9. The efficiency of heater is 70%. Time required to heat 2 kg of water from 10\xB0C to 60\xB0C is ______ s.
(Assume that the specific heat capacity of water remains constant over the temperature range of the water).`,n:300,s:`Heat needed Q = m c \u0394T = 2 \xD7 4200 \xD7 50 = 4.2 \xD7 10\u2075 J.
Useful power = 70% of 2000 W = 1400 W.
Time t = Q / (useful power) = 4.2 \xD7 10\u2075 / 1400 = 300 s.
Answer: 300 s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:`A block of ice of mass 120 g at temperature 0\xB0C is put in 300 g of water at 25\xB0C. The x g of ice melts as the temperature of the water reaches 0\xB0C. The value of x is ______.
[Use specific heat capacity of water = 4200 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9, Latent heat of ice = 3.5 \xD7 10\u2075 J kg\u207B\xB9]`,n:90,s:`Heat given by water cooling from 25\xB0C to 0\xB0C = 0.3 \xD7 4200 \xD7 25 = 31500 J.
This heat melts the ice: m L = 31500, so m = 31500 / (3.5 \xD7 10\u2075) = 0.09 kg.
So x = 90 g (less than 120 g, so the final temperature 0\xB0C is consistent).
Answer: 90.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`A geyser heats water flowing at a rate of 2.0 kg per minute from 30\xB0C to 70\xB0C. If geyser operates on a gas burner, the rate of combustion of fuel will be ______ g min\u207B\xB9.
[Heat of combustion = 8 \xD7 10\xB3 J g\u207B\xB9, Specific heat of water = 4.2 J g\u207B\xB9 \xB0C\u207B\xB9]`,n:42,s:`Water per minute = 2000 g.
Heat needed per minute = 2000 \xD7 4.2 \xD7 (70 \u2212 30) = 3.36 \xD7 10\u2075 J.
Fuel burnt per minute = 3.36 \xD7 10\u2075 / (8 \xD7 10\xB3) = 42 g.
Answer: 42 g min\u207B\xB9.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"M grams of steam at 100\xB0C is mixed with 200 g of ice at its melting point in a thermally insulated container. If it produces liquid water at 40\xB0C [heat of vaporization of water is 540 cal/g and heat of fusion of ice is 80 cal/g], the value of M is ______.",n:40,s:`Heat lost by steam = M \xD7 540 (condensing) + M \xD7 1 \xD7 (100 \u2212 40) = 600 M cal.
Heat gained by ice = 200 \xD7 80 (melting) + 200 \xD7 1 \xD7 40 (heating to 40\xB0C) = 24000 cal.
Equate: 600 M = 24000, so M = 40 g.
Answer: 40.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:`Three containers C\u2081, C\u2082 and C\u2083 have water at different temperatures. The table below shows the final temperature T when different amounts of water (given in liters) are taken from each container and mixed (assume no loss of heat during the process)
C\u2081 | C\u2082 | C\u2083 | T
1L | 2L | \u2014 | 60\xB0C
\u2014 | 1L | 2L | 30\xB0C
2L | \u2014 | 1L | 60\xB0C
1L | 1L | 1L | \u03B8
The value of \u03B8 (in \xB0C to the nearest integer) is ______.`,n:50,s:`Let the container temperatures be t\u2081, t\u2082, t\u2083. Equal volumes of water have equal heat capacity, so T is the volume-weighted mean.
Row 1: t\u2081 + 2t\u2082 = 180. Row 2: t\u2082 + 2t\u2083 = 90. Row 3: 2t\u2081 + t\u2083 = 180.
From row 1, t\u2081 = 180 \u2212 2t\u2082. From row 3, t\u2083 = 180 \u2212 2t\u2081 = 4t\u2082 \u2212 180.
Put in row 2: t\u2082 + 8t\u2082 \u2212 360 = 90, so t\u2082 = 50\xB0C, t\u2081 = 80\xB0C, t\u2083 = 20\xB0C.
\u03B8 = (80 + 50 + 20)/3 = 50\xB0C.
Answer: 50.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:`A liquid at 30\xB0C is poured very slowly into a calorimeter that is at temperature of 110\xB0C. The boiling temperature of the liquid is 80\xB0C. It is found that the first 5 gm of the liquid completely evaporates. After pouring another 80 gm of liquid the equilibrium temperature is found to be 50\xB0C. The ratio of the Latent heat of the liquid to its specific heat will be ______ \xB0C.
[Neglect the heat exchange with surrounding]`,n:270,s:`Let C = heat capacity of the calorimeter, s = specific heat and L = latent heat of the liquid.
Step 2 (80 g poured, final 50\xB0C): the calorimeter cools from 80\xB0C to 50\xB0C, the liquid warms from 30\xB0C to 50\xB0C. So C \xD7 30 = 80 \xD7 s \xD7 20 = 1600 s.
Step 1 (first 5 g): the calorimeter cools from 110\xB0C to 80\xB0C, giving C \xD7 30 = 5 s (80 \u2212 30) + 5 L = 250 s + 5 L.
Equate: 1600 s = 250 s + 5 L, so L = 270 s.
L/s = 270 \xB0C.
Answer: 270.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"A piece of ice (heat capacity = 2100 J kg\u207B\xB9 \xB0C\u207B\xB9 and latent heat = 3.36 \xD7 10\u2075 J kg\u207B\xB9) of mass m grams is at \u22125\xB0C at atmospheric pressure. It is given 420 J of heat so that the ice starts melting. Finally when the ice-water mixture is in equilibrium, it is found that 1 gm of ice has melted. Assuming there is no other heat exchange in the process, the value of m is ______.",n:8,s:`Heat to warm the ice from \u22125\xB0C to 0\xB0C = (m/1000) \xD7 2100 \xD7 5 = 10.5 m joule (m in grams).
Heat to melt 1 g of ice = (1/1000) \xD7 3.36 \xD7 10\u2075 = 336 J.
Total heat given: 10.5 m + 336 = 420.
So 10.5 m = 84 and m = 8 g.
Answer: 8.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"pst",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`0.05 kg steam at 373 K and 0.45 kg of ice at 253 K are mixed in an insulated vessel. Find the equilibrium temperature of the mixture.
Given, L(fusion) = 80 cal/g = 336 J/g, L(vaporisation) = 540 cal/g = 2268 J/g, s(ice) = 2100 J/kg K = 0.5 cal/g K and s(water) = 4200 J/kg K = 1 cal/g K`,a:"0 \xB0C",s:`Heat the steam can give up while becoming water at 0\xB0C = 50 \xD7 540 + 50 \xD7 1 \xD7 100 = 27000 + 5000 = 32000 cal.
Heat the ice needs to reach 0\xB0C = 450 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 20 = 4500 cal.
Heat the ice needs to melt completely = 450 \xD7 80 = 36000 cal.
Total needed to melt all ice = 40500 cal, which is more than 32000 cal.
So the steam is fully condensed and cooled to 0\xB0C, but only part of the ice melts: (32000 \u2212 4500)/80 \u2248 344 g.
The mixture stays at the melting point: equilibrium temperature = 0\xB0C.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"pse",st:"Elastic energy and thermal stress",q:`A 5 m long cylindrical steel wire with radius 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m is suspended vertically from a rigid support and carries a bob of mass 100 kg at the other end. If the bob gets snapped, calculate the change in temperature of the wire ignoring radiation losses.
(For the steel wire: Young's modulus = 2.1 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 Pa; Density = 7860 kg/m\xB3; Specific heat = 420 J/kg\xB7\xB0C.)`,a:"0.00457 \xB0C",s:`The elastic energy stored in the stretched wire is turned into heat when the bob snaps. Take g = 10 m/s\xB2, so F = 1000 N.
Area A = \u03C0 r\xB2 = \u03C0 (2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 = 1.257 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2.
Stored energy U = F\xB2 L / (2 A Y) = (10\u2076 \xD7 5) / (2 \xD7 1.257 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 2.1 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9) = 0.947 J.
Mass of wire m = \u03C1 A L = 7860 \xD7 1.257 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 5 = 0.494 kg.
\u0394T = U / (m c) = 0.947 / (0.494 \xD7 420) = 4.57 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xB0C.
Answer: about 0.00457 \xB0C.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"An ice cube of mass 0.1 kg at 0\xB0C is placed in an isolated container which is at 227\xB0C. The specific heat s of the container varies with temperature T according to the empirical relation s = A + BT, where A = 100 cal/kg-K and B = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 cal/kg-K\xB2. If the final temperature of the container is 27\xB0C, determine the mass of the container. (Latent heat of fusion of water = 8 \xD7 10\u2074 cal/kg, Specific heat of water = 10\xB3 cal/kg-K).",a:"0.495 kg",s:`Temperatures in kelvin: container cools from 500 K to 300 K (B is given per K\xB2).
Heat lost by container = m \u222B (A + BT) dT = m [A (500 \u2212 300) + (B/2)(500\xB2 \u2212 300\xB2)] = m [20000 + 0.01 \xD7 160000] = 21600 m cal.
Heat gained by ice = melting 0.1 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u2074 = 8000 cal, plus warming the water 0.1 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 27 = 2700 cal. Total = 10700 cal.
21600 m = 10700, so m = 0.495 kg.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"The temperature of 100 g of water is to be raised from 24\xB0C to 90\xB0C by adding steam to it. Calculate the mass of the steam required for this purpose.",a:"12 g",s:`Heat needed by the water = 100 \xD7 1 \xD7 (90 \u2212 24) = 6600 cal.
Each gram of steam at 100\xB0C gives 540 cal on condensing, then 1 \xD7 (100 \u2212 90) = 10 cal on cooling to 90\xB0C. Total 550 cal per gram.
Mass of steam m = 6600 / 550 = 12 g.
(Latent heat of vaporisation taken as 540 cal/g.)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1980",tp:"pst",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A solid material is supplied with heat at constant rate. The temperature of the material is changing with the heat input as shown in the graph in figure. Study the graph carefully and answer the following questions.
(i) What do the horizontal region AB and CD represent?
(ii) If CD is equal to 2AB, what do you infer?
(iii) What does the slope of DE represent?
(iv) The slope of OA > the slope of BC. What does this indicate?`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Temperature against heat input: a rising line OA, a flat part AB, a rising part BC, a flat part CD and a final rising part DE.",d:[["l",50,170,50,15,"a"],["l",50,170,320,170,"a"],["p","M50 170 L85 120 L135 120 L170 75 L270 75 L305 30"],["t",38,184,"O","e"],["t",81,111,"A","e"],["t",135,137,"B"],["t",168,67,"C","e"],["t",272,67,"D"],["t",305,21,"E","m"],["t",58,20,"Temperature"],["t",320,186,"Heat input","e"]]},a:"(i) AB: melting (solid to liquid); CD: boiling (liquid to vapour), at constant temperature   (ii) Latent heat of vaporisation = 2 \xD7 latent heat of fusion   (iii) Slope of DE = 1/(m c of vapour), the reciprocal of the heat capacity of the vapour   (iv) Specific heat of the solid < specific heat of the liquid",s:`Heat is supplied at a constant rate, so the horizontal length on the heat axis is proportional to the heat absorbed.
(i) On AB and CD the temperature stays constant while heat is absorbed: this is a change of state. AB is melting (solid to liquid) and CD is boiling (liquid to vapour).
(ii) Heat absorbed on AB is m L(fusion) and on CD is m L(vaporisation) for the same mass. If CD = 2 AB then L(vaporisation) = 2 L(fusion).
(iii) Slope dT/dQ = 1/(m c). So the slope of DE is the reciprocal of the heat capacity of the vapour (m \xD7 specific heat of vapour).
(iv) Slope of OA is 1/(m c(solid)) and slope of BC is 1/(m c(liquid)). A larger slope means a smaller specific heat, so the solid has a smaller specific heat than the liquid.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"psp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A block of ice with a lead shot embedded in it is floating on water contained in a vessel. The temperature of the system is maintained at 0\xB0C as the ice melts. When the ice melts completely the level of water in the vessel rises.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`Let the ice have mass M and the lead shot mass m. Floating: the water displaced has mass M + m, so displaced volume = (M + m)/\u03C1(water).
After melting, the ice gives water of volume M/\u03C1(water) and the lead sinks, displacing only its own volume m/\u03C1(lead).
Since \u03C1(lead) is greater than \u03C1(water), m/\u03C1(lead) < m/\u03C1(water). So the total volume is less than before.
The water level falls, so the statement is false.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:`Earth receives 1400 W/m\xB2 of solar power. If all the solar energy falling on a lens of area 0.2 m\xB2 is focused on to a block of ice of mass 280 gram, the time taken to melt the ice will be ......... minute.
(Latent heat of fusion of ice = 3.3 \xD7 10\u2075 J/kg.)`,n:5.5,s:`Power collected by the lens = 1400 \xD7 0.2 = 280 W.
Heat needed to melt the ice = m L = 0.28 \xD7 3.3 \xD7 10\u2075 = 9.24 \xD7 10\u2074 J.
Time = 9.24 \xD7 10\u2074 / 280 = 330 s = 5.5 minute.
Answer: 5.5 minute.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"A substance of mass M requires a power input of P watt to remain in the molten state at its melting point. When the power source is turned off, the sample completely solidifies in time t second. The latent heat of fusion of the substance is .........",a:"Pt/M",s:`To stay molten at the melting point, the power P just replaces the heat lost to the surroundings each second.
So the sample loses heat at the rate P.
When the source is off, the sample solidifies completely in time t, releasing heat M L = P t.
Therefore L = P t / M.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"pst",st:"Latent heat and change of state",q:"300 gram of water at 25\xB0C is added to 100 gram of ice at 0\xB0C. The final temperature of the mixture is ........\xB0C.",a:"0 \xB0C",s:`Heat available if the water cools to 0\xB0C = 300 \xD7 1 \xD7 25 = 7500 cal.
Heat needed to melt all the ice = 100 \xD7 80 = 8000 cal.
Since 7500 < 8000, the water cannot melt all the ice. Some ice remains (about 94 g melts).
Ice and water stay together, so the final temperature is 0\xB0C.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"pst",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`The variation of temperature of a material, as heat is given to it at a constant rate, is shown in the figure. The material is in solid state at the point O.
The state of the material at the point P is ................`,fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Temperature against heat added: a rising line from O to A, a flat part from A to B with point P on it, then a rise to C and a flat part CD.",d:[["l",50,160,50,15,"a"],["l",50,160,320,160,"a"],["p","M50 160 L90 105 L150 105 L195 60 L260 60"],["c",120,105,2.5,"F"],["t",38,174,"O","e"],["t",90,96,"A","m"],["t",120,96,"P","m"],["t",150,124,"B","m"],["t",195,52,"C","m"],["t",266,64,"D"],["t",58,20,"T"],["t",320,176,"heat added","e"]]},a:"Partly solid and partly liquid (the solid is melting at constant temperature)",s:`At first the solid warms up (rising part OA).
The flat part AB is where the temperature stays constant while heat is still being absorbed. This is the melting stage.
P lies on this flat part, so some of the solid has melted but not all of it.
The material at P is a mixture of solid and liquid.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Two spherical bodies of same materials having radii 0.2 m and 0.8 m are placed in same atmosphere. The temperature of the smaller body is 800 K and temperature of the bigger body is 400 K. If the energy radiated from the smaller body is E, the energy radiated from the bigger body is (assume, effect of the surrounding temperature to be negligible).",o:["E","16E","64E","256E"],c:0,s:`Energy radiated per second = \u03C3 A T\u2074 = \u03C3 (4\u03C0 r\xB2) T\u2074, so it is proportional to r\xB2 T\u2074.
Ratio (bigger : smaller) = (0.8/0.2)\xB2 \xD7 (400/800)\u2074 = 16 \xD7 (1/16) = 1.
Both radiate the same energy.
So the bigger body radiates E.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"The temperature of a body in air falls from 40\xB0C to 24\xB0C in 4 minutes. The temperature of the air is 16\xB0C. The temperature of the body in the next 4 minutes will be",o:["{42;3}\xB0C","{28;3}\xB0C","{56;3}\xB0C","{14;3}\xB0C"],c:2,s:`Use Newton's law in the average form: fall in temperature / time = k \xD7 (mean temperature \u2212 surroundings).
First 4 min: (40 \u2212 24)/4 = k \xD7 ((40 + 24)/2 \u2212 16), so 4 = 16k and k = 1/4.
Next 4 min: (24 \u2212 T)/4 = (1/4)((24 + T)/2 \u2212 16), so 24 \u2212 T = T/2 \u2212 4.
28 = 1.5 T, so T = 56/3 \xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"A cup of coffee cools from 90\xB0C to 80\xB0C in t minutes when the room temperature is 20\xB0C. The time taken by a similar cup of coffee to cool from 80\xB0C to 60\xB0C at the same room temperature is",o:["{5;13}t","{13;10}t","{10;13}t","{13;5}t"],c:3,s:`Newton's law (average form): fall/time = k \xD7 (mean temperature \u2212 room temperature).
First cooling: 10/t = k (85 \u2212 20) = 65 k, so k = 2/(13 t).
Second cooling: 20/t\u2032 = k (70 \u2212 20) = 50 k, so t\u2032 = 20/(50 k) = 0.4/k.
t\u2032 = 0.4 \xD7 13 t / 2 = 2.6 t = 13t/5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"The difference of temperature in a material can convert heat energy into electrical energy. To harvest the heat energy, the material should have",o:["low thermal conductivity and high electrical conductivity","high thermal conductivity and high electrical conductivity","high thermal conductivity and low electrical conductivity","low thermal conductivity and low electrical conductivity"],c:0,s:`To convert a temperature difference into electrical energy, the difference must be maintained across the material. This needs low thermal conductivity, otherwise heat flows through quickly and the difference dies out.
The electric current generated must flow with little loss. This needs high electrical conductivity.
So the material should have low thermal conductivity and high electrical conductivity.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"A body cools in 7 minutes from 60\xB0C to 40\xB0C. The temperature of the surrounding is 10\xB0C. The temperature of the body after the next 7 minutes will be",o:["30\xB0C","34\xB0C","28\xB0C","32\xB0C"],c:2,s:`Average form of Newton's law: fall/time = k \xD7 (mean temperature \u2212 surroundings).
First 7 min: 20/7 = k (50 \u2212 10) = 40 k, so k = 1/14.
Next 7 min: (40 \u2212 T)/7 = (1/14)((40 + T)/2 \u2212 10), so 2(40 \u2212 T) = T/2 + 10.
80 \u2212 2T = 0.5 T + 10, so 2.5 T = 70 and T = 28\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"A body cools from 80\xB0C to 60\xB0C in 5 minutes. The temperature of the surrounding is 20\xB0C. The time it takes to cool from 60\xB0C to 40\xB0C is",o:["{25;3} s","500 s","420 s","450 s"],c:1,s:`Average form of Newton's law: fall/time = k \xD7 (mean temperature \u2212 surroundings).
First stage: 20/5 = k (70 \u2212 20) = 50 k, so k = 0.08 per minute.
Second stage: 20/t = k (50 \u2212 20) = 30 k = 2.4, so t = 20/2.4 = 25/3 minute = 500 s.
(The time 25/3 is in minutes, which equals 500 s.)`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"A bowl filled with very hot soup cools from 98\xB0C to 86\xB0C in 2 minutes when the room temperature is 22\xB0C. How long will it take to cool from 75\xB0C to 69\xB0C?",o:["2 minutes","1 minute","1.4 minutes","0.5 minute"],c:2,s:`Average form of Newton's law: fall/time = k \xD7 (mean temperature \u2212 room temperature).
First stage: 12/2 = k (92 \u2212 22) = 70 k, so k = 3/35 per minute.
Second stage: 6/t = k (72 \u2212 22) = 50 k = 30/7, so t = 6 \xD7 7/30 = 1.4 minutes.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"If K\u2081 and K\u2082 are the thermal conductivities, L\u2081 and L\u2082 are the lengths and A\u2081 and A\u2082 are the cross sectional areas of steel and copper rods respectively such that {K\u2082;K\u2081} = 9, {A\u2081;A\u2082} = 2, {L\u2081;L\u2082} = 2. Then for the arrangement as shown in the figure, the value of temperature T of the steel-copper junction in the steady state will be",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"A furnace at 450 degrees C, a steel rod and a copper rod joined in series, and an ice box at 0 degrees C, with insulating material around the rods; T is the steel-copper junction.",d:[["r",10,40,60,50],["t",40,62,"Furnace","ms"],["t",40,78,"450\xB0C","ms"],["r",70,55,90,20],["t",115,69,"Steel","ms"],["r",160,55,90,20],["t",205,69,"Copper","ms"],["r",250,40,70,50],["t",285,62,"Ice box","ms"],["t",285,78,"0\xB0C","ms"],["l",70,48,250,48,"d"],["l",70,82,250,82,"d"],["l",160,36,160,55,"d"],["t",160,30,"T","m"],["t",160,112,"Insulating material","ms"]]},o:["18\xB0C","14\xB0C","45\xB0C","150\xB0C"],c:2,s:`In the steady state the same heat flows through both rods: K\u2081A\u2081(450 \u2212 T)/L\u2081 = K\u2082A\u2082(T \u2212 0)/L\u2082.
So 450 \u2212 T = T \xD7 (K\u2082/K\u2081)(A\u2082/A\u2081)(L\u2081/L\u2082).
Put K\u2082/K\u2081 = 9, A\u2082/A\u2081 = 1/2, L\u2081/L\u2082 = 2: 450 \u2212 T = T \xD7 9 \xD7 (1/2) \xD7 2 = 9T.
450 = 10 T, so T = 45\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:`Two metallic blocks M\u2081 and M\u2082 of same area of cross-section are connected to each other (as shown in figure). If the thermal conductivity of M\u2082 is K then the thermal conductivity of M\u2081 will be
[Assume steady state heat conduction.]`,fig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"Two metal blocks in series: M1 of length 16 cm between 100 degrees C and 80 degrees C, and M2 of length 8 cm between 80 degrees C and 0 degrees C.",d:[["r",60,45,160,40],["r",220,45,80,40],["t",140,70,"M\u2081","m"],["t",260,70,"M\u2082","m"],["t",55,70,"100\xB0C","e"],["t",305,70,"0\xB0C"],["t",220,38,"80\xB0C","m"],["l",60,105,220,105,"b"],["t",140,121,"16 cm","m"],["l",220,105,300,105,"b"],["t",260,121,"8 cm","m"]]},o:["10 K","8 K","12.5 K","2 K"],c:1,s:`In steady state the heat current is the same in both blocks (same area A): K\u2081 A (100 \u2212 80)/16 = K A (80 \u2212 0)/8.
So K\u2081 \xD7 20/16 = K \xD7 10.
K\u2081 = 10 K \xD7 16/20 = 8 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Two thin metallic spherical shells of radii r\u2081 and r\u2082 (r\u2081 < r\u2082) are placed with their centres coinciding. A material of thermal conductivity K is filled in the space between the shells. The inner shell is maintained at temperature \u03B8\u2081 and the outer shell at temperature \u03B8\u2082 (\u03B8\u2081 < \u03B8\u2082). The rate at which heat flows radially through the material is",o:["{K(\u03B8\u2082\u2212\u03B8\u2081);r\u2082\u2212r\u2081}","{K(\u03B8\u2082\u2212\u03B8\u2081)(r\u2082\u2212r\u2081);4\u03C0r\u2081r\u2082}","{4\u03C0Kr\u2081r\u2082(\u03B8\u2082\u2212\u03B8\u2081);r\u2082\u2212r\u2081}","{\u03C0r\u2081r\u2082(\u03B8\u2082\u2212\u03B8\u2081);r\u2082\u2212r\u2081}"],c:2,s:`Take a thin shell of radius r and thickness dr. Heat current H = \u2212K (4\u03C0 r\xB2) d\u03B8/dr, so H dr / r\xB2 = \u22124\u03C0 K d\u03B8.
Integrate from r\u2081 to r\u2082: H (1/r\u2081 \u2212 1/r\u2082) = 4\u03C0 K (\u03B8\u2082 \u2212 \u03B8\u2081) in magnitude.
1/r\u2081 \u2212 1/r\u2082 = (r\u2082 \u2212 r\u2081)/(r\u2081 r\u2082).
So H = 4\u03C0 K r\u2081 r\u2082 (\u03B8\u2082 \u2212 \u03B8\u2081)/(r\u2082 \u2212 r\u2081).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pst",st:"Calorimetry and specific heat",q:"Two different metal bodies A and B of equal mass are heated at a uniform rate under similar conditions. The variation of temperature of the bodies is graphically represented as shown in the figure. The ratio of specific heat capacities is",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"Temperature against time graph for bodies A and B heated at the same rate. Line A rises faster, reaching 120 degrees C at 3 s. Line B rises slowly, reaching 90 degrees C at 6 s. Both start from the origin.",d:[["l",50,175,50,12,"a"],["l",50,175,320,175,"a"],["l",50,145,320,145,"d"],["l",50,115,320,115,"d"],["l",50,85,320,85,"d"],["l",50,55,320,55,"d"],["t",46,179,"30","es"],["t",46,149,"60","es"],["t",46,119,"90","es"],["t",46,89,"120","es"],["t",46,59,"150","es"],["l",80,175,80,170],["l",110,175,110,170],["l",140,175,140,170],["l",170,175,170,170],["l",200,175,200,170],["l",230,175,230,170],["l",260,175,260,170],["l",290,175,290,170],["t",80,190,"1","ms"],["t",110,190,"2","ms"],["t",140,190,"3","ms"],["t",170,190,"4","ms"],["t",200,190,"5","ms"],["t",230,190,"6","ms"],["t",260,190,"7","ms"],["t",290,190,"8","ms"],["l",50,175,162,25],["l",50,175,320,40],["t",166,28,"A"],["t",296,38,"B"],["t",56,10,"T (\xB0C)"],["t",320,203,"Time t (s)","e"]]},o:["{3;4}","{8;3}","{3;8}","{4;3}"],c:2,s:`Both bodies get heat at the same rate P, so P = m c (dT/dt). Hence c = P / (m \xD7 slope), and for equal masses c is inversely proportional to the slope of the T\u2013t graph.
From the graph, A reaches 120\xB0C in 3 s (slope 40\xB0C/s). B reaches 90\xB0C in 6 s (slope 15\xB0C/s).
c(A)/c(B) = slope(B)/slope(A) = 15/40 = 3/8.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"A body takes 4 min to cool from 61\xB0C to 59\xB0C. If the temperature of the surroundings is 30\xB0C, the time taken by the body to cool from 51\xB0C to 49\xB0C is",o:["3 min","4 min","8 min","6 min"],c:3,s:`Average form of Newton's law: fall/time = k \xD7 (mean temperature \u2212 surroundings).
First: 2/4 = k (60 \u2212 30) = 30 k, so k = 1/60 per minute.
Second: 2/t = k (50 \u2212 30) = 20 k = 1/3, so t = 6 min.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Two identical metal wires of thermal conductivities K\u2081 and K\u2082 respectively are connected in series. The effective thermal conductivity of the combination is",o:["{K\u2081+K\u2082;2K\u2081K\u2082}","{2K\u2081K\u2082;K\u2081+K\u2082}","{K\u2081K\u2082;K\u2081+K\u2082}","{K\u2081+K\u2082;K\u2081K\u2082}"],c:1,s:`Wires of the same length l and area A in series: thermal resistances add.
2l/(K A) = l/(K\u2081 A) + l/(K\u2082 A), where K is the effective conductivity.
2/K = 1/K\u2081 + 1/K\u2082 = (K\u2081 + K\u2082)/(K\u2081 K\u2082).
So K = 2K\u2081K\u2082/(K\u2081 + K\u2082).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"The temperature \u03B8 at the junction of two insulating sheets, having thermal resistances R\u2081 and R\u2082 as well as top and bottom temperatures \u03B8\u2081 and \u03B8\u2082 (as shown in figure) is given by",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"Two insulating sheets stacked: the upper sheet with thermal resistance R2 has its top surface at temperature theta 2, the lower sheet with resistance R1 has its bottom surface at theta 1, and the junction between them is at theta.",d:[["r",70,30,200,40,"f"],["r",70,70,200,40,"f"],["t",170,55,"R\u2082","m"],["t",170,95,"R\u2081","m"],["t",278,38,"\u03B8\u2082"],["t",278,74,"\u03B8"],["t",278,118,"\u03B8\u2081"]]},o:["{\u03B8\u2081R\u2081+\u03B8\u2082R\u2082;R\u2081+R\u2082}","{\u03B8\u2082R\u2082\u2212\u03B8\u2081R\u2081;R\u2082\u2212R\u2081}","{\u03B8\u2081R\u2082\u2212\u03B8\u2082R\u2081;R\u2082\u2212R\u2081}","{\u03B8\u2081R\u2082+\u03B8\u2082R\u2081;R\u2081+R\u2082}"],c:3,s:`The sheets are in series, so the same heat current flows through both: (\u03B8\u2082 \u2212 \u03B8)/R\u2082 = (\u03B8 \u2212 \u03B8\u2081)/R\u2081 (the sheet of resistance R\u2082 lies next to \u03B8\u2082).
Cross multiply: R\u2081(\u03B8\u2082 \u2212 \u03B8) = R\u2082(\u03B8 \u2212 \u03B8\u2081).
\u03B8 (R\u2081 + R\u2082) = \u03B8\u2082R\u2081 + \u03B8\u2081R\u2082.
\u03B8 = (\u03B8\u2081R\u2082 + \u03B8\u2082R\u2081)/(R\u2081 + R\u2082).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"A metallic sphere cools from 50\xB0C to 40\xB0C in 300 s. If atmospheric temperature around is 20\xB0C, then the sphere's temperature after the next 5 minutes will be close to",o:["31\xB0C","33\xB0C","28\xB0C","35\xB0C"],c:1,s:`Average form of Newton's law: fall/time = k \xD7 (mean temperature \u2212 surroundings).
First 300 s: 10/300 = k (45 \u2212 20) = 25 k, so k = 1/750 per second.
Next 300 s: (40 \u2212 T)/300 = (1/750)((40 + T)/2 \u2212 20) = T/1500.
So 40 \u2212 T = T/5, giving 1.2 T = 40 and T \u2248 33\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Three rods of identical cross-section and lengths are made of three different materials of thermal conductivity K\u2081, K\u2082 and K\u2083, respectively. They are joined together at their ends to make a long rod (see figure). One end of the long rod is maintained at 100\xB0C and the other at 0\xB0C (see figure). If the joints of the rod are at 70\xB0C and 20\xB0C in steady state and there is no loss of energy from the surface of the rod, the correct relationship between K\u2081, K\u2082 and K\u2083 is",fig:{w:340,h:120,alt:"Three rods of equal length joined end to end: K1 from 100 degrees C to 70 degrees C, K2 from 70 to 20 degrees C, K3 from 20 to 0 degrees C.",d:[["r",50,40,80,30,"f"],["r",130,40,80,30],["r",210,40,80,30,"f"],["t",90,34,"K\u2081","m"],["t",170,34,"K\u2082","m"],["t",250,34,"K\u2083","m"],["t",46,60,"100\xB0C","e"],["t",296,60,"0\xB0C"],["l",130,70,130,82],["l",210,70,210,82],["t",130,96,"70\xB0C","m"],["t",210,96,"20\xB0C","m"]]},o:["K\u2081 : K\u2083 = 2 : 3, K\u2082 : K\u2083 = 2 : 5","K\u2081 < K\u2082 < K\u2083","K\u2081 : K\u2082 = 5 : 2, K\u2081 : K\u2083 = 3 : 5","K\u2081 > K\u2082 > K\u2083"],c:0,s:`Same cross-section, same length l and same heat current in all rods, so K \u0394T is the same for each rod.
K\u2081 (100 \u2212 70) = K\u2082 (70 \u2212 20) = K\u2083 (20 \u2212 0), i.e. 30 K\u2081 = 50 K\u2082 = 20 K\u2083.
K\u2081 : K\u2082 : K\u2083 = 1/30 : 1/50 : 1/20 = 10 : 6 : 15.
So K\u2081 : K\u2083 = 10 : 15 = 2 : 3 and K\u2082 : K\u2083 = 6 : 15 = 2 : 5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"Two identical beakers A and B contain equal volumes of two different liquids at 60\xB0C each and left to cool down. Liquid in A has density of 8 \xD7 10\xB2 kg m\u207B\xB3 and specific heat of 2000 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 while liquid in B has density of 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3 and specific heat of 4000 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9. Which of the following best describes their temperature versus time graph schematically? (Assume the emissivity of both the beakers to be the same.)",fig:{w:340,h:222,alt:"Four temperature against time graphs, each starting at 60 degrees C and falling: in (a) A is above B, in (b) B is above A, in (c) A and B coincide, in (d) A is above B but with different end values.",d:[["l",30,95,165,95,"a"],["l",30,95,30,23,"a"],["t",165,23,"(a)","es"],["t",34,33,"T","s"],["t",165,107,"t","es"],["t",27,37,"60\xB0C","es"],["p","M30 35 Q70 63 160 69"],["p","M30 35 Q50 81 160 87"],["t",100,55,"A","s"],["t",100,85,"B","s"],["l",195,95,330,95,"a"],["l",195,95,195,23,"a"],["t",330,23,"(b)","es"],["t",199,33,"T","s"],["t",330,107,"t","es"],["t",192,37,"60\xB0C","es"],["p","M195 35 Q235 63 325 69"],["p","M195 35 Q215 81 325 87"],["t",265,55,"B","s"],["t",265,85,"A","s"],["l",30,205,165,205,"a"],["l",30,205,30,133,"a"],["t",165,133,"(c)","es"],["t",34,143,"T","s"],["t",165,217,"t","es"],["t",27,147,"60\xB0C","es"],["p","M30 145 Q60 183 160 190"],["t",100,171,"A and B","s"],["l",195,205,330,205,"a"],["l",195,205,195,133,"a"],["t",330,133,"(d)","es"],["t",199,143,"T","s"],["t",330,217,"t","es"],["t",192,147,"60\xB0C","es"],["p","M195 145 Q255 153 325 167"],["p","M195 145 Q210 195 325 199"],["t",285,159,"A","s"],["t",265,197,"B","s"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:1,s:`The rate of fall of temperature is (radiated power)/(heat capacity). Both beakers are alike and have the same emissivity, so at the same temperature they radiate the same power.
Heat capacity of A per unit volume: \u03C1 s = 8 \xD7 10\xB2 \xD7 2000 = 1.6 \xD7 10\u2076 J m\u207B\xB3 K\u207B\xB9.
Heat capacity of B per unit volume: \u03C1 s = 10\xB3 \xD7 4000 = 4 \xD7 10\u2076 J m\u207B\xB3 K\u207B\xB9. This is larger.
So B cools more slowly than A: at any time T(B) > T(A).
Both start at 60\xB0C and fall towards the surrounding temperature with the curve flattening. This is graph (b), where B lies above A.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Two materials having coefficients of thermal conductivity '3K' and 'K' and thickness 'd' and '3d', respectively, are joined to form a slab as shown in the figure. The temperatures of the outer surfaces are '\u03B8\u2082' and '\u03B8\u2081' respectively, (\u03B8\u2082 > \u03B8\u2081). The temperature at the interface is",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"A composite slab: left layer of thickness d and conductivity 3K at temperature theta 2 on its outer face, right layer of thickness 3d and conductivity K at theta 1 on its outer face.",d:[["r",50,40,60,70,"f"],["r",110,40,180,70],["t",80,80,"3K","m"],["t",200,80,"K","m"],["l",50,125,110,125,"b"],["t",80,141,"d","m"],["l",110,125,290,125,"b"],["t",200,141,"3d","m"],["t",46,78,"\u03B8\u2082","e"],["t",294,78,"\u03B8\u2081"]]},o:["{\u03B8\u2081;10}+{9\u03B8\u2082;10}","{\u03B8\u2081;6}+{5\u03B8\u2082;6}","{\u03B8\u2082+\u03B8\u2081;2}","{\u03B8\u2081;3}+{2\u03B8\u2082;3}"],c:0,s:`Layers are in series, so the heat current is the same: 3K A (\u03B8\u2082 \u2212 \u03B8)/d = K A (\u03B8 \u2212 \u03B8\u2081)/(3d).
So 9 (\u03B8\u2082 \u2212 \u03B8) = \u03B8 \u2212 \u03B8\u2081.
10 \u03B8 = 9 \u03B8\u2082 + \u03B8\u2081.
\u03B8 = \u03B8\u2081/10 + 9\u03B8\u2082/10.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Temperature difference of 120\xB0C is maintained between the two ends of a uniform rod AB of length 2L. Another bent rod PQ, of same cross-section as AB and length {3L;2}, is connected across AB (see figure). In steady state, temperature difference between P and Q will be close to",fig:{w:340,h:175,alt:"A straight rod AB of length 2L. A bent rod of length 3L/2 is joined across the points P and Q: AP is L/2, PQ is L, and QB is L/2; the bent rod rises L/4, runs L, and comes back down L/4.",d:[["r",40,110,240,10,"f"],["p","M100 110 V80 H220 V110"],["t",34,125,"A","e"],["t",286,125,"B"],["t",100,138,"P","m"],["t",220,138,"Q","m"],["l",40,150,100,150,"b"],["t",70,166,"L/2","ms"],["l",100,150,220,150,"b"],["t",160,166,"L","ms"],["l",220,150,280,150,"b"],["t",250,166,"L/2","ms"],["l",236,80,236,110,"b"],["t",242,99,"L/4","s"]]},o:["35\xB0C","45\xB0C","60\xB0C","75\xB0C"],c:1,s:`Same material and cross-section, so thermal resistance is proportional to length. Take R = 1 unit for length L.
AP has resistance 0.5 and QB has resistance 0.5.
Between P and Q two paths are in parallel: straight length L (resistance 1) and bent length 3L/2 (resistance 1.5). Combined: (1 \xD7 1.5)/(1 + 1.5) = 0.6.
Total resistance = 0.5 + 0.6 + 0.5 = 1.6, and the same heat current flows through all parts.
Temperature difference across PQ = 120 \xD7 0.6/1.6 = 45\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"A heat source at T = 10\xB3 K is connected to another heat reservoir at T = 10\xB2 K by a copper slab which is 1 m thick. Given that the thermal conductivity of copper is 0.1 W K\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB9, the energy flux through it in the steady state is",o:["120 W m\u207B\xB2","90 W m\u207B\xB2","200 W m\u207B\xB2","65 W m\u207B\xB2"],c:1,s:`Heat flux (heat per second per unit area) = K \u0394T / d.
\u0394T = 1000 \u2212 100 = 900 K, d = 1 m, K = 0.1 W K\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB9.
Flux = 0.1 \xD7 900 / 1 = 90 W m\u207B\xB2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"A cylinder of radius R is surrounded by a cylindrical shell of inner radius R and outer radius 2R. The thermal conductivity of the material of the inner cylinder is K\u2081 and that of the outer cylinder is K\u2082. Assuming no loss of heat, the effective thermal conductivity of the system for heat flowing along the length of the cylinder is",o:["{2K\u2081+3K\u2082;5}","{K\u2081+K\u2082;2}","K\u2081 + K\u2082","{K\u2081+3K\u2082;4}"],c:3,s:`Heat flows along the length, so the inner cylinder and the shell conduct in parallel (same length, same temperature difference).
Area of inner cylinder = \u03C0 R\xB2. Area of shell = \u03C0(2R)\xB2 \u2212 \u03C0 R\xB2 = 3\u03C0 R\xB2.
Effective K \xD7 (total area 4\u03C0 R\xB2) = K\u2081 \u03C0 R\xB2 + K\u2082 3\u03C0 R\xB2.
K = (K\u2081 + 3K\u2082)/4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"A body takes 10 minutes to cool from 60\xB0C to 50\xB0C. The temperature of surroundings is constant at 25\xB0C. Then, the temperature of the body after next 10 minutes will be approximately",o:["47\xB0C","43\xB0C","41\xB0C","45\xB0C"],c:1,s:`Average form of Newton's law: fall/time = k \xD7 (mean temperature \u2212 surroundings).
First 10 min: 10/10 = k (55 \u2212 25) = 30 k, so k = 1/30 per minute.
Next 10 min: (50 \u2212 T)/10 = (1/30)((50 + T)/2 \u2212 25) = T/60.
So 3 (50 \u2212 T) = T/2, 150 = 3.5 T, T \u2248 42.9\xB0C \u2248 43\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"The ends Q and R of two thin wires, PQ and RS, are soldered (joined) together. Initially each of the wires has a length of 1 m at 10\xB0C. Now the end P is maintained at 10\xB0C, while the end S is heated and maintained at 400\xB0C. The system is thermally insulated from its surroundings. If the thermal conductivity of wire PQ is twice that of the wire RS and the coefficient of linear thermal expansion of PQ is 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9, the change in length of the wire PQ is",o:["0.78 mm","0.90 mm","1.56 mm","2.34 mm"],c:0,s:`The wires are in series with equal length and area, so the same heat current flows: 2K (\u03B8 \u2212 10) = K (400 \u2212 \u03B8), where \u03B8 is the junction temperature.
2\u03B8 \u2212 20 = 400 \u2212 \u03B8, so \u03B8 = 140\xB0C.
Temperature varies linearly along PQ from 10\xB0C to 140\xB0C, so the average rise is (0 + 130)/2 = 65\xB0C.
\u0394L = L \u03B1 \u0394T(avg) = 1 \xD7 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 65 = 7.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m = 0.78 mm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2014",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Three rods of Copper, Brass and Steel are welded together to form a Y-shaped structure. Area of cross-section of each rod = 4 cm\xB2. End of copper rod is maintained at 100\xB0C whereas ends of brass and steel are kept at 0\xB0C. Lengths of the copper, brass and steel rods are 46, 13 and 12 cm respectively. The rods are thermally insulated from surroundings except at ends. Thermal conductivities of copper, brass and steel are 0.92, 0.26 and 0.12 CGS units respectively. Rate of heat flow through copper rod is",o:["6.0 cal/s","1.2 cal/s","2.4 cal/s","4.8 cal/s"],c:3,s:`Let the junction temperature be T. Heat through copper = heat through brass + heat through steel (same area A).
0.92 (100 \u2212 T)/46 = 0.26 (T \u2212 0)/13 + 0.12 (T \u2212 0)/12.
0.02 (100 \u2212 T) = 0.02 T + 0.01 T, so 2 = 0.05 T and T = 40\xB0C.
Rate through copper = K A \u0394T / l = 0.92 \xD7 4 \xD7 60 / 46 = 4.8 cal/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"A black coloured solid sphere of radius R and mass M is inside a cavity with vacuum inside. The walls of the cavity are maintained at temperature T\u2080. The initial temperature of the sphere is 3T\u2080. If the specific heat of the material of the sphere varies as \u03B1T\xB3 per unit mass with the temperature T of the sphere, where \u03B1 is a constant, then the time taken for the sphere to cool down to temperature 2T\u2080 will be (\u03C3 is Stefan Boltzmann constant)",o:["{M\u03B1;4\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3} ln(3/2)","{M\u03B1;4\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3} ln(16/3)","{M\u03B1;16\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3} ln(16/3)","{M\u03B1;16\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3} ln(3/2)"],c:2,s:`Net power lost by the sphere = 4\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3 (T\u2074 \u2212 T\u2080\u2074). Heat lost when T falls by dT: \u2212M \u03B1 T\xB3 dT.
So M \u03B1 T\xB3 (\u2212dT/dt) = 4\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3 (T\u2074 \u2212 T\u2080\u2074).
t = (M \u03B1 / 4\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3) \u222B T\xB3 dT/(T\u2074 \u2212 T\u2080\u2074) from 2T\u2080 to 3T\u2080 = (M \u03B1 / 4\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3) \xD7 (1/4) ln[(T\u2074 \u2212 T\u2080\u2074)] between the limits.
Ratio = (81 \u2212 1)T\u2080\u2074 / (16 \u2212 1)T\u2080\u2074 = 80/15 = 16/3.
t = (M \u03B1 / 16\u03C0R\xB2\u03C3) ln(16/3).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Parallel rays of light of intensity I = 912 W m\u207B\xB2 are incident on a spherical black body kept in surroundings of temperature 300 K. Take Stefan-Boltzmann constant \u03C3 = 5.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 W m\u207B\xB2 K\u207B\u2074 and assume that the energy exchange with the surroundings is only through radiation. The final steady state temperature of the black body is close to",o:["330 K","660 K","990 K","1550 K"],c:0,s:`The sphere of radius R absorbs power I \xD7 \u03C0 R\xB2 (its shadow area).
It radiates 4\u03C0 R\xB2 \u03C3 T\u2074 and absorbs 4\u03C0 R\xB2 \u03C3 T\u2080\u2074 from the surroundings.
Steady state: I \u03C0 R\xB2 = 4\u03C0 R\xB2 \u03C3 (T\u2074 \u2212 T\u2080\u2074), so T\u2074 = I/(4\u03C3) + T\u2080\u2074.
I/(4\u03C3) = 912/(4 \xD7 5.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078) = 4 \xD7 10\u2079 and T\u2080\u2074 = 8.1 \xD7 10\u2079, so T\u2074 = 1.21 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070.
T \u2248 332 K, close to 330 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"If a piece of metal is heated to temperature \u03B8 and then allowed to cool down in a room which is at temperature \u03B8\u2080, the graph between the temperature T of the metal and time t will be closest to",fig:{w:340,h:222,alt:"Four temperature against time graphs with the room temperature theta 0 shown dashed: (a) a curve falling steeply to theta 0 and then flat, (b) a straight line falling below theta 0, (c) a straight line falling to theta 0 and then flat, (d) a decaying exponential curve approaching theta 0.",d:[["l",30,95,165,95,"a"],["l",30,95,30,23,"a"],["t",165,23,"(a)","es"],["t",34,33,"T","s"],["t",165,107,"t","es"],["l",30,70,165,70,"d"],["t",27,73,"\u03B8\u2080","es"],["p","M30 33 C65 33 90 45 102 70 H160"],["l",195,95,330,95,"a"],["l",195,95,195,23,"a"],["t",330,23,"(b)","es"],["t",199,33,"T","s"],["t",330,107,"t","es"],["l",195,70,330,70,"d"],["t",192,73,"\u03B8\u2080","es"],["l",195,33,300,93],["l",30,205,165,205,"a"],["l",30,205,30,133,"a"],["t",165,133,"(c)","es"],["t",34,143,"T","s"],["t",165,217,"t","es"],["l",30,180,165,180,"d"],["t",27,183,"\u03B8\u2080","es"],["p","M30 143 L90 180 H160"],["l",195,205,330,205,"a"],["l",195,205,195,133,"a"],["t",330,133,"(d)","es"],["t",199,143,"T","s"],["t",330,217,"t","es"],["l",195,180,330,180,"d"],["t",192,183,"\u03B8\u2080","es"],["p","M195 143 Q225 178 325 179"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:3,s:`By Newton's law, the rate of cooling is proportional to (T \u2212 \u03B8\u2080). So T \u2212 \u03B8\u2080 = (\u03B8 \u2212 \u03B8\u2080) e^(\u2212kt).
T falls quickly at first when the difference is large, and the fall slows down as T gets close to \u03B8\u2080.
T approaches \u03B8\u2080 gradually (asymptotically) and never goes below it, and the curve has no kink or straight part.
This is the decaying exponential curve of graph (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Two rectangular blocks, having identical dimensions, can be arranged either in configuration I or in configuration II as shown in the figure. One of the blocks has thermal conductivity \u03BA and the other 2\u03BA. The temperature difference between the ends along the x-axis is the same in both the configurations. It takes 9 s to transport a certain amount of heat from the hot end to the cold end in the configuration I. The time to transport the same amount of heat in the configuration II is",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Configuration I: two blocks of conductivity K and 2K placed one after the other along x. Configuration II: the same two blocks placed one above the other, so heat flows through both side by side.",d:[["t",10,18,"Configuration I"],["r",60,28,70,40],["r",130,28,70,40],["t",95,52,"K","m"],["t",165,52,"2K","m"],["l",215,48,290,48,"a"],["t",296,52,"x"],["t",10,100,"Configuration II"],["r",60,110,70,35],["r",60,145,70,35],["t",95,132,"2K","m"],["t",95,167,"K","m"],["l",145,145,220,145,"a"],["t",226,149,"x"]]},o:["2.0 s","3.0 s","4.5 s","6.0 s"],c:0,s:`Let each block have length l and area A, with the same temperature difference \u0394T.
Configuration I (blocks end to end, series): total resistance = l/(\u03BAA) + l/(2\u03BAA) = 3l/(2\u03BAA). Heat current H\u2081 = 2\u03BAA\u0394T/(3l).
Configuration II (blocks side by side, parallel): conductance = \u03BAA/l + 2\u03BAA/l = 3\u03BAA/l. Heat current H\u2082 = 3\u03BAA\u0394T/l.
H\u2082/H\u2081 = 3 \xD7 3/2 = 9/2 = 4.5.
Same heat Q is moved, so time is inversely proportional to H: t\u2082 = 9/4.5 = 2 s.`},{y:"AIEEE 2012",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"A liquid in a beaker has temperature \u03B8(t) at time t and \u03B8\u2080 is temperature of surroundings, then according to Newton's law of cooling the correct graph between log\u2091(\u03B8 \u2212 \u03B8\u2080) and t is",fig:{w:340,h:222,alt:"Four graphs of log of temperature difference against time: (a) a concave downward falling curve, (b) an S-shaped falling curve, (c) a convex decaying curve, (d) a straight line falling.",d:[["l",30,95,165,95,"a"],["l",30,95,30,23,"a"],["t",165,23,"(a)","es"],["t",34,33,"log\u2091(\u03B8\u2212\u03B8\u2080)","s"],["t",165,107,"t","es"],["p","M30 33 Q125 33 155 87"],["l",195,95,330,95,"a"],["l",195,95,195,23,"a"],["t",330,23,"(b)","es"],["t",199,33,"log\u2091(\u03B8\u2212\u03B8\u2080)","s"],["t",330,107,"t","es"],["p","M195 35 C265 35 255 80 320 83"],["l",30,205,165,205,"a"],["l",30,205,30,133,"a"],["t",165,133,"(c)","es"],["t",34,143,"log\u2091(\u03B8\u2212\u03B8\u2080)","s"],["t",165,217,"t","es"],["p","M30 143 Q50 195 155 197"],["l",195,205,330,205,"a"],["l",195,205,195,133,"a"],["t",330,133,"(d)","es"],["t",199,143,"log\u2091(\u03B8\u2212\u03B8\u2080)","s"],["t",330,217,"t","es"],["l",195,143,320,197]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:3,s:`Newton's law gives \u03B8 \u2212 \u03B8\u2080 = (\u03B8_i \u2212 \u03B8\u2080) e^(\u2212kt).
Take the natural log: log\u2091(\u03B8 \u2212 \u03B8\u2080) = log\u2091(\u03B8_i \u2212 \u03B8\u2080) \u2212 k t.
This is a straight line in t with negative slope \u2212k and a positive intercept.
So the graph is a falling straight line, graph (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2012 \xB7 Online",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"A large cylindrical rod of length L is made by joining two identical rods of copper and steel of length (L/2) each. The rods are completely insulated from the surroundings. If the free end of the copper rod is maintained at 100\xB0C and that of steel at 0\xB0C then the temperature of junction is (Thermal conductivity of copper is 9 times that of steel)",o:["90\xB0C","50\xB0C","10\xB0C","67\xB0C"],c:0,s:`Same area and same length L/2, and the same heat current through both rods: K_cu (100 \u2212 T) = K_st (T \u2212 0).
9 K_st (100 \u2212 T) = K_st T.
900 \u2212 9T = T, so T = 90\xB0C.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Three very large plates of same area are kept parallel and close to each other. They are considered as ideal black surfaces and have very high thermal conductivity. The first and third plates are maintained at temperatures 2T and 3T respectively. The temperature of the middle (i.e. second) plate under steady state condition is",o:["(65/2)^(1/4) T","(97/4)^(1/4) T","(97/2)^(1/4) T","(97)^(1/4) T"],c:2,s:`The plates are black, so each absorbs all radiation falling on it. Per unit area, the middle plate absorbs \u03C3(2T)\u2074 from plate 1 and \u03C3(3T)\u2074 from plate 3.
It emits from both its faces, 2\u03C3 x\u2074, where x is its temperature.
Steady state: 2\u03C3 x\u2074 = \u03C3(16 + 81) T\u2074 = 97 \u03C3 T\u2074.
x\u2074 = (97/2) T\u2074, so x = (97/2)^(1/4) T.`},{y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"A long metallic bar is carrying heat from one of its ends to the other end under steady-state. The variation of temperature \u03B8 along the length x of the bar from its hot end is best described by which of the following figures?",fig:{w:340,h:222,alt:"Four graphs of temperature against distance from the hot end: (a) a hump, (b) a straight line falling, (c) a convex decaying curve, (d) a concave falling curve.",d:[["l",30,95,165,95,"a"],["l",30,95,30,23,"a"],["t",165,23,"(a)","es"],["t",34,33,"\u03B8","s"],["t",165,107,"x","es"],["p","M30 65 Q90 0 155 87"],["l",195,95,330,95,"a"],["l",195,95,195,23,"a"],["t",330,23,"(b)","es"],["t",199,33,"\u03B8","s"],["t",330,107,"x","es"],["l",195,33,320,87],["l",30,205,165,205,"a"],["l",30,205,30,133,"a"],["t",165,133,"(c)","es"],["t",34,143,"\u03B8","s"],["t",165,217,"x","es"],["p","M30 143 Q50 197 155 200"],["l",195,205,330,205,"a"],["l",195,205,195,133,"a"],["t",330,133,"(d)","es"],["t",199,143,"\u03B8","s"],["t",330,217,"x","es"],["p","M195 143 Q295 143 320 197"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:1,s:`In steady state the heat current H = \u2212K A d\u03B8/dx is the same at every section of a uniform bar (no loss from the sides).
So d\u03B8/dx is constant, meaning temperature falls linearly with x from the hot end.
This is a straight line with negative slope, graph (b).`},{y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"One end of a thermally insulated rod is kept at a temperature T\u2081 and the other at T\u2082. The rod is composed of two sections of lengths l\u2081 and l\u2082 and thermal conductivities K\u2081 and K\u2082 respectively. The temperature at the interface of the two sections is",fig:{w:340,h:120,alt:"A rod made of two sections in series: length l1 with conductivity K1 on the left, end at temperature T1, and length l2 with conductivity K2 on the right, end at temperature T2.",d:[["r",50,50,100,40,"f"],["r",150,50,100,40],["t",100,74,"K\u2081","m"],["t",200,74,"K\u2082","m"],["t",100,44,"l\u2081","m"],["t",200,44,"l\u2082","m"],["t",46,74,"T\u2081","e"],["t",254,74,"T\u2082"]]},o:["{K\u2081l\u2081T\u2081+K\u2082l\u2082T\u2082;K\u2081l\u2081+K\u2082l\u2082}","{K\u2082l\u2082T\u2081+K\u2081l\u2081T\u2082;K\u2081l\u2081+K\u2082l\u2082}","{K\u2082l\u2081T\u2081+K\u2081l\u2082T\u2082;K\u2082l\u2081+K\u2081l\u2082}","{K\u2081l\u2082T\u2081+K\u2082l\u2081T\u2082;K\u2081l\u2082+K\u2082l\u2081}"],c:3,s:`Let the interface temperature be T. The same heat current flows in both sections: K\u2081 A (T\u2081 \u2212 T)/l\u2081 = K\u2082 A (T \u2212 T\u2082)/l\u2082.
Multiply by l\u2081 l\u2082: K\u2081 l\u2082 (T\u2081 \u2212 T) = K\u2082 l\u2081 (T \u2212 T\u2082).
T (K\u2081 l\u2082 + K\u2082 l\u2081) = K\u2081 l\u2082 T\u2081 + K\u2082 l\u2081 T\u2082.
T = (K\u2081 l\u2082 T\u2081 + K\u2082 l\u2081 T\u2082)/(K\u2081 l\u2082 + K\u2082 l\u2081).`},{y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Assuming the sun to be a spherical body of radius R at a temperature of T K, evaluate the total radiant power, incident on earth, at a distance r from the sun, where r\u2080 is the radius of the earth and \u03C3 is Stefan's constant.",o:["{R\xB2\u03C3T\u2074;r\xB2}","{4\u03C0r\u2080\xB2R\xB2\u03C3T\u2074;r\xB2}","{\u03C0r\u2080\xB2R\xB2\u03C3T\u2074;r\xB2}","{r\u2080\xB2R\xB2\u03C3T\u2074;4\u03C0r\xB2}"],c:2,s:`Power radiated by the sun = 4\u03C0 R\xB2 \u03C3 T\u2074.
At distance r this spreads over a sphere of area 4\u03C0 r\xB2, so intensity = 4\u03C0 R\xB2 \u03C3 T\u2074/(4\u03C0 r\xB2) = R\xB2 \u03C3 T\u2074/r\xB2.
The earth intercepts the light on its cross-section area \u03C0 r\u2080\xB2.
Power received = \u03C0 r\u2080\xB2 R\xB2 \u03C3 T\u2074 / r\xB2.`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"The figure shows a system of two concentric spheres of radii r\u2081 and r\u2082 and kept at temperatures T\u2081 and T\u2082, respectively. The radial rate of flow of heat in a substance between the two concentric spheres is proportional to",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Two concentric circles: the inner sphere of radius r1 at temperature T1 and the outer sphere of radius r2 at temperature T2.",d:[["c",150,90,70],["c",150,90,35],["l",150,90,185,90],["l",150,90,203.6,45],["t",168,86,"r\u2081","ms"],["t",192,62,"r\u2082","s"],["t",124,94,"T\u2081","m"],["t",230,94,"T\u2082"]]},o:["{r\u2081r\u2082;r\u2082\u2212r\u2081}","(r\u2082\u2212r\u2081)","{r\u2082\u2212r\u2081;r\u2081r\u2082}","ln({r\u2082;r\u2081})"],c:0,s:`Take a thin spherical shell of radius r and thickness dr. Heat current H = \u2212K (4\u03C0 r\xB2) dT/dr.
So H dr/r\xB2 = \u22124\u03C0 K dT. Integrate from r\u2081 to r\u2082: H (1/r\u2081 \u2212 1/r\u2082) = 4\u03C0 K (T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082) in magnitude.
1/r\u2081 \u2212 1/r\u2082 = (r\u2082 \u2212 r\u2081)/(r\u2081 r\u2082), so H = 4\u03C0 K (T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082) r\u2081 r\u2082/(r\u2082 \u2212 r\u2081).
Hence H is proportional to r\u2081 r\u2082/(r\u2082 \u2212 r\u2081).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"A spherical body of area A and emissivity e = 0.6 is kept inside a perfectly black body. Energy radiated per second by the body at temperature T is",o:["0.4\u03C3AT\u2074","0.8\u03C3AT\u2074","0.6\u03C3AT\u2074","1.0\u03C3AT\u2074"],c:2,s:`The power radiated by a body of emissivity e, area A and temperature T is e \u03C3 A T\u2074.
Here e = 0.6, so the energy radiated per second = 0.6 \u03C3 A T\u2074.
(The surrounding black body only affects the energy absorbed, not what this body emits.)`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"In which of the following processes, convection does not take place primarily?",o:["sea and land breeze","boiling of water","heating air around a furnace","warming of glass of bulb due to filament."],c:3,s:`Convection needs a fluid whose heated part moves and carries heat.
Sea and land breeze, boiling of water and heating of air around a furnace all involve moving air or water, which is convection.
The glass of a bulb is a solid. It is warmed mainly by radiation and conduction from the filament, so convection is not the primary process.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Variation of radiant energy emitted by sun, filament of tungsten lamp and welding arc as a function of its wavelength is shown in figure. Which of the following option is the correct match?",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Graph of radiant energy against wavelength with three curves labelled T3 (highest, peak at the shortest wavelength), T2 and T1 (lowest, peak at the longest wavelength).",d:[["l",40,150,40,15,"a"],["l",40,150,300,150,"a"],["p","M45 148 C70 30 100 25 130 40 S200 75 270 90"],["p","M45 148 C80 60 110 55 140 68 S210 100 270 110"],["p","M45 148 C90 95 120 90 160 98 S220 120 270 128"],["t",276,92,"T\u2083"],["t",276,112,"T\u2082"],["t",276,132,"T\u2081"],["t",46,18,"E\u03BB"],["t",300,168,"\u03BB","e"]]},o:["Sun\u2013T\u2083, tungsten filament\u2013T\u2081, welding arc\u2013T\u2082","Sun\u2013T\u2082, tungsten filament\u2013T\u2081, welding arc\u2013T\u2083","Sun\u2013T\u2083, tungsten filament\u2013T\u2082, welding arc\u2013T\u2081","Sun\u2013T\u2081, tungsten filament\u2013T\u2082, welding arc\u2013T\u2083"],c:0,s:`A hotter body radiates more energy at every wavelength, and its peak moves to shorter wavelengths (Wien's law). So in the graph T\u2083 is the hottest and T\u2081 the coolest.
The sun (about 6000 K) is the hottest, so Sun\u2013T\u2083.
The welding arc (about 3500 K or more) is hotter than a tungsten filament (about 2500 to 3000 K).
So welding arc\u2013T\u2082 and tungsten filament\u2013T\u2081. This is option (a).`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"The temperature of the two outer surfaces of a composite slab, consisting of two materials having coefficients of thermal conductivity K and 2K and thickness x and 4x, respectively are T\u2082 and T\u2081 (T\u2082 > T\u2081). The rate of heat transfer through the slab, in a steady state is [{A(T\u2082\u2212T\u2081)K;x}] f, with f equal to",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"A composite slab: a layer of thickness x and conductivity K at temperature T2 on its outer face, then a layer of thickness 4x and conductivity 2K at T1 on its outer face.",d:[["r",70,40,40,70,"f"],["r",110,40,160,70],["t",90,80,"K","m"],["t",190,80,"2K","m"],["l",70,125,110,125,"b"],["t",90,141,"x","m"],["l",110,125,270,125,"b"],["t",190,141,"4x","m"],["t",66,78,"T\u2082","e"],["t",274,78,"T\u2081"]]},o:["1","1/2","2/3","1/3"],c:3,s:`The layers are in series (heat flows through one and then the other). Thermal resistances add: R = x/(K A) + 4x/(2K A) = x/(K A) + 2x/(K A) = 3x/(K A).
Heat rate H = (T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081)/R = K A (T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081)/(3x).
Compare with [A (T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081) K / x] f: f = 1/3.`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"If the temperature of the sun were to increase from T to 2T and its radius from R to 2R, then the ratio of the radiant energy received on earth to what it was previously will be",o:["4","16","32","64"],c:3,s:`Power radiated by the sun = \u03C3(4\u03C0R\xB2)T\u2074, so P \u221D R\xB2T\u2074.
The distance of the earth from the sun does not change, so the energy received on earth is proportional to P.
New P / old P = (2)\xB2 \xD7 (2)\u2074 = 4 \xD7 16 = 64.
So the ratio is 64.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Two identical rods are connected between two containers. One of them is at 100\xB0C containing water and another is at 0\xB0C containing ice. If rods are connected in parallel then the rate of melting of ice is q\u2081 g/s. If they are connected in series then the rate is q\u2082 g/s. The ratio q\u2082/q\u2081 is",o:["2","4","1/2","1/4"],c:3,s:`Let each rod have conductance G = KA/L. The rate of melting is proportional to the heat current.
Parallel: total conductance = 2G, so q\u2081 \u221D 2G \xD7 100.
Series: total conductance = G/2, so q\u2082 \u221D (G/2) \xD7 100.
q\u2082/q\u2081 = (G/2)/(2G) = 1/4.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Three discs A, B and C having radii 2, 4 and 6 cm respectively are coated with carbon black. Wavelengths for maximum intensity for the three discs are 300, 400 and 500 nm respectively. If Q_A, Q_B and Q_C are powers emitted by A, B and C respectively, then",o:["Q_A will be maximum","Q_B will be maximum","Q_C will be maximum","Q_A = Q_B = Q_C"],c:1,s:`Wien's law: T \u221D 1/\u03BB_m. Stefan's law: power Q \u221D r\xB2T\u2074 \u221D r\xB2/\u03BB_m\u2074.
Take \u03BB in units of 100 nm: Q_A \u221D 2\xB2/3\u2074 = 4/81 = 0.049.
Q_B \u221D 4\xB2/4\u2074 = 16/256 = 0.0625.
Q_C \u221D 6\xB2/5\u2074 = 36/625 = 0.0576.
Q_B is the largest.`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"According to Newton's law of cooling, the rate of cooling of a body is proportional to (\u0394\u03B8)\u207F, where \u0394\u03B8 is the difference of the temperature of the body and the surroundings, and n is equal to",o:["two","three","four","one"],c:3,s:`Newton's law of cooling says that the rate of cooling is directly proportional to the temperature difference between the body and its surroundings.
So rate \u221D (\u0394\u03B8)\xB9, which gives n = 1.`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"The earth radiates in the infra-red region of the spectrum. The wavelength of the maximum intensity of the spectrum is correctly given by",o:["Rayleigh Jeans law","Planck's law of radiation","Stefan's law of radiation","Wien's law"],c:3,s:`Wien's displacement law, \u03BB_m T = constant, gives the wavelength at which the emitted intensity is maximum for a given temperature.
Stefan's law gives only the total power; Rayleigh-Jeans and Planck's laws give the full spectrum shape.
So the answer is Wien's law.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"pst",st:"Temperature scales and thermal expansion",q:"Two rods, one of aluminum and the other made of steel, having initial lengths l\u2081 and l\u2082 are connected together to form a single rod of length l\u2081 + l\u2082. The co-efficients of linear expansion for aluminum and steel are \u03B1\u2090 and \u03B1\u209B respectively. If the length of each rod increases by the same amount when their temperature are raised by t\xB0C, then find the ratio l\u2081/(l\u2081 + l\u2082).",o:["\u03B1\u209B/\u03B1\u2090","\u03B1\u2090/\u03B1\u209B","\u03B1\u209B/(\u03B1\u2090 + \u03B1\u209B)","\u03B1\u2090/(\u03B1\u2090 + \u03B1\u209B)"],c:2,s:`Aluminium rod has length l\u2081, steel rod has length l\u2082.
Equal increase in length: l\u2081\u03B1\u2090t = l\u2082\u03B1\u209Bt, so l\u2081\u03B1\u2090 = l\u2082\u03B1\u209B, i.e. l\u2082 = l\u2081\u03B1\u2090/\u03B1\u209B.
l\u2081/(l\u2081 + l\u2082) = l\u2081 / (l\u2081 + l\u2081\u03B1\u2090/\u03B1\u209B) = \u03B1\u209B/(\u03B1\u209B + \u03B1\u2090).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"The graph, shown in the diagram, represents the variation of temperature (T) of two bodies, x and y having same surface area, with time (t) due to the emission of radiation. Find the correct relation between the emissivity and absorptivity powers of the two bodies.",fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"Temperature T against time t for two bodies x and y, both starting at the same high temperature; curve x falls faster than curve y.",d:[["l",50,150,50,20,"a"],["l",50,150,300,150,"a"],["t",42,28,"T","e"],["t",306,154,"t"],["p","M55 30 C 70 90 100 130 190 140"],["p","M55 30 C 90 60 150 100 250 118"],["t",196,138,"x"],["t",256,114,"y"]]},o:["E_x > E_y and a_x < a_y","E_x < E_y and a_x > a_y","E_x > E_y and a_x > a_y","E_x < E_y and a_x < a_y"],c:2,s:`Both bodies have the same surface area and start at the same temperature, but x cools faster (steeper curve).
A faster rate of cooling means a larger rate of emission, so the emissivity E_x > E_y.
By Kirchhoff's law, a good emitter is also a good absorber, so the absorptivity a_x > a_y.
So E_x > E_y and a_x > a_y.`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Two spheres of the same material have radii 1 m and 4 m and temperatures 4000 K and 2000 K respectively. The ratio of the energy radiated per second by the first sphere to that by the second is",o:["1 : 1","16 : 1","4 : 1","1 : 9"],c:0,s:`Energy radiated per second P = \u03C3(4\u03C0r\xB2)T\u2074 \u221D r\xB2T\u2074.
P\u2081/P\u2082 = (1/4)\xB2 \xD7 (4000/2000)\u2074 = (1/16) \xD7 16 = 1.
So the ratio is 1 : 1.`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Which of the following is more close to a black body?",o:["Black board paint","Green leaves","Black holes","Red roses"],c:0,s:`A good black body absorbs almost all radiation falling on it and emits the full thermal spectrum.
Of the given choices, black board paint absorbs nearly all visible light, while green leaves and red roses reflect green and red light.
So black board paint is the closest to a black body (the book key gives (a)).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"An ideal black-body at room temperature is thrown into a furnace. It is observed that",o:["initially it is the darkest body and at later times the brightest","it is the darkest body at all times","it cannot be distinguished at all times","initially it is the darkest body and at later times it cannot be distinguished"],c:0,s:`At room temperature the black body absorbs all light and emits very little visible light, so it looks the darkest.
When it is in the furnace it heats up and, being the best emitter, it emits more than any other body at the same temperature.
So it is the darkest at first and the brightest later.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Three rods made of same material and having the same cross-section have been joined as shown in the figure. Each rod is of the same length. The left and right ends are kept at 0\xB0C and 90\xB0C respectively. The temperature of the junction of the three rods will be",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Three equal rods meeting at a junction: one rod to the left with its end at 0\xB0C, and two rods going up-right and down-right with their ends at 90\xB0C.",d:[["l",70,100,170,100,"B"],["l",170,100,250,48,"B"],["l",170,100,250,152,"B"],["c",170,100,3,"F"],["t",64,104,"0\xB0C","e"],["t",256,50,"90\xB0C"],["t",256,158,"90\xB0C"],["t",170,124,"junction at T","m"]]},o:["45\xB0C","60\xB0C","30\xB0C","20\xB0C"],c:1,s:`Let the junction be at temperature T. All rods have the same K, A and L, so heat current \u221D temperature difference.
Heat arriving from the two 90\xB0C rods = heat leaving through the 0\xB0C rod.
2(90 \u2212 T) = T \u2212 0, so 180 = 3T.
T = 60\xB0C.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"The plots of intensity versus wavelength for three black bodies at temperatures T\u2081, T\u2082 and T\u2083 respectively are as shown. Their temperature are such that",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Intensity I against wavelength \u03BB for three curves: T\u2081 peaks at the shortest wavelength, T\u2083 peaks in the middle and is tallest, T\u2082 peaks at the longest wavelength.",d:[["l",30,170,30,20,"a"],["l",30,170,320,170,"a"],["t",22,28,"I","e"],["t",326,174,"\u03BB"],["p","M40 168 C 70 168 85 72 100 72 C 115 72 130 168 165 168"],["p","M70 168 C 110 168 135 40 155 40 C 175 40 200 168 250 168"],["p","M140 168 C 180 168 200 85 220 85 C 240 85 260 168 305 168"],["t",90,66,"T\u2081","m"],["t",155,34,"T\u2083","m"],["t",224,78,"T\u2082"]]},o:["T\u2081 > T\u2082 > T\u2083","T\u2081 > T\u2083 > T\u2082","T\u2082 > T\u2083 > T\u2081","T\u2083 > T\u2082 > T\u2081"],c:1,s:`Wien's law: \u03BB_m T = constant, so a shorter peak wavelength means a higher temperature.
From the graph, the peak wavelengths are in the order \u03BB(T\u2081) < \u03BB(T\u2083) < \u03BB(T\u2082).
So the temperatures are in the order T\u2081 > T\u2083 > T\u2082.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"A spherical black body with a radius of 12 cm radiates 450 W power at 500 K. If the radius is halved and the temperature doubled, the power radiated in watt would be",o:["225","450","900","1800"],c:3,s:`P = \u03C3(4\u03C0r\xB2)T\u2074 \u221D r\xB2T\u2074.
New radius = r/2 gives a factor (1/2)\xB2 = 1/4; doubling T gives a factor 2\u2074 = 16.
New power = 450 \xD7 (1/4) \xD7 16 = 450 \xD7 4 = 1800 W.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"The intensity of radiation emitted by the sun has its maximum value at a wavelength of 510 nm and that emitted by the North Star has the maximum value at a wavelength of 350 nm. If these stars behave like black bodies, then the ratio of the surface temperatures of the sun and the North Star is",o:["1.46","0.69","1.21","0.83"],c:1,s:`Wien's law: \u03BB_m T = constant, so T \u221D 1/\u03BB_m.
T_sun / T_star = \u03BB_star / \u03BB_sun = 350/510.
350/510 = 0.686, which is about 0.69.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1995",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Three rods of identical cross-sectional area and made from the same metal form the sides of an isosceles triangle ABC, right-angled at B. The points A and B are maintained at temperatures T and (\u221A2)T respectively. In the steady state, the temperature of the point C is T_C. Assuming that only heat conduction takes place, T_C/T is",o:["{1;2(\u221A2 \u2212 1)}","{3;\u221A2 + 1}","{1;\u221A3(\u221A2 \u2212 1)}","{1;\u221A2 + 1}"],c:1,s:`Let AB = BC = l, so AC = \u221A2 l. Heat flows from B (hottest) to C and from C to A.
Heat current in BC = KA(\u221A2T \u2212 T_C)/l. Heat current in CA = KA(T_C \u2212 T)/(\u221A2 l).
In steady state these are equal: \u221A2(\u221A2T \u2212 T_C) = T_C \u2212 T, so 2T \u2212 \u221A2T_C = T_C \u2212 T.
3T = T_C(\u221A2 + 1), so T_C/T = 3/(\u221A2 + 1).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1995",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Two metallic spheres S\u2081 and S\u2082 are made of the same material and have got identical surface finish. The mass of S\u2081 is thrice that of S\u2082. Both the spheres are heated to the same high temperature and placed in the same room having lower temperature but are thermally insulated from each other. The ratio of the initial rate of cooling of S\u2081 to that of S\u2082 is",o:["{1;3}","{1;\u221A3}","{\u221A3;1}","({1;3})^(1/3)"],c:3,s:`Rate of cooling = (heat lost per second)/(m s) = e\u03C3A(T\u2074 \u2212 T\u2080\u2074)/(m s). With the same material and finish and same temperatures, rate \u221D A/m.
m \u221D r\xB3 and A \u221D r\xB2, so rate \u221D 1/r \u221D m^(\u22121/3).
S\u2081 has 3 times the mass, so its rate of cooling is smaller by the factor 3^(\u22121/3).
Ratio = (1/3)^(1/3).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1980",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"A wall has two layers A and B, each made of different material. Both the layers have the same thickness. The thermal conductivity of the material of A is twice that of B. Under thermal equilibrium, the temperature difference across the wall is 36\xB0C. The temperature difference across the layer A is",o:["6\xB0C","12\xB0C","18\xB0C","24\xB0C"],c:1,s:`The layers are in series, so the same heat current flows through both: K_A A \u0394T_A / d = K_B A \u0394T_B / d.
Since K_A = 2K_B, \u0394T_A = \u0394T_B / 2.
\u0394T_A + \u0394T_B = 36, so 1.5 \u0394T_B = 36, \u0394T_B = 24\xB0C.
\u0394T_A = 12\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"A human body has a surface area of approximately 1 m\xB2. The normal body temperature is 10 K above the surrounding room temperature T\u2080. Take the room temperature to be T\u2080 = 300 K. For T\u2080 = 300 K, the value of \u03C3T\u2080\u2074 = 460 W m\u207B\xB2 (where \u03C3 is the Stefan Boltzmann constant). Which of the following option/s is/are correct?",o:["If the body temperature rises significantly then the peak in the spectrum of electromagnetic radiation emitted by the body would shift to longer wavelengths","If the surrounding temperature reduces by a small amount \u0394T\u2080 << T\u2080, then to maintain the same body temperature the same (living) human being needs to radiate \u0394W = 4\u03C3T\u2080\xB3\u0394T\u2080 more energy per unit time","The amount of energy radiated by the body in 1 second is close to 60 Joules","Reducing the exposed surface area of the body (e.g. by curling up) allows humans to maintain the same body temperature while reducing the energy lost by radiation"],c:[1,2,3],s:`(a) False. By Wien's law a hotter body has its peak at a shorter wavelength.
(b) True. Net loss W = \u03C3(T\u2074 \u2212 T\u2080\u2074) per m\xB2. Differentiating with T fixed: \u0394W = 4\u03C3T\u2080\xB3\u0394T\u2080 (more loss when the room is colder).
(c) True. Net energy lost in 1 s = \u03C3(310\u2074 \u2212 300\u2074) = 460 \xD7 [(310/300)\u2074 \u2212 1] = 460 \xD7 0.14 \u2248 64 J, which is close to 60 J.
(d) True. Net radiated power is proportional to the exposed area, so curling up reduces the loss.
So (b), (c) and (d) are correct.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"A composite block is made of slabs A, B, C, D and E of different thermal conductivities (given in terms of a constant K) and sizes (given in terms of length, L) as shown in the figure. All slabs are of same width. Heat Q flows only from left to right through the blocks. Then in steady state",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"Composite block: slab A (conductivity 2K, width 1L, full height 4L) on the left, slab E (6K, width 1L, full height) on the right, and between them slabs B (3K, height 1L) on top, C (4K, height 2L) in the middle and D (5K, height 1L) at the bottom, each 4L long. Heat flows left to right.",d:[["r",70,40,36,144],["r",106,40,144,36],["r",106,76,144,72],["r",106,148,144,36],["r",250,40,36,144],["t",88,108,"A","m"],["t",88,126,"2K","m"],["t",178,62,"B  3K","m"],["t",178,116,"C  4K","m"],["t",178,170,"D  5K","m"],["t",268,108,"E","m"],["t",268,126,"6K","m"],["t",70,32,"0","ms"],["t",106,32,"1L","ms"],["t",250,32,"5L","ms"],["t",286,32,"6L","ms"],["l",20,58,66,58,"a"],["l",20,112,66,112,"a"],["l",20,166,66,166,"a"],["t",26,40,"heat","s"],["t",64,60,"1L","es"],["t",64,160,"1L","es"],["t",292,60,"B: 1L","s"],["t",292,76,"C: 2L","s"],["t",292,92,"D: 1L","s"]]},o:["heat flow through A and E slabs are same","heat flow through slab E is maximum","temperature difference across slab E is smallest","heat flow through C = heat flow through B + heat flow through D"],c:[0,2,3],s:`Width is the same for all slabs, so use area \u221D height. A and E are 4L high and in series with the middle section (B, C, D in parallel).
(a) True. The same total heat Q passes through A and E.
(c) True. Resistance R = length/(K \xD7 height): A = 1/(8K), middle = 4L/(3K+8K+5K)L = 1/(4K), E = 1/(24K). E has the smallest R, so the smallest temperature drop (same Q flows through all).
(d) True. Heat in B : C : D \u221D K \xD7 height = 3K\xD71L : 4K\xD72L : 5K\xD71L = 3 : 8 : 5, so C = B + D.
(b) E carries the same Q as A (the whole current), so it is not the maximum, and the key does not accept it.
So (a), (c), (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Initially a black body at absolute temperature T is kept inside a closed chamber at absolute temperature T\u2080. Now the chamber is slightly opened to allow sun rays to enter. It is observed that temperatures T and T\u2080 remains constant. Which of the following statements is/are true?",o:["The rate of emission of energy from the black body remains the same","The rate of emission of energy from the blackbody increases","The rate of absorption of energy by the blackbody increases","The energy radiated by the black body equals the energy absorbed by it"],c:[0,3],s:`The rate of emission of a black body depends only on its own temperature (\u03C3T\u2074 \xD7 area). T stays constant, so emission stays the same: (a) true, (b) false.
Since T does not change, the body is in thermal equilibrium: energy absorbed per second equals energy radiated per second: (d) true.
Because emission is unchanged and equals absorption, the absorption rate also stays the same, so (c) is false (the sun rays only replace part of what the chamber walls supplied).
So (a) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"A black body is at a temperature of 2880 K. The energy of radiation emitted by this object with wavelength between 499 nm and 500 nm is U\u2081, between 999 nm and 1000 nm is U\u2082 and between 1499 nm and 1500 nm is U\u2083. The Wien constant b = 2.88 \xD7 10\u2076 nm K. Then",o:["U\u2081 = 0","U\u2083 = 0","U\u2081 > U\u2082","U\u2082 > U\u2081"],c:3,s:`Wien's law: \u03BB_m = b/T = 2.88\xD710\u2076 / 2880 = 1000 nm.
So the band 999-1000 nm contains the peak of the spectrum, where the emitted energy per unit wavelength is maximum: U\u2082 is the largest.
A black body emits at all wavelengths, so U\u2081 and U\u2083 are not zero.
U\u2081 (near 500 nm) is on the rising part of the curve, so it is less than U\u2082, i.e. U\u2082 > U\u2081.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Two bodies A and B have thermal emissivities of 0.01 and 0.81 respectively. The outer surface areas of the two bodies are the same. The two bodies emit total radiant power at the same rate. The wavelength \u03BB_B corresponding to maximum spectral radiancy in the radiation from B shifted from the wavelength corresponding to maximum spectral radiancy in the radiation from A, by 1.00 \u03BCm. If the temperature of A is 5802 K",o:["the temperature of B is 1934 K","\u03BB_B = 1.5 \u03BCm","the temperature of B is 11604 K","the temperature of B is 2901 K"],c:[0,1],s:`Same power and area: e_A T_A\u2074 = e_B T_B\u2074, so T_B\u2074 = (0.01/0.81) \xD7 T_A\u2074.
(0.01/0.81)^(1/4) = 1/3, so T_B = 5802/3 = 1934 K. So (a) is true.
Wien's law (b = 2898 \u03BCm K): \u03BB_A = 2898/5802 = 0.5 \u03BCm; \u03BB_B = 2898/1934 = 1.5 \u03BCm. The shift is 1.0 \u03BCm as given, so (b) is true.
(c) and (d) are wrong. Answer: (a) and (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"A cylinder of radius R, made of a material of thermal conductivity K\u2081, is surrounded by a cylindrical shell of inner radius R and outer radius 2R made of a material of thermal conductivity K\u2082. The two ends of the combined system are maintained at two different temperatures. There is no loss of heat across the cylindrical surface and the system is in steady state. The effective thermal conductivity of the system is",o:["K\u2081 + K\u2082","K\u2081K\u2082/(K\u2081 + K\u2082)","(K\u2081 + 3K\u2082)/4","(3K\u2081 + K\u2082)/4"],c:2,s:`The core and the shell lie side by side along the heat flow, so they are in parallel: total current = K\u2081A\u2081\u0394T/L + K\u2082A\u2082\u0394T/L.
Core area A\u2081 = \u03C0R\xB2. Shell area A\u2082 = \u03C0(2R)\xB2 \u2212 \u03C0R\xB2 = 3\u03C0R\xB2.
K_eff (A\u2081 + A\u2082) = K\u2081A\u2081 + K\u2082A\u2082, so K_eff \xD7 4\u03C0R\xB2 = K\u2081\u03C0R\xB2 + 3K\u2082\u03C0R\xB2.
K_eff = (K\u2081 + 3K\u2082)/4.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2025",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Two identical plates P and Q, radiating as perfect black bodies, are kept in vacuum at constant absolute temperatures T_P and T_Q, respectively, with T_Q < T_P, as shown in Fig. 1. The radiated power transferred per unit area from P to Q is W\u2080. Subsequently, two more plates, identical to P and Q, are introduced between P and Q, as shown in Fig. 2. Assume that heat transfer takes place only between adjacent plates. If the power transferred per unit area in the steady state in the direction from P to Q (Fig. 2) is W_S, then the ratio W\u2080/W_S is ____.",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Fig. 1: two parallel plates P and Q with an arrow W\u2080 from P to Q. Fig. 2: four parallel plates, P, two extra plates and Q, with an arrow W_S between each adjacent pair.",d:[["r",50,40,6,80,"F"],["r",130,40,6,80,"F"],["l",58,80,128,80,"a"],["t",92,72,"W\u2080","ms"],["t",53,32,"P","m"],["t",133,32,"Q","m"],["t",53,136,"T_P","ms m"],["t",133,136,"T_Q","ms m"],["t",92,158,"Fig. 1","m"],["r",190,40,6,80,"F"],["r",230,40,6,80,"F"],["r",270,40,6,80,"F"],["r",310,40,6,80,"F"],["l",198,80,228,80,"a"],["l",238,80,268,80,"a"],["l",278,80,308,80,"a"],["t",213,72,"W_S","ms m"],["t",253,72,"W_S","ms m"],["t",293,72,"W_S","ms m"],["t",193,32,"P","m"],["t",313,32,"Q","m"],["t",253,158,"Fig. 2","m"]]},n:3,s:`Black plates: net power per unit area between adjacent plates at T\u2081 and T\u2082 is \u03C3(T\u2081\u2074 \u2212 T\u2082\u2074).
Fig. 1: W\u2080 = \u03C3(T_P\u2074 \u2212 T_Q\u2074).
Fig. 2 has 4 plates, so 3 gaps in series. In steady state the same W_S crosses every gap, and the three temperature-fourth-power drops add up: 3W_S = \u03C3(T_P\u2074 \u2212 T_Q\u2074) = W\u2080.
So W\u2080/W_S = 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:`A wire of length 10 cm and diameter 0.5 mm is used in a bulb. The temperature of the wire is 1727\xB0C and power radiated by the wire is 94.2 W. Its emissivity is x/8 where x = ____.
(Given: \u03C3 = 6.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 W m\u207B\xB2 K\u207B\u2074, \u03C0 = 3.14 and assume that the emissivity of wire material is same at all wavelength.)`,n:5,s:`T = 1727 + 273 = 2000 K. Surface area A = \u03C0dL = 3.14 \xD7 0.5\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 0.1 = 1.57\xD710\u207B\u2074 m\xB2.
P = e\u03C3AT\u2074 = e \xD7 6\xD710\u207B\u2078 \xD7 1.57\xD710\u207B\u2074 \xD7 (2000)\u2074 = e \xD7 150.7 W.
94.2 = 150.7 e, so e = 0.625 = 5/8.
So x = 5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Two cylindrical rods A and B made of different materials, are joined in a straight line. The ratios of lengths, radii and thermal conductivities of these rods are: {L_A;L_B} = {1;2}, {r_A;r_B} = 2 and {K_A;K_B} = {1;2}. The free ends of rods A and B are maintained at 400 K, 200 K, respectively. The temperature of rods interface is ____ K, when equilibrium is established.",n:360,s:`Heat current H = KA \u0394T/L with area A \u221D r\xB2.
Conductance ratio G_A/G_B = (K_A/K_B)(r_A/r_B)\xB2(L_B/L_A) = (1/2)(4)(2) = 4.
In series, same current: G_A(400 \u2212 T) = G_B(T \u2212 200), so 4(400 \u2212 T) = T \u2212 200.
1800 = 5T, so T = 360 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:`Three conductors of same length having thermal conductivity k\u2081, k\u2082 and k\u2083 are connected as shown in figure. Area of cross sections of 1st and 2nd conductor are same and for 3rd conductor it is double of the 1st conductor. The temperatures are given in the figure. In steady state condition, the value of \u03B8 is ____ \xB0C.
(Given: k\u2081 = 60 J s\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9, k\u2082 = 120 J s\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9, k\u2083 = 135 J s\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9)`,fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Conductors 1 (k\u2081) and 2 (k\u2082) lie side by side on the left, one above the other, with their left ends at 100\xB0C; conductor 3 (k\u2083) on the right has double the cross-section; the junction is at \u03B8 and the right end at 0\xB0C.",d:[["r",70,60,100,40],["r",70,100,100,40],["r",170,60,100,80],["t",120,84,"1.  k\u2081","m"],["t",120,124,"2.  k\u2082","m"],["t",220,104,"3.  k\u2083","m"],["t",70,50,"100\xB0C","m"],["t",170,50,"\u03B8","m"],["t",270,50,"0\xB0C","m"],["t",170,160,"Area of 3 = 2 \xD7 area of 1","m","s"]]},n:40,s:`Conductors 1 and 2 are in parallel (same temperatures at both ends), and this combination is in series with conductor 3.
Take A as the area of 1: conductance \u221D kA/L. Parallel part: (60 + 120)A = 180A. Conductor 3: 135 \xD7 2A = 270A.
Same current in both parts: 180A(100 \u2212 \u03B8) = 270A(\u03B8 \u2212 0).
18000 = 450\u03B8, so \u03B8 = 40\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Two plates A and B have thermal conductivities 84 W m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 and 126 W m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 respectively. They have same surface area and same thickness. They are placed in contact along their surfaces. If the temperatures of the outer surfaces of A and B are kept at 100\xB0C and 0\xB0C respectively, then the temperature of the surface of contact in steady state is ____ \xB0C.",n:40,s:`The plates are in series, so the same heat current flows: K_A A (100 \u2212 T)/d = K_B A (T \u2212 0)/d.
84(100 \u2212 T) = 126T.
8400 = 210T.
T = 40\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"A body cools from 60\xB0C to 40\xB0C in 6 minutes. If, temperature of surroundings is 10\xB0C. Then, after the next 6 minute, its temperature will be ____ \xB0C.",n:28,s:`Use the average-temperature form of Newton's law: (T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082)/t = k[(T\u2081 + T\u2082)/2 \u2212 T\u2080].
First 6 min: (60 \u2212 40)/6 = k(50 \u2212 10), so k = 1/12 per minute.
Next 6 min: (40 \u2212 T)/6 = (1/12)[(40 + T)/2 \u2212 10] = (T + 20)/24.
4(40 \u2212 T) = T + 20, so 140 = 5T and T = 28\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"In an experiment to verify Newton's law of cooling, a graph is plotted between the temperature difference (\u0394T) of the water and surroundings and time as shown in figure. The initial temperature of water is taken as 80\xB0C. The value of t\u2082 as mentioned in the graph will be ____.",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"Graph of temperature difference \u0394T (\xB0C) against time in minutes: an exponential decay from 60 at t = 0 to 40 at t = 6 minutes and to 20 at t = t\u2082.",d:[["l",50,170,50,30,"a"],["l",50,170,330,170,"a"],["t",56,26,"\u0394T (\xB0C)","s"],["t",335,200,"Time (minute)","es"],["l",46,65,50,65],["l",46,100,50,100],["l",46,135,50,135],["t",42,69,"60","e"],["t",42,104,"40","e"],["t",42,139,"20","e"],["p","M50 65 L83 84.3 L116 100 L149 112.9 L182 123.3 L229 135 L270 142.9 L314 149.3"],["l",116,170,116,100,"d"],["l",50,100,116,100,"d"],["l",229,170,229,135,"d"],["l",50,135,229,135,"d"],["t",116,186,"6","m"],["t",229,186,"t\u2082","m"]]},n:16,s:`Newton's law: \u0394T = \u0394T\u2080 e^(\u2212kt), with \u0394T\u2080 = 60\xB0C at t = 0.
At t = 6 min, \u0394T = 40: e^(\u22126k) = 2/3, so 6k = ln(3/2) = 0.405.
At t = t\u2082, \u0394T = 20: e^(\u2212kt\u2082) = 1/3, so kt\u2082 = ln 3 = 1.1.
t\u2082 = 6 \xD7 ln3 / ln1.5 = 6 \xD7 1.1/0.405 \u2248 16 minutes.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"As per the given figure, two plates A and B of thermal conductivity K and 2K are joined together to form a compound plate. The thickness of plates are 4.0 cm and 2.5 cm respectively and the area of cross-section is 120 cm\xB2 for each plate. The equivalent thermal conductivity of the compound plate is (1 + 5/\u03B1)K, then the value of \u03B1 will be ____.",fig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"Compound plate: plate A of conductivity K and thickness 4.0 cm joined to plate B of conductivity 2K and thickness 2.5 cm; the free face of A is at 100\xB0C and the free face of B at 0\xB0C.",d:[["r",70,60,80,40],["r",150,60,50,40],["t",110,84,"K","m"],["t",175,84,"2K","m"],["t",74,56,"A","s"],["t",194,56,"B","s"],["t",66,84,"100\xB0C","e"],["t",206,84,"0\xB0C"],["l",70,42,150,42,"b"],["l",150,42,200,42,"b"],["t",110,36,"4.0 cm","ms m"],["t",175,36,"2.5 cm","ms m"]]},n:21,s:`The plates are in series, so K_eq = (d\u2081 + d\u2082)/(d\u2081/K\u2081 + d\u2082/K\u2082).
K_eq = (4 + 2.5) / (4/K + 2.5/(2K)) = 6.5 / (5.25/K) = (6.5/5.25)K = (26/21)K.
26/21 = 1 + 5/21.
Comparing with 1 + 5/\u03B1 gives \u03B1 = 21.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"A small object is placed at the center of a large evacuated hollow spherical container. Assume that the container is maintained at 0 K. At time t = 0, the temperature of the object is 200 K. The temperature of the object becomes 100 K at t = t\u2081 and 50 K at t = t\u2082. Assume the object and the container to be ideal black bodies. The heat capacity of the object does not depend on temperature. The ratio (t\u2082/t\u2081) is ____.",n:9,s:`The container is at 0 K, so the object only loses heat: C dT/dt = \u2212\u03C3AT\u2074, i.e. dT/dt = \u2212kT\u2074.
Integrating: 1/T\xB3 \u2212 1/T\u2080\xB3 = 3kt.
At t\u2081 (T = 100): 1/100\xB3 \u2212 1/200\xB3 = 10\u207B\u2076 \u2212 1.25\xD710\u207B\u2077 = 8.75\xD710\u207B\u2077 = 3kt\u2081.
At t\u2082 (T = 50): 1/50\xB3 \u2212 1/200\xB3 = 8\xD710\u207B\u2076 \u2212 1.25\xD710\u207B\u2077 = 7.875\xD710\u207B\u2076 = 3kt\u2082.
t\u2082/t\u2081 = 7.875/0.875 = 9.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"psh",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"A container with 1 kg of water in it is kept in sunlight, which causes the water to get warmer than the surroundings. The average energy per unit time per unit area received due to the sunlight is 700 W m\u207B\xB2 and it is absorbed by the water over an effective area of 0.05 m\xB2. Assuming that the heat loss from the water to the surroundings is governed by Newton's law of cooling, the difference (in \xB0C) in the temperature of water and the surroundings after a long time will be ____. (Ignore effect of the container, and take constant for Newton's law of cooling = 0.001 s\u207B\xB9, Heat capacity of water = 4200 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9)",n:8.33,s:`Power absorbed = 700 \xD7 0.05 = 35 W.
Newton's law: heat loss rate = k \xD7 (mc) \xD7 \u0394T = 0.001 \xD7 4200 \xD7 \u0394T = 4.2 \u0394T watts.
After a long time the water stops warming, so loss equals gain: 4.2 \u0394T = 35.
\u0394T = 35/4.2 = 8.33\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Two conducting cylinders of equal length but different radii are connected in series between two heat baths kept at temperatures T\u2081 = 300 K and T\u2082 = 100 K, as shown in the figure. The radius of the bigger cylinder is twice that of the smaller one and the thermal conductivities of the materials of the smaller and the larger cylinders are K\u2081 and K\u2082 respectively. If the temperature at the junction of the two cylinders in the steady state is 200 K, then K\u2081/K\u2082 = ____.",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Two cylinders of equal length L in series between a bath at T\u2081 on the left and a bath at T\u2082 on the right; the left cylinder (K\u2081) is thinner, the right cylinder (K\u2082) has twice the radius; the whole is surrounded by insulating material.",d:[["r",12,76,24,48,"f"],["r",38,82,110,36],["r",148,64,110,72],["r",258,76,24,48,"f"],["l",38,52,258,52,"d"],["l",38,148,258,148,"d"],["t",148,44,"Insulating material","ms m"],["t",24,70,"T\u2081","m"],["t",270,70,"T\u2082","m"],["t",93,104,"K\u2081","m"],["t",203,104,"K\u2082","m"],["l",38,166,148,166,"b"],["l",148,166,258,166,"b"],["t",93,182,"L","m"],["t",203,182,"L","m"]]},n:4,s:`In series, the same heat current flows through both cylinders: K\u2081A\u2081(300 \u2212 200)/L = K\u2082A\u2082(200 \u2212 100)/L.
Area \u221D r\xB2, and the bigger radius is twice the smaller, so A\u2082 = 4A\u2081.
K\u2081 \xD7 100 = K\u2082 \xD7 4 \xD7 100.
K\u2081/K\u2082 = 4 (4.00).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"A metal is heated in a furnace where a sensor is kept above the metal surface to read the power radiated (P) by the metal. The sensor has a scale that displays log\u2082(P/P\u2080), where P\u2080 is a constant. When the metal surface is at a temperature of 487\xB0C, the sensor shows a value 1. Assume that the emissivity of the metallic surface remains constant. What is the value displayed by the sensor when the temperature of the metal surface is raised to 2767\xB0C?",n:9,s:`Convert to kelvin: 487\xB0C = 760 K and 2767\xB0C = 3040 K, a ratio of exactly 4.
At 760 K the sensor shows 1, so P/P\u2080 = 2\xB9 = 2.
P \u221D T\u2074 (constant emissivity), so at 3040 K the power is 4\u2074 = 256 times larger: P/P\u2080 = 2 \xD7 256 = 512.
log\u2082 512 = 9.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Two spherical stars A and B emit blackbody radiation. The radius of A is 400 times that of B and A emits 10\u2074 times the power emitted from B. The ratio (\u03BB_A/\u03BB_B) of their wavelengths \u03BB_A and \u03BB_B at which the peaks occur in their respective radiation curves is ____.",n:2,s:`Power P = \u03C3(4\u03C0R\xB2)T\u2074 \u221D R\xB2T\u2074.
P_A/P_B = (400)\xB2 (T_A/T_B)\u2074 = 10\u2074, so (T_A/T_B)\u2074 = 10\u2074/(1.6\xD710\u2075) = 1/16.
T_A/T_B = 1/2.
Wien's law: \u03BB \u221D 1/T, so \u03BB_A/\u03BB_B = T_B/T_A = 2.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"Two spherical bodies A (radius 6 cm) and B (radius 18 cm) are at temperatures T\u2081 and T\u2082, respectively. The maximum intensity in the emission spectrum of A is at 500 nm and in that of B is at 1500 nm. Considering them to be black bodies, what will be the ratio of the rate of total energy radiated by A to that of B?",n:9,s:`Wien's law: T \u221D 1/\u03BB_m, so T\u2081/T\u2082 = 1500/500 = 3.
Rate of radiation P = \u03C3(4\u03C0r\xB2)T\u2074 \u221D r\xB2T\u2074.
P_A/P_B = (6/18)\xB2 \xD7 3\u2074 = (1/9) \xD7 81.
The ratio is 9.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"A metal rod AB of length 10x has one end A in ice at 0\xB0C and the other end B in water at 100\xB0C. If a point P on the rod is maintained at 400\xB0C, then it is found that equal amounts of water and ice evaporate and melt per unit time. The latent heat of evaporation of water is 540 cal/g and latent heat of melting of ice is 80 cal/g. If the point P is at a distance of \u03BBx from the ice end A, find the value of \u03BB. [Neglect any heat loss to the surrounding.]",n:9,s:`Heat flows from P to A (melting ice) and from P to B (evaporating water). Equal masses m per unit time are changed.
P to A: KA(400 \u2212 0)/(\u03BBx) = m \xD7 80.
P to B: KA(400 \u2212 100)/((10 \u2212 \u03BB)x) = m \xD7 540.
Divide: [400/\u03BB] / [300/(10 \u2212 \u03BB)] = 80/540 = 4/27, so (4/3)(10 \u2212 \u03BB)/\u03BB = 4/27.
(10 \u2212 \u03BB)/\u03BB = 1/9, so 90 \u2212 9\u03BB = \u03BB, hence \u03BB = 9.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pst",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I: (A) Heat capacity of body; (B) Specific heat capacity of body; (C) Latent heat; (D) Thermal conductivity
List-II: (I) J kg\u207B\xB9; (II) J K\u207B\xB9; (III) J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9; (IV) J m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 s\u207B\xB9
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)"],c:3,s:`Heat capacity = Q/\u0394T, unit J K\u207B\xB9: (A)-(II).
Specific heat capacity = Q/(m \u0394T), unit J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9: (B)-(III).
Latent heat = Q/m, unit J kg\u207B\xB9: (C)-(I).
Thermal conductivity K = Q L/(A \u0394T t), unit J m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 s\u207B\xB9 (same as W m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9): (D)-(IV).
So A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"psh",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Column I gives some devices and column II gives some processes on which the functioning of these devices depend. Match the device in column I with the processes in column II.
Column I: (a) Bimetallic strip; (b) Steam engine; (c) Incandescent lamp; (d) Electric fuse
Column II: (p) Radiation from a hot body; (q) Energy conversion; (r) Melting; (s) Thermal expansion of solids`,a:"a\u2192(s); b\u2192(q); c\u2192(p); d\u2192(r)",s:`(a) A bimetallic strip bends because its two metals expand by different amounts: thermal expansion of solids (s).
(b) A steam engine converts heat energy into mechanical work: energy conversion (q).
(c) An incandescent lamp glows because its hot filament radiates light: radiation from a hot body (p).
(d) A fuse wire melts when too much current heats it: melting (r).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"psh",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A cylindrical rod of length l, thermal conductivity K and area of cross-section A has one end in the furnace at temperature T\u2081 and the other end in surrounding at temperature T\u2082. Surface of the rod exposed to the surrounding has emissivity e. Also T\u2082 = T\u209B + \u0394T and T\u209B >> \u0394T.
If T\u2081 \u2212 T\u209B \u221D \u0394T, find the proportionality constant.`,a:"1 + {4e\u03C3 l T\u209B\xB3;K}",s:`Steady state: heat conducted along the rod = heat radiated from the exposed end face.
KA(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082)/l = e\u03C3A(T\u2082\u2074 \u2212 T\u209B\u2074).
Since \u0394T << T\u209B: T\u2082\u2074 \u2212 T\u209B\u2074 = (T\u209B + \u0394T)\u2074 \u2212 T\u209B\u2074 \u2248 4T\u209B\xB3\u0394T.
So T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082 = (4e\u03C3 l T\u209B\xB3/K) \u0394T.
T\u2081 \u2212 T\u209B = (T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082) + (T\u2082 \u2212 T\u209B) = [1 + 4e\u03C3 l T\u209B\xB3/K] \u0394T, so the constant is 1 + 4e\u03C3 l T\u209B\xB3/K.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"psh",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Hot oil is circulated through an insulated container with a wooden lid at the top whose conductivity K = 0.149 J/(m-\xB0C-sec), thickness t = 5 mm, emissivity = 0.6. The oil under the lid is at temperature T\u2080. Temperature of the top of the lid is maintained at T_l = 127\xB0C. The ambient temperature T_a = 27\xB0C. Calculate
(a) rate of heat loss per unit area due to radiation from the lid.
(b) temperature of the oil.
(Given \u03C3 = {17;3} \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 W m\u207B\xB2 K\u207B\u2074)`,a:"(a) 595 W/m\xB2   (b) 420 K (147\xB0C)",s:`(a) Radiation loss per unit area = e\u03C3(T_l\u2074 \u2212 T_a\u2074), with T_l = 400 K and T_a = 300 K.
= 0.6 \xD7 (17/3)\xD710\u207B\u2078 \xD7 (400\u2074 \u2212 300\u2074) = 0.6 \xD7 5.67\xD710\u207B\u2078 \xD7 1.75\xD710\xB9\u2070 \u2248 595 W/m\xB2.
(b) In steady state the heat conducted through the lid equals this loss: K(T\u2080 \u2212 T_l)/t = 595.
T\u2080 \u2212 127 = 595 \xD7 0.005/0.149 \u2248 20\xB0C.
T\u2080 = 147\xB0C = 420 K.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"psh",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A solid body X of heat capacity C is kept in an atmosphere whose temperature is T_A = 300 K. At time t = 0, the temperature of X is T\u2080 = 400 K. It cools according to Newton's law of cooling. At time t\u2081, its temperature is found to be 350 K. At this time (t\u2081), the body X is connected to a large box Y at atmospheric temperature T_A, through a conducting rod of length L, cross-sectional area A and thermal conductivity K. The heat capacity of Y is so large that any variation in its temperature may be neglected. The cross-sectional area A of the connecting rod is small compared to the surface area of X. Find the temperature of X at time t = 3t\u2081.",a:"T = 300 + 12.5 e^(\u22122KAt\u2081/(CL)) kelvin",s:`Newton's law with the atmosphere: T \u2212 300 = 100 e^(\u2212kt). At t\u2081: 50 = 100 e^(\u2212kt\u2081), so e^(\u2212kt\u2081) = 1/2.
After t\u2081 the rod gives an extra heat loss KA(T \u2212 300)/L. So C dT/dt = \u2212CkT' \u2212 (KA/L)T', with T' = T \u2212 300.
So T' decays with rate constant k + KA/(CL).
From t\u2081 to 3t\u2081 (a time 2t\u2081): T' = 50 \xD7 e^(\u22122kt\u2081) \xD7 e^(\u22122KAt\u2081/(CL)) = 50 \xD7 (1/4) \xD7 e^(\u22122KAt\u2081/(CL)).
T = 300 + 12.5 e^(\u22122KAt\u2081/(CL)) K.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"psh",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A double-pane window used for insulating a room thermally from outside consists of two glass sheets each of area 1 m\xB2 and thickness 0.01 m separated by a 0.05 m thick stagnant air space. In the steady state, the room glass inter-face and the glass-outdoor interface are at constant temperatures of 27\xB0C and 0\xB0C respectively. Calculate the rate of heat flow through the window pane. Also find the temperature of other interfaces. Given thermal conductivities of glass and air as 0.8 and 0.08 W m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 respectively.",a:"41.54 J/s; interface temperatures 26.48\xB0C and 0.52\xB0C",s:`The glass, air, glass layers are in series. Thermal resistance of each layer R = d/(KA).
R_glass = 0.01/(0.8 \xD7 1) = 0.0125 K/W (each); R_air = 0.05/(0.08 \xD7 1) = 0.625 K/W.
Total R = 0.0125 + 0.625 + 0.0125 = 0.65 K/W, so rate of heat flow H = 27/0.65 = 41.54 J/s.
Temperature drop across one glass sheet = H \xD7 R_glass = 41.54 \xD7 0.0125 = 0.52\xB0C.
So the glass-air interface on the room side is at 27 \u2212 0.52 = 26.48\xB0C, and the air-glass interface on the outer side is at 0 + 0.52 = 0.52\xB0C.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"psh",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A cylindrical block of length 0.4 m and area of cross-section 0.04 m\xB2 is placed coaxially on a thin metal disc of mass 0.4 kg and of the same cross-section. The upper face of the cylinder is maintained at a constant temperature of 400 K and the initial temperature of the disc is 300 K. If the thermal conductivity of the material of the cylinder is 10 watt/mK and the specific heat of the material of the disc is 600 J/kgK, how long will it take for the temperature of the disc to increase to 350 K? Assume, for purpose of calculation, the thermal conductivity of the disc to be very high and the system to be thermally insulated except for the upper face of the cylinder.",a:"166.38 s",s:`Heat flows through the cylinder into the disc at the rate KA(400 \u2212 T)/L, where T is the disc temperature.
This heats the disc: ms dT/dt = KA(400 \u2212 T)/L.
So dT/(400 \u2212 T) = [KA/(Lms)] dt, with KA/(Lms) = (10 \xD7 0.04)/(0.4 \xD7 0.4 \xD7 600) = 0.4/96 = 1/240 s\u207B\xB9.
Integrate from 300 K to 350 K: ln[(400 \u2212 300)/(400 \u2212 350)] = t/240, so t = 240 ln 2.
t = 240 \xD7 0.693 \u2248 166.4 s (166.38 s).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"psh",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"An electric heater is used in a room of total wall area 137 m\xB2 to maintain a temperature of +20\xB0C inside it, when the outside temperature is \u221210\xB0C. The walls have three different layer materials. The innermost layer is of wood of thickness 2.5 cm, the middle layer is of cement of thickness 1.0 cm and the outermost layer is of brick of thickness 25.0 cm. Find the power of the electric heater. Assume that there is no heat through the floor and the ceiling. The thermal conductivities of wood, cement and brick are 0.125, 1.5 and 1.0 watt/m\xB0C respectively.",a:"9000 W",s:`The three layers are in series. Thermal resistance per unit area = \u03A3 d/K.
Wood: 0.025/0.125 = 0.2. Cement: 0.01/1.5 = 0.00667. Brick: 0.25/1.0 = 0.25. Total = 0.4567 m\xB2 \xB0C/W.
Heat flow per unit area = \u0394T/R = 30/0.4567 = 65.7 W/m\xB2.
Total heat lost = 65.7 \xD7 137 \u2248 9000 W.
The heater must supply 9000 W to balance this loss.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"psh",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A solid sphere of copper of radius R and a hollow sphere of the same material of inner radius r and outer radius R are heated to the same temperature and allowed to cool in the same environment. Which of them starts cooling faster?",a:"The hollow sphere",s:`Both spheres have the same outer surface area (4\u03C0R\xB2), the same temperature and the same surface, so both lose heat by radiation at the same rate at the start.
Rate of fall of temperature = (heat lost per second)/(m s).
The hollow sphere has less mass but the same heat loss, and the material (so s) is the same.
So its temperature falls faster: the hollow sphere starts cooling faster.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"psh",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Two spheres of the same material have radii 1 m and 4 m and temperatures 4000 K and 2000 K respectively. The energy radiated per second by the first sphere is greater than that by the second.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`Energy radiated per second P \u221D r\xB2T\u2074.
P\u2081/P\u2082 = (1/4)\xB2 \xD7 (4000/2000)\u2074 = (1/16) \xD7 16 = 1.
The two spheres radiate equal energy per second, so the statement is false.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"psh",st:"Conduction",q:"Two metal cubes A and B of same size are arranged as shown in figure. The extreme ends of the combination are maintained at the indicated temperatures (100\xB0C and 0\xB0C). The arrangement is thermally insulated. The co-efficients of thermal conductivity of A and B are 300 W/m\xB0C and 200 W/m\xB0C, respectively. After steady state is reached the temperature T of the interface will be ____ \xB0C.",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Two cubes A (conductivity K\u2081) and B (conductivity K\u2082) touching side by side; the free face of A is at 100\xB0C, the free face of B at 0\xB0C, and the interface is at T.",d:[["r",110,60,60,60],["r",170,60,60,60],["t",140,96,"A","m"],["t",200,96,"B","m"],["t",104,94,"100\xB0C","e"],["t",236,94,"0\xB0C"],["t",140,138,"K\u2081","m"],["t",170,138,"T","m"],["t",200,138,"K\u2082","m"]]},n:60,s:`The cubes have the same size (same A and d) and are in series, so the same heat current flows through both.
K\u2081(100 \u2212 T) = K\u2082(T \u2212 0), with K\u2081 = 300 and K\u2082 = 200.
300(100 \u2212 T) = 200T, so 30000 = 500T.
T = 60\xB0C.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1991",tp:"psh",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A point source of heat of power P is placed at the centre of a spherical shell of mean radius R. The material of the shell has thermal conductivity K. If the temperature difference between the outer and inner surface of the shell is not to exceed T, the thickness of the shell should not be less than ____.",a:"4\u03C0R\xB2KT/P",s:`In steady state, all the heat P from the source flows out through the shell.
Treat the shell as thin compared with R, so its area is about 4\u03C0R\xB2 (the mean radius).
P = K (4\u03C0R\xB2) T/d, where d is the thickness and T the allowed temperature difference.
So d = 4\u03C0R\xB2KT/P; the thickness must not be less than this.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1991",tp:"psh",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A solid copper sphere (density \u03C1 and specific heat s) of radius r at an initial temperature 200 K is suspended inside a chamber whose walls are at almost 0 K. The time required for the temperature of the sphere to drop to 100 K is ____.",a:"\u2248 1.71 \u03C1 r s seconds (in SI units)",s:`Treat the sphere as a black body (e = 1) in a chamber at about 0 K, so it only loses heat: m s dT/dt = \u2212\u03C3(4\u03C0r\xB2)T\u2074.
With m = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C1: dT/dt = \u22123\u03C3T\u2074/(r\u03C1s).
Separate and integrate from 200 K to 100 K: t = [r\u03C1s/(3\u03C3)] \xD7 (1/3)(1/100\xB3 \u2212 1/200\xB3) = [r\u03C1s/(3\u03C3)] \xD7 2.917\xD710\u207B\u2077.
Put \u03C3 = 5.67\xD710\u207B\u2078 W m\u207B\xB2 K\u207B\u2074: t = r\u03C1s \xD7 2.917\xD710\u207B\u2077/(1.70\xD710\u207B\u2077) \u2248 1.71 \u03C1 r s seconds.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"psh",st:"Convection and radiation",q:"The earth receives radiation, at its surface, from the sun at the rate of 1400 W m\u207B\xB2. The distance of the centre of the sun from the surface of the earth is 1.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9 m and the radius of the sun is 7 \xD7 10\u2078 m. Treating the sun as a black body, it follows from the above data that its surface temperature is ____ K.",n:5803,s:`Power emitted by the sun = \u03C3(4\u03C0R_s\xB2)T\u2074 (black body).
This spreads over a sphere of radius d, so the intensity at the earth is I = \u03C3R_s\xB2T\u2074/d\xB2 = 1400.
\u03C3T\u2074 = 1400 \xD7 (d/R_s)\xB2 = 1400 \xD7 (1.5\xD710\xB9\xB9/7\xD710\u2078)\xB2 = 1400 \xD7 4.59\xD710\u2074 = 6.43\xD710\u2077 W/m\xB2.
T\u2074 = 6.43\xD710\u2077/(5.67\xD710\u207B\u2078) = 1.134\xD710\xB9\u2075, so T \u2248 5803 K.`}]})();
